th the integer lattice points  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe “triangular’’ set that has to be covered.\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma (Base case)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) there exists a set of three distinct lines covering \\(T_3\\) and containing exactly one sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nTake the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+4 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_1\\) and \\(\\ell_2\\) are non‑sunny (vertical and horizontal).  \n\\(\\ell_3\\) has slope \\(-2\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\), hence it is sunny.  \n\\(\\ell_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); \\(\\ell_2\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); \\(\\ell_3\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and exactly one line (\\(\\ell_3\\)) is sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive Construction\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\);\n2. exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) is sunny (call it \\(\\ell_s\\));\n3. among the non‑sunny lines there is at least one vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\le n\\) and at least one horizontal line \\(y=d\\) with \\(d\\le n\\).\n\n(The existence of such a vertical and a horizontal line is guaranteed by the construction in the base case and will be preserved by the inductive step.)\n\nWe shall produce a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines satisfying the same two properties for the larger triangle \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step 1 – covering the new border points\n\nThe set \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\nB_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points on the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) with both coordinates at least \\(1\\).  \nWe cover all points of \\(B_{n+1}\\) by adding a **new vertical line**  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{new}: x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b=n+2\\), we have \\(a=n+1\\) precisely when \\(b=1\\); thus \\(\\ell_{new}\\) contains the point \\((n+1,1)\\).  \nAll remaining points of \\(B_{n+1}\\) have abscissa \\(a\\le n\\); each such point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=n+2-a\\ge 2\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step 2 – adjusting an existing horizontal line\n\nBecause of property 3 there exists a horizontal line \\(\\ell_h\\) in \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of the form \\(y=d\\) with \\(d\\le n\\).  \nReplace \\(\\ell_h\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h' : y = d+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_h'\\) is still non‑sunny (horizontal) and is distinct from all previous lines because its ordinate has increased by one.  \n\n*Why does this replacement preserve coverage of the old points?*  \n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) that lay on \\(\\ell_h\\) has coordinates \\((a,d)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+d\\le n+1\\).  \nSince \\(d\\le n\\), we have \\(a+d\\le n+1\\Rightarrow a\\le n+1-d\\).  \nNow \\(a+d\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+(d+1)\\le n+2\\); consequently the point \\((a,d+1)\\) belongs to \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \nThus each point formerly covered by \\(\\ell_h\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell_h'\\) (its ordinate has simply increased by one).  \n\nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered because we have not altered any of the other lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step 3 – the resulting family\n\nDef