eted lines with the corresponding diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) (which are non‑sunny), we obtain configurations with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=1+2+\\dots + (n-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nConsequently a non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or has equation \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).\n\nWe are asked for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\nDenote by \\(s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines; then \\(s=n-k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  An upper bound: at least two lines must be non‑sunny  \n\nWe prove that \\(s\\ge 2\\); equivalently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(s\\le 1\\); i.e. at most one line among the \\(n\\) is non‑sunny, so at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\nEach sunny line has a slope belonging to the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) write its equation in the form  \n\n\\[\ny=m_\\ell x+b_\\ell ,\\qquad m_\\ell\\in\\mathcal S .\n\\]\n\nConsider the residues of the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(T_n\\) modulo a large integer \\(M\\) that will be chosen later.  \nBecause the points of \\(T_n\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates ranging from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\), the set  \n\n\\[\nX=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct residues modulo \\(M\\) provided \\(M>n\\).\n\nFix such an \\(M>n\\). For each sunny line \\(\\ell\\) the map  \n\n\\[\nx\\longmapsto y=m_\\ell x+b_\\ell\n\\]\n\nis an affine map on \\(\\mathbb Z\\). Reducing modulo \\(M\\) we obtain a map  \n\n\\[\n\\varphi_\\ell :\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\n\\varphi_\\ell(\\bar x)=\\overline{m_\\ell x+b_\\ell } .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m_\\ell\\not\\equiv 0\\pmod M\\) (otherwise the line would be horizontal) and \\(m_\\ell\\not\\equiv -1\\pmod M\\) (otherwise it would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), each \\(\\varphi_\\ell\\) is a bijection of the residue class set \\(\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z\\).\n\nNow we have at least \\(n-1\\) bijections \\(\\varphi_{\\ell_1},\\dots ,\\varphi_{\\ell_{n-1}}\\) acting on the set of residues \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\subset\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z\\).  \nBy the pigeon‑hole principle, there exists a residue \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) that is **not** a fixed point of any of these bijections; i.e. for every sunny line \\(\\ell\\),\n\n\\[\n\\varphi_\\ell(r)\\neq r .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, for the point \\((r,\\,r)\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nr+r=2r\\le 2n\\le n+1\\quad\\text{(since }n\\ge 3\\text{)},\n\\]\n\nso \\((r,r)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\((r,r)\\) were covered by a sunny line \\(\\ell\\), we would have  \n\n\\[\nr = m_\\ell r + b_\\ell \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; (1-m_\\ell)r = b_\\ell .\n\\]\n\nReducing modulo \\(M\\) gives \\(\\varphi_\\ell(r)=r\\), contradicting the choice of \\(r\\).  \n\nThus \\((r,r)\\) cannot lie on any sunny line. The only remaining possibility is that it lies on the (at most one) non‑sunny line. But a non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or of the form \\(x+y=c\\). None of these three families contains a point with equal coordinates unless the line is \\(x=y\\), which has slope \\(1\\) and is therefore sunny – a contradiction. Hence \\((r,r)\\) is uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\nThe contradiction shows that our assumption \\(s\\le 1\\) is false; therefore  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k=n-s\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Existence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1  The case \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{\\text{vert}} &: x=1,\\\\\nL_{\\text{horiz}}&: y=1,\\\\\nL_i &: y=\\frac{i}{i-1}\\,(x-1)+1\\qquad (i=2,3,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*The two lines \\(L_{\\text{vert}},L_{\\text{horiz}}\\) are non‑sunny* (vertical and horizontal).  \nFor each \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope \\(\\frac{i}{i-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n\nAll these lines are distinct because they have different slopes or, in the case of the first two, different directions.\n\nNow we verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{\\text{vert}}\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{\\text{horiz}}\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\). Set \\(i=a\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(i=a\\le n-1\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  y=\\frac{i}{i-1}(a-1)+1=\\frac{a}{a-1}(a-1)+1=a+1-1=a,\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. the point \\((a,a)\\) belongs to \\(L_a\\).  \n  For a general \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) we note that the line \\(L_a\\) contains all points \\((a,b')\\) with  \n\n  \\[\n  b'=\\frac{a}{a-1}(a-1)+1=a,\n  \\]\n\n  so only the diagonal points \\((a,a)\\) are covered directly.  \n  However, any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq a\\) can be written as \\((1,b')\\) after a translation by the vector \\((-a+1,0)\\); this point is already covered by \\(L_{\\text{vert}}\\).  \n\nA more convenient verification is the following direct one: for each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(s-1\\) points. The line \\(x+y=s\\) (non‑sunny) would cover them all, but we replace it as follows:\n\n* For \\(s=2\\) we keep the point \\((1,1)\\) on \\(L_{\\text{vert}}\\cap L_{\\text{horiz}}\\).\n* For \\(s\\ge 3\\) we let the sunny line \\(L_{s-1}\\) pass through the two extreme points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\); all intermediate points of the same sum lie on the segment between them and therefore also belong to \\(L_{s-1}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered. The construction uses exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, proving the existence for \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Reducing the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose we have a configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines as above.  \nTo obtain a configuration with \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines, replace one of the sunny lines, say \\(L_i\\), by the diagonal line \\(x+y=i+1\\). The diagonal line is non‑sunny, and it still covers all points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously covered by \\(L_i\\) (because \\(L_i\\) was chosen precisely to contain the two extreme points of the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=i+1\\}\\)).  \n\nRepeating this replacement we can turn any chosen subset of the sunny lines into diagonal lines. Each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property. Consequently we obtain configurations with any number  \n\n\\[\nk=n-2,\\;n-3,\\;\\dots ,\\;0 .\n\\]\n\nWhen all sunny lines have been replaced, we are left with the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\); this is the obvious configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* **Necessity:** at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n* **Sufficiency:** for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) an explicit construction provides \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n\\]"
