            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=1+2+\\dots +(n-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nConsequently a **non‑sunny** line is either\n\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical:   \\(x=c\\);\n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nLet a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines be \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\).  \nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and by \\(s=n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\nOur goal is to determine all possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  At least two lines must be non‑sunny  \n\nWe prove \\(s\\ge 2\\) (equivalently \\(k\\le n-2\\)).\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(s\\le 1\\); i.e. at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny, so at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\nChoose a prime number \\(p>n\\).  \nFor a sunny line we may write its equation as  \n\n\\[\ny=m x+b ,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nReducing the coefficients modulo \\(p\\) we obtain a map  \n\n\\[\n\\varphi_{m,b}:\\mathbb Z/p\\mathbb Z\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z/p\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\n\\varphi_{m,b}(\\bar x)=\\overline{mx+b}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\not\\equiv0\\pmod p\\) and \\(m\\not\\equiv-1\\pmod p\\), the map \\(\\varphi_{m,b}\\) is a bijection of the finite field \\(\\mathbb Z/p\\mathbb Z\\).\n\nConsider the set of residues  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\subset\\mathbb Z/p\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nWe have at least \\(n-1\\) bijections \\(\\varphi_{1},\\dots ,\\varphi_{n-1}\\) (one for each sunny line).  \nIf every element of \\(R\\) were a fixed point of at least one of these bijections, then each bijection would have at most one fixed point in \\(R\\) (a non‑trivial affine bijection over a field has at most one fixed point).  \nThus the total number of fixed points contributed by the \\(n-1\\) bijections would be at most \\(n-1\\), which is strictly smaller than \\(|R|=n\\).  \nHence there exists a residue \\(r\\in R\\) that is **not** a fixed point of any sunny line, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\varphi_{i}(r)\\neq r\\qquad\\text{for every sunny line }i .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow look at the lattice point \\((r,r)\\).  \nSince \\(1\\le r\\le n\\) and \\(p>n\\),\n\n\\[\n2r\\le 2n\\le n+1\\quad(\\text{because }n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\nso \\((r,r)\\in T_n\\).\n\nIf \\((r,r)\\) lay on a sunny line \\(y=m x+b\\), then substituting \\(x=r\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nr=m r+b\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; (1-m)r=b .\n\\]\n\nReducing modulo \\(p\\) yields \\(\\overline{mr+b}=\\bar r\\), i.e. \\(\\varphi_{m,b}(r)=r\\), contradicting (1).  \nTherefore \\((r,r)\\) cannot belong to any sunny line.\n\nThe only remaining possibility is that \\((r,r)\\) belongs to the unique non‑sunny line (if it exists).  \nA non‑sunny line is of one of the three forms listed above.  \nNone of those three families contains a point with equal coordinates except the line \\(y=x\\), whose slope is \\(1\\) and hence is sunny – a contradiction.\n\nThus our assumption \\(s\\le1\\) is impossible; consequently  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k=n-s\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Constructions achieving every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1  A configuration with \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{\\text{vert}} &: x=1,\\\\[2mm]\nL_{\\text{horiz}}&: y=1,\\\\[2mm]\nL_i &: y=\\frac{i}{i-1}(x-1)+1\\qquad (i=2,3,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n* The first two lines are vertical and horizontal, hence non‑sunny.  \n* For each \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope \\(\\frac{i}{i-1}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n* All lines are distinct because they have different slopes (the \\(L_i\\)’s) or different directions (the two axis‑parallel lines).\n\n**Coverage of \\(T_n\\).**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(L_{\\text{vert}}\\).  \n*If \\(b=1\\)*, it lies on \\(L_{\\text{horiz}}\\).  \n\nOtherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\).  \nSet \\(s=a+b\\) (so \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nThe two extreme points of the set  \n\n\\[\nS_s=\\{(x,y)\\in T_n\\mid x+y=s\\}\n\\]\n\nare \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\).  \nBoth satisfy the equation of \\(L_{s-1}\\) because\n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{s-1}{s-2}(x-1)+1\n\\]\n\nholds for \\((x,y)=(1,s-1)\\) and for \\((x,y)=(s-1,1)\\).  \nSince a line is uniquely determined by two points, the whole set \\(S_s\\) is contained in \\(L_{s-1}\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\in L_{s-1}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family (3).  \nThe family contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, proving the existence for \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Obtaining fewer sunny lines  \n\nFor any \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_i :\\; x+y=i+1\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely the same set \\(S_{i+1}\\) of points of \\(T_n\\) that were covered by the sunny line \\(L_i\\).  \n\\(D_i\\) is non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nReplace any chosen sunny line \\(L_i\\) by the corresponding diagonal \\(D_i\\).  \nThe covering property is preserved, and the number of sunny lines decreases by one.\n\nStarting from the configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines, perform \\(n-2-k\\) such replacements.  \nWe obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and now contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWhen all sunny lines have been replaced (\\(k=0\\)) the resulting family is  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich clearly satisfies the required conditions.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* **Necessity:** at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, giving the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n* **Sufficiency:** for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) an explicit construction provides \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
