**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); all are non\u2011sunny and together they contain every point of \\(S\\).  \n   *\\(k=s_{\\max}\\).*  Use exactly \\(t_{\\min}\\) non\u2011sunny lines that each contain \\(n\\) points (for instance the lines \\(y=1,\\ x=1,\\ x+y=n+1\\) and, if more are needed, additional horizontal or vertical lines). The remaining points are at most \\(2s_{\\max}\\); they can be covered by \\(s_{\\max}\\) sunny lines, each passing through two uncovered points.  \n   *Intermediate \\(k\\).*  Starting from the construction for \\(k=s_{\\max}\\), replace one sunny line by a suitable non\u2011sunny line (which can cover at least as many points as the sunny line it replaces). Repeating this replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving a covering of all points. Hence every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(s_{\\max}\\) is attainable.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the interval described in (2).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition \u201cfor all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines\u2019\u2019 means that the family of \\(n\\) lines covers the whole set \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Counting the points of \\(S\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=c\\) has exactly \\(c-1\\) positive integer solutions. Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many points can a single line contain?\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); it contains the points \\((a,b)\\) for all \\(a\\) such that \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a=1,\\dots ,n+1-b\\). Thus it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nThe same bound holds for lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=a\\)) and for lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (equations \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)); each of these contains at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nLet a sunny line contain three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S\\). The slope of the line is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nSince all coordinates are positive integers and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), a short case analysis shows that the only possible slopes are \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. Hence a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Pigeonhole inequality\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns=\\text{number of sunny lines},\\qquad\nt=n-s=\\text{number of non\u2011sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the bounds from \u00a72, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2s+tn .\n\\]\n\nBecause all points of \\(S\\) must be covered, we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2s+tn\\ge |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nReplace \\(s\\) by \\(n-t\\) in (4):\n\n\\[\n2(n-t)+tn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow\ntn-2t\\ge\\frac{n(n-3)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFactorising the left\u2013hand side gives  \n\n\\[\nt(n-2)\\ge\\frac{n(n-3)}2 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is an integer, the minimal possible value of \\(t\\) is  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the maximal possible number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\ns_{\\max}=n-t_{\\min}\n        =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le s_{\\max}\\)\n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nEach line is non\u2011sunny and together they contain every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Construction for \\(k=s_{\\max}\\).*  \nLet \\(t_{\\min}=n-s_{\\max}\\). Choose \\(t_{\\min}\\) non\u2011sunny lines that each contain exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nand, if more are needed, additional horizontal or vertical lines with distinct constants. These \\(t_{\\min}\\) lines cover \\(t_{\\min}n\\) points. By (5) we have  \n\n\\[\n|S|-t_{\\min}n\\le 2s_{\\max},\n\\]\n\nso the remaining uncovered points are at most \\(2s_{\\max}\\). Pair them arbitrarily; each pair determines a unique line, and because no pair lies on a line parallel to the three forbidden directions (otherwise that line would have been one of the non\u2011sunny lines already used), each of these lines is sunny. Thus we obtain exactly \\(s_{\\max}\\) sunny lines and a total of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(S\\).\n\n*Intermediate values.*  \nStarting from the configuration for \\(k=s_{\\max}\\), replace one sunny line by a suitable non\u2011sunny line (for example, a horizontal line that passes through at least one of the points previously covered by the sunny line). This reduces the number of sunny lines by one while still covering all points. Repeating the replacement yields configurations with \\(k=s_{\\max}-1, s_{\\max}-2,\\dots ,0\\) sunny lines. Hence every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,s_{\\max}]\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions exists, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible. \u220e"
