ints \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S\\). The slope of the line is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nAll coordinates are positive integers and satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\). A straightforward case analysis (checking the possible differences of the coordinates) shows that the only way three such points can be collinear is when the slope equals \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Pigeonhole inequality\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns=\\text{number of sunny lines},\\qquad\nt=n-s=\\text{number of non‑sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\nFrom §2 the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2s+tn .\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(S\\) must be covered, we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2s+tn\\ge |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nReplace \\(s\\) by \\(n-t\\) in (4):\n\n\\[\n2(n-t)+tn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow\ntn-2t\\ge\\frac{n(n-3)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFactorising the left–hand side gives  \n\n\\[\nt(n-2)\\ge\\frac{n(n-3)}2 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is an integer, the minimal possible value of \\(t\\) is  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the maximal possible number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\ns_{\\max}=n-t_{\\min}\n        =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le s_{\\max}\\)\n\n**Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nEach line is non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(S\\).\n\n**Construction for \\(k=s_{\\max}\\).**  \nLet \\(t_{\\min}=n-s_{\\max}\\). Choose \\(t_{\\min}\\) non‑sunny lines that each contain exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad x=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nand, if more are needed, additional horizontal or vertical lines with distinct constants (they remain non‑sunny). These \\(t_{\\min}\\) lines cover \\(t_{\\min}n\\) points. By (5),\n\n\\[\n|S|-t_{\\min}n\\le 2s_{\\max},\n\\]\n\nso at most \\(2s_{\\max}\\) points of \\(S\\) remain uncovered. Pair these remaining points arbitrarily; each pair determines a unique line. Because no pair lies on a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or \\(x+y=0\\) (otherwise that line would already be among the non‑sunny lines we selected), each of these lines is sunny. Thus we obtain exactly \\(s_{\\max}\\) sunny lines, together with the \\(t_{\\min}\\) non‑sunny lines, giving a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(S\\).\n\n**Intermediate values.**  \nStarting from the configuration for \\(k=s_{\\max}\\), replace one sunny line by a suitable non‑sunny line (for example, a horizontal line that passes through at least one of the points previously covered by the sunny line). This replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while still covering every point of \\(S\\). Repeating the replacement yields configurations with any number of sunny lines \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le s_{\\max}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions exists, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
