ontain at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}t=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor a line \\(\\ell\\) write \\(N(\\ell)=|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Horizontal/Vertical bound)\n\nAssume \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, i.e. \\(\\ell:\\;y=b\\) with an integer \\(b\\) satisfying \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) must also satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), hence  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nN(\\ell)=n+1-b\\le n+1-\\max\\{a,b\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe same argument works for a vertical line \\(\\ell:x=a\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Diagonal bound)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); then \\(\\ell\\) has an equation \\(x+y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\).  \nIf \\(c\\le 1\\) or \\(c\\ge n+2\\) the line does not intersect \\(T_n\\).  \nFor \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,c-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nN(\\ell)=c-1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Total bound)\n\nSuppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, and let  \n\n\\[\ns=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\},\\qquad\nt=n-s=\\#\\{\\text{non‑sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (because any line meets the triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points).  \nUsing Lemma 1 for horizontal/vertical lines and Lemma 2 for diagonal lines, the maximal total number of points that can be covered by the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\max_{\\,\\text{choice of directions}} \\sum_{\\ell\\text{ non‑sunny}} N(\\ell)\n