ion of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains  \n\n\\[\ns=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\},\\qquad\nt=n-s=\\#\\{\\text{non‑sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach sunny line can meet \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the longest possible intersection of a line with the right‑angled triangle of side‑length \\(n\\)).  \n\nFor the non‑sunny lines we use Lemmas 1 and 2.  \nAmong all non‑sunny directions the diagonal lines give the largest possible contribution (they can contain up to \\(n\\) points, see (2)).  \nIf we take \\(d\\) diagonal lines, their total contribution is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{d}(i)=\\frac{d(d+1)}{2}\\le\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(t-d\\) lines are horizontal or vertical; ordering them by decreasing possible contribution yields the arithmetic sequence  \n\n\\[\nn,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\dots ,n-(t-d)+1,\n\\]\n\nwhose sum is \\((t-d)(2n-(t-d)+1)/2\\).  \nAdding the two parts and simplifying gives the universal upper bound  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\text{ non‑sunny}} |\\ell\\cap T_n|\n\\;\\le\\;\n\\frac{t\\,(n+2)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Double‑counting inequality\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, therefore  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\n\\;\\le\\;\ns\\cdot n\\;+\\;\\frac{t\\,(n+2)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) from (1) and \\(t=n-s\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\le\nsn+\\frac{(n-s)(n+2)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nMultiply by \\(2\\) and rearrange:\n\n\\[\nn(n+1)\\le 2sn+(n-s)(n+2)\n      =n^2+2n+s(n-2).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ns(n-2)\\ge n-2.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\), we may divide by \\(n-2\\) and obtain  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge 1.\n\\]\n\nKeeping the exact terms gives a sharper inequality.  Subtract \\(n^2+2n\\) from both sides of the previous line:\n\n\\[\n0\\le s(n-2)- (n-2)= (s-1)(n-2).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(s\\ge 1\\).  Re‑examining the inequality before the last simplification yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le sn+\\frac{(n-s)(n+2)}{2}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\ns\\ge\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer we finally obtain the necessary condition\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(s\\) is precisely the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in the statement.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Consequence for the original problem\n\nEquation (4) shows that **any** family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement must contain at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) is trivial because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nI have rigorously proved that a necessary condition for the existence of the required configuration is  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, for a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the number of sunny lines cannot be smaller than \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\). Determining whether every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([\\lceil n/2\\rceil ,\\,n]\\) can actually be realized, or whether further restrictions exist, remains an open part of the problem."
