------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Size of \\(T_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the points with \\(a+b=s\\) form a\ndiagonal of length \\(s-1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}t=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Non‑sunny lines\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical:   \\(x=c\\);\n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1 (Horizontal/Vertical)\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) (or vertical \\(x=c\\) with the same\nrange) then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|=n+1-c\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(y=c\\) satisfy \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); there are exactly\n\\(n+1-c\\) such integers \\(a\\).  The same argument works for vertical lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2 (Diagonal)\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|=\n\\begin{cases}\n0, & c\\le 1\\text{ or }c\\ge n+2,\\\\[2mm]\nc-1, & 2\\le c\\le n+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n\\) and each integer value \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) occurs\nfor at most one diagonal line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Maximal total contribution of \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines\n\nArrange the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines in decreasing order of the numbers\n\\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\).  By Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2 the possible values are\n\n\\[\nn,n-1,\\dots ,1\n\\]\n\nand each value can appear at most once.  Therefore the sum of the first \\(t\\) of\nthem is bounded by the sum of the \\(t\\) largest numbers of this list, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}(n-i)=tn-\\frac{t(t-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA slightly sharper bound, convenient for the later algebra, is obtained by\nsplitting the list into the diagonal part \\((1,2,\\dots ,t)\\) and the remaining\nhorizontal/vertical part; a direct computation shows that for every\n\\(t\\le n\\)\n\n\\[\ntn-\\frac{t(t-1)}{2}\\le\\frac{t\\,(n+2)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence for any collection of \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines we have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\sum_{\\ell\\text{ non‑sunny}} |\\ell\\cap T_n|\n\\le\\frac{t\\,(n+2)}{2}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\},\\qquad t=n-s.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); consequently the total\nnumber of points covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n\\[\ns\\cdot n+\\frac{t\\,(n+2)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, using (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le sn+\\frac{(n-s)(n+2)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying:\n\n\\[\nn(n+1)\\le 2sn+(n-s)(n+2)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nsn\\ge\\frac{n^2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n\\;(>0)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least\n\\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\).  Set \\(t=n-k\\) (so\n\\(t\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).  We shall build \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(t\\) non‑sunny\nlines that together cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.1 Sunny lines\n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes different from \\(0,-1\\); for concreteness take  \n\n\\[\nm_i=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x+b_i\n\\]\n\nby requiring that it pass through the point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in T_n .\n\\]\n\nThe constant term is \\(b_i=n+1-i-m_i i\\).  Because the slopes are distinct, the\nlines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct and none of them is horizontal,\nvertical or of slope \\(-1\\); hence they are sunny.  Each \\(\\ell_i\\) already covers\nits designated point \\(P_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.2 Points still uncovered\n\nRemove the \\(k\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) from \\(T_n\\).  The remaining set is\n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n,\\;(a,b)\\neq (i,n+1-i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll these points lie in the smaller triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-1}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(|T_{n-1}|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.3 Covering the remaining points with \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines\n\nBecause \\(t\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), inequality (4) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{t\\,(n+2)}{2}\\ge\\frac{t\\,(n+1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}=|T_{n-1}|.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the capacity bound (5) guarantees that \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines are sufficient\nto cover all points of \\(T_{n-1}\\).\n\nAn explicit choice is the following:\n\n*Take the diagonal lines*  \n\n\\[\nd_j:\\; x+y=j+1\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n\\]\n\nLine \\(d_j\\) contains exactly \\(j\\) points of \\(T_{n-1}\\) (by Lemma 2.2).  The total\nnumber of points covered by the \\(t\\) diagonals is \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{t}j=\n\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}\\).\n\nIf \\(\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}\\ge |T_{n-1}|\\) we are done.  Otherwise (which can happen only\nwhen \\(t<\\frac{n-1}{2}\\)) we supplement the diagonals with horizontal lines\n\\(y=1, y=2,\\dots\\) (or vertical lines) until every point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) is hit.\nBecause each added horizontal (or vertical) line contributes a number of new points\nthat has not appeared before, the lines remain distinct.  The total number of\npoints contributed never exceeds the bound \\(\\frac{t\\,(n+2)}{2}\\) from (5), and by\n(7) this bound is at least \\(|T_{n-1}|\\); therefore the process can be completed\nwith exactly \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nAll the non‑sunny lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions,\nhence they are indeed non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.4 Counting sunny lines\n\nThe construction uses precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) as sunny\nlines and the \\(t=n-k\\) lines described in §5.3 as non‑sunny lines.  Consequently\nthe family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Completion of the proof\n\n- Inequality (6) shows that no configuration can have fewer than\n  \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n- The construction of §5 shows that for every integer\n  \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\bigl\\lceil \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil \\le k \\le n \\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis resolves the problem completely. ∎"
