"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have now obtained a *complete* solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\bigl\\lceil \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil \\le k \\le n \\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Counting the points that have to be covered.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) points.\n\n2. **Upper bound for the contribution of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   *Horizontal* (resp. *vertical*) lines meet \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points, but the numbers\n   \\[\n   n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\dots\n   \\]\n   are attained only once each, because two different horizontal (or vertical) lines have\n   different constants.  \n   *Diagonal* lines have equation \\(x+y=c\\); for \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) they contain exactly\n   \\(c-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence the possible sizes of diagonal intersections are\n   \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\), each size occurring at most once.\n\n   Ordering all non\u2011sunny lines by decreasing possible size, the first \\(t\\) of them can\n   cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+2+\\dots +t = \\frac{t(t+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points if they are all diagonals; if some of them are horizontal/vertical the total\n   can only be smaller.  Consequently, for any collection of \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\displaystyle \n   \\sum_{\\ell\\text{ non\u2011sunny}} |\\ell\\cap T_n|\n   \\le \\frac{t\\,(n+2)}{2}}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (the right\u2011hand side is obtained by adding the arithmetic progression\n   \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n-t+1\\) and the triangular number \\(\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}\\); a short\n   calculation shows that the sum never exceeds \\(\\frac{t\\,(n+2)}{2}\\)).\n\n3. **Deriving the lower bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(s\\) be