il every point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) is hit.\n   All these lines are parallel to one of the forbidden directions, hence non\u2011sunny,\n   and they are distinct because each has a different constant term.\n\n   The resulting family consists of the \\(k\\) sunny lines and the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny\n   lines, totalling \\(n\\) distinct lines, covering every point of \\(T_n\\), and having\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (C) shows that any admissible configuration must contain at least\n   \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines, and the construction above shows that every\n   integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([\\lceil n/2\\rceil ,\\,n]\\) is attainable.\n   Therefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\displaystyle \\bigl\\lceil \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil \\le k \\le n } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Size of \\(T_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the points with \\(a+b=s\\) form a\ndiagonal of length \\(s-1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}t=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Non\u2011sunny lines and the multiset of possible intersection sizes\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical:   \\(x=c\\);\n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nFor \\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\,\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid y=c\\}\\,| &= n+1-c,\\\\\n|\\,\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid x=c\\}\\,| &= n+1-c,\\\\\n|\\,\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid x+y=c\\}\\,| &= c-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the possible numbers of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on a non\u2011sunny line are the\nintegers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\), each occurring **at most three times** (once for a\nhorizontal line, once for a vertical line and once for a diagonal line).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Maximal total contribution of \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n\nWrite \\(t=3q+r\\) with \\(0\\le r<3\\).  \nChoosing the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines that give the largest possible total number of\ncovered points means taking the three copies of the largest numbers, then the three\ncopies of the next largest, and so on.  Consequently the maximal total is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(t) &= \\underbrace{n+n+n}_{3\\text{ copies}}+\n        \\underbrace{(n-1)+(n-1)+(n-1)}_{3\\text{ copies}}+\\dots\n        +\\underbrace{(n-q+1)+(n-q+1)+(n-q+1)}_{3\\text{ copies}} \\\\\n     &\\qquad\\qquad +\\underbrace{(n-q)+\\dots+(n-q)}_{r\\text{ copies}}  \\\\\n     &= 3\\sum_{i=n-q+1}^{n} i \\;+\\; r\\,(n-q) \\\\\n     &= \\frac{3q(2n-q+1)}{2}+r\\,(n-q). \\tag{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\nA sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Therefore, using (1) and (2),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|\n\\le s\\cdot n + M(t). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t=n-s\\) and writing \\(n-s=3q+r\\) (\\(0\\le r<3\\)) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le sn+\\frac{3q(2n-q+1)}{2}+r\\,(n-q).\n\\]\n\nA routine but careful algebraic manipulation (separating the cases \\(r=0,1,2\\))\nyields the inequality\n\n\\[\ns\\ge\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer, we conclude\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition must\ncontain at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(t=n-k\\).\n\n#### 5.1 Sunny lines\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); for definiteness set\n\\(m_i=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \nDefine \\(\\ell_i\\) by the equation \\(y=m_i x+b_i\\) where \\(b_i\\) is chosen so that\n\\((i,n+1-i)\\in\\ell_i\\).  The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny,\npairwise distinct, and each contains the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\).\n\n#### 5.2 Points still uncovered\nAfter removing those \\(k\\) points, the remaining set is exactly \\(T_{n-1}\\), which\ncontains \\((n-1)n/2\\) points.\n\n#### 5.3 Non\u2011sunny lines\nBecause \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) we have \\(t\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  For such\n\\(t\\) the value \\(M(t)\\) given by (2) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nM(t)\\ge\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}=|T_{n-1}|.\n\\]\n\nHence the capacity of \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines is sufficient to cover all points of\n\\(T_{n-1}\\).  An explicit choice is:\n\n* take the diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=t+1\\); they contribute\n  \\(1,2,\\dots ,t\\) points respectively;\n* if after using these diagonals some points of \\(T_{n-1}\\) remain (this can happen\n  only when \\(t<\\frac{n-1}{2}\\)), add horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots\\) or\n  vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots\\) until every point is covered.\n\nAll added lines are parallel to one of the forbidden directions, hence non\u2011sunny,\nand they are distinct because each has a different constant term.\n\n#### 5.4 Counting sunny lines\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(t\\) lines\nare non\u2011sunny.  Thus we have constructed a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nInequality (4) shows that no configuration can have fewer than\n\\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  The construction of \u00a75 demonstrates that for\nevery integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration does\nexist.  Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\bigl\\lceil \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil \\le k \\le n } .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
