al lines.*  \n   Take the diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   d_j:\\; x+y=j+1\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,q)\n   \\]\n\n   each of which contains \\(j\\) points of \\(U\\).  This uses \\(q\\) non‑sunny lines\n   and contributes \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{q}j=\\frac{q(q+1)}{2}\\) points.\n\n   *Horizontal lines (if needed).*  \n   If \\(r\\ge1\\) add the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); it contains \\(n\\) points of\n   \\(U\\) but only the points with \\(x\\le n\\) and \\(x+1\\le n+1\\), i.e. exactly\n   \\(n\\) points, of which at most \\(n-q\\) are still uncovered after the diagonals.\n   Choose the constant term so that the line is distinct from all previously used\n   lines.\n\n   *Vertical line (if needed).*  \n   If \\(r=2\\) add the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which similarly covers the remaining\n   uncovered points.\n\n   Because each added line belongs to one of the three forbidden families, all\n   are non‑sunny, and their constants are distinct, the total number of non‑sunny\n   lines is exactly \\(t\\).  By construction they together hit every point of\n   \\(U\\).\n\n   *Step 5.4 – Count sunny lines.*  \n   The configuration consists of the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) and the\n   \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines described above; thus it contains precisely \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines, uses \\(n\\) distinct lines, and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (3) shows that any admissible family must have at least\n   \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  The construction of step 5 proves that for\n   every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) such a family exists.\n   Consequently the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\displaystyle \\bigl\\lceil \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil \\le k \\le n } .\n   \\]\n\n∎"
