  \\[\n   d_c:\\; x+y=c .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(d_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n   The line \\(d_c\\) contains **all** points of \\(U\\) whose coordinates satisfy\n   \\(a+b=c\\); none of those points has been removed because the only removed points on a\n   diagonal are those with \\(c\\le k+1\\).  \n   The number of such diagonals is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n+1)-(k+2)+1 = n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{d_{k+2},d_{k+3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. of \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines in total, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Existence for the extreme values.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines \\(\\{d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\\); they\n   cover all of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) defined in Step\u202f3.1; each\n   of them already covers a distinct point of \\(T_n\\), and together they cover the whole\n   set because the remaining points lie on the already used diagonals, which are now\n   redundant.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), and the obvious\n   upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no larger value is possible. Therefore the set of\n   admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n\u220e"
