vious ones.\n\nThus, assuming the statement holds for \\(n\\), we have produced configurations for \\(n+1\\) with every possible number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n+1\\). By induction the claim holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the integer lattice points  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe \u201ctriangular\u201d set that must be covered.\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThus a line is non\u2011sunny iff its slope belongs to the set \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following families of three distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | family of lines | remarks |\n|------|-----------------|---------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) | three vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=1\\) | two vertical, one horizontal |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=2\\) | one vertical, two horizontal |\n| 3 | \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3\\) | three horizontal (all sunny? actually horizontal is non\u2011sunny; to obtain three sunny lines we use slopes \\(2,3,4\\) e.g. \\(y=2x-1,\\;y=3x-2,\\;y=4x-3\\); each passes through a distinct point of \\(T_3\\) and together they cover all six points.) |\n\nHence the statement is true for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set  \n> \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive step: construction for \\(n+1\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).  \nWe shall produce a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines satisfying the required properties.\n\nStart from a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\) given by the hypothesis, where  \n\n\\[\nk'=\n\\begin{cases}\nk, &\\text{if }k\\le n,\\\\[2pt]\nn, &\\text{if }k=n+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\) contains exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines and covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe new set \\(T_{n+1}\\) consists of \\(T_n\\) together with the points whose coordinates satisfy \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \nThese new points are  \n\n\\[\nP_{n+1}=\\{(1,n+1),(2,n),\\dots ,(n+1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe add one extra line \\(L\\) to the family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\).  \nTwo possibilities are considered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Keeping the number of sunny lines unchanged  \n\nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{vert}}:\\;x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIt is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nEvery point of \\(P_{n+1}\\) has abscissa at most \\(n+1\\); the point \\((n+1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{\\text{vert}}\\), while the remaining points of \\(P_{n+1}\\) are already covered by the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\) (they belong to \\(T_n\\)).  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{L_{\\text{vert}}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\nSuppose we need a configuration with \\(k=k'+1\\) sunny lines 