on:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{(0)} = \\mathcal L_n(k')\\cup\\{L_{0}\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow every point of \\(T_{n+1}=T_n\\cup P_{n+1}\\) is covered, and the number of sunny lines is still \\(k'\\).\n\n*Increase the number of sunny lines when needed.*  \nIf we need a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(k>k'\\), we repeatedly perform the following operation:\n\n1. Choose a slope \\(m\\) that is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes of all lines already present in the current family.  \n   Because at most \\(n+1\\) slopes are forbidden, such an \\(m\\) exists.\n\n2. Define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{s}: y=m\\bigl(x-(n+1)\\bigr)+1,\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through the point \\((n+1,1)\\in P_{n+1}\\).\n\n3. Add \\(L_{s}\\) to the family.  The point \\((n+1,1)\\) was already covered by \\(L_{0}\\), so the covering property is preserved.  The new line is sunny and distinct from all previous lines, so the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\nRepeating this operation \\(k-k'\\) times yields a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\) consisting of exactly \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covering all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\), and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Upper bound on \\(k\\).*  \nSince the family contains exactly \\(n+1\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n+1\\).  In the original problem the number of lines is \\(n\\); consequently for the given \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n\\).  The constructions above show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) is verified directly.  Assuming the statement for some \\(n\\ge3\\), the inductive step produces configurations for \\(n+1\\) with every admissible number of sunny lines.  Hence, by mathematical induction, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2