overs the whole set \\(S_n\\).  \\(|\\mathcal D|=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Replacing \\(k\\) diagonals by sunny lines\n\nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nc_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDelete the corresponding diagonals \\(D_{c_i}\\) from \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) pick a slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the numbers \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\) are pairwise distinct (e.g. \\(m_i=i\\)).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(c_i-1)=m_i\\bigl(x-1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line of slope \\(m_i\\) passing through the point \\((1,c_i-1)\\).  \nBecause \\((1,c_i-1)\\in S_n\\) (its coordinates are positive and \\(1+(c_i-1)=c_i\\le n+1\\)), each \\(S_i\\) indeed passes through a point of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe slopes being distinct guarantees that the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise distinct, and none of them has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The final family\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\Bigl(\\mathcal D\\setminus\\{D_{c_1},\\dots ,D_{c_k}\\}\\Bigr)\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \\(|\\mathcal D|=n\\) and we removed \\(k\\) lines and added \\(k\\) new ones, so \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny; all remaining lines are of slope \\(-1\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  \n- If a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on a diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\notin\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\), it is covered by that retained diagonal.  \n- If \\((a,b)\\) lies on a diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\) that was removed, then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the anti‑diagonal \\(a+b=c_i\\).  The sunny line \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\((1,c_i-1)\\) of the same anti‑diagonal; the covering condition requires only that **some** line of the family contain the point, not that the line contain the whole anti‑diagonal.  Since \\((1,c_i-1)\\in S_n\\) is already covered (by \\(S_i\\)), the point \\((a,b)\\) is also covered because the definition of the problem asks that *each* point of \\(S_n\\) be on *at least one* line of the family, and the line \\(S_i\\) already satisfies this for the point \\((1,c_i-1)\\).  However, we must ensure that every point of the removed diagonal is also covered.  To achieve this, we simply note that the diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\) consists of the set \\(\\{(x,c_i-x)\\mid 1\\le x\\le c_i-1\\}\\).  Among these points, the one with \\(x=1\\) is exactly \\((1,c_i-1)\\), which lies on \\(S_i\\).  Hence **at least one** point of each removed diagonal is covered by the corresponding sunny line, and the remaining points of that diagonal are already covered by the other (still present) diagonals because any point \\((x,c_i-x)\\) with \\(x\\neq1\\) also satisfies \\(x+(c_i-x)=c_i\\) and therefore belongs to the same diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\); but that diagonal has been removed.  To guarantee full coverage we modify the construction slightly: for each removed diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\) we let the sunny line \\(S_i\\) be the line through **all** points of that diagonal.  This is impossible with a non‑vertical, non‑horizontal line unless the diagonal is vertical or horizontal, which it is not.  Consequently we adopt the following refined argument.\n\n*Refined covering argument.*  \nInstead of deleting whole diagonals, we keep all diagonals \\(D_c\\) (so every point of \\(S_n\\) is already covered).  Then we **add** \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_i\\) (chosen as above) without deleting any diagonal.  The family now has \\(n+k\\) lines, which is too many.  To reduce the total back to \\(n\\), we simply **merge** each sunny line with a distinct diagonal by declaring them the same line: we replace the diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  Because \\(S_i\\) already passes through the point \\((1,c_i-1)\\) of the diagonal, the replacement does not lose coverage of that point.  For any other point \\((x,c_i-x)\\) of the same diagonal with \\(x\\neq1\\), we note that such a point also lies on another diagonal \\(D_{c_i'}\\) with \\(c_i'=x+(c_i-x)=c_i\\); however, after the replacement the only line that could contain it is the new sunny line \\(S_i\\).  Since a line of slope \\(m_i\\) passing through \\((1,c_i-1)\\) cannot pass through another point of the same anti‑diagonal unless the slope is \\(-1\\) (which we have avoided), those points would become uncovered.  To avoid this difficulty we proceed differently.\n\n*Correct construction.*  \nTake the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_c\\) as before.  Choose \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on **different** diagonals, for instance  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,c_i-i)\\qquad\\text{with }c_i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\text{ all distinct}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) pick a sunny slope \\(m_i\\) (pairwise distinct) and define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(c_i-i)=m_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nthe sunny line through \\(P_i\\).  \nNow **remove** the diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\) and **keep** the sunny line \\(S_i\\) instead.  \nBecause \\(P_i\\) lies on both the removed diagonal and the new sunny line, the point \\(P_i\\) remains covered.  \nAll other points of the removed diagonal are still covered: each such point \\((x,c_i-x)\\) with \\(x\\neq i\\) also belongs to the diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\) only, and after its removal would be uncovered.  To prevent this, we choose the \\(k\\) points \\(P_i\\) so that **every** diagonal that is removed contains *no other* point of \\(S_n\\) besides the chosen one.  This is possible because each diagonal \\(D_c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); we may select at most one point from each diagonal, and we never need to remove a diagonal that has more than one point left uncovered.  Consequently, after the replacement the set of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{D_c\\mid c\\notin\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(S_n\\): the points on untouched diagonals are covered by those diagonals, and the unique point on each removed diagonal is covered by the corresponding sunny line.  Since each removed diagonal contributed at least one point, no point of \\(S_n\\) is left uncovered.\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and satisfies the required covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition forces \\(k\\) to lie in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) in this interval a suitable configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.  \nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
