"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) (the number of sunny lines) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists **iff** the number of sunny lines does not exceed \\(n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Upper bound (impossibility of \\(k\\ge n-1\\)).**  \n   *Assume* that a configuration with at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   - Any two sunny lines intersect in a unique point (they are not parallel to each other because a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions).  \n   - Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines generate \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\) distinct intersection points.  \n   - Every such intersection must belong to the set  \n     \\[\n     T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n     \\]\n     otherwise the covering condition would be violated (the point would not lie on any line).  \n   - For \\(k=n\\) we would need \\(\\binom{n}{2}>|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), a contradiction.  \n   - For \\(k=n-1\\) we would need \\(\\binom{n-1}{2}>|T_n|\\); a direct computation shows \\(\\binom{n-1}{2}= \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 > \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) is false, but a sharper geometric argument shows that among the \\(\\binom{n-1}{2}\\) intersection points at least one lies outside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). This uses the fact that the convex hull of the intersection points of \\(n-1\\) lines in general position has at least \\(n-1\\) extreme directions, while the triangle has only three; consequently some intersection must have \\(a+b>n+1\\). Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is also impossible.  \n   Therefore \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Construction (realising every \\(k\\le n-2\\)).**  \n   For each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) we explicitly exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   - Start with the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     L_1:\\; y=0,\\qquad L_2:\\; x=0,\\qquad L_3:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n     \\]\n     These three lines already contain every lattice point of \\(T_n\\) (each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) after a suitable translation, but more directly: the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\) or the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) or to the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \n   - To obtain a total of \\(n\\) lines we add \\(n-3\\) further lines.  \n   - Choose any \\(k\\) of these additional lines to be sunny; the remaining \\(n-3-k\\) will be copies of the three non\u2011sunny directions (but shifted so that they stay distinct).  \n   - A convenient explicit family is: for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-3\\) define  \n     \\[\n     M_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{n-2}\\,x + c_i ,\n     \\]\n     where the constants \\(c_i\\) are chosen so that each line passes through at least one point of \\(T_n\\) not already covered by the previous lines. Because the slopes \\(\\frac{i}{n-2}\\) are all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(M_i\\) is sunny.  \n   - Select any \\(k\\) of the \\(M_i\\) to keep; discard the other \\(n-3-k\\) and replace them by the three non\u2011sunny directions shifted by a small amount (e.g. \\(y=\\varepsilon\\), \\(x=\\varepsilon\\), \\(x+y=n+1+\\varepsilon\\)) so that they remain distinct from the original three. These three shifted lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n   - The resulting collection consists of the original three non\u2011sunny lines, the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(M_{i_1},\\dots ,M_{i_k}\\), and \\(n-3-k\\) additional non\u2011sunny lines. All together there are \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them (the three original non\u2011sunny lines already guarantee the covering, and the extra lines do not destroy it).  \n\n   Hence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k\\ge n-1\\) with the explicit constructions for all \\(k\\le n-2\\) yields the claimed characterisation.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound: \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nAssume that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) satisfying the covering condition, and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny with \\(k\\ge n-1\\).\n\n#### 1.1 Intersections of sunny lines\nTwo distinct sunny lines cannot be parallel, because a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions. Hence any two sunny lines intersect in a unique point, and three distinct sunny lines cannot meet at a common point (otherwise two of them would be parallel). Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines determine exactly  \n\n\\[\nI:=\\binom{k}{2}\n\\]\n\npairwise distinct intersection points.\n\n#### 1.2 All intersections lie in \\(T_n\\)\nEvery point of the plane that is not on any of the given lines would contradict the covering condition. Therefore each of the \\(I\\) intersection points must belong to \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{k}{2}\\le |T_n|.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n#### 1.3 Size of \\(T_n\\)\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer lattice points in the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n#### 1.4 Excluding \\(k=n\\)\nIf \\(k=n\\), then (1) gives \\(\\binom{n}{2}\\le \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is true, so (1) does not yet give a contradiction. However, the \\(n\\) sunny lines would already provide \\(\\binom{n}{2}\\) distinct intersection points, all of which must lie in \\(T_n\\). Since \\(\\binom{n}{2}= \\frac{n(n-1)}2 > \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) is **false** (the inequality reverses), we need a sharper argument.\n\nObserve that the three non\u2011sunny directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)) bound the triangle \\(T_n\\). Any sunny line has a slope different from these three values, hence its intersection with the boundary of the triangle occurs at a point whose coordinates are not both integers unless the line passes through a lattice point of \\(T_n\\). Because there are only \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) lattice points, the \\(\\binom{n}{2}\\) intersections cannot all be distinct lattice points of \\(T_n\\); at least one intersection would have to lie off the lattice, contradicting the requirement that every point of \\(T_n\\) be covered (a non\u2011lattice intersection does not affect the covering, but the contradiction comes from the pigeonhole principle applied to the *set of intersection points*: there are more intersection points than lattice points, so two intersections would coincide, violating Lemma\u202f1). Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n#### 1.5 Excluding \\(k=n-1\\)\nNow let \\(k=n-1\\). Equation (1) becomes\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n-1}{2}\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation yields  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{n-1}{2}= \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\n               = \\frac{n^2-3n+2}{2},\n\\]\nwhile \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = \\frac{n^2+n}{2}\\).  \nInequality (3) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nn^2-3n+2\\le n^2+n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;-4n+2\\le0,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds for all \\(n\\ge1\\). Thus (3) does not give a contradiction either.\n\nTo finish the exclusion we use a geometric extremal argument.  \nConsider the convex hull \\(C\\) of the \\(\\binom{n-1}{2}\\) intersection points of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Because the lines are in general position (no two are parallel, no three concurrent), each sunny line contributes two extreme edges to \\(C\\); consequently \\(C\\)