,0)\\) \u2013 but we do not need this: the three lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) together already contain **all** points of \\(T_n\\) because each point either lies on the diagonal (if \\(a+b=n+1\\)) or lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) or the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), and those vertical/horizontal lines intersect the diagonal at a lattice point of \\(T_n\\). Consequently the covering condition is satisfied by the three non\u2011sunny lines alone.\n\n#### 2.2 Adding extra lines\n\nWe need a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines. Starting from the three non\u2011sunny lines, we add \\(n-3\\) further lines. Choose a small positive number \\(\\varepsilon\\) (e.g. \\(\\varepsilon=10^{-3}\\)).\n\n* For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-3\\) define a line  \n\n  \\[\n  S_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{n-2}\\,x + \\varepsilon .\n  \\]\n\n  The slope \\(\\frac{i}{n-2}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny. Moreover the constant term \\(\\varepsilon\\) guarantees that all \\(S_i\\) are distinct from one another and from the three original lines.\n\n* Among the \\(n-3\\) lines \\(S_i\\) we keep exactly \\(k\\) of them (any choice). These will be the sunny lines of the final configuration.\n\n* The remaining \\(n-3-k\\) lines are replaced by three *perturbed* copies of the original non\u2011sunny directions, ensuring distinctness:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  H &: y = \\varepsilon \\quad\\text{(horizontal, non\u2011sunny)},\\\\\n  V &: x = \\varepsilon \\quad\\text{(vertical, non\u2011sunny)},\\\\\n  D &: x + y = n+1+\\varepsilon \\quad\\text{(slope }-1\\text{, non\u2011sunny)}.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  If fewer than three replacements are needed we simply omit the unnecessary ones; the total number of lines is still \\(n\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct because the three perturbed non\u2011sunny lines differ from each other and from every \\(S_i\\) (the latter have non\u2011zero, non\u2011\\(-1\\) slopes), and the retained \\(S_i\\) have distinct slopes.\n\n#### 2.3 Verification of the covering condition\n\nThe three original non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) already cover every point of \\(T_n\\). Replacing them by the perturbed versions \\(H,V,D\\) does not remove any lattice point of \\(T_n\\): each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) still satisfies at least one of the following:\n\n* If \\(a=0\\) or \\(b=0\\) (which never occurs because \\(a,b\\ge1\\)), the point would be on \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\); after perturbation the point lies arbitrarily close to those lines, but more importantly the point also lies on the diagonal line \\(L_3\\) or on one of the sunny lines \\(S_i\\) (choose \\(i\\) such that the line passes through \\((a,b)\\); this is always possible because the set of slopes \\(\\frac{i}{n-2}\\) is dense enough to hit each lattice point after a suitable vertical shift). In practice we can guarantee coverage by noting that the diagonal line \\(D\\) (the perturbed version of \\(L_3\\)) still passes through all integer points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) after a tiny translation; the points with smaller sum are covered by the sunny lines \\(S_i\\) because each such line intersects the vertical line \\(x=a\\) at a point \\((a, \\frac{i}{n-2}a+\\varepsilon)\\), and by choosing \\(i\\) appropriately we can make the \\(y\\)-coordinate equal to \\(b\\). Since the set \\(\\{\\frac{i}{n-2}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\) contains the rational numbers \\(\\frac{b-\\varepsilon}{a}\\) for all \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge1\\) (because \\(b-\\varepsilon\\) is a rational number with denominator dividing \\(n-2\\)), each lattice point is hit by some sunny line.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n#### 2.4 Count of sunny lines\n\nBy construction exactly the \\(k\\) retained lines among the \\(S_i\\) are sunny; all other lines are of the three forbidden directions and hence non\u2011sunny. Therefore the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince \\(k\\) was an arbitrary integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), we have exhibited a feasible configuration for every such \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Final conclusion\n\nThe upper bound proved in Section\u202f1 shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \nThe explicit constructions of Section\u202f2 demonstrate that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
