unny lines are not parallel to each other, so each pair meets in a unique point. However, the claim that **no three** sunny lines can be concurrent is **not justified**: three non\u2011parallel lines can still intersect at a single point (e.g., three lines through the origin with slopes \\(1,2,3\\)). The argument \u201cotherwise two of them would be parallel\u201d is false. Hence this is a **Justification Gap**. The conclusion that the number of distinct intersection points equals \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\) is therefore not guaranteed.\n\n#### 2.3 \u201cAll intersections lie in \\(T_n\\)\u201d\n\n> \u201cEvery point of the plane that is not on any of the given lines would contradict the covering condition. Therefore each of the \\(I\\) intersection points must belong to \\(T_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.** The covering condition only requires that *every* point of \\(T_n\\) lies on **at least one** of the given lines. It imposes **no restriction** on points of the plane that are *outside* \\(T_n\\). An intersection point of two sunny lines may lie anywhere; there is no reason it must belong to \\(T_n\\). The statement confuses \u201ca point not on any of the given lines\u201d (which would indeed violate the covering condition if that point were in \\(T_n\\)) with \u201can intersection point of two given lines\u201d (which is *by definition* on two of the given lines). Hence the conclusion that all \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\) intersections lie in \\(T_n\\) is false, breaking the logical chain.\n\nBecause this is a **Critical Error**, any subsequent inequality that depends on it (e.g., \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\le|T_n|\\)) is unsupported.\n\n#### 2.4 Size of \\(T_n\\)\n\n> \u201cThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer lattice points in the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is \\(|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\u201d\n\nThe computation is correct, but the solution does **not** explain why the sum runs from \\(s=2\\) to \\(