ecause the set of slopes \\(\\frac{i}{n-2}\\) is dense enough to hit each lattice point after a suitable vertical shift.\u201d\n* \u201c\u2026the set \\(\\{\\frac{i}{n-2}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\) contains the rational numbers \\(\\frac{b-\\varepsilon}{a}\\) for all \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) \u2026\u201d\n\n**Critical Errors and Justification Gaps:**\n* The set \\(\\{\\frac{i}{n-2}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\) has only \\(n-3\\) distinct rational numbers, all with denominator dividing \\(n-2\\). There is no reason it should contain \\(\\frac{b-\\varepsilon}{a}\\) for *every* pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\). In fact, for many \\((a,b)\\) the ratio \\(\\frac{b-\\varepsilon}{a}\\) is not of that form.\n* The claim that \u201cthe set of slopes is dense enough\u201d is false: a finite set of slopes cannot be dense in any interval.\n* No rigorous argument is provided that the perturbed diagonal line \\(D\\) still passes through the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\); a translation by \\(\\varepsilon\\) moves the line off those lattice points.\n* The overall covering argument is therefore **invalid**.\n\nBecause the construction fails to guarantee that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, the lower\u2011bound claim is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Final conclusion  \n\n> \u201cThe upper bound proved in Section\u202f1 shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\). The explicit constructions of Section\u202f2 demonstrate that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable. Hence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) \u2026 is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\u201d\n\nSince both the upper\u2011bound proof and the lower\u2011bound construction contain critical errors, the final statement is **unsubstantiated**.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution fails to provide a correct proof of the upper bound (it misapplies the covering condition to intersection points) and the claimed construction for the lower bound do