gonal.\n* If the line is parallel to \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\) (i.e. has slope \\(-1\\)), it coincides with exactly one diagonal and therefore contains only points of that diagonal.\n\nThus each non\u2011sunny line is associated with a unique diagonal, and all points it covers belong to that diagonal.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Maximum number of points covered by \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nAssume among the \\(n\\) distinct lines there are \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines and \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause a non\u2011sunny line can cover points only from a single diagonal, the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most the sum of the sizes of the \\(s\\) largest diagonals.\n\nFrom (3) the \\(s\\) largest diagonal sizes are  \n\n\\[\nn-1,\\; n-2,\\; \\dots ,\\; n-s .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(s)\n   &=\\sum_{i=n-s}^{\\,n-1} i\n    =\\frac{s\\bigl((n-s)+(n-1)\\bigr)}{2}   \\\\\n   &=\\frac{s\\,(2n-s-1)}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Necessity of covering all points  \n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered by the whole family of \\(n\\) lines, therefore  \n\n\\[\nM(s)\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInsert (4) into (5):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{s\\,(2n-s-1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ns\\,(2n-s-1)\\ge n(n-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nTreat (6) as a quadratic inequality in the integer variable \\(s\\).  \nRearranging,\n\n\\[\ns^{2}-(2n-1)s+n(n-1)\\le 0.\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(2n-1)^{2}-4n(n-1)=4n-3.\n\\]\n\nThe two real roots are  \n\n\\[\ns_{1,2}= \\frac{2n-1\\pm\\sqrt{4n-3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince the quadratic opens upward, inequality (6) holds precisely for  \n\n\\[\ns\\le \\frac{2n-1-\\sqrt{4n-3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\) is an integer, the maximal admissible \\(s\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{\\max}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2n-1-\\sqrt{4n-3}}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n        = \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows from a straightforward case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\).)\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ns\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(k=n-s\\). From (7) we obtain the lower bound for the number of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), if there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines among them must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\ge\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\,}.\n\\]\n\nThis inequality is a direct consequence of the pigeon\u2011hole principle applied to the partition of the required points into the diagonals \\(D_t\\) and the restriction that each non\u2011sunny line can occupy at most one diagonal.  \n\nThe problem of determining whether every integer \\(k\\) meeting this lower bound (and of course \\(k\\le n\\)) is actually attainable remains open; the present argument establishes only the necessary condition."
