nny lines can cover is at most the sum of the sizes of the \\(s\\) largest diagonals.  \n   The \\(s\\) largest diagonal sizes are \\(n-1,n-2,\\dots ,n-s\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   M(s)=\\sum_{i=n-s}^{\\,n-1} i\n        =\\frac{s\\,(2n-s-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Pigeonhole (covering) inequality.**  \n   All points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   M(s)\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (1) into (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\,(2n-s-1)\\ge n(n-1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving the quadratic inequality (3) for the integer variable \\(s\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lower bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Since \\(k=n-s\\), inequality (4) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n     =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\nThus any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  \nThe argument is a direct application of the pigeon‑hole principle: the “holes’’ are the diagonals \\(D_t\\) and the “pigeons’’ are the non‑sunny lines, each pigeon being allowed to occupy only one hole.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=t\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_t\\) are pairwise disjoint, each lies on the line \\(x+y=t\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), and  \n\n\\[\n|D_t|=t-1\\qquad(2\\le t\\le n+1). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence the multiset of diagonal cardinalities is  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_y:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\nConsequences:\n\n* If a line is vertical (\\(\\ell_x\\)) or horizontal (\\(\\ell_y\\)), its intersection with any line of slope \\(-1\\) is a single point; therefore it can meet at most one diagonal \\(D_t\\).\n* If a line is parallel to \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) (i.e. has slope \\(-1\\)), it coincides with exactly one diagonal and contains only points of that diagonal.\n\nThus each non‑sunny line can contain points from **only one** diagonal, and all points it contains belong to that diagonal.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Maximum number of points covered by \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\nAssume among the \\(n\\) distinct lines there are \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause a non‑sunny line can use points only from a single diagonal, the total number of points that the \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines can cover is bounded above by the sum of the sizes of the \\(s\\) largest diagonals.  \n\nFrom (6) the \\(s\\) largest diagonal sizes are \\(n-1,n-2,\\dots ,n-s\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(s)&=\\sum_{i=n-s}^{\\,n-1} i\n     =\\frac{s\\bigl((n-s)+(n-1)\\bigr)}{2}   \\\\\n    &=\\frac{s\\,(2n-s-1)}{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Covering all points  \n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered by the whole family of \\(n\\) lines, therefore  \n\n\\[\nM(s)\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInsert (7) into (8):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{s\\,(2n-s-1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\ns\\,(2n-s-1)\\ge n(n-1). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nTreat (9) as a quadratic inequality in the integer variable \\(s\\).  \nRearranging,\n\n\\[\ns^{2}-(2n-1)s+n(n-1)\\le 0.\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(2n-1)^{2}-4n(n-1)=4n-3.\n\\]\n\nThe two real roots are  \n\n\\[\ns_{1,2}= \\frac{2n-1\\pm\\sqrt{4n-3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince the quadratic opens upward, inequality (9) holds precisely for  \n\n\\[\ns\\le \\frac{2n-1-\\sqrt{4n-3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\) is an integer, the maximal admissible value of \\(s\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{\\max}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2n-1-\\sqrt{4n-3}}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n        = \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows by a simple parity check: if \\(n=2m\\) then the expression equals \\(\\lfloor (2m-2)/2\\rfloor=m-1\\); if \\(n=2m+1\\) it equals \\(\\lfloor (2m-1)/2\\rfloor=m-1\\). In both cases \\(\\lfloor (n-2)/2\\rfloor=m-1\\).)\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ns\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(k=n-s\\). From (10) we obtain the lower bound for the number of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), if there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines among them necessarily satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\ge\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\,}.\n\\]\n\nThe proof rests solely on the pigeon‑hole principle: the “holes’’ are the diagonals \\(D_t\\) (each hole can be occupied by at most one non‑sunny line), and the inequality (11) follows from counting how many points can be placed into the holes versus how many must be covered.  \n\nThe problem of determining whether every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) can actually be realized remains open; the argument above provides a rigorous necessary condition."
