oints of the former horizontal row \\(y=r\\).  \n\n   The remaining rows \\(y=r\\) with \\(r>k\\) are kept as the original horizontal lines, which are non‑sunny.  \n   Consequently we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones and covering every required lattice point.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions of steps 1 and 3 give configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\); step 2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on the chosen lines.  \nFor a line we use the usual terminology:\n\n* a line is **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\);\n* otherwise the line is **non‑sunny** (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and \\(a\\le n+1-b\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) satisfies the two required conditions with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  No configuration with \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct **sunny** lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nA sunny line is not horizontal; therefore it meets any horizontal line in at most one point.  \nThe row \\(y=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n\\]\n\nSince each of these points must belong to some line of \\(\\math