            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line is neither horizontal, nor vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and that all its lines are sunny.  \nBecause no line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is horizontal, each line meets the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point.  \n\nThe row \\(y=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\in T_n .\n\\]\n\nTo cover these \\(n\\) points we need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, and the only lines available are the \\(n\\) sunny ones. Consequently every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is already used to cover a point of the first row, leaving **no** line to cover any point with ordinate \\(b\\ge 2\\). This contradicts the requirement that all points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.  \n\nTherefore any admissible family must contain at least one non‑sunny line, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-1}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_n\\).  \nAll lines of \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) satisfies the covering condition with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Configurations with an arbitrary \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n-1)\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n#### 3.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{n-k}=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_{n-k}\\).\n\nHence the only points that may remain uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\nn-k+1\\le b\\le n .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal T_k\\) is a right‑angled triangular lattice consisting of  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=n-k+1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n#### 3.2  The sunny part  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = \\frac{t+1}{t}\\,x + (n-k)\\qquad (t\\ge 1),\\qquad\nL_0:\\; y = x + (n-k).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The case \\(t=0\\) is interpreted as slope \\(1\\).)  \n\nAll lines \\(L_t\\) have slopes different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_t\\) is **sunny**.  \nBecause the slopes \\(\\frac{t+1}{t}\\) are distinct for different \\(t\\), the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n#### 3.3  Verification that \\(\\mathcal H_{n-k}\\cup\\{L_t\\}\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Points with \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nSuch a point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_{n-k}\\).\n\n*Points with \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\). Set  \n\n\\[\nt:=b-a-(n-k)\\;\\;(\\ge 0\\text{ because }b\\ge n-k+1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); substituting \\(b=a+(n-k)+t\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n2a+t\\le k+1.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe inequality \\(b\\le n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na+(n-k)+t\\le n\\Longrightarrow a+t\\le k.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (8) we obtain \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) and \\(a\\le k-t\\).  \nNow rewrite the equation of \\(L_t\\) from (5) as  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t+1}{t}\\,x + (n-k)\\quad (t\\ge 1),\\qquad\ny = x + (n-k)\\quad (t=0).\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(t\\) (and interpreting the case \\(t=0\\) separately) we obtain the equivalent linear relation  \n\n\\[\nt\\,y = (t+1)x + t\\,(n-k).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(y = a+(n-k)+t\\) and \\(x=a\\) into (9) gives an identity; therefore \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(L_t\\).\n\n#### 3.4  The resulting family  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = \\mathcal H_{n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_0,L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H_{n-k}|+|\\{L_t\\}| = (n-k)+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_t\\) are sunny, while the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are not.  \nBy the discussion above, \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom (4) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nSections 2 and 3 provide explicit constructions for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader.