\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_n\\).  \nAll lines of \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) satisfies the covering condition with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Configurations with an arbitrary \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n-1)\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n#### 3.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{n-k}=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_{n-k}\\).\n\nHence the only points that may remain uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\nn-k+1\\le b\\le n .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal T_k\\) is a right‑angled triangular lattice consisting of  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=n-k+1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n#### 3.2  The sunny part  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = x + (n-k+t).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_t\\) have slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_t\\) is **sunny**.  \nBecause the intercepts \\(n-k,\\,n-k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\) are distinct, the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n#### 3.3  Verification that \\(\\mathcal H_{n-k}\\cup\\{L_t\\}\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Points with \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nSuch a point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_{n-k}\\).\n\n*Points with \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) (i.e. points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\)).*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\in\\{\\,n-k,\\;n-k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the smallest possible value of \\(b-a\\) occurs when \\(a=1\\) and \\(b=n-k+1\\), giving \\(b-a=n-k\\); the largest occurs when \\(a=1\\) and \\(b=n\\), giving \\(b-a=n-1\\).  \nConversely, if an integer \\(c\\) satisfies \\(n-k\\le c\\le n-1\\) and a positive integer \\(a\\) satisfies \\(a+(a+c)\\le n+1\\) (i.e. \\(2a+c\\le n+1\\)), then the point \\((a,a+c)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) and lies on the line \\(y=x+c\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) satisfies \\(b-a=n-k+t\\) for a unique \\(t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\), and consequently lies on the sunny line \\(L_t\\) defined in (4).\n\n#### 3.4  The resulting family  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = \\mathcal H_{n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_0,L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H_{n-k}|+|\\{L_t\\}| = (n-k)+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_t\\) are sunny, while the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are not.  \nBy the discussion above, \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom (3) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nSections 2 and 3 provide explicit constructions for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
