            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=0\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;x=0\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;x+y=0\\quad(\\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line is neither horizontal, nor vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition.\n\n#### 1.1  At least one horizontal line is needed  \n\nThe first row  \n\n\\[\nR_1=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\subset T_n\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points.  \nAny line that is **not** horizontal meets the line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point.  \nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, covering the \\(n\\) points of \\(R_1\\) forces each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) to pass through a distinct point of \\(R_1\\). Consequently **every** line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) meets the first row in exactly one point.\n\n#### 1.2  At least one vertical line is needed  \n\nConsider the first column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nA line that is **not** vertical meets the line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point.  \nBecause each line already uses its unique intersection with the first row, the \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) cannot all be covered unless at least one of the lines is vertical.  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain at least one vertical line.\n\n#### 1.3  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown that \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain at least one horizontal line (from §1.1) and at least one vertical line (from §1.2). Both horizontal and vertical lines are non‑sunny. Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_n\\).  \nAll lines of \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore none is sunny.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) satisfies the requirements with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Configurations for arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) such that \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 3.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{n-k}=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_{n-k}\\).\n\nHence the only points that may remain uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge n-k+1 .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), for points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le k\\); consequently \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) is a right‑angled triangular lattice consisting of \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n#### 3.2  The sunny part  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = x + (n-k+t). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_t\\) have slope \\(1\\); this slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), so every \\(L_t\\) is **sunny**.  \nThe intercepts \\(n-k,\\,n-k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\) are distinct, hence the \\(k\\) lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n#### 3.3  Coverage of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\).  \nSince \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\nn-k\\le b-a\\le n-1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\nb-a\\ge (n-k+1)-k = n-2k+1\\ge n-k\\quad(\\text{because }k\\le n-2),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nb-a\\le (n+1-a)-a = n+1-2a\\le n-1\\quad(\\text{as }a\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b-a\\) is an integer, (3) implies that there exists a unique \\(t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nb-a = n-k+t . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) is precisely the condition that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_t\\) defined in (2). Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) belongs to exactly one of the sunny lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\).\n\n#### 3.4  Full covering  \n\nPoints with \\(b\\le n-k\\) are covered by the horizontal lines \\(\\mathcal H_{n-k}\\); points with \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(\\{L_t\\}\\). Therefore the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = \\mathcal H_{n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_0,L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (1) together with the explicit constructions for all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step