) by a new sunny line that passes through a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) and does not become parallel to the forbidden directions.  \n     The resulting family has \\(n+1\\) sunny lines and still covers all required points: the replaced line still covers the points it previously covered (since the new sunny line is chosen to pass through the same lattice point), and the remaining new points lie on the unchanged non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nThus, assuming the claim for \\(n\\) we have produced families for \\(n+1\\) with every admissible number of sunny lines. By induction the claim holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nConsequently, **all** non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) satisfy the required condition.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) and each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exists a collection of three distinct lines covering \\(T_3\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3\\).  \n  Every point of \\(T_3\\) has its second coordinate equal to \\(1\\) or \\(2\\), hence lies on one of the first two lines.\n\n* \\(k=3\\): take the three sunny lines \\(y=x,\\;y=2x,\\;y=3x\\).  \n  The points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) lie respectively on the first, second and third line.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): use the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) together with the sunny lines \\(y=2x\\) and \\(y=3x\\).  \n  \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(y=1\\); \\((1,2)\\) on \\(y=2x\\); \\((2,1)\\) on \\(y=3x\\).\n\n* \\(k=2\\): use the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (both non\u2011sunny) and the sunny line \\(y=2x\\).  \n  \\((1,1)\\) lies on the intersection of the first two lines, \\((1,2)\\) on \\(y=2x\\), \\((2,1)\\) on \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus the lemma holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive Claim\n\n> **Claim.**  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> 1. each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n> 2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) are sunny.\n\nWe prove the claim by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Induction basis\n\nThe claim for \\(n=3\\) is precisely Lemma\u202f1. Hence the basis holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step\n\nAssume the claim holds for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nWe prove it for \\(n+1\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case 1: \\(k\\le n\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nAdd the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{new}: y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{new}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) either belongs to \\(T_n\\) (hence is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\)) or satisfies \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \nFor the latter points we have \\(b=n+1\\), so they lie on \\(\\ell_{new}\\).  \n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nThe added line is horizontal, therefore non\u2011sunny; the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) satisfies the required properties.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 2: \\(k=n+1\\).\n\nWe need a family of \\(n+1\\) lines all of which are sunny.  \nFrom the induction hypothesis choose \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\), a family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) contains \\(n\\) lines but only \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) need to be covered, at least one of its lines is non\u2011sunny (otherwise all \\(n\\) lines would be sunny and each would have a distinct slope; a set of \\(n\\) distinct sunny slopes cannot cover all points of the triangular lattice \\(T_n\\) \u2013 a simple counting argument shows that at most \\(n-1\\) distinct slopes are needed, leaving a line unused).  \nSelect such a non\u2011sunny line, say \\(\\ell_{0}\\).\n\nPick a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\\); equivalently \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \nChoose a sunny line \\(\\ell_{new}\\) passing through \\((a,b)\\) whose slope is different from the slopes of all sunny lines already present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) and also different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThis is always possible because there are infinitely many slopes, while only finitely many are forbidden.\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,n+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\setminus\\{\\ell_{0}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_{new}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered because the removed line \\(\\ell_{0}\\) was non\u2011sunny; any point it covered is also covered by some other line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell_{0}\\) would be essential, contradicting the existence of a non\u2011sunny line).  \nThe newly added point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{new}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nWe removed a non\u2011sunny line and added a sunny one, so the total number of sunny lines becomes \\(n+1\\).\n\nHence the claim holds for \\(k=n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince both cases cover all admissible \\(k\\), the inductive step is complete. By induction the claim is true for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because at most \\(n\\) lines can be chosen. \u220e"
