hat the set of feasible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points that must lie on at least one of the lines.  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nLet \\(L\\) be the unique non‑sunny line among them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑sunny line has one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).  \nBecause all points of \\(T_n\\) have integer coordinates and satisfy \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\), the constant \\(c\\) must be an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\) for the first two cases, and between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\) for the third case.  \n\n*Horizontal* (\\(y=c\\)): admissible points are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  \n\n*Vertical* (\\(x=c\\)): analogous.  \n\n*Diagonal* (\\(x+y=c\\)): admissible points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\ge1\\); again at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at