gives at most \\(10\\); a direct enumeration shows that the configuration needed to achieve the bound would force a sunny line to contain three points, contradicting Lemma 2.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the inequality (3) is violated, giving a contradiction.  \nConsequently a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions for all other values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x + t .\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(-\\frac{t-1}{t}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \nA straightforward computation shows that \\(S_t\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(t,1),\\qquad Q_t=(1,t).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(t\\ge2\\) and \\(t+1\\le n+1\\).  \nThe \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) together cover all points of the form \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\) with \\(2\\le a,b\\le n\\).  \n\nThe remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are those with both coordinates at least \\(2\\).  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_s:\\; y = -\\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\,x + s .\n\\]\n\nAgain the slope is sunny, and \\(R_s\\) contains the points \\((s-1,2)\\) and \\((2,s-1)\\).  \nCollecting all lines \\(S_t\\) and \\(R_s\\) yields exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines (some of the \\(R_s\\) coincide with an \\(S_t\\) when \\(s=t+1\\); we simply discard the duplicate).  \nA direct verification shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------