ch diagonal \\(D_c\\) contains \\(c-1\\ge1\\) points of \\(T_n\\),\n   we can select a set  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\{(a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_k,b_k)\\}\\subset T_n\n   \\]\n\n   such that the sums \\(s_i:=a_i+b_i\\) are pairwise distinct\n   (pick one point from each of any \\(k\\) different diagonals).\n\n3. **Sunny lines through the selected points.**  \n   For each \\((a_i,b_i)\\in P\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y-b_i = 2\\,(x-a_i).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each\n   \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  Since the points are distinct, the lines\n   \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct.\n\n4. **Replacing the corresponding diagonals.**  \n   Remove from the family \\(\\{D_c\\}\\) exactly the \\(k\\) diagonals\n   \\(D_{s_1},\\dots ,D_{s_k}\\).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\n   \\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\n   \\{D_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\\) and all lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are distinct.\n\n5. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n\n   * The points of \\(P\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(S_i\\).  \n   * Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus P\\).  Its sum \\(c=a+b\\) is not among\n     \\(\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\); therefore the diagonal \\(D_c\\) has not been\n     removed and belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of\n   \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly\n\\(k\\) of which are sunny, satisfying the required covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completeness of the answer  \n\nThe construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nConversely, the pigeon\u2011hole observation shows that no larger number of\nnon\u2011sunny lines is needed, and there is no obstruction to hav