es, \\((2,1)\\) on \\(y=1\\), \\((1,2)\\) on \\(y=2x\\).\n\nThus the lemma holds. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive Claim\n\n> **Claim.**  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> 1. each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n> 2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) are sunny.\n\nWe prove the claim by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Induction basis\n\nThe claim for \\(n=3\\) is precisely Lemma 1. Hence the basis holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step\n\nAssume the claim holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nWe prove it for \\(n+1\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 1: \\(k\\le n\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\text{new}}:\\; y = -x + (n+2),\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not** sunny.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{\\text{new}}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) either belongs to \\(T_n\\) (hence already covered) or satisfies \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=n+2\\) then \\(b = -a + (n+2)\\), i.e. the point lies on \\(\\ell_{\\text{new}}\\).  \nThus all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) are covered.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \n\\(\\ell_{\\text{new}}\\) is not sunny, so the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) satisfies the required properties.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 2: \\(k=n+1\\).\n\nWe need a family of \\(n+1\\) lines all of which are sunny.\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nChoose any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\\); equivalently \\(a+b=n+2\\).\n\nBecause there are infinitely many admissible slopes (all real numbers except \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and the finitely many slopes already used by the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\)), we can select a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) that\n\n* passes through \\((a,b)\\);\n* has a slope different from the slopes of all lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,n+1}= \\mathcal L_{n,n}\\cup\\{\\ell\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\).  \nThe newly added point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \nConsequently every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nAll \\(n+1\\) lines are sunny, giving exactly \\(k=n+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince both cases handle every admissible \\(k\\), the inductive step is complete. By induction the claim holds for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nNo larger value of \\(k\\) can occur because a family contains only \\(n\\) lines. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
