ly the points with \\(a+b=n+2\\); all points with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \n   Thus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{\\text{new}}\\}\\) satisfies the required properties.  \n\n   By induction, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) with the constructive existence for all \\(k\\le n-1\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Notation and definitions  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\); equivalently the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nWith exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines we can have at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so any admissible integer \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3. The case \\(k=n\\) is impossible  \n\nDefine the \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019  \n\n\\[\nD_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 3.1.*  \nIf a line is sunny, it contains **at most one** point of \\(D_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_n\\).  \nBecause \\(a_i+b_i=n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1 = (n+1-a_2)-(n+1-a_1)=a_1-a_2 .\n\\]\n\nHence the slope of the line through them equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{a_1-a_2}{a_2-a_1}= -1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden for a sunny line. \u220e\n\nSince \\(|D_n|=n\\), a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines would have to assign a **different** line to each point of \\(D_n\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal S=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be such a family, where \\(\\ell_i\\) is the unique line of the family that contains the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\).\n\nConsider now a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\le n-1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) is **different** from every point of \\(D_n\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) lay on some \\(\\ell_i\\), then \\(\\ell_i\\) would contain the two points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((a,b)\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) would then be  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-(n+1-i)}{a-i}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, this slope cannot be \\(-1\\).  However, using the inequality \\(a+b\\le n\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb-(n+1-i) \\le n-a-(n+1-i)=i-a-1,\n\\]\n\nso  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-(n+1-i)}{a-i}\\le \\frac{i-a-1}{a-i}= -1-\\frac{1}{a-i}< -1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the slope would be **strictly less than \\(-1\\)**, contradicting the fact that the line already contains the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) whose slope with any other point of \\(D_n\\) is exactly \\(-1\\).  Consequently no sunny line of the family can contain a point with \\(a+b\\le n\\).\n\nBut the set \\(T_n\\) contains points with \\(a+b\\le n\\) (for example \\((1,1)\\)), which would remain uncovered.  Hence a configuration with \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot satisfy the covering requirement. \u220e\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nk=n\\quad\\text{is impossible}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4. Existence for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.1 Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following three families satisfy the required conditions.\n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines (written as equations) | Sunny lines |\n|------|------------------------------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=4\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=2x-1\\) | \\(y=2x-1\\) (slope \\(2\\)) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\; y=2x-1,\\; y=-\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac52\\) | the last two lines (slopes \\(2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) |\n\nEach family contains three distinct lines, all points of \\(T_3\\) lie on at least one of them, and the number of sunny lines equals the indicated \\(k\\).  Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n#### 4.2 Inductive step  \n\nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\): for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\text{new}}:\\; y=-x+(n+2).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{\\text{new}}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nObserve that the points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that are **not** already in \\(T_n\\) are precisely those with \\(a+b=n+2\\); each such point satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_{\\text{new}}\\).  Consequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{\\text{new}}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).  The number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) is the same as in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\), namely \\(k\\).  The lines remain distinct because \\(\\ell_{\\text{new}}\\) has slope \\(-1\\), a slope none of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) possesses (otherwise they would have been non\u2011sunny, contradicting the definition of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\)).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n+1\\).  By induction it holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Final answer  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3.1 and the argument of Section\u202f3 we have shown that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  \nSections\u202f4.1 and\u202f4.2 provide explicit constructions for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\nHence, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,