quire \\(\\frac{i-n}{a-i}=-1\\), i.e. \\(i-n=-(a-i)\\), whence \\(i=a\\). Again the line would be vertical.  \n\n   Hence for any \\(a\\) the only way that \\((a,1)\\) can belong to a sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) is when \\(i=a\\) and the line is vertical, which is **not** sunny. Consequently each point \\((a,1)\\) needs a **non‑sunny** line.\n\n   Since there are \\(n\\) points \\((a,1)\\) and only \\(n\\) lines in total, at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny. Therefore a covering with **all** \\(n\\) lines sunny is impossible:\n\n   \\[\n   k=n\\text{ cannot occur.}\n   \\tag{3.2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Existence for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   We give an explicit construction that works for any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n   *Construction.*  \n   - For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; \\text{through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(i+1,n-i).\n     \\tag{4.1}\n     \\]\n\n     Its slope is  \n\n     \\[\n     m_i=\\frac{1-(n-i)}{i-(i+1)}=\\frac{i-n}{-1}=n-i\\ge 1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(m_i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   - For every integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+2\\le j\\le n\\) include the vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j:\\; x=j .\n     \\tag{4.2}\n     \\]\n\n   - Finally add the vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     V_{k+1}:\\; x=k+1 .\n     \\tag{4.3}\n     \\]\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-(k+1)+1)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.*\n\n   - **Points with \\(x\\le k\\).**  \n     Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).  \n     If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(L_a\\) (the line through \\((a,1)\\)).  \n     If \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Because \\(L_a\\) passes through \\((a+1,n-a)\\), the segment of \\(L_a\\) between \\(x=a\\) and \\(x=a+1\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-a\\). Since \\(n-a\\ge b-1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) indeed belongs to \\(L_a\\)