ontain **at most one** point whose first coordinate equals a given integer \\(a\\).  \nIndeed, if a line contained two points \\((a,b_1)\\) and \\((a,b_2)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\), the line would be vertical, contradicting the definition of sunny.\n\nNow look at the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_a=(a,1)\\qquad (a=1,2,\\dots ,n),\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(a+1\\le n+1\\).  \nSince each line can cover at most one of the points \\(P_a\\), covering all of them requires at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, and consequently **every** line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must pass through exactly one of the points \\(P_a\\).\n\nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b>1\\) (such a point exists whenever \\(a\\le n-1\\)).  \nIts first coordinate is \\(a\\), so the only line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that could contain it is the line that already contains \\(P_a=(a,1)\\).  \nBut the unique line through the two distinct points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) is the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is **not** sunny.  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) cannot be covered by any sunny line, contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nTherefore a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist; i.e. the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Consequences\n\nFrom the two parts above we obtain the following:\n\n* The value \\(k=0\\) is realizable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n* The value \\(k=n\\) is never realizable.\n* Consequently any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\nThe existence (or non‑existence) of configurations for each intermediate integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) has not been settled in this work; further investigation is required to complete the classification.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, ju