two of them can be distinct multiples of \\(q\\) unless \\(q=1\\).  \n\nIf \\(q=1\\) then \\(m\\) is an integer \\(\\neq0\\).  Equation (2) now forces \\(b_i-b_j\\) to be a multiple of \\(|m|\\).  Since \\(1\\le b_i\\le n\\), again at most two distinct \\(b_i\\) are possible.  \n\nThus a sunny line cannot contain three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequences of Lemma\u202f1:\n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\);\n* each non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\).  \n  Such a line can intersect \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the whole column, row or diagonal of the triangular lattice).  \n\nHence the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(n(n-k)\\) points.  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) must be covered, so\n\n\\[\n2k+n(n-k)\\ge |\\mathcal{P}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n2k+n^{2}-nk\\ge\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\)**.  This proves the required upper bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) actually occurs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (pairing on the main diagonal)  \nFor any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) there exists a set of \\(t\\) sunny lines, each containing exactly two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\), and the remaining points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) lie on distinct vertical lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the points on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\):\n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) with maximal sum of coordinates.  \nChoose any \\(t\\) disjo