n\\{k,\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\}\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 we obtain \\(t\\) sunny lines, each covering two points, and the rest of the points lie on distinct vertical lines.  \n\nThere are exactly \\(n-2t\\) vertical lines needed to cover the points that are not already on a sunny line (because each vertical line can contain at most one of the unused points of the set \\(A\\) and at most one unused diagonal point, and the total number of unused points equals \\(n-2t\\)).  \n\nThus we have constructed \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\(n-2t\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines, altogether \\(n-t\\) lines.  \nIf \\(k=t\\) we are done.  \n\nIf \\(k>t\\) (which can happen only when \\(k\\le n-2\\) and \\(t=\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\)), we simply replace some of the unused vertical lines by additional sunny lines that each pass through a single uncovered point and any other point already covered (for instance, through \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) with \\(b\\neq1\\)).  Such a line has slope \\(\\frac{b-1}{0}\\) \u2013 i.e. it is vertical \u2013 so we must avoid verticality; instead we join \\((a,b)\\) with \\((a+1,1)\\) (possible because \\(a\\le n-2\\)).  The resulting line has slope \\(\\frac{b-1}{1}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and is sunny.  Each replacement increases the number of sunny lines by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \n\nRepeating this replacement \\(k-t\\) times yields a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny and which together cover every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\nHence **for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final conclusion  \n\nThe contradiction argument shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be realised.  \n