covering by only non\u2011sunny lines).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad v=n-h.\n\\]\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=h\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=v\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le h\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nIf \\(b>h\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Turning non\u2011sunny lines into sunny lines).\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nDenote by \\(P(L)\\subseteq S_n\\) the set of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\).  \nChoose any two distinct points of \\(P(L)\\); they determine a unique line \\(L'\\) that is not parallel to the three forbidden directions (otherwise the two points would have the same \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinate or would satisfy \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\), contradicting the definition of \\(P(L)\\)).  \nReplace \\(L\\) by \\(L'\\).  \nSince \\(L'\\) contains the same two points, it still covers all points of \\(P(L)\\) that were needed for the overall covering, and it does not introduce any new parallelism with the remaining lines.  \nThus the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines is increased by one.\n\nApplying this replacement to any chosen subset of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) we obtain, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(S_n\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the replaced ones) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the unreplaced ones).\n\n\u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n###