\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\n(the vertical direction) and \\(-1\\).  Hence the three non‑sunny directions\nare horizontal, vertical and diagonal of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A covering by non‑sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\).  Consider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_m=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=m\\}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-m\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-axis,\nhence non‑sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal L_m|=m+(n-m)=n\\).\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore either\n\\(b\\le m\\) or \\(a\\le n-m\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n\\(y=b\\) (if \\(b\\le m\\)) or on the line \\(x=a\\) (if \\(a\\le n-m\\)).\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal\nL_m\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) is a valid covering consisting solely of\nnon‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Replacing lines by sunny ones\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  We shall modify a covering\n\\(\\mathcal L_m\\) to obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) choose a slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) such that\n\\(\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\) for\n\\(i\\neq j\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha_i=i\\)).  Set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and, because its slope\n\\(\\alpha_i\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), it is sunny.  The lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct.\n\nNow delete any \\(k\\) lines from \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) (for instance the first\n\\(k\\) horizontal lines when \\(m\\ge k\\); otherwise delete the first \\(k\\)\nvertical lines).  Denote the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines by\n\\(\\mathcal L'_m\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_k=\\mathcal L'_m\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal C_k|=n\\) and exactly the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\)\nare sunny, i.e. \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The covering property is preserved\n\n*Points of \\(S_k\\).*  \nEach \\((i,n+1-i)\\in S_k\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n*Points of \\(T_n\\setminus S_k\\).*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus S_k\\).  As before, either \\(b\\le m\\) or\n\\(a\\le n-m\\).  If \\(b\\le m\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) was not\ndeleted, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on that line; if it was deleted, then\n\\(b\\le k\\) (because at most the first \\(k\\) horizontals were removed) and\n\\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(S_k\\), contradicting the assumption.  Hence a\nremaining horizontal line covering \\((a,b)\\) always exists.  The same\nargument works for the vertical case.  Consequently every point of\n\\(T_n\\setminus S_k\\) is still covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L'_m\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal\nC_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines,\nno value \\(k>n\\) is possible.  Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\)\nare precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
