neq j)\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (for instance \\(\\alpha_i=i\\)).  Define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\in T_n\\) and,\n   because its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), it is sunny.\n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct.\n\n   Delete the \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\) from\n   \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n-k}\\) and insert the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n   Denote the resulting family by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_k=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(|\\mathcal C_k|=n\\) and exactly the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\)\n   are sunny, i.e. \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Verification that all required points remain covered.**  \n\n   *If \\(b\\le n-k\\)*, the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line\n   \\(y=b\\), which is still present in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\n   *If \\(b>n-k\\)*, then by (3) we have \\(a\\le k\\) and the point is\n   \\((a,b)=(i,n+1-i)\\) for the unique index \\(i=a\\).  This point lies on\n   the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) defined in (5).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n   \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\),\nall these values are attainable, and no larger value is possible because\nthe number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical)\nand \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A covering by horizontal and vertical lines\n\nFix an integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\) and consider  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_m=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=m\\}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-m\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) are horizontal or vertical, hence non\u2011sunny,\nand \\(|\\mathcal L_m|=n\\).\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore either\n\\(b\\le m\\) or \\(a\\le n-m\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n\\(y=b\\) (if \\(b\\le m\\)) or on the line \\(x=a\\) (if \\(a\\le n-m\\)).\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal\nL_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Choice of \\(m\\) adapted to a prescribed \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\,n-k}= \\underbrace{\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have the exclusive alternative  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nb\\le n-k &\\Longrightarrow (a,b)\\text{ lies on the horizontal line }y=b,\\\\[2pt]\nb> n-k   &\\Longrightarrow a\\le n+1-b\\le k\\text{ and }(a,b)\\text{ lies on the vertical line }x=a .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on **exactly one** line of the family\n\\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n-k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Replacing the vertical lines by sunny ones\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) choose a slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j)\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(e.g. \\(\\alpha_i=i\\)).  Define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\in T_n\\) and,\nbecause its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), it is sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct.\n\nDelete the \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\) from \\(\\mathcal\nL_{\\,n-k}\\) and insert the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  Denote\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_k=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal C_k|=n\\); the only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) are\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\), so \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The covering property is preserved\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\)*, the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line\n\\(y=b\\), which is still present in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\n*If \\(b>n-k\\)*, then by (5) we have \\(a\\le k\\) and necessarily\n\\((a,b)=(i,n+1-i)\\) for the unique index \\(i=a\\).  This point lies on\nthe sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) defined in (7).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal\nC_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines,\nno value \\(k>n\\) is possible.  Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\)\nare precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
