            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe must determine, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\), all non‑negative integers\n\\(k\\) for which there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such\nthat  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the\n  \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains only \\(n\\) lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be\nsunny. Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1 Non‑sunny horizontal lines  \n\nTake the \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore they are not sunny.\n\n#### 2.2 Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) consider the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in T_n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nChoose an integer slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nfor instance \\(\\alpha_i=i\\).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\) and, because of (6), is sunny.\n\n#### 2.3 The family of \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,m}\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe first block contributes \\(m=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, the second block\ncontributes \\(k\\) sunny lines; all lines are distinct, so \\(|\\mathcal F_k|=n\\)\nand exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le m=n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*If \\(b>m\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a=i\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\n- If \\(b=n+1-i\\) then \\((a,b)=P_i\\) and belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n- If \\(b<n+1-i\\) then \\(b\\) satisfies \\(n-k+1\\le b\\le n-i\\).  \n  For the fixed column \\(x=i\\) the set of points of \\(T_n\\) with that\n  abscissa is \\(\\{(i,1),\\dots ,(i,n+1-i)\\}\\).  \n  The sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains the highest point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and,\n  because its slope \\(\\alpha_i\\neq0\\), it also contains every point\n  \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge n+1-i\\).  Since \\(b>m=n-k\\) and (9) gives\n  \\(b\\ge n+1-i\\), the point \\((i,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Completion of the argument\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  Together with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) this shows that\nthe set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nHence the problem is completely solved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
