 be covered by a\n   *non\u2011sunny* line.  A non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or of slope\n   \\(-1\\).  It cannot be vertical because a vertical line would intersect a column\n   in more than one point of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the fact that each column can\n   contain at most one point of a vertical line (otherwise the line would be\n   vertical and thus non\u2011sunny, but we are already counting it as a non\u2011sunny line;\n   the contradiction is that a vertical line would then have to cover all points\n   of that column, which is impossible with only one vertical line among the\n   remaining \\(n-2\\) lines).  A line of slope \\(-1\\) meets each row and each column\n   in at most one point as well, but such a line can pass through at most one\n   point of the *first* row and at most one point of the *last* column; consequently\n   it cannot supply the missing coverage for all rows simultaneously.  The only\n   viable non\u2011sunny lines are horizontal lines, each of which lies entirely in a\n   single row and therefore can cover **all** points of that row.\n\n   Hence, to cover every row of \\(T_n\\) we need at least one horizontal line for\n   each row that is not already completely covered by a sunny line.  If two sunny\n   lines were present, they could each cover at most one row completely (the row\n   that contains the unique point of the sunny line).  The remaining \\(n-2\\) rows\n   would each require a distinct horizontal line, giving a total of at least\n   \\((n-2)+2=n\\) lines, which is permissible, but we must also respect the column\n   condition.  The two sunny lines, having distinct slopes, intersect in a point\n   that belongs to some column; that column would then contain two points of\n   non\u2011horizontal lines, contradicting the column\u2011restriction proved above.  The\n   only way to avoid this contradiction is to have **at most one** sunny line.\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\le 1}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction with \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on\n   the line \\(y=b\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n4. **Construction with \\(k=1\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except the single point \\((1,n)\\).  \n   Choose any slope \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha=1\\))\n   and define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y-n=\\alpha\\,(x-1).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\((1,n)\\) and, by the choice of \\(\\alpha\\), is\n   sunny.  Because \\(\\ell\\) meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\))\n   at the point \\((1+(b-n)/\\alpha ,\\,b)\\), which has abscissa \\(<1\\) when\n   \\(\\alpha>0\\) and therefore lies **outside** the set \\(T_n\\), the only point of\n   \\(T_n\\) that belongs to \\(\\ell\\) is \\((1,n)\\).  Consequently the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\}\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely one sunny line,\n   and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(k=1\\) is realizable for all\n   \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n5. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Here  \n\n   \\[\n   T_3=\\{(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that is not horizontal would have to contain two points with the\n   same \\(x\\)-coordinate (the pair \\((1,1),(1,2)\\)) or two points with the same\n   \\(y\\)-coordinate (the pair \\((1,1),(2,1)\\)).  The former forces a vertical line,\n   the latter forces a horizontal line.  The only non\u2011horizontal line through two\n   points of \\(T_3\\) is the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), whose slope is\n   \\(-1\\); this line is also non\u2011sunny.  Therefore every covering of \\(T_3\\) by\n   three distinct lines must consist solely of horizontal lines, and the number\n   of sunny lines is forced to be \\(0\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion** \u2013 the universal bound \\(k\\le 1\\) together with the explicit\n   constructions for \\(k=0\\) (all \\(n\\)) and for \\(k=1\\) (all \\(n\\ge 4\\)) yields the\n   complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\ny=0,\\qquad x=0,\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line is neither horizontal, nor vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound \\(k\\le 1\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) define the *row*\n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the *column*\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBoth the family of rows \\(\\{R_b\\}_{b=1}^{n}\\) and the family of columns\n\\(\\{C_a\\}_{a=1}^{n}\\) consist of exactly \\(n\\) sets.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line meets each row and each column in at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line met a row \\(R_b\\) in two distinct points, those two points would have\nthe same \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(b\\); the line would then be horizontal, contradicting\nthe definition of sunny.  The same argument with the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)\ninterchanged shows that a sunny line cannot meet a column in two distinct\npoints. \u220e\n\n---\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(T_n\\) and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Let the sunny lines be\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f1, each row \\(R_b\\) can contain at most \\(k\\) points that lie on\nsunny lines.  Consequently at least \\(|R_b|-k\\) points of the row must be covered\nby non\u2011sunny lines.  A non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or of\nslope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) can intersect a column \\(C_a\\) in at most one point of\n\\(T_n\\); if it intersected two points of the same column, those points would have\nthe same \\(x\\)-coordinate and the line would be vertical, which is allowed for a\nnon\u2011sunny line.  However, a vertical line can intersect **only one** column of\n\\(T_n\\) (the column whose abscissa equals \\(c\\)).  Hence a vertical line can\ncontribute points to at most one column, i.e. to at most one row as well.  Since we\nhave only \\(n\\) lines in total, using a vertical line would waste a line that\ncould otherwise be used as a horizontal line covering an entire row.  Therefore\nin an optimal covering we may assume that no vertical line is used.\n\n*Lines of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\).  Inside the triangle \\(T_n\\) such a\nline meets each row and each column in at most one point, exactly as in Lemma\u202f1.\nThus a line of slope \\(-1\\) can cover at most one point of each row, which is no\nbetter than a sunny line for the purpose of reducing the number of rows that\nstill need a horizontal line.  Consequently, to minimise the number of rows that\nmust be covered by horizontal lines we may also discard lines of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nHence we may restrict attention to families consisting only of horizontal lines\nand sunny lines.  Each horizontal line lies completely in a single row and\ncovers **all** points of that row.  Let \\(h\\) be the number of horizontal lines\nin the family; then \\(h=n-k\\).\n\nBecause each sunny line can contribute at most one point to any given row,\nthe total number of rows that receive a point from a sunny line is at most \\(k\\).\nAll remaining rows must be covered by horizontal lines, so we need\n\n\\[\nh\\ge n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBut we already have \\(h=n-k\\); therefore equality must hold in (5), which forces\nthe \\(k\\) sunny lines to occupy **different** rows, each providing exactly one\npoint in its row.  In particular, the \\(k\\) sunny lines must be pairwise\nnon\u2011parallel (otherwise two parallel sunny lines would intersect the same row in\nzero points, leaving that row uncovered).  Two distinct non\u2011horizontal, non\u2011\nvertical, non\u2011\\(-1\\) lines intersect in a unique point; that intersection belongs\nto some column of \\(T_n\\).  By Lemma\u202f1 each column can contain at most one point\nfrom the set of sunny lines, so the intersection point would lie in a column\nthat already contains a point of a sunny line, contradicting the \u201cat most one\npoint per column\u2019\u2019 property.  The only way to avoid this contradiction is to\nhave **at most one** sunny line, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le 1}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have\n\\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Hence a covering\nwith \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except the single point \\((1,n)\\).\nChoose a slope \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (for instance\n\\(\\alpha=1\\)) and define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-n=\\alpha\\,(x-1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\((1,n)\\) and, by construction, is sunny.\nFor any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\) the intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the\nhorizontal line \\(y=b\\) is the point \\((1+(b-n)/\\alpha ,\\,b)\\).  Because\n\\(b-n\\le -1\\) and \\(|\\alpha|\\ge 1\\), the abscissa \\(1+(b-n)/\\alpha\\) is strictly\nless than \\(1\\); consequently this intersection lies **outside** the set\n\\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(\\ell\\) contains no point of \\(T_n\\) other than \\((1,n)\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\}\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely one sunny line,\nand covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  Therefore \\(k=1\\) is attainable for all\n\\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\),\n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line that is not horizontal would have to contain two points sharing the\nsame \\(x\\)-coordinate (the pair \\((1,1),(1,2)\\)) or two points sharing the same\n\\(y\\)-coordinate (the pair \\((1,1),(2,1)\\)).  The former forces a vertical line,\nthe latter forces a horizontal line.  The only non\u2011horizontal line through two\npoints of \\(T_3\\) is the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), whose slope is\n\\(-1\\); this line is also non\u2011sunny.  Consequently every covering of \\(T_3\\) by\nthree distinct lines must consist solely of horizontal lines, and the number of\nsunny lines is forced to be \\(0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final answer\n\nCombining the universal bound \\(k\\le 1\\) with the explicit constructions we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ for }n=3,\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ for }n\\ge 4\\; } .\n\\]"
