es it non\u2011sunny.\n\n     Because the set of missing points lies on a single line of slope \\(-1\\), any line that is not parallel to the three forbidden directions can be chosen to pass through all of them (by a suitable translation). Moreover, the new line can be chosen distinct from all existing lines because we may shift it slightly while preserving the required slope.\n\n  3. **Resulting families.**  \n     By the three possibilities above we obtain, from a family with \\(k\\) sunny lines, families with \\(k-1\\), \\(k\\), and \\(k+1\\) sunny lines, now containing \\(n+1\\) lines. Repeating the operation as needed yields families for every \\(k'\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\nThus the induction is complete, and the claim holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe integer points of the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exist three distinct lines covering \\(T_3\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{family of three lines} \\\\ \\hline\n0 & \\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=2\\} \\\\\n1 & \\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;y=2x-1\\} \\\\\n2 & \\{y=1,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y= -\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac52\\} \\\\\n3 & \\{y=2x-1,\\;y= -\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac52,\\;y= \\tfrac13 x+ \\tfrac23\\}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIn each row the three lines are distinct, the required points belong to at least one of them, and the number of sunny lines equals the indicated \\(k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the following statement holds:\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step\n\nWe prove (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**).  \nLet \\(k'\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k'\\le n+1\\). We shall construct a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k')\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines satisfying the required properties.\n\n1. **Start from a suitable \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\).**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) so that \\(|k'-k|\\le1\\); such a \\(k\\) exists because \\(k\\) can be taken as \\(k'\\), \\(k'-1\\) or \\(k'+1\\) (all lie in \\([0,n]\\) when \\(k'\\) is in \\([0,n+1]\\)).  \n   By the inductive hypothesis we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\).\n\n2. **Add the horizontal line \\(y=n+1\\).**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L' = \\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{y=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   The line \\(y=n+1\\) is not sunny.  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal L'\\) now contains \\(n+1\\) lines and covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) **except** the points\n   \\[\n   M_{n+1}= \\{(1,n+1),(2,n),\\dots ,(n+1,1)\\},\n   \\]\n   which lie on the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n3. **Repair the missing points.**  \n   Pick any line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) and delete it; denote the remaining set by \\(\\mathcal L''=\\mathcal L'\\setminus\\{L\\}\\).  \n   We shall insert a new line \\(L^{*}\\) that passes through **all** points of \\(M_{n+1}\\) and whose \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 status is chosen so that the total number of sunny lines becomes exactly \\(k'\\).\n\n   The points of \\(M_{n+1}\\) are collinear on the line \\(x+y=n+2\\).  \n   For any prescribed slope \\(m\\) (including the three forbidden slopes) t