se case \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_3(k)\\) of three distinct lines covering \\(T_3\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\). The following families work:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\mathcal L_3(k) \\\\ \\hline\n0 & \\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=2\\} \\\\\n1 & \\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;y=2x-1\\} \\\\\n2 & \\{y=1,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\} \\\\\n3 & \\{y=2x-1,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\;y=\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac23\\}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIn each row the three lines are distinct, every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of them, and the number of sunny lines equals the indicated \\(k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step: proof of (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**)\n\nLet \\(k'\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k'\\le n+1\\).  \nChoose an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(|k'-k|\\le1\\); such a \\(k\\) always exists (take \\(k=k'\\) if \\(k'\\le n\\), otherwise take \\(k=n\\)).  \nBy (**H\\(_n\\)**) we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\).\n\n1. **Add the diagonal line**  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(D_{n+1}\\) is not sunny (its slope is \\(-1\\)).  \n   The set \\(T_{n+1}\\) consists of \\(T_n\\) together with the points satisfying \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \n   All those new points lie on \\(D_{n+1}\\) except the two corner points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(n+1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal M:=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) except \\(P_1,P_2\\).\n\n2. **Repair the uncovered points.**  \n   Select any line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) and delete it, obtaining  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal M'=\\mathcal M\\setminus\\{L\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   We now insert a new line \\(L^{*}\\) that passes through both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\).  \n   Because \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) are distinct, the set of all lines through them is the one‑parameter family of lines parallel to the line \\(x+y=n+2\\); each such line has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   By translating a line of any prescribed slope parallel to itself we can make it pass through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) (the translation does not change the slope). Consequently we may choose the slope of \\(L^{*}\\) arbitrarily, subject only to the requirement that the resulting line be distinct from the lines already present in \\(\\mathcal M'\\).\n\n   We consider three possibilities.\n\n   * **(i) Preserve the sunny count.**  \n     Choose the slope of \\(L^{*}\\) equal to the slope of the removed line \\(L\\).  \n     Then \\(L^{*}\\) has the same sunny status as \\(L\\); the total number of sunny lines in the final family remains \\(k\\).\n\n   * **(ii) Increase the sunny count by one.**  \n     Choose a slope \\(m\\notin S\\) and also different from the slopes of all lines in \\(\\mathcal M'\\) (there are only finitely many forbidden slopes, so such an \\(m\\) exists).  \n     The resulting \\(L^{*}\\) is sunny, so the total number of sunny lines becomes \\(k+1\\).\n\n   * **(iii) Decrease the sunny count by one.**  \n     Choose \\(m\\in S\\) (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n     Then \\(L^{*}\\) is non‑sunny, and the total number of sunny lines becomes \\(k-1\\).\n\n   In each case we may shift the line slightly (keeping its slope) to guarantee that it does not coincide with any line already in \\(\\mathcal M'\\); the shift does not affect the fact