owed by (1).\n\n   *Case \\(k=n\\).*  \n   Choose \\(n\\) distinct sunny slopes (e.g. \\(m_i=i\\)).  \n   Because \\(|S|\\ge n\\), select \\(n\\) distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S\\) and let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x+b_i-m_i a_i .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, passes through \\((a_i,b_i)\\), and the lines are distinct.  \n   The remaining points of \\(S\\) (if any) lie on some of these lines because a sunny line can contain arbitrarily many points; thus all points of \\(S\\) are covered.\n\n   *Case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Start with the construction for \\(k=n\\) and delete one of the sunny lines; let the two points that were on the deleted line be \\((c,n+1-c)\\) and \\((c+1,n-c)\\).  \n   These two points lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n   Adding this single non\u2011sunny line to the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly one non\u2011sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n   *Case \\(k=n-2\\).*  \n   Begin with the construction for \\(k=n\\) and delete two sunny lines, leaving four uncovered points of \\(S\\).  \n   Arrange the four points as two opposite pairs on two lines of slope \\(-1\\):\n   \\[\n   (c,\\,n+1-c),\\;(c+1,\\,n-c)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   (d,\\,n+1-d),\\;(d+1,\\,n-d).\n   \\]\n   Each pair lies on a distinct line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Adding these two non\u2011sunny lines to the remaining \\(n-2\\) sunny lines gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n   In all three cases the required covering condition is satisfied and the lines are distinct.\n\nCombining the necessary condition (1) with the explicit constructions shows that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Size of \\(S\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. How many points of \\(S\\) can lie on a non\u2011sunny line?\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to one of\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:x=c,\\qquad \\ell_{y}:y=c,\\qquad \\ell_{d}:x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n*Vertical or horizontal.*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on \\(x=c\\) then \\(a=c\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\), there is at most one admissible \\(b\\);\nhence a vertical line contains at most one point of \\(S\\).  \nThe same argument works for a horizontal line.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on \\(x+y=c\\) then \\(c\\le n+1\\).  \nWriting the points of \\(S\\) on this line gives the list  \n\n\\[\n(c-1,1),\\;(c-2,2),\\dots ,\\;(1,c-1).\n\\]\n\nThe condition \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) forces \\(c\\ge 2\\); the additional inequality\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) eliminates all but the two points with maximal first coordinate,\nnamely  \n\n\\[\n(c-1,\\,n+2-c)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(c,\\,n+1-c),\n\\]\n\nprovided they are defined.  Consequently a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at\nmost two points of \\(S\\).\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A non\u2011sunny line contains }\\le 2\\text{ points of }S.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\), and let\n\\(t\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines among them.  \nBy (3) the non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(2t\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nThe remaining \\(n-t\\) sunny lines are distinct.  Two distinct sunny lines can\nshare many points, but each *new* line can introduce at most one point of