(n-1\\) sunny\nlines yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(S\\) and having\nexactly one non\u2011sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3. \\(k=n-2\\) (exactly two non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nAgain begin with the \\(k=n\\) construction and delete two sunny lines,\nleaving four uncovered points of \\(S\\).  \nChoose the four points as two opposite pairs on two distinct lines of slope\n\\(-1\\):\n\n\\[\n(c,\\,n+1-c),\\;(c+1,\\,n-c)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n(d,\\,n+1-d),\\;(d+1,\\,n-d),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(1\\le c<d\\le n-1\\).  Each pair lies on a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); the\ntwo lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.  Adding these two\nnon\u2011sunny lines to the remaining \\(n-2\\) sunny lines gives a family of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) with exactly two non\u2011sunny lines,\ni.e. \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nInequality (5) shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\\(k\\ge n-2\\).  The explicit constructions of Sections\u202f4.1\u20134.3 demonstrate that\neach of the three values \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) is attainable.  Hence these are\nprecisely the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible }k\\text{ are }n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n.}\n\\]"
