d a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the *anti\u2011diagonal*\n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(T_n\\) is the disjoint union of the \\(n\\) anti\u2011diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound\n\nExactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are used, therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny\nlines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\) is impossible\n\nA *sunny* line is a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  \nIf \\(L\\) meets an anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_s\\) in a point \\((a,b)\\) then\n\\(a+b=s\\).  Because the slope of \\(L\\) is not \\(-1\\), the equation of \\(L\\)\ncannot be of the form \\(x+y=c\\); consequently the intersection of \\(L\\) with the family\n\\(\\{D_s\\}\\) consists of at most **two** distinct anti\u2011diagonals (a line can meet a family of parallel lines in at most one point each).  \n\nHence a single sunny line can contain points from at most two different\n\\(D_s\\).  With \\(k=n\\) sunny lines we could cover at most \\(2n\\) anti\u2011diagonals,\nbut there are exactly \\(n\\) anti\u2011diagonals, so a covering *is* possible in principle.\nThe obstruction is that a sunny line cannot be parallel to any anti\u2011diagonal,\nso each sunny line can intersect **exactly one** anti\u2011diagonal in at most one point.\nIndeed, if a sunny line intersected two distinct anti\u2011diagonals, the two\nintersection points would have different sums \\(a+b\\) and therefore could not\nlie on the same line whose slope is not \\(-1\\).  Consequently a sunny line can\ncontain **at most one** point of each anti\u2011diagonal, and because each\nanti\u2011diagonal contains at