es, but this is\nirrelevant: the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) is already covered by the vertical\nline \\(V\\) (all its points have abscissa \\(a\\ge 1\\), and those with \\(a=1\\) lie\non \\(V\\)).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{C}_1 = \\mathcal{F}\\cup\\{S\\},\n\\]\n\nand \\(\\mathcal{C}_1\\) contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n#### 4.2 Obtaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines\n\nRotate the whole picture \\(\\mathcal{C}_1\\) by \\(90^{\\circ}\\) about the origin.\nA rotation sends a non\u2011sunny horizontal line to a non\u2011sunny vertical line,\nand a non\u2011sunny line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) to another line parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\); the sunny line \\(S\\) is sent to a different sunny line (its slope\nbecomes \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  Performing this rotation three times we obtain three\nconfigurations, each with exactly one sunny line, and the three sunny lines\nare distinct because their slopes are \\(2,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,\\tfrac12\\).\n\nFinally, add the original vertical line \\(V\\) (which is non\u2011sunny) to the\nunion of the three rotated families.  The resulting set consists of\n\n* the vertical line \\(V\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* three families of \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines (parallel to the axes or to\n  \\(x+y=0\\));\n* three distinct sunny lines.\n\nAltogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(n-1\\) are sunny.\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  No other values of \\(k\\) are possible\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, where \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Each sunny line* can intersect at most two anti\u2011diagonals, because its slope\nis different from \\(-1\\).  Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together intersect at\nmost \\(2k\\) distinct anti\u2011diagonals.\n\nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; they are of three possible types:\n\n* horiz