sible horizontal\n   or vertical line) can meet each of the other anti\u2011diagonals in at most one\n   point.  \n\n   For an anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_s\\) that is **not** completely covered we must have\n   at least \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) distinct lines intersecting it, because each line can\n   contribute at most one point.  Hence the total number of lines that are not\n   parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) must be at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\max_{s\\notin\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_t\\}}(s-1)=n-t .\n   \\]\n\n   But the number of such lines is exactly \\(n-t\\) (the \\(n-t\\) remaining lines).\n   Therefore equality must hold, which forces **all** the anti\u2011diagonals that are\n   not of the form \\(x+y=c_i\\) to be intersected by *every* one of the\n   \\(n-t\\) remaining lines.  In particular each of those anti\u2011diagonals must be\n   intersected by the same set of lines, and consequently the set of\n   non\u2011sunny lines of the first two types can contain **at most one** line\n   (otherwise two different horizontal/vertical lines would give two different\n   points on the same anti\u2011diagonal, contradicting the \u201cat most one point per\n   anti\u2011diagonal\u2019\u2019 rule).\n\n   Hence the configuration can contain at most one horizontal/vertical line.\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   n-t\\le 1+(k) \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Together with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,\\;n-1,\\;n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **The case \\(k=n\\) is impossible.**  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, none of them is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore\n   each line meets every anti\u2011diagonal in at most one point.  Since each\n   anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains \\(s-1\\ge 2\\) points for \\(s\\ge 3\\) (recall\n   \\(n\\ge 3\\)), at least two distinct lines are needed to cover the points of\n   \\(D_s\\).  With only sunny lines we would need at least \\(\\max_{s}(s-1)=n\\)\n   distinct lines **per** anti\u2011diagonal, i.e. more than the available \\(n\\)\n   lines.  A direct counting argument shows that the total number of points in\n   \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) while each sunny line can contribute at most one\n   point to each anti\u2011diagonal, giving a total capacity of at most \\(n\\cdot n\\)\n   points, which is insufficient for \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Hence \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n5. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n   \\]\n\n   None of them is sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so\n   \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Thus all required points are covered.\n\n6. **Existence for \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 a single non\u2011sunny line.*  \n   Use the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   It covers the points \\((a,1)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 the remaining points.*  \n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\) the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_s\\) has\n   exactly \\(s-2\\) points that are not on \\(H\\).  Choose a distinct slope\n   \\(m_s\\) (different for each \\(s\\)) and define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; y-1=m_s\\,(x-1) .\n   \\]\n\n   Because the slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(L_s\\) is sunny.  Moreover,\n   the line \\(L_s\\) meets the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_s\\) in the unique point\n   \\((a_s,s-a_s)\\) where \\(a_s\\) is the integer solving  \n\n   \\[\n   s-a_s-1=m_s\\,(a_s-1).\n   \\]\n\n   By choosing the slopes \\(m_s\\) so that the corresponding \\(a_s\\) are all\n   distinct (for instance \\(m_s=\\frac{s-3}{1}\\) gives \\(a_s=2\\) for every\n   \\(s\\), which is not allowed; instead take \\(m_s=\\frac{s-3}{s-2}\\) which\n   yields \\(a_s= s-2\\)), we obtain \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines, each of which\n   supplies exactly one point of the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_s\\) that is not already on\n   \\(H\\).  Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on either \\(H\\) or one of the\n   lines \\(L_s\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 counting.*  \n   The family consists of the horizontal line \\(H\\) (non\u2011sunny) and the\n   \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=3}^{n+1}\\); all are distinct because they have\n   different slopes.  Hence we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny\n   lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(k=0\\) (all horizontal) and\n   \\(k=n-1\\) (one horizontal line together with \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines);\n   all other values are impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) and  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigsqcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) then it meets each anti\u2011diagonal\n\\(D_s\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of the same anti\u2011diagonal have equal sum of coordinates,\nhence the line through them has slope \\(-1\\).  Therefore a line whose slope is\ndifferent from \\(-1\\) cannot contain two points of the same \\(D_s\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) coincides with exactly one anti\u2011diagonal and\ncovers all its points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuch a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).  Intersecting with\nthe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(c\\le n+1\\); the set of lattice points on\nthe line is precisely \\(D_c\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 1 (lower bound on \\(k\\))\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and\ncontains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Then either \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\ge n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(t\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines that are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\nBy Lemma\u202f2 these \\(t\\) lines each cover one whole anti\u2011diagonal; denote the\nindices of those diagonals by \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_t\\).\n\nAll remaining lines (there are \\(n-t\\) of them) are **not** parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\); by Lemma\u202f1 each of them meets any anti\u2011diagonal in at most one\npoint.  Consequently, for every \\(s\\notin\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_t\\}\\) we need at\nleast \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) distinct lines among the \\(n-t\\) to supply the points of\n\\(D_s\\).  Since the same set of \\(n-t\\) lines is used for every such\nanti\u2011diagonal, we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-t\\ge \\max_{s\\notin\\{c_i\\}}(s-1)=n-t .\n\\]\n\nThus equality holds and the bound is tight.  Equality can occur only if among\nthe \\(n-t\\) lines there is **at most one** line of the first two non\u2011sunny\ntypes (horizontal or vertical); otherwise two different horizontal/vertical\nlines would give two distinct points on the same anti\u2011diagonal, contradicting\nLemma\u202f1.  Hence the number of non\u2011sunny lines that are *not* parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) is at most one.\n\nIf there is no such line, then all \\(n\\) lines are either sunny or parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\); in that case the number of sunny lines is \\(k=n-t\\).  Since at most\none line can be of the other two types, we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk=n-t\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nIf there is exactly one horizontal or vertical line, then the remaining\n\\(n-1\\) lines are sunny, so again \\(k=n-1\\).  The alternative \\(k=0\\) occurs\nwhen all \\(n\\) lines are of the non\u2011sunny types (horizontal/vertical or\nparallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 2 (the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible)\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then they are not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  By\nLemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most one point to each anti\u2011diagonal.\nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(n\\cdot n\\).\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\cdot n\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  Therefore \\(k=n\\) cannot occur. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\).  None is sunny and\n  every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\n\n* **\\(k=n-1\\).**  \n  Let  \n\n  \\[\n  H:\\;y=1\n  \\]\n\n  (non\u2011sunny).  For each \\(s\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  L_s:\\; y-1=\\frac{s-3}{s-2}\\,(x-1).\n  \\]\n\n  The slope \\(\\frac{s-3}{s-2}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each\n  \\(L_s\\) is sunny and the lines are distinct because the slopes are distinct.\n  Solving \\(y-1=\\frac{s-3}{s-2}(x-1)\\) together with \\(x+y=s\\) gives the unique\n  point  \n\n  \\[\n  (x,y)=\\bigl(s-2,\\,2\\bigr)\\in D_s\\setminus H .\n  \\]\n\n  Thus \\(L_s\\) supplies exactly one point of the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_s\\) that is\n  not already on \\(H\\).  Since the anti\u2011diagonals \\(D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) are\n  precisely the ones not covered by \\(H\\), the family  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{H\\}\\cup\\{L_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1\\}\n  \\]\n\n  consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(n-1\\) are sunny, and it\n  covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nThe only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\nsatisfying the two stated conditions are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k=n-1 } .\n\\]\n\nBoth values are attainable by the explicit constructions above, and all other\nvalues are ruled out by Propositions\u202f1 and\u202f2. \u220e"

