ag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **A necessary inequality.**  \n\n   Consider an anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_s\\) that is **not** one of the \\(t\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each of the other \\(n-t\\) lines can contribute at most one point of \\(D_s\\); therefore we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   n-t\\;\\ge\\;|D_s|=s-1 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(S\\) be the set of indices of the anti\u2011diagonals that are covered by a slope \\(-1\\) line; \\(|S|=t\\).  \n   The strongest condition (3) is obtained for the **largest** index that is *not* in \\(S\\).  \n   To make this largest index as small as possible we may assume that the \\(t\\) slope \\(-1\\) lines cover the \\(t\\) largest anti\u2011diagonals, i.e. those with indices  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1,\\;n,\\;\\dots ,\\;n+2-t .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the largest uncovered index equals \\(n+1-t\\), and (3) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   n-t\\;\\ge\\;(n+1-t)-1\\;=\\;n-t .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality holds, so (3) imposes **no further restriction** on \\(t\\).  \n   The only information we have extracted from (3) is that the set of the remaining \\(n-t\\) lines must be able to meet **every** uncovered anti\u2011diagonal, which is possible exactly when each uncovered anti\u2011diagonal receives a distinct line among those \\(n-t\\) lines.\n\n5. **How many non\u2011sunny lines can coexist?**  \n\n   Suppose that among the \\(n-t\\) non\u2011\\((-1)\\) lines we have **both** a horizontal and a vertical line.  \n   These two lines intersect at a point \\((x_0,y_0)\\) with \\(x_0,y_0\\ge 1\\).  \n   The point \\((x_0,y_0)\\) lies on the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{x_0+y_0}\\).  \n   Consequently the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{x_0+y_0}\\) would contain **two** points coming from the two distinct lines, contradicting Lemma\u202f1 (which says that any line not of slope \\(-1\\) can contribute at most one point to a given anti\u2011diagonal, but does **not** forbid two different lines from contributing two different points; however the crucial 