            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k=n-1 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Anti‑diagonals.**  \n   For \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=s\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}= \\bigsqcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(D_s\\) is a straight line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Two elementary lemmas.**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line **not** parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. not of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Then \\(\\ell\\) meets every anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point.\n\n   *Proof.*  The anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) is the line \\(x+y=s\\), which also has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Two distinct non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point; therefore a line whose slope is different from \\(-1\\) can intersect \\(x+y=s\\) in at most one point. ∎\n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) coincides with exactly one anti‑diagonal \\(D_c\\) (namely the one with \\(c\\) equal to its intercept) and contains **all** points of that anti‑diagonal.\n\n   *Proof.*  If a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\), then by definition its set of points is precisely \\(D_c\\). ∎\n\n3. **Notation for the three non‑sunny types.**  \n\n   * horizontal lines \\(y=\\text{const}\\);\n   * vertical lines  \\(x=\\text{const}\\);\n   * slope \\(-1\\) lines \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).\n\n   Let  \n\n   * \\(t\\) = number of lines of slope \\(-1\\) (hence \\(0\\le t\\le n\\));\n   * \\(h\\) = number of horizontal lines;\n   * \\(v\\) = number of vertical lines.\n\n   The remaining \\(n-t-h-v\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-t-h-v .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **A necessary inequality.**  \n\n   Consider an anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) that is **not** one of the \\(t\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   By Lemma 1 each of the other \\(n-t\\) lines can contribute at most one point of \\(D_s\\); therefore we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   n-t\\;\\ge\\;|D_s|=s-1 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(S\\) be the set of indices of the anti‑diagonals that are covered by a slope \\(-1\\) line; \\(|S|=t\\).  \n   The strongest condition (3) is obtained for the **largest** index that is *not* in \\(S\\).  \n   To make this largest index as small as possible we may assume that the \\(t\\) slope \\(-1\\) lines cover the \\(t\\) largest anti‑diagonals, i.e. those with indices  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1,\\;n,\\;\\dots ,\\;n+2-t .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the largest uncovered index equals \\(n+1-t\\), and (3) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   n-t\\;\\ge\\;(n+1-t)-1\\;=\\;n-t .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality holds, so (3) imposes **no further restriction** on \\(t\\).  \n   The only information we have extracted from (3) is that the set of the remaining \\(n-t\\) lines must be able to meet **every** uncovered anti‑diagonal, which is possible exactly when each uncovered anti‑diagonal receives a distinct line among those \\(n-t\\) lines.\n\n5. **How many non‑sunny lines can coexist?**  \n\n   Suppose that among the \\(n-t\\) non‑\\((-1)\\) lines we have **both** a horizontal and a vertical line.  \n   These two lines intersect at a point \\((x_0,y_0)\\) with \\(x_0,y_0\\ge 1\\).  \n   The point \\((x_0,y_0)\\) lies on the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{x_0+y_0}\\).  \n   Consequently the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{x_0+y_0}\\) would contain **two** points coming from the two distinct lines, contradicting Lemma 1 (which says that any line not of slope \\(-1\\) can contribute at most one point to a given anti‑diagonal, but does **not** forbid two different lines from contributing two different points; however the crucial observation is that the two points would have the same *\\(x\\)*‑coordinate or the same *\\(y\\)*‑coordinate, which forces one of the lines to be vertical or horizontal – a situation that cannot happen simultaneously for the same anti‑diagonal because the anti‑diagonal has slope \\(-1\\). A more direct argument is the following.)\n\n   **Claim.** Among the \\(n-t\\) lines that are not of slope \\(-1\\) there can be **at most one** line that is horizontal **or** vertical.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Assume there are two distinct horizontal lines, say \\(y=c_1\\) and \\(y=c_2\\) with \\(c_1\\neq c_2\\).  \n   Both intersect the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{c_1+1}\\) (the point \\((1,c_1)\\)) and \\(D_{c_2+1}\\) (the point \\((1,c_2)\\)).  \n   No difficulty arises yet.  \n   The problem appears when we also have a vertical line \\(x=d\\).  \n   The vertical line meets the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{d+ c_1}\\) at \\((d,c_1)\\) and also meets \\(D_{d+ c_2}\\) at \\((d,c_2)\\).  \n   Thus the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{d+ c_1}\\) would contain the point \\((d,c_1)\\) coming from the vertical line and also the point \\((1,c_1)\\) coming from the horizontal line \\(y=c_1\\).  \n   These two points are distinct, so \\(D_{d+ c_1}\\) would have **two** points supplied by two different non‑\\((-1)\\) lines, which is allowed.  \n   However, the set of all non‑\\((-1)\\) lines must be able to cover each uncovered anti‑diagonal with a *different* line (otherwise some anti‑diagonal