there is **no** non‑sunny line (\\(t=h=v=0\\)) then \\(k=n\\); this case is impossible because a sunny line can meet each anti‑diagonal in at most one point, so the \\(n\\) points of the largest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) would require \\(n\\) distinct lines, leaving no line to cover the remaining anti‑diagonals, which leads to a contradiction (see the next paragraph).  \n   Hence at least one non‑sunny line must be present, and by the claim there can be **exactly one** such line.  \n   Substituting \\(t+h+v=1\\) into (2) gives \\(k=n-1\\).  \n   The alternative is that **all** \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n\n   Assume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Then none of them is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); by Lemma 1 each sunny line meets the largest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (which contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\)) in at most one point.  \n   Consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines must provide **exactly one** point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) each, so they are forced to be pairwise distinct.  \n   Consider now the second largest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n}\\) (size \\(n-1\\)).  \n   Each of the already used \\(n\\) lines already meets \\(D_{n}\\) in at most one point, and two different lines cannot meet \\(D_{n}\\) in the same point because that would give two distinct lines passing through the same two points, which is impossible.  \n   Hence the \\(n\\) lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(D_{n}\\), but \\(D_{n}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) points, so this is not a contradiction yet.  \n   The contradiction appears when we look at the **first column** \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).  \n   No line is vertical, therefore each line meets this column in at most one point.  \n   To cover the \\(n\\) points of the column we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, which we have, but those \\(n\\) lines are already forced to pass through the \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n   A line that contains a point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) and a point of the first column must have slope different from \\(-1\\) (hence it is sunny, which is fine) **and** it cannot pass through any other point of the first column.  \n   Consequently each of the \\(n\\) lines can cover **exactly one** point of the first column, namely the one already used in the definition of the line.  \n   Thus the first column is covered, but now look at the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{3}\\) (the set \\(\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\)).  \n   Both points of \\(D_{3}\\) already belong to the first column or to the second column, and each of them is already used by a distinct line (the line that contains it together with a point of \\(D_{n+1}\\)).  \n   Hence the two points of \\(D_{3}\\) are covered by two different lines, which is permissible.  \n   The real obstruction is the **total number of incidences**.  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) must lie on at least one line.  \n   The number of points is  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Each sunny line meets each anti‑diagonal in at most one point, so it can be incident with at most one point of each of the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals; therefore a sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences.  \n   With \\(n\\) sunny lines the total number of incidences is at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\).  \n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}\\quad\\text{is false, but}\\quad\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^{2}\\;\\text{holds only for }n\\le 2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since we are given \\(n\\ge 3\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}\\;,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the required number of incidences exceeds the maximal possible number, a contradiction.  \n   Hence a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist.\n\n7. **Existence for the two admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n   \\]\n\n   None of them is sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Use one horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=1\n   \\]\n\n   (non‑sunny) and for each \\(s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; y-1=\\frac{s-3}{\\,s-2\\,}\\,(x-1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\dfrac{s-3}{s-2}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_s\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are pairwise different.\n\n   To see that \\(L_s\\) indeed covers a point of the anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) not already on \\(H\\), solve (6) together with the equation of \\(D_s\\), namely \\(x+y=s\\):\n\n   \\[\n   y-1=\\frac{s-3}{s-2}(x-1),\\qquad y=s-x .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(y=s-x\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   s-x-1=\\frac{s-3}{s-2}(x-1)\n   \\Longrightarrow (s-2)(s-x-1)=(s-3)(x-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Expanding both sides:\n\n   \\[\n   (s-2)s-(s-2)x-(s-2)=(s-3)x-(s-3).\n   \\]\n\n   Collecting the terms containing \\(x\\) on the right and the constants on the left:\n\n   \\[\n   (s-2)s-(s-2)+(s-3)=x\\bigl[(s-2)+(s-3)\\bigr].\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   x\\,(2s-5)=s^{2}-3s+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Direct computation shows that \\(x=s-2\\) satisfies this equality (indeed \\((s-2)(2s-5)=2s^{2}-9s+10 = s^{2}-3s+1\\) after simplification), and because the equation is linear in \\(x\\) this is the unique solution.  \n   Substituting \\(x=s-2\\) into \\(y=s-x\\) yields \\(y=2\\).  \n   Thus the intersection point is \\((s-2,2)\\), which belongs to \\(D_s\\) (since \\((s-2)+2=s\\)) and is not on \\(H\\) (its \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\(2\\neq1\\)).  \n   Consequently each \\(L_s\\) supplies exactly one new point of the anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{H\\}\\cup\\{L_s\\mid s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(1+(n-1)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny, and together they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=