            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nRecall that a line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), undefined (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nEach of them is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge 1\\), then \\(a\\le n\\); consequently the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus all required points are covered and we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad (0\\le m\\le n).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=m .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence they are not sunny.\n\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{j}:\\; y = x-(m+j-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_{j}\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions (slope \\(0\\), undefined, \\(-1\\)). Consequently each \\(L_{j}\\) is sunny.  \nThe intercepts \\(-(m+j-1)\\) are distinct, so the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are distinct.\n\nThus we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(m=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on one of the constructed lines\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le m\\).*  \nThen the point belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (1).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\ge m+1\\).*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nj:=a-b-m+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1=m+k+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le m+k+1-a\\le k ,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n1\\le j = a-b-m+1 \\le a-1-m+1 \\le (m+k)-1-m+1 = k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(j\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \nFrom (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb = a-(m+j-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of the line \\(L_{j}\\) in (2).  \nTherefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{j}\\).\n\nIn both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) is contained in at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Determination of all possible \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction above works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); it yields \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince there are only \\(n\\) lines in total, it is impossible to have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Consequently no value \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution abov