tive integers, the left–hand side of (3) is an integer; consequently \\(m\\) must be rational.  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\).  \nEquation (3) then yields  \n\n\\[\nq(b_1-b_2)=p(a_1-a_2).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the integer \\(q\\) divides \\(a_1-a_2\\).  \nBut \\(a_1,a_2\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(|a_1-a_2|\\le n-1\\).  \nIf a third distinct point \\((a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) also lay on the line, the same reasoning would force \\(q\\mid a_1-a_3\\) and \\(q\\mid a_2-a_3\\); hence \\(q\\) would divide the difference of any two of the three abscissae, and therefore \\(q\\) would divide their greatest common divisor, which is at most \\(1\\).  \nThus \\(q=1\\) and \\(m\\) is an integer.  \n\nIf \\(m\\ge 2\\) or \\(m\\le -2\\), the line \\(y=mx+c\\) would intersect the region \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge 1\\}\\) in at most one lattice point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), because the step in the \\(y\\)-direction exceeds the step in the \\(x\\)-direction by at least one unit.  \nIf \\(m=1\\) the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  \nIf \\(m=0\\) the line is horizontal, again contradicting sunny.  \n\nHence the only possible integer slopes for a sunny line are \\(m=-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) or \\(m=0\\) (horizontal), both excluded.  \nTherefore a sunny line cannot have integer slope; consequently it can contain at most two points of the integer lattice, and in particular at most two points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line (i.e. a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b_0\\) with \\(1\\le b_0\\le n\\), then the condition \\(a+b_0\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_0\\); thus there are exactly \\(n+1-b_0\\le n\\) admissible points.  \nThe vertical case is symmetric.  \nIf the line is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+