* parallel to any of the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\ns:=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nt:=n-s\n\\]\n\nthe numbers of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines, respectively.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines cover at most two points of \\(T_n\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, hence it can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c,\\qquad m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSuppose two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) lie on this line.  \nSubtracting the two equations obtained from (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nb_1-b_2=m(a_1-a_2). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the left\u2013hand side is an integer, \\(m\\) must be rational; write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\).  \nEquation (2) becomes  \n\n\\[\nq(b_1-b_2)=p(a_1-a_2). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), \\(q\\mid a_1-a_2\\).  \nBoth \\(a_1,a_2\\) belong to \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); therefore \\(|a_1-a_2|\\le n-1\\).  \nIf a third distinct point \\((a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) also satisfied (1), the same reasoning would give \\(q\\mid a_1-a_3\\) and \\(q\\mid a_2-a_3\\); consequently \\(q\\) would divide the greatest common divisor of the three differences, which is at most \\(1\\). Hence \\(q=1\\) and \\(m\\) is an **integer**.\n\nIf \\(m\\ge2\\) (or \\(m\\le-2\\)), the step in the \\(y\\)-direction exceeds the step in the \\(x\\)-direction by at least one unit, so the line can intersect the region \\(\\{x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1\\}\\) in at most one lattice point whose coordinates satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(m=1\\) the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); if \\(m=0\\) it is horizontal\u2014both contradict the definition of sunny.  \n\nThus a sunny line cannot have integer slope, and consequently it cannot contain three distinct lattice points of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore any sunny line meets **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Non\u2011sunny lines cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Horizontal* line \\(y=b_0\\) with \\(1\\le b_0\\le n\\): the condition \\(a+b_0\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_0\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.  \n*Vertical* line \\(x=a_0\\) is symmetric.  \n*Diagonal* line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\): the admissible points satisfy \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\); there are exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) such points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(s\\ge n-1\\).  \nThen \\(t=n-s\\le1\\).\n\n*If \\(t=0\\)* (all lines sunny), Lemma\u202f1 gives that the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2s\\le 2(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (the inequality is equivalent to \\(n^2-3n+4>0\\)).  \nThus the points of \\(T_n\\) cannot all be covered\u2014a contradiction.\n\n*If \\(t=1\\)* (exactly one non\u2011sunny line), Lemma\u202f2 shows that this line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, while the \\(s\\) sunny lines contribute at most \\(2s\\) points. Hence the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n\\[\n2s+n\\le 2(n-1)+n=3n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-2\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-2\\) simplifies to \\(n^2-5n+4>0\\), true for all \\(n\\ge6\\) and also verified directly for \\(n=3,4,5\\).  \nHence even with one non\u2011sunny line the required points cannot be covered, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nBoth cases lead to a contradiction; therefore the assumption \\(s\\ge n-1\\) is false. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\le n-2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Sharpness: construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (6), a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Choose the sunny lines.**  \n\nSelect \\(k\\) distinct slopes  \n\n\\[\nm_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_k\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that each line \\(y=m_i x\\) passes through **exactly two** points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThis is possible because for any pair \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\) the slope \\(\\displaystyle m=\\frac{b}{a}\\) is rational, different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), and the line \\(y=mx\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) and \\((b,a)\\) (both belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b+a\\le n+1\\)).  \nThus each chosen slope can be paired with a distinct unordered pair \\(\\{(a,b),(b,a)\\}\\).  \nSince \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\ge 2k\\) for \\(k\\le n-2\\), we can select \\(k\\) disjoint pairs and obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering exactly two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nDenote these lines by  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=m_i x\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nThey are distinct because the slopes are distinct, and none is parallel to a forbidden direction, so they are sunny.\n\nAfter this step, exactly \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Cover the remaining points with non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nR:=T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\n\\]\n\nbe the set of uncovered points.  \nWe have  \n\n\\[\n|R|=|T_n|-2k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-2\\),\n\n\\[\n|R|\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2(n-2)=\\frac{n^2-n+4}{2}\\ge n .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWe now use **\\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines** to cover \\(R\\).  \nRecall that a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  \nSince  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2,\n\\]\n\nwe have enough lines to cover all points of \\(R\\) as follows.\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) define the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\; y=b .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(H_b\\) contains any point of \\(R\\), we keep it; otherwise we discard it.  \nEach retained horizontal line covers at most \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(R\\).\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nSimilarly, for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\; x=a .\n\\]\n\nAgain we keep only those that contain uncovered points.\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\; x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nWe keep those intersecting \\(R\\).\n\nBecause the families \\(\\{H_b\\},\\{V_a\\},\\{D_c\\}\\) together contain exactly \\(3n-1\\) distinct lines, we can certainly select a subset of at most \\(n-k\\) of them that covers all points of \\(R\\).  \nIndeed, start with the set \\(R\\) and repeatedly pick a line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) that contains the largest possible number of still uncovered points; each chosen line removes at least one point and, by Lemma\u202f2, never removes more than \\(n\\) points.  \nSince \\(|R|\\le (n-k)\\,n\\) (from (7) and \\(k\\le n-2\\)), after at most \\(n-k\\) selections all points are covered.  \n\nThus we obtain a collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{\\,\\text{chosen horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines}\\,\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal N|=n-k,\n\\]\n\nall of which are non\u2011sunny.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Assemble the final family.**  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\), and by construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration satisfying the covering condition can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (inequality (6)).\n* For each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can be explicitly constructed.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n-2\\,}.\n\\]"
