            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n\\(|S|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\); geometrically \\(S\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle bounded by the lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A family with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it is **non‑sunny**.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) are distinct and every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\in D_{a+b}\\).  Thus these \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines cover \\(S\\).  Therefore \\(k=0\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines that replace a block of diagonals\n\nFor a positive integer \\(t\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;y = t\\,x-(t-1).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_t\\) equals \\(t\\neq 0,-1\\); consequently \\(L_t\\) is **sunny**.\n\nLet \\(a\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (2.1) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = t a-(t-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le n+1-t\\),\n\n\\[\nb = t a-(t-1)\\ge t-(t-1)=1,\\qquad\nb = t a-(t-1)\\le t(n+1-t)-(t-1)=n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\).  Moreover  \n\n\\[\na+b = a + t a-(t-1) = (t+1)a-(t-1),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}=D_{(t+1)a-(t-1)}\\).  Varying \\(a\\) from \\(1\\) to \\(n+1-t\\) shows that  \n\n\\[\nL_t\\cap S = D_{t+1}\\cup D_{t+2}\\cup\\cdots\\cup D_{n+1}.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThus the single sunny line \\(L_t\\) covers **all** points that belong to the diagonals whose indices are at least \\(t+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Take the sunny lines*  \n\n\\[\nL_1,\\;L_2,\\;\\dots ,\\;L_k .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nBy (2.2) the union of these lines covers the points of the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{k+1}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n*Cover the remaining diagonals* by the non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nD_{k+2},\\;D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nThe set (3.3) consists of exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct lines, each of which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) belongs either to a diagonal listed in (3.2) (hence to a sunny line) or to a diagonal listed in (3.3) (hence to a non‑sunny line).  Consequently the family consisting of the \\(k\\) sunny lines (3.1) together with the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny diagonals (3.3) satisfies the required covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustion of possibilities\n\nAny admissible family contains precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which is either sunny or non‑sunny.  Therefore the number of sunny lines must be an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  The construction of §3 shows that **every** integer in this interval can be realised.  Hence no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
