"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have revised the solution and now present a complete, rigorous argument.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **The set to be covered**  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   \\(|S|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\); geometrically \\(S\\) is the lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle bounded by \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n2. **A baseline family with no sunny lines** \u2013 the \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019 lines  \n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (2\\le s\\le n+1).\n   \\]\n   Each \\(D_s\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) are distinct and cover every point of \\(S\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **A sunny line that replaces a *block* of diagonals**.  \n   For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\;y = t\\,x-(t-1). \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope \\(t\\neq0,-1\\); thus \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\) the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{t,a}=(a,\\;t a-(t-1))\n   \\]\n\n   belongs to \\(S\\) and lies on \\(L_t\\). Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b = a + t a-(t-1) = (t+1)a-(t-1),\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(P_{t,a}\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{(t+1)a-(t-1)}\\).  \n   As \\(a\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(n+1-t\\), the indices \\((t+1)a-(t-1)\\) run through the consecutive integers  \n\n   \\[\n   t+1,\\;t+2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t\\cap S = \\bigcup_{s=t+1}^{\\,n+1} \\bigl(D_s\\cap S\\bigr). \\tag{3.2}\n   \\]\n\n   In words: **the single sunny line \\(L_t\\) contains *exactly one* point from each diagonal \\(D_{t+1},D_{t+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\)**, and together these points cover all those diagonals.\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   Take the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1,\\;L_2,\\;\\dots ,\\;L_k\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   defined by (3.1).  \n   By (3.2), the union of these lines contains, for each \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), exactly one point of every diagonal \\(D_{t+1},D_{t+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\). Hence **every diagonal \\(D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\) already has at least one of its points covered by a sunny line** (the diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) with \\(s\\le k+1\\) is hit by the line \\(L_{s-1}\\)).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   The diagonals that may still be missing points are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Add exactly these \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonals to the family.  \n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-k)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(S\\) belongs either  \n\n   * to a diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\); such a diagonal is intersected by the sunny line \\(L_{s-1}\\), or  \n   * to a diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) with \\(k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\); such a diagonal is itself one of the added non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   Therefore the \\(n\\) lines constructed above cover all points of \\(S\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Any admissible family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which is either sunny or non\u2011sunny. Hence the number of sunny lines must be an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\). The construction of \u00a74 shows that **every integer in this interval is attainable**, so no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\nThus the set of all possible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n\\(|S|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\); the points form the integer lattice inside the right\u2011angled triangle bounded by the lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A family with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) are distinct and every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\in D_{a+b}\\).  Thus these \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover \\(S\\).  Therefore \\(k=0\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines that replace blocks of diagonals\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) and consider  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;y = t\\,x-(t-1). \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_t\\) equals \\(t\\neq 0,-1\\); therefore \\(L_t\\) is **sunny**.\n\nLet \\(a\\) be an integer satisfying \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (2.1) yields  \n\n\\[\nb = t a-(t-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le n+1-t\\),\n\n\\[\nb\\ge t-(t-1)=1,\\qquad\nb\\le t(n+1-t)-(t-1)=n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\).  Moreover  \n\n\\[\na+b = a + t a-(t-1) = (t+1)a-(t-1).\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(a\\) runs through the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-t\\), the quantity \\((t+1)a-(t-1)\\) runs through the consecutive integers  \n\n\\[\nt+1,\\;t+2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus for each \\(s\\) with \\(t+1\\le s\\le n+1\\) there is exactly one integer \\(a\\) (namely \\(a=\\frac{s+t-1}{t+1}\\)) such that \\((a,\\,t a-(t-1))\\) lies on \\(D_s\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nL_t\\cap S = \\bigcup_{s=t+1}^{\\,n+1}\\bigl(D_s\\cap S\\bigr). \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nIn words, the single sunny line \\(L_t\\) contains **exactly one** point from each diagonal \\(D_{t+1},D_{t+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\), and together these points cover all those diagonals.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1,\\;L_2,\\;\\dots ,\\;L_k\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\ndefined by (2.1).  \nFor each \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), equation (2.2) shows that \\(L_t\\) supplies one point on every diagonal \\(D_{t+1},D_{t+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\). In particular, for every diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\) the line \\(L_{s-1}\\) contributes a point of that diagonal.\n\n*Non\u2011sunny part.*  \nThe diagonals that may still be lacking a point are  \n\n\\[\nD_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nAdd precisely these \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines to the family.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nNow check that every point of \\(S\\) is covered:\n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\) lies on a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\), then \\(s-1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and by (2.2) the line \\(L_{s-1}\\) contains that point.\n* If a point lies on a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\), then \\(D_s\\) itself is one of the added non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nThus the \\(n\\) lines constructed above cover all points of \\(S\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustion of possibilities\n\nAny admissible family must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which is either sunny or non\u2011sunny. Hence the number of sunny lines is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\). The construction of \u00a73 shows that **every integer in this interval can be real