nt \\((1,1)\\). To cover this point without using \\(D_{2}\\) we would need a sunny line passing through \\((1,1)\\). Such a line would intersect every other diagonal \\(D_{t}\\;(t\\ge 3)\\) in at most one point, leaving the remaining \\(t-2\\) points of \\(D_{t}\\) uncovered. Since we have no further lines (all other \\(n-1\\) lines are the diagonals \\(D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\)), those points stay uncovered – a contradiction.\n\n   Hence **no** sunny line can be present in a covering family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n   The only possible number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\). The construction with the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_{2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) shows that this value is indeed attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n*Every point of \\(S\\) lies on exactly one diagonal*: indeed, if \\((a,b)\\in S\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\), and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains the lattice points  \n\n\\[\n(1,s-1),\\;(2,s-2),\\dots ,\\;(s-1,1),\n\\]\n\nhence \\(|D_s\\cap S|=s-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny lines\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, each is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **non‑sunny**.  \nSince every \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), we have \\((a,b)\\in D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal D\\) covers all points of \\(S\\). Consequently \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Properties of a sunny line\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and undefined.  \nHence a sunny line is **not** parallel to any diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line meets each diagonal in at most one point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of having a sunny line\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least one sunny line.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal D\\subseteq\\mathcal F\\) be the set of non‑sunny lines and let \\(m=|\\mathcal D|\\).  \nEvery non‑sunny line that meets \\(S\\) must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise a vertical or horizontal line would intersect \\(S\\) in at most one point, which cannot help to cover the whole set. Hence each line of \\(\\mathcal D\\) is one of the diagonals \\(D_s\\).  \n\nDenote by \\(D_{s_1},\\dots ,D_{s_{n-m}}\\) the diagonals **not** belonging to \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \nAll points of these omitted diagonals have to be covered by the sunny lines.  \n\nBecause a sunny line meets a given diagonal in at most one point, covering all points of \\(D_{s_i}\\) requires at least \\(|D_{s_i}\\cap S|=s_i-1\\) distinct sunny lines. Therefore the number of sunny lines, which equals \\(n-m\\), satisfies\n\n\\[\nn-m\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-m}(s_i-1). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe integers \\(s_i\\) are distinct elements of \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \nThe right–hand side of (3.1) is minimized when the omitted diagonals are the ones with the smallest indices, i.e. \\(s_i=i+1\\). In that case\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-m}(s_i-1)=\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-m} i =\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}2 . \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3.1) and (3.2) gives\n\n\\[\nn-m\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n-m\\ge 2\\) the right–hand side exceeds the left–hand side, a contradiction. Hence \\(n-m\\le 1\\); at most one diagonal can be omitted.\n\n*Case \\(n-m=0\\).* All diagonals are present, so there are no sunny lines – contradicting the assumption that a sunny line exists.\n\n*Case \\(n-m=1\\).* Exactly one diagonal, say \\(D_{s}\\), is omitted. From (3.1) we obtain \\(1\\ge s-1\\), i.e. \\(s\\le 2\\). The only possible omitted diagonal is \\(D_{2}\\), which consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\).\n\nIf \\(D_{2}\\) is omitted, the point \\((1,1)\\) must be covered by a sunny line \\(L\\). Since \\(L\\) is not parallel to any diagonal, it meets each diagonal \\(D_{t}\\;(t\\ge 3)\\) in at most one point, leaving at least \\((t-1)-1 = t-2\\) points of \\(D_{t}\\) uncovered. All other lines of the family are the diagonals \\(D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\), which do not contain those uncovered points. Hence many points of \\(S\\) remain uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nThus the assumption that a sunny line can appear leads to an impossibility. Consequently **every** covering family of \\(n\\) lines must consist solely of non‑sunny lines, i.e. of diagonals.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nThe only admissible number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\). The family \\(\\{D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) shows that this value is attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]"
