e remaining ones have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). One checks directly that every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one listed line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following statement \\(\\mathcal P(n)\\) holds:\n\n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set  \n> \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step: from \\(n\\) to \\(n+1\\)\n\nWe prove \\(\\mathcal P(n+1)\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction of a configuration with \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nTake the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) given by the hypothesis.  \nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}= \\{(x,y)\\mid x=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{v}\\) is not sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nNo point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(x=n+1\\), so the covering property for the old points is unchanged.  \nThe new points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that are not in \\(T_n\\) are precisely those with \\(x=n+1\\) or \\(y=n+1\\).  \nAll points with \\(x=n+1\\) are now covered by \\(L_{v}\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{L_{v}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\), and still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThus a configuration exists for every \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Raising the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we already have a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\) with \\(k\\le n\\) (the construction above provides it).  \nWe will modify it to obtain a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nObserve that in all our constructions the vertical line \\(x=1\\) belongs to the family (it appears in the base case and is never removed in the inductive step).  \nReplace this line by the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}= \\{(x,y)\\mid y = x + n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{s}\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L_{s}\\) is sunny.  \n\\(L_{s}\\) passes through the point \\((1,n+1)\\), which is a new point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) (since \\(1+(n+1)=n+2\\le (n+1)+1\\)).  \nAll other points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) remain covered because the line that was removed, \\(x=1\\), covered only points with \\(x=1\\) and \\(y\\le n\\); those points are still covered by the remaining lines of the original configuration (for instance, the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) or other lines that intersect the column \\(x=1\\)).  \n\nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k+1)=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\setminus\\{x=1\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_{s}\\}\n\\]\n\nhas \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\), and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nStarting from the configuration for \\(k=0\\) (obtained by repeatedly adding the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) to the base case), we may apply the replacement operation repeatedly, each time increasing the number of sunny lines by one, until we reach \\(k=n+1\\). Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nA family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, because each line is either sunny or not. Therefore no integer \\(k>n\\) can satisfy the requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).  \nAssuming it holds for some \\(n\\ge3\\) we have constructed families for \\(n+1\\) covering