]\n\nConsider the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence **non\u2011sunny**.  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \nConsequently the vertical lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) cover all points of \\(S\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Description of the uncovered points\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S\\) is **not** covered by the vertical lines, then \\(a\\ge m+1=n-k+1\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we also have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); substituting the lower bound for \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\text{ and }b\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the possible values of \\(a\\) are  \n\n\\[\na=n-k+1,\\;n-k+2,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nCorrespondingly \\(b\\) ranges from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\).  \nHence \\(|U|=k\\); a convenient enumeration is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(n-k+i,\\;i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Adding exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines to cover \\(U\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\; y = i\\,x + c_i ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the constant \\(c_i\\) is chosen so that \\(M_i\\) passes through the point \\((n-k+i,i)\\).  \nSolving for \\(c_i\\),\n\n\\[\ni = i\\,(n-k+i)+c_i\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nc_i = i - i\\,(n-k+i)= i\\bigl(1-(n-k+i)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\; y = i\\,x + i\\bigl(1-(n-k+i)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(M_i\\) is \\(i\\).  \nSince \\(i\\ge 1\\) and \\(i\\neq -1\\), and the slope is finite, each \\(M_i\\) is **sunny** (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nThe lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.  \nBy construction, each \\(M_i\\) contains the point \\((n-k+i,i)\\); consequently the whole set \\(U\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Verification of the whole construction\n\nWe have produced the following family of lines:\n\n* the \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* the \\(k\\) lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) (sunny).\n\nAll these lines are distinct, because the vertical lines have undefined slope while the \\(M_i\\) have finite, pairwise different slopes.  \nEvery point of \\(S\\) belongs either to some \\(L_i\\) (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(\\le m\\)) or to some \\(M_i\\) (if it belongs to \\(U\\)).  \nHence the two required conditions of the problem are satisfied, and the total number of sunny lines equals \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Exhaustion of all possible \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction works for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConversely, the problem asks for all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists.  \nSince we have exhibited a configuration for every \\(k\\) in that interval, the set of admissible values is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]"
