"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous construction for the case \\(k=n\\).  \nHowever, I have proved that for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1\n\\]\n\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is at least \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThe case \\(k=n\\) remains unresolved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Cover most points with non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   - For a given \\(k\\) (with \\(k\\le n-1\\)) let \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   - Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\); they are non\u2011sunny and cover all points \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with \\(a\\le m\\).\n\n2. **Identify the uncovered points.**  \n   - The points not covered by the vertical lines form the set  \n     \\[\n     U=\\{(n-k+i,i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\},\n     \\]\n     which contains exactly \\(k\\) points.\n\n3. **Cover the uncovered points with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   - For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n     \\[\n     M_i:\\; y = i\\,x + i\\bigl(1-(n-k+i)\\bigr),\n     \\]\n     which passes through \\((n-k+i,i)\\) and has slope \\(i\\).  \n   - Since the slope \\(i\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), each \\(M_i\\) is sunny.  \n   - The slopes are distinct, so the \\(M_i\\) are distinct lines.\n\n4. **Verification.**  \n   - The family \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\}\\cup\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\}\\) consists of \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and together they cover every point of  \n     \\[\n     S=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n5. **Remaining case.**  \n   - When \\(k=n\\) the above construction would require \\(m=0\\) non\u2011sunny lines, leaving the whole set \\(S\\) to be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n   - I have not succeeded in finding a universal construction that works for every \\(n\\) in this extreme case; consequently the existence of a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines remains open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad (1\\le m\\le n).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 1. Non\u2011sunny vertical lines\n\nConsider the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nThus the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) cover all points of \\(S\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Uncovered points\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) not covered by the vertical lines must satisfy \\(a\\ge m+1=n-k+1\\).  \nSince also \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=n-k+i\\) with \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) gives the convenient description  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(n-k+i,\\;i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(|U|=k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Sunny lines covering \\(U\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\; y = i\\,x + c_i,\n\\]\n\nwhere the constant \\(c_i\\) is chosen so that \\((n-k+i,i)\\) lies on the line:\n\n\\[\ni = i\\,(n-k+i)+c_i\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nc_i = i - i\\,(n-k+i)= i\\bigl(1-(n-k+i)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\; y = i\\,x + i\\bigl(1-(n-k+i)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(M_i\\) is \\(i\\).  \nBecause \\(i\\ge 1\\), the slope is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), nor \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)); consequently each \\(M_i\\) is a **sunny** line.  \nThe slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) are distinct.\n\nEach \\(M_i\\) contains the point \\((n-k+i,i)\\); therefore the whole set \\(U\\) is covered by the sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Completion of the construction\n\nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}= \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\cup\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines.  \nAmong them, precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) are sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(S\\) belongs either to some \\(L_i\\) (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(\\le m\\)) or to some \\(M_i\\) (if it lies in \\(U\\)).  \nHence the two requirements of the problem are satisfied.\n\n---\n\n### 5. The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=n\\) we would have \\(m=0\\); the above scheme would need to cover the whole set \\(S\\) with \\(n\\) sunny lines and no non\u2011sunny lines.  \nI have not succeeded in producing a uniform construction that works for every \\(n\\) in this extreme case; therefore the existence of a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines remains an open sub\u2011problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1,\n\\]\n\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.  \n\nThe existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines has not been established here."
