tion (2) is false, and we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Realisation for all \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers \\(S\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base configuration (no sunny lines)\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\;x+y=t\\qquad (t=2,3,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_{t}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non‑sunny.\nFor a fixed \\(t\\) the integer solutions of \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(x,y\\ge1\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,t-1),(2,t-2),\\dots ,(t-1,1),\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(t-1\\) points, all belonging to \\(S\\).  \nCollecting all \\(t\\) from \\(2\\) to \\(n\\) we obtain precisely the set \\(S\\); indeed\n\n\\[\nS=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{n}(L_{t}\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2})\\;\\cup\\;(x+y=n+1).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (also non‑sunny) supplies the remaining \\(n\\) points with\n\\(a+b=n+1\\). Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering \\(S\\).\nThus the case \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Introducing sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) replace the diagonal line \\(L_{i+1}\\) (i.e. the line\n\\(x+y=i+1\\)) by a sunny line \\(\\Sigma_{i}\\) defined as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\Sigma_{i}:\\; y=\\frac{1}{i}\\,x+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{1}{i}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore\n\\(\\Sigma_{i}\\) is sunny. Moreover \\(\\Sigma_{i}\\) passes through the lattice point\n\\((i,1)\\in S\\) and, by Lemma 1, contains no other point of \\(S\\).\n\nAll other diagonal lines \\(L_{t}\\) with \\(t\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) are left\nunchanged. Consequently every point of \\(S\\) is still covered:\n\n* the points on the retained diagonals are covered exactly as before;\n* the point \\((i,1)\\) (which formerly lay on \\(L_{i+1}\\)) is now covered by\n  \\(\\Sigma_{i}\\);\n* the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are covered by the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThe family now consists of\n\n* the \\(n-1-k\\) unchanged diagonal lines,\n* the line \\(x+y=n+1\\),\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\Sigma_{1},\\dots ,\\Sigma_{k}\\),\n\nin total \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k\\ge n-1\\) (inequality (4)) with the explicit\nconstructions for all \\(k\\le n-2\\), we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n∎"
