text{ (sunny)}\\;+\\;(n-k)\\text{ (new horizontal/diagonal)}=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we obtain a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) **except** the case \\(k=n-1\\), because then \\(n-k=1\\) and we would need to add **one** horizontal (or diagonal) line while we have already removed \\(n-1\\) vertical lines, leaving only a single vertical line as the non‑sunny line. The counting above shows that for \\(k=n-1\\) we would need **two** distinct non‑sunny lines (one horizontal and the diagonal) to cover the lost points, which would exceed the allowed total of \\(n\\) lines. Hence the method fails for \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n\n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   Then there is only **one** non‑sunny line, call it \\(\\ell\\).  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, vertical or diagonal, it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the points lying on a single row, column, or on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \n\n   The remaining points, at least  \n\n   \\[\n     |S_n|-n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\ge 3\n   \\]\n\n   must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   **Incidence bound for a sunny line.**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  \n   - If \\(L\\) contains two points of \\(S_n\\) with the same abscissa, then its slope would be \\(0\\) (horizontal), contradicting sunniness; hence a sunny line contains **at most one** point from each vertical line \\(x=t\\).  \n   - Similarly, a sunny line contains at most one point from each horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   - Finally, a sunny line contains at most one point from each diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   The set \\(S_n\\) can be partitioned into the three families of parallel lines:\n   \\[\n     \\mathcal V=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\},\\qquad\n     \\mathcal H=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\},\\qquad\n     \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   Each point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to exactly one line of each family.  \n   Consequently a sunny line can meet at most **three** points of \\(S_n\\) (one from each family).  \n   Moreover, if a sunny line meets three points, those three points would be collinear with slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a triple indeed exists (e.g. \\((1,1),(2,3),(3,5)\\) for slope \\(2\\)). Hence the maximal possible number of points of \\(S_n\\) that a single sunny line can cover is **three**.\n\n   Therefore the total number of points that the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover is at most \\(3(n-1)\\).  \n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n   \\[\n     3(n-1)<\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad 6< n,\n   \\]\n\n   and the inequality also holds for \\(n=3,4,5\\) by direct verification:\n   \\[\n     \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n       n & 3(n-1) & \\dfrac{n(n-1)}2\\\\ \\hline\n       3 & 6 & 3\\\\\n       4 & 9 & 6\\\\\n       5 & 12 & 10\n     \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   In every case \\(3(n-1)\\) is strictly smaller than the number of points that must be covered by the sunny lines, a contradiction.  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) except \\(k=n-1\\) can be realized, and \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}} .\n\\]"
