ical, etc.\n\n#### 5.4. Conclusion \u201ca sunny line can meet at most three points of \\(S_n\\)\u201d\n> \u201cConsequently a sunny line can meet at most **three** points of \\(S_n\\) (one from each family).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** This follows from the previous paragraph: a line can intersect at most one member of each of the three families, so at most three points overall. The argument is sound, though the author could have explicitly stated that the three families are pairwise intersecting only at a single point, but the reasoning is acceptable. No gap here.\n\n#### 5.5. Upper bound on total points covered by sunny lines\n> \u201cTherefore the total number of points that the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover is at most \\(3(n-1)\\).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Correct.\n\n#### 5.6. Comparison with required number of points\n> \u201cFor \\(n\\ge 3\\), \\(3(n-1) < \\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) \u2026\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** The inequality holds for all \\(n\\ge 7\\); the author checks \\(n=3,4,5\\) directly, but omits \\(n=6\\). For \\(n=6\\):\n\\[\n3(5)=15,\\quad \\frac{6\\cdot5}{2}=15,\n\\]\nso the bound is **not** strict; the argument would give equality, not a contradiction. The author\u2019s statement \u201c\\(3(n-1)<\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\)\u201d is false for \\(n=6\\). This is a **Justification Gap** (the case \\(n=6\\) needs separate handling). However, even if equality occurs, one would need to show that the configuration cannot achieve the exact bound, which is not done.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Conclusion of the solution\n> \u201cAll integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) except \\(k=n-1\\) can be realized \u2026\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Because of the **Critical Error** concerning \\(k=n\\) (and the unproved counting for many intermediate \\(k\\)), this conclusion is false. The impossibility proof for \\(k=n-1\\) also contains a gap for \\(n=6\\). Hence the final statement is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Evaluation\nThe solution presents an interesting construction idea b