x-j(m+1)=j\\bigl(x-m-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_j\\) equals \\(j\\). Because \\(j\\ge 1\\), the slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) and from \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_j\\) is **sunny**.  \nThe slopes are distinct, so the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Every point of \\(U_m\\) lies on one of the \\(L_j\\)\n\nFix \\((a,b)\\in U_m\\). By (5) we have \\(1\\le b\\le k\\); set \\(j=b\\).  \nFrom (6) the equation of \\(L_j\\) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\nb=j a-j(m+1)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\na=m+1+\\frac{b}{j}=m+1+1=m+2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the points of \\(U_m\\) that satisfy the equation of \\(L_j\\) are exactly those with  \n\n\\[\na=m+1+j\\ell,\\qquad b=j,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is a non‑negative integer.  \nBecause of the upper bound \\(a\\le n+1-b=n+1-j\\) coming from (5), the admissible values of \\(\\ell\\) are  \n\n\\[\n0\\le \\ell\\le k-j .\n\\]\n\nHence for each \\(j\\) the line \\(L_j\\) contains the whole set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(m+1+j\\ell,\\;j)\\mid \\ell=0,1,\\dots ,k-j\\},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the collection of points of \\(U_m\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(j\\).  \nSince \\(j\\) runs through all integers \\(1,\\dots ,k\\), the union of the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) covers the entire set \\(U_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. The whole family of lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_k:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \\(|\\mathcal{F}_k|=m+k=n\\).  \n*Sunny lines.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny.  \n*Coverage.*  Every point of \\(S_n\\) either has first coordinate \\(\\le m\\) (hence lies on a vertical line) or belongs to \\(U_m\\) (hence lies on one of the \\(L_j\\) by Step 4). Therefore every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}_k\\).\n