le b\\le n\\)). Substituting \\(x=2\\) and \\(j=b\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = b\\cdot2-b = b ,\n\\]\n\nso \\((2,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\neq2\\).*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nj = n+2-a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), we have \\(1\\le j\\le n\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) and this value of \\(j\\) into (1) yields  \n\n\\[\ny = (n+2-a)a-(n+2-a)= (n+2-a)(a-1)=n+2-a .\n\\]\n\nBut from the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b = n+2-a\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_j\\) and therefore belongs to \\(L_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\). The family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, so \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) exists\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there is a configuration \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(0<k<n\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) contains at least one non‑sunny line.\n\nA non‑sunny line is either\n\n* vertical (\\(x=c\\)),\n* horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), or\n* of slope \\(-1\\) (equation \\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nConsider the **leftmost column** of points of \\(S_n\\),\n\n\\[\nC:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach point of \\(C\\) must be covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\).\n\n*If a point of \\(C\\) is covered by a sunny line*, then that sunny line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) at exactly one point, because two distinct points of \\(C\\) have different \\(y\\)-coordinates while a line of fixed slope can intersect \\(x=1\\) at only one \\(y\\)-value. Consequently a single sunny line can cover **at most one** point of \\(C\\).\n\n*If a point of \\(C\\) is covered by a non‑sunny line*, the only non‑sunny line that can contain a point with \\(x=1\\) is the vertical line \\(x=1\\) itself (a horizontal line has no point with \\(x=1\\), and a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets \\(x=1\\) at a single point). Hence **all** points of \\(C\\) can be covered either by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or by distinct sunny lines, each covering a single point.\n\nSince \\(|C|=n\\) and the configuration contains only \\(n\\) lines in total, the only way to cover the whole column is to use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) **and** \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering one of the remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C\\). This forces the configuration to contain **exactly one** non‑sunny line.\n\nNow look at the **bottom row** of \\(S_n\\),\n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA symmetric argument shows that to cover all points of \\(R\\) we would need either the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) or \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering a single point of \\(R\\). Because the configuration already uses the vertical line \\(x=1\\) as its unique non‑sunny line, it cannot also contain the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). Hence we would need \\(n-1\\) additional sunny lines to cover \\(R\\), but the total number of lines is only \\(n\\). This is impossible.\n\nTherefore a configuration containing both a sunny line and a non‑sunny line cannot exist when the total number of lines is \\(n\\). Consequently the only feasible values of \\(k\\) are the two extreme cases:\n\n* all lines non‑sunny (\\(k=0\\));\n* all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gap