ing \\(x=1\\) into (2) gives \\(y=t\\cdot1-(t-1)=1\\). Hence the point \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\) (choose \\(t=b\\)).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\ge 2\\).*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nt = n+2-a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), we have \\(1\\le t\\le n\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) and this value of \\(t\\) into (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (n+2-a)a-\\bigl((n+2-a)-1\\bigr)  \\\\\n  &= (n+2-a)a-(n+1-a)                \\\\\n  &= (n+2-a)(a-1)+1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSince \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b = n+2-a\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\ny = (n+2-a)(a-1)+1 = b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\) and belongs to \\(L_t\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\). The family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, so \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) exists\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(0<k<n\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) contains at least one non‑sunny line.\n\nA non‑sunny line is either\n\n* vertical (\\(x=c\\));\n* horizontal (\\(y=c\\));\n* of slope \\(-1\\) (equation \\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nConsider the leftmost column of points of \\(S_n\\),\n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, because two distinct points of \\(C\\) have different \\(y\\)-coordinates while a line of fixed slope can intersect \\(x=1\\) at only one \\(y\\)-value. Hence a sunny line can cover **at most one** point of \\(C\\).\n\nIf a point of \\(C\\) is covered by a non‑sunny line, the only possibility is the vertical line \\(x=1\\) itself (a horizontal line never meets \\(x=1\\), and a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets it at a single point). Consequently, to cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(C\\) we need either\n\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\) together with \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines, or\n* \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines.\n\nBecause the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\), the first alternative forces **exactly one** non‑sunny line, namely \\(x=1\\).\n\nNow look at the bottom row of \\(S_n\\),\n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA symmetric argument shows that to cover all points of \\(R\\) we would need either the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) together with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, or \\(n\\) distinct horizontal lines. Since our configuration already uses the vertical line \\(x=1\\) as its unique non‑sunny line, it cannot also contain the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). Therefore we would need \\(n-1\\) additional sunny lines to cover \\(R\\), which together with the already existing vertical line would give at least \\(n\\) lines **plus** the vertical line, i.e. more than \\(n\\) lines – a contradiction.\n\nThus a configuration that contains both a sunny line and a non‑sunny line cannot exist when the total number of lines is \\(n\\). Hence any admissible configuration must consist entirely of sunny lines (\\(k=n\\)) or entirely of non‑sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
