            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains no sunny line. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations with \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = t\\,x-(t-1).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_t\\) equals \\(t\\); because \\(t\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(L_t\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nWe prove that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a=1\\).*  \nThen \\(b\\le n\\). Substituting \\(x=1\\) into (2.1) gives \\(y=t-(t-1)=1\\) when \\(t=1\\) and, more generally, \\(y=t-(t-1)=1\\). To obtain the required ordinate \\(b\\) we simply choose \\(t=b\\); indeed for \\(t=b\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ny = b\\cdot1-(b-1)=b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\ge 2\\).*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nt_0 = n+2-a\\qquad (1\\le t_0\\le n).\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x=a\\) and \\(t=t_0\\) into (2.1):\n\n\\[\ny = t_0 a-(t_0-1)\n  = (n+2-a)a-(n+2-a-1)\n  = (n+2-a)(a-1)+1 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = (n+2-a)-1 .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\delta = (n+2-a)(a-1)+1-b .\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.2) and (2.3) we obtain \\(0\\le\\delta\\le a-2\\).  \n\nIf \\(\\delta=0\\) then (2.2) gives \\(y=b\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{t_0}\\).\n\nIf \\(\\delta>0\\) set \\(t = t_0-\\delta\\) (note that \\(t\\ge 1\\) because \\(\\delta\\le a-2\\) and \\(t_0\\ge 1\\)).  \nNow\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt a-(t-1)\n   &= (t_0-\\delta)a-(t_0-\\delta-1) \\\\\n   &= \\bigl(t_0 a-(t_0-1)\\bigr)-\\delta(a-1) \\\\\n   &= \\bigl((n+2-a)(a-1)+1\\bigr)-\\delta(a-1) \\\\\n   &= b .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\) with \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, so \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No mixed configurations (\\(0<k<n\\))\n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains both a sunny line and a non‑sunny line.  \nA non‑sunny line is either\n\n* vertical (\\(x=c\\));\n* horizontal (\\(y=c\\));\n* of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n#### 3.1 Column argument\nConsider the leftmost column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset S_n .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, so at most one point of \\(C\\) can be covered by a sunny line. All remaining points of \\(C\\) must be covered by vertical lines. Since the points of \\(C\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, each requires a distinct vertical line; therefore at least \\(n\\) vertical lines are needed. Adding the sunny line would give more than \\(n\\) lines, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n#### 3.2 Row argument\nThe same reasoning applied to the bottom row  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nshows that a configuration containing a sunny line cannot contain any horizontal line either.\n\n#### 3.3 Slope \\(-1\\) lines\nA line of slope \\(-1\\) meets the column \\(C\\) in exactly one point and the row \\(R\\) in exactly one point. If such a line were present together with a sunny line, the column argument would still require \\(n-1\\) vertical lines to cover the remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C\\); together with the slope \\(-1\\) line and the sunny line we would already have at least \\(n+1\\) lines, again impossible.\n\nHence a configuration that uses at least one sunny line cannot contain any non‑sunny line; it must consist entirely of sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n\\). By symmetry, a configuration that uses at least one non‑sunny line must consist entirely of non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nThe two explicit families constructed in Sections 1 and 2 realise the two admissible values of \\(k\\), and Section 3 proves that no other value of \\(k\\) is possible. Therefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,\\;n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\