guration does **not** exist.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The vertical\u2011line construction shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \n   Lemma\u202f2 together with the impossibility proved in step\u202f4 shows that no configuration can contain a sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\neq n\\) and, more generally, \\(k\\) cannot be positive.  \n\n   Therefore the only possible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains no sunny line.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Preliminary lemmas\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line meets the column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nin at most one point, and it meets each horizontal row  \n\n\\[\nR_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\n\\]\n\nin at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  A sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal, so it can intersect a given vertical line (in particular \\(x=1\\)) in at most one point, and similarly it can intersect a given horizontal line in at most one point. \u220e\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains a sunny line, then every line in the configuration is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a sunny line \\(L\\) c