unny line.  Let  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the leftmost column of points of \\(S_n\\).  The column \\(C\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points.\n\nBecause a sunny line is not vertical, by Lemma\u202f1 it can meet the column \\(C\\) in **at most one** point.  Consequently, if \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a sunny line, at most one point of \\(C\\) can be covered by a sunny line; the remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C\\) must be covered by the other lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nEach of the remaining lines is either\n\n* vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), in which case it covers **all** points of \\(C\\) that lie on its own \\(x\\)-coordinate, or\n* non\u2011vertical, in which case, again by Lemma\u202f1, it can meet \\(C\\) in at most one point.\n\nHence, to cover the \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C\\) that are not already covered by the sunny line, we need at least \\(n-1\\) additional lines **unless** one of those lines is vertical and passes through the whole column \\(C\\).  But a vertical line has equation \\(x=1\\); it would then coincide with the entire column \\(C\\) and would already cover **all** \\(n\\) points of \\(C\\).  If such a line were present, the sunny line would be unnecessary for covering \\(C\\), and we could discard it, reducing the total number of lines to at most \\(n-1\\), contradicting the requirement that exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are used.\n\nTherefore the only way to cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(C\\) with \\(n\\) lines while one of them is sunny is to have **exactly** one sunny line and **exactly** \\(n-1\\) non\u2011vertical, non\u2011sunny lines, each covering a distinct single point of \\(C\\).  Consequently each of those \\(n-1\\) lines must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal), because a non\u2011vertical, non\u2011sunny line that is not horizontal would have slope \\(-1\\) and would intersect the column \\(C\\) in at most one point, but would also intersect the bottom row (see below) in at most o