\\neq b\\) and \\(a+b\\neq n+1\\).  \n  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).  \n  If \\(a\\ge 2\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is one of the \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\ge 2\\)).  \n  If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and \\(b\\neq 1\\) (otherwise the point would be on \\(L_1\\)).  \n  Since \\(a+b\\neq n+1\\), we have \\(b\\neq n\\); consequently \\((1,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is \\(L_1\\) only when \\(b=1\\).  \n  In all remaining cases the point is already covered by one of the lines listed above.\n\nA straightforward case‑by‑case check shows that every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is indeed covered, and exactly one sunny line (\\(L_1\\)) appears. Hence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma 1 (Intersection bound)\n\nA sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal; therefore it meets any fixed vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point, and likewise meets any fixed horizontal line \\(y=d\\) in at most one point. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma 2 (At most one sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least two sunny lines; denote their number by \\(s\\) (\\(s\\ge 2\\)).\n\nDefine the leftmost column and the bottom row:\n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth sets contain exactly \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Coverage of \\(C\\).*  \nEach sunny line meets \\(C\\) in at most one point (Lemma 1).  \nA non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) also meets \\(C\\) in at most one point.  \nThe only non‑sunny line that can meet **more than one** point of \\(C\\) is the vertical line \\(x=1\\); it meets all \\(n\\) points of \\(C\\).\n\nConsequently, after the \\(s\\) sunny lines have covered at most \\(s\\) points of \\(C\\), the remaining \\(n-s\\) points of \\(C\\) must be covered by distinct lines, each of which can contribute at most one new point of \\(C\\) unless we use the special line \\(x=1\\).  \nThus we need at least \\(n-s\\) additional lines, and we may optionally use the line \\(x=1\\) (which would replace one of those \\(n-s\\) lines). Hence the number of lines required to cover \\(C\\) is at least\n\n\\[\ns + (n-s) - \\varepsilon_C,\n\\qquad\\varepsilon_C\\in\\{0,1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon_C=1\\) precisely when the line \\(x=1\\) is present.\n\n*Coverage of \\(R\\).*  \nA symmetric argument shows that, apart from the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), every line meets \\(R\\) in at most one point.  \nTherefore covering the \\(n\\) points of \\(R\\) also demands at least\n\n\\[\ns + (n-s) - \\varepsilon_R,\n\\qquad\\varepsilon_R\\in\\{0,1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon_R=1\\) iff the line \\(y=1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Putting the two requirements together.*  \nThe total number of lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at least\n\n\\[\n\\max\\bigl\\{\\,s+(n-s)-\\varepsilon_C,\\; s+(n-s)-\\varepsilon_R\\,\\bigr\\}\n= n - \\min\\{\\varepsilon_C,\\varepsilon_R\\}+s .\n\\]\n\nSince at most one of the two special lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) can be used (they are distinct), we have \\(\\varepsilon_C+\\varepsilon_R\\le 1\\); consequently \\(\\min\\{\\varepsilon_C,\\varepsilon_R\\}=0\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|\\;\\ge\\; n + s \\;\\ge\\; n+2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\) by hypothesis, a contradiction.  \nHence a covering of size \\(n\\) cannot contain two or more sunny lines; i.e. **any admissible configuration has \\(k\\le 1\\).** ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final determination of admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Lemma 2 we have the universal bound \\(k\\le 1\\).  \nThe explicit families constructed in Section 1 show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,\\;1\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
