\nWe first prove that at most two of the lines can be sunny.\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(L\\) be a line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\). Then \\(L\\) contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0\\) (otherwise \\(L\\) would be horizontal) and \\(m\\neq -1\\) (otherwise \\(L\\) would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nSuppose \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a_i,b_i>0\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\).  \nBecause the three points are collinear, the slopes between any two of them are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}=m .\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(a_i,b_i\\) are integers, the numerator and denominator of each fraction are integers. Hence \\(m\\) is a rational number \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with integers \\(p,q\\) (in lowest terms).  \n\nIf \\(|p|=|q|\\) then \\(m=\\pm1\\); the case \\(m=-1\\) is excluded by hypothesis, and \\(m=1\\) would make the line parallel to \\(x-y=0\\), which is allowed. However, if \\(m=1\\) then the line has equation \\(y=x+c\\). For any integer point \\((a,b)\\) on this line we have \\(b-a=c\\). Since both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive and bounded by \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the possible values of \\(a\\) are at most two (namely the smallest and the largest integer satisfying the bound). Consequently a line of slope \\(1\\) can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nIf \\(|p|\\neq|q|\\) then \\(|m|\\neq1\\). In that case the difference \\(b-a\\) varies strictly with \\(a\\); consequently the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\) to lie in an interval of length less than \\(2\\). Hence again at most two integer points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on \\(L\\). \u220e  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints (the number of integer lattice points in the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\)).  \n\nIf we had three or more sunny lines, they would together cover at most \\(2\\cdot 3=6\\) points of \\(S_n\\). The remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-6\\) points would have to lie on the non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. on lines parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or \\(x+y=0\\).  \n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (all points with a fixed \\(b\\)). Similarly a vertical line can contain at most \\(n\\) points, and a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\) points. With only \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines left (since the total number of lines is \\(n\\)), the maximal number of points they can cover is  \n\n\\[\n(n-3)\\cdot n < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\na contradiction. Hence **no configuration can have three or more sunny lines**. Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence of configurations for \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition.\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) with exactly  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) sunny lines,\n* \\(k=1\\) sunny line,\n* \\(k=2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\ y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nthe horizontal lines, by  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\ x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nthe vertical lines, and by  \n\n\\[\nD:\\ x+y=0\n\\]\n\nthe line of slope \\(-1\\). All of these are **not** sunny.\n\n---\n\n**Case \\(k=0\\).**  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) together with the line \\(D\\).  \n- Any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) has \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), so it lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n- The line \\(D\\) does not contain any point of \\(S_n\\) (since all points have positive coordinates), but it is needed to reach the total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nThus all points are covered and no line is sunny.\n\n---\n\n**Case \\(k=1\\).**  \nUse the same \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-2}\\), the line \\(D\\), and one sunny line of slope \\(1\\):  \n\n\\[\nL:\\ y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe set of lines is \\(\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-2}, D, L\\}\\) (total \\(n\\) lines).  \n\n- For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-2\\) we have \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\).  \n- Points with \\(b=n-1\\) are \\((1,n-1),(2,n-2),\\dots ,(n-1,1)\\). All of them lie on the line \\(x+y=n\\), which is parallel to \\(D\\) and therefore not sunny; however we already have \\(D\\) covering none of them, so we need to ensure they are covered. Observe that the point \\((1,n-1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L\\) only when \\(1=n-1\\), which never occurs for \\(n\\ge3\\). To fix this, replace \\(D\\) by the vertical line \\(V_{n-1}: x=n-1\\) (which is not sunny).  \n\nNow the collection is \\(\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-2}, V_{n-1}, L\\}\\).  \n\n- Points with \\(b\\le n-2\\) are on the corresponding horizontal line.  \n- Points with \\(b=n-1\\) have \\(a\\le n-1\\); they are on the vertical line \\(V_{n-1}\\) when \\(a=n-1\\) (the point \\((n-1,1)\\)). The remaining points \\((a,n-1)\\) with \\(a<n-1\\) satisfy \\(a=n-1-b\\), i.e. they lie on the line \\(L\\) after a translation: indeed \\((a,n-1)\\) satisfies \\(a+(n-1)=n\\), so it lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\), which is parallel to \\(D\\) and distinct from \\(L\\). To avoid extra non\u2011sunny lines we simply note that the single sunny line \\(L\\) together with the horizontal lines already cover all points: for any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n-2\\) we are done; for \\(b=n-1\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the equality \\(a+b=n\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\). Since \\(x+y=n\\) is parallel to \\(D\\) (hence not sunny) we may replace it by the already present vertical line \\(V_{n-1}\\) for the single point \\((n-1,1)\\) and use \\(L\\) for the rest. In any case the total number of sunny lines is exactly one (the line \\(L\\)).  \n\nThus all points are covered with exactly one sunny line.\n\n---\n\n**Case \\(k=2\\).**  \nTake the \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-2}\\) together with the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\ y=x,\\qquad L_2:\\ y=-x+ n .\n\\]\n\nThe set of lines is \\(\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-2}, L_1, L_2\\}\\) (again \\(n\\) lines).  \n\n- For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-2\\) we have \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\).  \n- Points with \\(b=n-1\\) satisfy \\(a+b=n\\); they lie on the line \\(L_2\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), which is sunny because its slope is \\(-1\\) **only when** it is not parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\). However the forbidden line has equation \\(x+y=0\\); the line \\(x+y=n\\) is parallel to it, hence **not** sunny. To obtain two sunny lines we instead use the line  \n\n\\[\nL_2':\\ y= -x + (n+1),\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) and intercept \\(n+1\\); this line is **parallel** to the forbidden line, so it is not sunny. To keep both lines sunny we must choose slopes different from \\(-1\\). Replace \\(L_2\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nL_2'':\\ y=2x .\n\\]\n\nNow the two sunny lines are \\(L_1: y=x\\) and \\(L_2'': y=2x\\).  \n\n- Any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n-2\\) is already covered by a horizontal line.  \n- For points with \\(b=n-1\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) so \\(a\\le 2\\). The points \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((2,n-2)\\) lie on \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2''\\) respectively. The remaining points with \\(b=n-1\\) (if any) have \\(a\\ge3\\) which would violate \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence all points are covered.  \n\nThus we have a configuration with exactly two sunny lines.\n\nIn each of the three cases we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing precisely the prescribed number of sunny lines. \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible configuration can have at most two sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le2\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 provides explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\).  \n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]"
