\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nOur goal is to prove:\n\n> **Claim.** For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there is **no** collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of them sunny, that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe claim immediately implies that in any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-1\\); equivalently, \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nThe proof is by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Border Lemma)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\) be a set of distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\). Then there exists a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) that lies on exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the set of points on the “border’’  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can intersect \\(B_n\\) in at most one point, because two distinct points of \\(B_n\\) have different sums of coordinates and therefore are not collinear with a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); a line of any other slope can meet the line \\(a+b=n+1\\) in at most one point.  \n\nSince \\(|B_n|=n\\) and the lines are distinct, the total number of incidences between \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(B_n\\) is at most \\(|\\mathcal L|\\). If every point of \\(B_n\\) lay on at least two lines, the number of incidences would be at least \\(2|B_n|=2n\\), contradicting \\(|\\mathcal L|\\le n\\) (the case of interest is \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\)). Hence some point of \\(B_n\\) is incident to exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny Necessity)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in B_n\\) be a point that belongs to exactly one line \\(L\\) of a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\). Then \\(L\\) cannot be sunny.\n\n*Proof.