t inspection shows that \\(|U|=k\\): indeed the points with\n\\(a=m+1\\) and \\(b=2,\\dots ,n-m\\) are uncovered, and there are exactly\n\\(n-(m+1)+1=n-m-1=k\\) of them.  \n\nFor each \\((a,b)\\in U\\) choose a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)\nthat passes through \\((a,b)\\) and does not coincide with any previously chosen\nline (this is always possible because through a given point there are infinitely\nmany sunny lines).  The \\(k\\) lines obtained in this way are sunny and are\npairwise distinct.\n\nConsequently we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  Hence every\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is **realizable**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non‑existence for \\(k\\ge n-1\\)\n\nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing\n\\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nConsequently at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) belongs to\n\\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\); denote this (possible) line by\n\\(\\ell_0\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Then \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most one** point of\n\\(T_n\\) that is not already on \\(\\ell_0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) were to contain two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\)\nwith \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) and \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\), the slope of \\(\\ell\\) would be\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause both points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), the numerator and denominator\nare integers of absolute value at most \\(n\\).  The only way for the slope to be\n\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (the three forbidden directions) is when\n\\(b_2=b_1\\), \\(a_2=a_1\\) or \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\), respectively.  Each of these\npossibilities would make \\(\\ell\\) parallel to a line of \\(\\mathcal V\\