1=2m+1\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le m\\) (hence lies on \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b\\le m\\) (hence lies on \\(y=b\\)).  \n   Thus for even \\(n\\) we obtain a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines, which respects the lower bound because \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil=m-1\\) and we can replace up to \\(m-1\\) of the non‑sunny lines by sunny ones (see step 5).  \n\n   *Case 2: \\(n\\) odd, \\(n=2m+1\\).*  \n   - Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,m\\) and the \\(m\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,m\\).  \n   - Add the diagonal line \\(x+y=m+2\\) (which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), hence non‑sunny).  \n   The total number of lines is \\(2m+1=n\\).  \n   Any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+2\\) either has \\(a\\le m\\) (on a vertical line), or \\(b\\le m\\) (on a horizontal line), or else \\(a,b\\ge m+1\\) which forces \\(a+b\\ge 2m+2\\); the only remaining possibility is \\(a+b=m+2\\), i.e. the point lies on the added diagonal.  \n   Hence all points are covered and the configuration contains **no sunny line**.  \n\n   In both parity cases we have exhibited a covering with **zero** sunny lines, which shows that the lower bound derived in step 3 is not tight for the existence problem; the only restriction comes from the pigeonhole argument when we *force* a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n5. **Realising any admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the constructions of step 4 (which use only non‑sunny lines), we may replace arbitrarily many of the non‑sunny lines by distinct sunny lines while preserving coverage:\n\n   - Choose a non‑sunny line that is *redundant*, i.e. every point it contains is also contained in at least one other line of the family.  \n   - Remove that line and insert any sunny line that passes through at least one uncovered point; because a sunny line can intersect each column, each row and each anti‑diagonal at most once, we can always select a sunny line that covers a point not yet covered after the removal.  \n\n   By iterating this replacement we can increase the number of sunny lines one by one, up to the total of \\(n\\).  \n   The process stops precisely when we have replaced all non‑sunny lines, giving a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines (trivially possible: take \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines through the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\)).  \n\n   The only obstruction is the lower bound from step 3: we cannot have fewer than \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines, because otherwise the remaining non‑sunny lines would be insufficient to cover all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points (pigeonhole principle).  \n\n   Consequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n,\n   \\]\n\n   we can construct a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nT_n=|\\mathcal P_n| .\n\\]\n\nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum points on a line)\n\nFor any line \\(\\ell\\) (sunny or not) we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, the value of \\(y\\) is fixed; among the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the \\(x\\)-coordinate can take at most the \\(n\\) values \\(1,\\dots ,n\\), so at most one point per column lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, the same argument applies to the rows.  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), the sum \\(x+y\\) is constant; the possible sums in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\), i.e. \\(n\\) distinct values, giving at most one point per anti‑diagonal.  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, it meets each column, each row and each anti‑diagonal at most once, so again at most \\(n\\) points can belong to \\(\\ell\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Pigeonhole lower bound)\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(s\\) of which are sunny.  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n(n-s)n\\;\\ge\\;T_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe \\(n-s\\) non‑sunny lines together contain at most \\((n-s)n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by Lemma 1.  \nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must belong to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), we need \\((n-s)n\\ge T_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence of Lemma 2\n\nInsert (1) into Lemma 2:\n\n\\[\n(n-s)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nn-s\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\ns\\ge n-\\frac{n+1}{2}\n=\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is integral,\n\n\\[\ns\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction achieving any \\(k\\) with the lower bound\n\nWe now show that for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\text{with}\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### Base families with no sunny lines\n\n*Even \\(n=2m\\).*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=m .\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(2m=n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le2m+1\\); therefore either \\(a\\le m\\) (point lies on \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b\\le m\\) (point lies on \\(y=b\\)).  \nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.\n\n*Odd \\(n=2m+1\\).*  \nUse the same \\(m\\) vertical and \\(m\\) horizontal lines as above and add the line  \n\n\\[\nx+y=m+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and thus non‑sunny.  \nNow \\(2m+1=n\\) lines are obtained.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) (resp. \\(b\\le m\\)) the point is on a vertical (resp. horizontal) line.  \nIf \\(a,b\\ge m+1\\) then \\(a+b\\ge2m+2\\); the only points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with both coordinates \\(\\ge m+1\\) satisfy \\(a+b=m+2\\) and therefore lie on the added diagonal.  \nThus all points are covered.\n\nConsequently, for every \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a covering with **zero** sunny lines.\n\n#### Increasing the number of sunny lines\n\nStarting from the zero‑sunny covering described above, we replace non‑sunny lines by sunny ones while preserving coverage.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a current covering containing \\(t\\) sunny lines (\\(t< n\\)).  \nBecause the total number of lines is \\(n\\), at least one non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\) is present.  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\mathcal P_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\substack{L\\in\\mathcal L\\\\L\\neq\\ell}}L,\n\\]\n\nthe points that would become uncovered if \\(\\ell\\) were removed.  \nIf \\(U=\\varnothing\\) then \\(\\ell\\) is redundant; we may delete it and insert any sunny line through any point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (such a line exists because through any lattice point there are infinitely many sunny lines).  \nThe new family still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and now has \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nIf \\(U\\neq\\varnothing\\), pick any \\((a,b)\\in U\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is the only line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) containing \\((a,b)\\), the slope of \\(\\ell\\) must be one of the three forbidden slopes (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be sunny).  \nChoose a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) passing through \\((a,b)\\) whose slope is distinct from the slopes of all other lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (possible because there are infinitely many sunny slopes).  \nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\).  \nNow \\((a,b)\\) is covered, and no point loses its covering because \\(\\ell\\) covered no other point that is not already covered by another line (otherwise that point would belong to \\(U\\)).  \nThus we have increased the number of sunny lines by one while maintaining a covering.\n\nRepeating this operation we can raise the number of sunny lines from \\(0\\) up to any prescribed \\(k\\) as long as the lower bound (2) is respected.  \nIndeed, Lemma 2 shows that when the number of sunny lines drops below \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) the remaining non‑sunny lines cannot possibly cover all \\(T_n\\) points, so the process must stop precisely at that bound.\n\nFinally, the extreme case \\(k=n\\) is trivial: take \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, for instance the lines through the points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) with pairwise distinct slopes.\n\nHence for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\left\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\;\\bigg|\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\right\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, any integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized, and no smaller \\(k\\) is possible. ∎"
