            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and basic counting\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices  \n\\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions in positive integers. Summing gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nAny line \\(\\ell\\) in the plane contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIntersect \\(\\ell\\) with the triangle \\(T_n=\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge 1,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\).  \nThe intersection is a (possibly degenerate) line segment whose projection on the \\(x\\)-axis lies inside the interval \\([1,n]\\); hence its length is at most \\(n-1\\).  \nSince the integer points of \\(S_n\\) have integer \\(x\\)-coordinates, at most \\(n\\) distinct integer \\(x\\)-values can appear on the segment, and therefore at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\). \\(\\qquad\\blacksquare\\)\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 3 (necessary number of lines)  \nIf a collection of \\(t\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\), then  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 2 each line covers at most \\(n\\) points, so \\(tn\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \nDividing by \\(n\\) yields \\(t\\ge (n+1)/2\\); rounding up gives the claimed integer bound. \\(\\qquad\\blacksquare\\)\n\n---\n\n### 2.  From a bound on lines to a bound on sunny lines\n\nRecall that a line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\nx=\\text{const},\\qquad y=\\text{const},\\qquad x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nLines belonging to the same family in (1) are parallel and therefore intersect the triangle \\(T_n\\) in disjoint sets of lattice points. Consequently, if we use only lines from a single family, we would need at least \\(\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) of them, which already exhausts the whole budget of \\(n\\) lines. Hence, to achieve the minimal possible total number of lines (Lemma 3) we must employ lines that are not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.\n\nFormally:\n\n#### Lemma 4 (necessary number of sunny lines)  \nIn any covering of \\(S_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that \\(k<\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\).  \nThen the number of non‑sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nn-k>\\;n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus more than \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) lines belong to the three families (1).  \nSince lines from the same family are parallel, each such family can contribute at most \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor+1\\) lines before the total number of lines exceeds the lower bound of Lemma 3.  \nSumming over the three families gives a total of at most \\(3\\bigl(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor+1\\bigr)\\) non‑sunny lines, which is strictly smaller than \\(n-k\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThis contradiction shows that our assumption is false, and the inequality for \\(k\\) follows. \\(\\qquad\\blacksquare\\)\n\nTogether with Lemma 3 we have proved the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n } .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIt remains to show that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2) can actually be realised.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Constructions achieving any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe treat the cases of even and odd \\(n\\) separately, but the ideas are the same.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  A base covering with the minimal number of sunny lines  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nWe first exhibit \\(m\\) sunny lines that already cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2r+1\\) so that \\(m=r+1\\).  \nConsider the \\(r+1\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{r+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{r+1},\n\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,r .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(\\frac{i}{r+1}\\), which is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence all \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny and distinct.  \n\nFor any lattice point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le 2r+2\\).  \nDefine \\(t:=\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\) (with the convention \\(t=+\\infty\\) when \\(a=1\\)).  \nSince \\(0\\le b-1\\le r+1\\) and \\(0\\le a-1\\le r+1\\), the ratio \\(t\\) lies in the interval \\([0,\\,r+1]\\).  \nConsequently there exists an integer \\(i\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{r+1}\\le t\\le\\frac{i+1}{r+1}.\n\\]\n\nA short computation shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_i\\) or on \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (3).  \n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, write \\(n=2r\\) so that \\(m=r\\).  \nTake the \\(r\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{r}\\,x + \\frac{1}{r},\n\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,r-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument as above (with the bound \\(a+b\\le 2r+1\\)) shows that each