er \\(n\\) replacements; no separate construction is needed.\n\n6. **Conclusion** \u2013 the only restriction is \\(k\\le n\\); every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. The set of points to be covered  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe inequalities imply \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\); a simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. A covering consisting only of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nWrite \\(n=2m\\) (even) or \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).\n\n*Even case (\\(n=2m\\)).*  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\cup\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\}.\n\\]\nThese are \\(m\\) vertical and \\(m\\) horizontal lines, all non\u2011sunny; \\(|\\mathcal L_0|=2m=n\\).\n\n*Odd case (\\(n=2m+1\\)).*  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\cup\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\}\\cup\\{x+y=m+2\\}.\n\\]\nAgain we have \\(2m+1=n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\):* \\(a+b\\le2m+1\\). Either \\(a\\le m\\) (so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b\\le m\\) (so it lies on \\(y=b\\)).\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\):* \\(a+b\\le2m+2\\). If \\(a\\le m\\) or \\(b\\le m\\) we are in the previous situation.  \nIf \\(a,b\\ge m+1\\) then \\(a+b\\ge2m+2\\); the only points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with both coordinates \\(\\ge m+1\\) satisfy \\(a+b=2m+2\\), i.e. they lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=m+2\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Private points for the initial family  \n\nFor each line we exhibit a point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that no other line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains.\n\n*Vertical line \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le m\\)):*  \n\\(P_i^{\\text{v}}:=(i,m+1)\\).  \nSince \\(i+(m+1)\\le m+(m+1)=2m+1\\) (or \\(2m+2\\) in the odd case) we have \\(P_i^{\\text{v}}\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nIts \\(y\\)-coordinate exceeds \\(m\\), so it is not on any horizontal line \\(y=j\\) (\\(j\\le m\\)), and clearly not on any other vertical line.\n\n*Horizontal line \\(y=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le m\\)):*  \n\\(P_j^{\\text{h}}:=(m+1,j)\\) is defined analogously and is private to \\(y=j\\).\n\n*Diagonal line (odd case only) \\(x+y=m+2\\):*  \n\\(P^{\\text{d}}:=(m+1,m+1)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and is not on any vertical or horizontal line of the family.\n\nHence every line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) possesses a **private point**\u2014a point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) covered only by that line.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Replacement operation and invariant  \n\nWe now describe a replacement that turns a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one while preserving the covering.\n\n> **Invariant (I).**  \n> After any number of replacements, each remaining non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) has a private point \\(Q_\\ell\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) that does **not** lie on any other line of the current family.\n\nThe invariant holds for the initial family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) by Section\u202f3.\n\n#### Replacement step  \n\nAssume the current family \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies (I) and contains at least one non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\). Let \\(Q_\\ell\\) be a private point of \\(\\ell\\).\n\nWe wish to replace \\(\\ell\\) by a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) that passes through \\(Q_\\ell\\) and does not destroy (I) for the other non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Choice of slope.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal S\\) be the finite set consisting of  \n\n* the slopes of all lines already present in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (these are forbidden because we need a new line distinct from the existing ones);\n* for each other non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) with private point \\(Q_{\\ell_i}\\), the unique slope that would make a line through \\(Q_\\ell\\) also pass through \\(Q_{\\ell_i}\\) (if such a slope exists).\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) is finite, we can pick a real number \\(m\\notin\\mathcal S\\).  \nDefine \\(\\ell'\\) to be the line through \\(Q_\\ell\\) with slope \\(m\\).  \n\n*Properties of \\(\\ell'\\).*  \n\n1. **Sunny:** by construction \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) (these three slopes are among the slopes of the original non\u2011sunny lines, hence belong to \\(\\mathcal S\\)).  \n2. **Distinct from all existing lines:** its slope is not in \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n3. **Does not hit any other private point:** if \\(\\ell'\\) passed through some \\(Q_{\\ell_i}\\) (\\(i\\neq\\ell\\)), then its slope would be exactly the forbidden slope added to \\(\\mathcal S\\), contradiction.\n\nConsequently, after replacing \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\) we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal L^{\\*}\\) that still covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the point \\(Q_\\ell\\) remains covered, and all other points keep at least one covering line). Moreover, each remaining non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) retains its original private point \\(Q_{\\ell_i}\\), which is untouched by \\(\\ell'\\); thus (I) holds for \\(\\mathcal L^{\\*}\\).\n\nThus the replacement operation is **valid** and **preserves the invariant**.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nStart from the all\u2011non\u2011sunny covering \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (which satisfies (I)).  \nApply the replacement step repeatedly:\n\n* after the first replacement we have exactly one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines, still satisfying (I);\n* after the second replacement we have two sunny lines, etc.\n\nBecause (I) guarantees that as long as a non\u2011sunny line remains we can perform another replacement, we can continue until we have performed the operation \\(k\\) times, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nIn particular, after \\(n\\) replacements all original non\u2011sunny lines have been eliminated, yielding a covering with **\\(k=n\\) sunny lines**. No separate ad\u2011hoc construction is required.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Exhaustiveness  \n\nObviously the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, so \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

