variant (I).\n\n6. **Realising any \\(k\\)** \u2013 start from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (which satisfies (I)) and apply the replacement step repeatedly. After \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. The process can be performed as long as a non\u2011sunny line remains, i.e. for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion** \u2013 the only restriction is \\(k\\le n\\); every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. The set of points to be covered  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are \\(s-1\\) solutions of \\(a+b=s\\); summing gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)). All other slopes are called **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. A covering consisting only of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nWrite \\(n=2m\\) (even) or \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).\n\n*Even case (\\(n=2m\\)).*  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\,\\}.\n\\]\nThese are \\(m\\) vertical and \\(m\\) horizontal lines, all non\u2011sunny; \\(|\\mathcal L_0|=2m=n\\).\n\n*Odd case (\\(n=2m+1\\)).*  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,x+y=m+2\\,\\}.\n\\]\nAgain we have \\(2m+1=n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\):* \\(a+b\\le2m+1\\). Either \\(a\\le m\\) (so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b\\le m\\) (so it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)).\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\):* \\(a+b\\le2m+2\\). If \\(a\\le m\\) or \\(b\\le m\\) we are in the previous situation.  \nIf \\(a,b\\ge m+1\\) then necessarily \\(a+b=2m+2\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=m+2\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Private points and the \u201conly\u2011private\u2011point\u2019\u2019 property  \n\nFor each line we exhibit a point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that belongs **only** to that line, and we prove that any other point of the line is also on at least one different line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*Vertical line \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le m\\)).*  \nDefine  \n\\[\nP_i^{\\text{v}}:=(i,m+1).\n\\]\nBecause \\(i+(m+1)\\le m+(m+1)=2m+1\\) (or \\(2m+2\\) when \\(n\\) is odd) we have \\(P_i^{\\text{v}}\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nIts \\(y\\)-coordinate exceeds \\(m\\), so it is **not** on any horizontal line \\(y=j\\) (\\(j\\le m\\)). It is obviously not on any other vertical line. Hence \\(P_i^{\\text{v}}\\) is private to \\(x=i\\).\n\n*Other points on \\(x=i\\).*  \nA point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\le m\\) satisfies \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(i+b\\le i+m\\le 2m\\le n+1\\); therefore \\((i,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\). Since \\(b\\le m\\), the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\)) also contains \\((i,b)\\). Consequently **no** point of \\(x=i\\) other than \\(P_i^{\\text{v}}\\) is private.\n\n*Horizontal line \\(y=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le m\\)).*  \nDefine  \n\\[\nP_j^{\\text{h}}:=(m+1,j),\n\\]\nwhich is private to \\(y=j\\) by the same reasoning as above. Any other point \\((a,j)\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n*Diagonal line (odd case only) \\(x+y=m+2\\).*  \nThe only point of this line that belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is \\((m+1,m+1)\\); it is therefore automatically private. No other point of the diagonal lies in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nThus each line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) possesses a **unique** private point, and **every** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that is covered *exclusively* by that line is precisely its private point.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Invariant (I)  \n\n> **Invariant (I).**  \n> After any number of replacements, each remaining non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) still has a private point \\(Q_\\ell\\in\\mathcal P_n\\), and the *only* points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that were covered solely by \\(\\ell\\) are exactly \\(Q_\\ell\\).\n\nThe initial family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) satisfies (I) by the discussion in Section\u202f3.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Replacement step and preservation of the covering  \n\nAssume the current family \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies (I) and contains at least one non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\). Let \\(Q_\\ell\\) be its private point.\n\n#### 5.1 Choice of a new sunny line  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S:=\\Bigl\\{\\text{slope of each line in }\\mathcal L\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{\\text{slope of the line through }Q_\\ell\\text{ and }Q_{\\ell'}\\;:\\;\\ell'\\neq\\ell\\text{ is non\u2011sunny}\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal S\\) is a finite set of real numbers.  \nPick a real number \\(m\\notin\\mathcal S\\) and also different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (these three slopes belong to the first part of \\(\\mathcal S\\)).  \n\nDefine \\(\\ell'\\) to be the line through \\(Q_\\ell\\) with slope \\(m\\).\n\n#### 5.2 Properties of \\(\\ell'\\)\n\n1. **Sunny.** By construction its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n2. **Distinct from all existing lines.** Its slope is not in the first part of \\(\\mathcal S\\), hence \\(\\ell'\\) coincides with none of the lines already present.  \n3. **Avoids other private points.** If \\(\\ell'\\) passed through the private point \\(Q_{\\ell'}\\) of another non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell'\\neq\\ell\\), then the slope of \\(\\ell'\\) would be exactly the forbidden slope added to \\(\\mathcal S\\) in the second part, contradicting the choice of \\(m\\).\n\n#### 5.3 Replacement  \n\nForm the new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\*}:=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that were covered by lines other than \\(\\ell\\) remain covered, because those lines are unchanged.  \nThe only point that lost its unique covering when \\(\\ell\\) was removed is its private point \\(Q_\\ell\\); by construction \\(Q_\\ell\\in\\ell'\\), so it is still covered. Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{\\*}\\).\n\n*Preservation of invariant (I).*  \nFor every non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell''\\neq\\ell\\) the private point \\(Q_{\\ell''}\\) is untouched (the new line \\(\\ell'\\) does not pass through it). Consequently each such line still has a private point, and the only points that could have become exclusively covered by \\(\\ell''\\) are unchanged. Thus (I) holds for \\(\\mathcal L^{\\*}\\).\n\nTherefore the replacement step is valid, reduces the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one, and keeps both the covering property and invariant (I).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nStart with the initial family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (which satisfies (I)).  \nApply the replacement step repeatedly:\n\n* after the first replacement we have exactly one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines, still satisfying (I);\n* after the second replacement we have two sunny lines, etc.\n\nBecause invariant (I) guarantees that as long as a non\u2011sunny line remains we can perform another replacement, we can continue until we have performed the operation \\(k\\) times, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nWhen \\(k=n\\) we have replaced all original non\u2011sunny lines; the construction still works because at each step the invariant ensured the existence of a suitable sunny line.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Exhaustiveness  \n\nObviously the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, so \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
