adicting the choice of \\(m\\).\n\n#### 5.3 Replacement  \n\nForm the new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\*}:=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that were covered by lines other than \\(\\ell\\) remain covered, because those lines are unchanged.  \nThe only point that lost its unique covering when \\(\\ell\\) was removed is its private point \\(Q_\\ell\\); by construction \\(Q_\\ell\\in\\ell'\\), so it is still covered. Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{\\*}\\).\n\n*Preservation of invariant (I).*  \nFor every non‑sunny line \\(\\ell''\\neq\\ell\\) the private point \\(Q_{\\ell''}\\) is untouched (the new line \\(\\ell'\\) does not pass through it). Consequently each such line still has a private point, and the only points that could have become exclusively covered by \\(\\ell''\\) are unchanged. Thus (I) holds for \\(\\mathcal L^{\\*}\\).\n\nTherefore the replacement step is valid, reduces the number of non‑sunny lines by one, and keeps both the covering property and invariant (I).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Explicit verification for the critical case \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\)\n\nHere \\(n=3\\) (odd), so \\(m=1\\).  \nThe initial family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=3\\,\\},\n\\]\nall three lines are non‑sunny.  \nTheir private points are  \n\n\\[\nQ_{x=1}=(1,2),\\qquad Q_{y=1}=(2,1),\\qquad Q_{x+y=3}=(2,2).\n\\]\n\n**First replacement.**  \nChoose the non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\) with private point \\((1,2)\\).  \nThe set \\(\\mathcal S\\) of forbidden slopes consists of  \n\n* slopes of the three existing lines: \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), \\(-1\\) (the diagonal);\n* slopes of lines joining \\((1,2)\\) to the other private points: slope of \\((1,2)-(2,1)\\) is \\(-1\\) (already forbidden) and slope of \\((1,2)-(2,2)\\) is \\(0\\) (already forbidden).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal S=\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nPick any slope not in \\(\\mathcal S\\); for concreteness take \\(m=2\\).  \nThe line through \\((1,2)\\) with slope \\(2\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y-2=2(x-1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; y=2x.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_1\\) is sunny, distinct from the three original lines, and does not pass through the other private points. Replacing \\(x=1\\) by \\(\\ell_1\\) yields a family with exactly one sunny line.\n\n**Second replacement.**  \nNow the remaining non‑sunny lines are \\(y=1\\) (private point \\((2,1)\\)) and \\(x+y=3\\) (private point \\((2,2)\\)).  \nSelect \\(y=1\\). Its private point is \\((2,1)\\).  \nCurrent slopes are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(-1\\) (diagonal), and \\(2\\) (the sunny line).  \nSlopes of lines joining \\((2,1)\\) to the other private points are: to \\((2,2)\\) slope \\( \\infty\\); to the point on the sunny line \\((1,2)\\) (which is not a private point) we do not need to forbid anything. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{0,-1,2,\\infty\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose a slope not in \\(\\mathcal S\\); for instance \\(m=3\\).  \nThe line through \\((2,1)\\) with slope \\(3\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_2:\\; y-1=3(x-2)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; y=3x-5.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_2\\) is sunny, avoids the remaining private point \\((2,2)\\), and is distinct from all existing lines. Replacing \\(y=1\\) by \\(\\ell_2\\) we obtain the final family  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,y=3x-5,\\; y=2x,\\; x+y=3\\,\\}}.\n\\]\n\nIt consists of three distinct lines, covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\), and contains exactly two sunny lines (\\(y=2x\\) and \\(y=3x-5\\)). Hence the case \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\) is indeed realizable, confirming that the general replacement argument works even when the forbidden set of slopes is small.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nStart with the initial family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (which satisfies invariant (I)).  \nApply the replacement step repeatedly. After each replacement the number of sunny lines increases by one while invariant (I) remains true, so the process can continue as long as a non‑sunny line exists. Consequently, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can perform exactly \\(k\\) replacements and obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWhen \\(k=n\\) all original non‑sunny lines have been replaced; the construction still works because at each intermediate stage the invariant guarantees the existence of a suitable new slope.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Exhaustiveness  \n\nObviously the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, so \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎"
