nfty,-1\\}\\).  \nPick any slope not in \\(\\mathcal S\\); for concreteness take \\(m=2\\).  \nThe line through \\((1,2)\\) with slope \\(2\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y-2=2(x-1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; y=2x.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_1\\) is sunny, distinct from the three original lines, and does not pass through the other private points. Replacing \\(x=1\\) by \\(\\ell_1\\) yields a family with exactly one sunny line.\n\n**Second replacement.**  \nNow the remaining non‑sunny lines are \\(y=1\\) (private point \\((2,1)\\)) and \\(x+y=3\\) (private point \\((2,2)\\)).  \nSelect \\(y=1\\). Its private point is \\((2,1)\\).  \nCurrent slopes are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(-1\\) (diagonal), and \\(2\\) (the sunny line).  \nSlopes of lines joining \\((2,1)\\) to the other private points are: to \\((2,2)\\) slope \\(\\infty\\); to the point on the sunny line \\((1,2)\\) we do not need to forbid anything. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{0,-1,2,\\infty\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose a slope not in \\(\\mathcal S\\); for instance \\(m=3\\).  \nThe line through \\((2,1)\\) with slope \\(3\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_2:\\; y-1=3(x-2)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; y=3x-5.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_2\\) is sunny, avoids the remaining private point \\((2,2)\\), and is distinct from all existing lines. Replacing \\(y=1\\) by \\(\\ell_2\\) we obtain the final family  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,y=3x-5,\\; y=2x,\\; x+y=3\\,\\}}.\n\\]\n\nIt consists of three distinct lines, covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\), and contains exactly two sunny lines (\\(y=2x\\) and \\(y=3x-5\\)). Hence the case \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\) is indeed realizable, confirming that the general replacement argument works even when the forbidden set of slopes is small.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nStart with the initial family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (which satisfies invariant (I)).  \nApply the replacement step repeatedly. After each replacement the number of sunny lines increases by one whi