}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=n+2\\},\n\\qquad\nT_{n}=T_{n+1}\\setminus L_{n+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(L_{n+1}\\) consists of the \\(n+1\\) lattice points lying on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{0}: x+y=n+2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNote that \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is **not** sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### (i) Obtaining \\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nTake the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) given by the induction hypothesis; it covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nAdd the line \\(\\ell_{0}\\) from (1).  \nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{0}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}=T_{n}\\cup L_{n+1}\\), and the number of sunny lines is still \\(k\\) (because \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is not sunny).  \nThus for each \\(k\\le n\\) we have a suitable configuration for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### (ii) Obtaining \\(k=n+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nStart with the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) (which exists by the induction hypothesis).  \nIn \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) exactly one of the lines is non‑sunny; denote it by \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\), the line \\(\\ell\\) must be one of the three forbidden‑direction lines, and in particular it can be taken to be the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell : x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) that still passes through all points of \\(L_{n+1}\\) except possibly one, and then use \\(\\ell_{0}\\) (the non‑sunny line (1)) to cover the remaining point.  \nA concrete choice is:\n\n* let \\(\\ell' : y = 2x-(n-1)\\) (slope \\(2\\), hence sunny);\n* \\(\\ell'\\) contains all points of \\(L_{n+1}\\) except \\((1,n+1)\\);\n* the point \\((1,n+1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{0}\\).\n\nNow define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,n+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell',\\ell_{0}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, all of which are sunny except \\(\\ell_{0}\\); consequently it contains exactly \\(n+1\\) sunny lines.  \nSince \\(\\ell'\\) together with the other \\(n-1\\) lines already cover \\(T_{n}\\) and \\(\\ell_{0}\\) covers the missing point of \\(L_{n+1}\\), every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered.\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n+1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nCombining (i) and (ii) we have produced, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\), a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the Induction\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) is settled by Lemma 1.  \nLemma 2 shows that if the statement holds for a given \\(n\\) then it holds for \\(n+1\\).  \nTherefore, by mathematical induction, the statement is true for all integers \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe have exhibited, for each admissible \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)), a concrete construction; hence every such \\(k\\) is attainable.  \nConversely, the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\), so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThus no integer \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
