\\[\n\\ell_{2}: y = 2x-(n-1) ,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(2\\) (hence sunny).  \n\\(\\ell_{2}\\) passes through all points of \\(L_{n+1}\\) **except** the point \\((1,n+1)\\); indeed, substituting \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) into (3) gives \\(b=2a-(n-1)\\), which fails only for \\(a=1\\).  \n\nFinally add the non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{0}\\) from (1) to cover the missing point \\((1,n+1)\\).  \nThe resulting family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,n}= (\\mathcal L_{n,n-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell_{1}\\})\\cup\\{\\ell_{2},\\ell_{0}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines: the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n-1}\\), the new sunny line \\(\\ell_{2}\\), and the non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{0}\\).  \nHence it has exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) are still covered (the original covering lines are unchanged except for \\(\\ell_{1}\\), which is replaced by \\(\\ell_{2}\\) that covers all of \\(L_{n+1}\\) except one point), and the remaining point of \\(L_{n+1}\\) is covered by \\(\\ell_{0}\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,n}\\) satisfies the required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining (a) and (b), for \\(n+1\\) we have constructed, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the Induction\n\nLemma\u202f1 establishes the statement for \\(n=3\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that if it holds for a given \\(n\\) then it holds for \\(n+1\\).  \nTherefore, by mathematical induction, the statement is true for all integers \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of the bound \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nThe problem requires exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nSince a line can be sunny at most once, the number of sunny lines cann