e equations of \\(\\ell,\\ell'\\) in the form  \n\n\\[\ny=m x +c,\\qquad y=m' x +c',\n\\]\n\nwith \\(m,m'\\) satisfying (1) and \\(m\\neq m'\\).  \nSolving for the intersection gives  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{c'-c}{m-m'},\\qquad y=\\frac{m c'-m' c}{m-m'} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\((x,y)\\) is integral, the numerator of each fraction must be an integer multiple of the denominator. Because \\(m,m'\\) are rational numbers whose denominators are not \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)), the only way both fractions become integers is that the difference \\(c'-c\\) is a non‑zero multiple of \\(m-m'\\). Consequently the intersection point cannot lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) or below it; otherwise the two lines would share the same slope, contradicting their distinctness. Hence \\(x+y>n+1\\). ∎  \n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA single sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\).  \nThen the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nBoth numerator and denominator are integers, and because the two points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n|b_2-b_1|\\le |a_2-a_1|.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|b_2-b_1|=|a_2-a_1|\\) then \\(m=\\pm1\\); the case \\(m=-1\\) is forbidden by (1), while \\(m=1\\) would make the line parallel to the line \\(x-y=0\\), which is not among the prohibited directions, but then the two points would lie on a line of slope \\(1\\) that necessarily passes through a point with sum \\(>n+1\\), contradicting Lemma 1.  \nIf \\(|b_2-b_1|<|a_2-a_1|\\) then \\(m\\) is a rational number with denominator larger than \\(1\\); the line would intersect the integer lattice at points whose coordinates differ by that denominator, forcing the second point to have sum exceeding \\(n+1\\). In all cases we obtain a contradiction, so at most one point of \\(T_n\\) can lie on a sunny line. ∎  \n\n---\n\n####