et of points that have to lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\nIts cardinality is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\equiv T .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  How many points can a single line contain?\n\n*Sunny line.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\(x=\\text{const}\\), \\(y=\\text{const}\\) and \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).\nConsequently it meets each of the three families of lines\n\\(\\{x=a\\}\\), \\(\\{y=b\\}\\) and \\(\\{x+y=c\\}\\) in at most one point.\nHence a sunny line can contain **at most three** points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny line.*  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, it is of the form \\(y=b\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  The points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) on it are \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.\nThe same bound holds for lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and for lines\nparallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (the line \\(x+y=c\\) contains the points\n\\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\le n\\)).  \nThus **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) given lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(3k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\nT-3k\n\\]\n\npoints must be covered by the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nSince each non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points, we must have  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;T-3k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(T=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) into (1):\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-nk\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2-3k .\n\\]\n\nMultiply by \\(2\\) and rearrange:\n\n\\[\n2n^{2}-2nk\\;\\ge\\;n^{2}+n-6k\n\\]\n\\[\nn^{2}-2nk+6k-n\\;\\ge\\;0\n\\]\n\\[\nn\\bigl(n-1-2k\\bigr)+6k\\;\\ge\\;0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(