e4\\) the coefficient \\(2n-6=2(n-3)\\) is positive, and (2) is\nequivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(2n-6\\bigr)\\;\\le\\;n(n-1) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence any feasible number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-3)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}\\qquad (n\\ge4).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the factor \\(2n-6\\) vanishes, and (2) reduces to\n\\(3-2k\\ge0\\), i.e. \\(k\\le3\\).  A direct construction (see below) shows\nthat all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are indeed attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Summary of the necessary condition\n\nCombining the obvious bounds \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) with the inequality derived\nabove, we obtain the following **necessary condition** for the existence\nof a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the problem’s\nrequirements:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n0\\le k\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n3, & n=3,\\\\[6pt]\n\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-3)}\\Bigr\\rfloor, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Remarks on sufficiency\n\nThe inequality (3) is obtained by the pigeonhole principle applied to\nthe maximal capacities of sunny and non‑sunny lines.  It is therefore a\nnecessary condition.  Whether it is also sufficient (i.e. whether for\nevery integer \\(k\\) satisfying the boxed inequality one can actually\nconstruct a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines) remains open in this\npartial solution.  The construction for the extremal cases\n\\(k=0\\) (all vertical lines) and \\(k=n\\) (possible only when\n\\(n\\ge\\lceil T/3\\rceil\\)) is straightforward, and for intermediate\nvalues one can try to allocate \\(k\\) sunny lines each covering three\ndistinct points and use the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to cover\nthe rest, exactly as the pigeonhole argument suggests.  A complete\nverification of this scheme for all admissible \\(k\\) is left for future\nwork."
