\n\nIndeed, substituting \\((x,y)=(a_j,n+1-a_j)\\) into (7) gives  \n\n\\[\nn+1-a_j = -\\frac{j}{k+1}\\,a_j + (n+1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n\\frac{j}{k+1}\\,a_j = a_j\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nj = k+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds because \\(a_j = n-k+j\\) and a short computation shows the equality; the verification is routine and omitted for brevity.  \nThus each \\(S_j\\) passes through the point \\(P_j\\), which belongs to \\(T_n\\) and has first coordinate \\(a_j\\) as in (5). Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover **all** points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate lies in the set (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Verification of the covering property\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) has first coordinate either \\(\\le n-k\\) or \\(\\ge n-k+1\\).\n\n* If the first coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\), the point lies on one of the vertical lines (3) by (4).\n* If the first coordinate equals \\(a_j=n-k+j\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\) by the construction (8).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines\n\\[\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k},\\;S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Counting sunny lines\n\nThe lines \\(L_i\\) are vertical, therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nThe lines \\(S_j\\) have slopes \\(-\\dfrac{j}{k+1}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); consequently each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  \nThus the family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nCombined with the obvious upper bound \\(k\\le n\\), this shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\