"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   A family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ).**  \n   * **Non\u2011sunny part:** take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\).  \n   * **Sunny part:** for each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) take the line  \n\n     \\[\n     S_j:\\; y = x + \\bigl(n+1-2(n-k+j)\\bigr)=x+(k+1-2j).\n     \\]\n\n     The slope of each \\(S_j\\) is \\(1\\); therefore it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)). Hence every \\(S_j\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because their intercepts \\(k+1-2j\\) are distinct.  \n\n   * **Covering argument:**  \n     \u2013 Points \\((a,b)\\in T_n:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n     \u2013 Points with \\(a=n-k+j\\;(1\\le j\\le k)\\) satisfy \\(b=n+1-a=k+1-j\\) and therefore belong to the line \\(S_j\\) because  \n\n       \\[\n       b = (k+1-j)= (n-k+j)+(k+1-2j)=a+(k+1-2j),\n       \\]\n\n       which is exactly the equation of \\(S_j\\).  \n\n     Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n   * **Counting sunny lines:** the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are not sunny, the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny, so the family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists, and together with the trivial upper bound this yields the complete set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{y}:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nIn any family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, hence  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a number \\(k\\) satisfying (2).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L_i\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the vertical lines (3) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = x + (k+1-2j). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S