ore  \n\n   \\[\n   |R|+k=n . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A necessary counting inequality.**  \n   For each discarded diagonal \\(D_c\\) (\\(c\\in R^{c}\\)) let \\(s_c\\) be the number of sunny lines that contain a point of \\(D_c\\).  \n   Because a sunny line is not parallel to \\(D_c\\), it can contain **at most one** point of \\(D_c\\).  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   s_c\\ge |D_c\\cap\\mathbb Z^2| = c-1 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Every sunny line that meets at least one discarded diagonal is counted in exactly those \\(s_c\\) for which it meets \\(D_c\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge \\max_{c\\in R^{c}} s_c \\ge \\max_{c\\in R^{c}} (c-1). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, summing (3) over all discarded diagonals gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c\\in R^{c}} s_c \\ge \\sum_{c\\in R^{c}} (c-1). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Since each sunny line can meet at most one point of each discarded diagonal, the left‑hand side of (5) is at most \\(|R^{c}|\\,k\\).  Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   |R^{c}|\\,k \\;\\ge\\; \\sum_{c\\in R^{c}} (c-1). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Elimination of the case \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) exists.\n\n   *If \\(|R^{c}|=1\\).*  \n   Then exactly one diagonal, say \\(D_{c_0}\\), is discarded.  Equation (6) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   k \\ge c_0-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\ge 2\\), we must have \\(c_0\\ge 3\\).  But then (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n = |R|+k = (n-1)+k \\ge (n-1)+2 = n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Hence \\(|R^{c}|\\neq 1\\).\n\n   *If \\(|R^{c}|\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Let \\(c_{\\min}=\\min R^{c}\\) and \\(c_{\\max}=\\max R^{c}\\).  From (6) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k \\ge \\frac{\\displaystyle\\sum_{c\\in R^{c}} (c-1)}{|R^{c}|}\n        \\ge \\frac{(c_{\\min}-1)+(c_{\\max}-1)}{2}\n        \\ge \\frac{1+2}{2}= \\tfrac32 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer, this forces \\(k\\ge 2\\).  Substituting \\(k\\ge 2\\) into (2) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   n = |R|+k \\le (n-2)+k = n-2+k .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives \\(k\\le 2\\).  Thus the only possible value in this subcase is \\(k=2\\) and \\(|R^{c}|=2\\) with the two discarded diagonals being exactly \\(D_{2}\\) and \\(D_{3}\\) (the smallest possible pair, because any larger pair would make the right‑hand side of (6) exceed \\(2|R^{c}|\\)).  For this pair we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c\\in R^{c}} (c-1) = (2-1)+(3-1)=3,\n   \\qquad |R^{c}|\\,k = 2\\cdot 2 =4,\n   \\]\n\n   which satisfies (6).  However, the three points that belong to the discarded diagonals are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Any sunny line can contain at most one of them, because two of them lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\) (the diagonal \\(D_{3}\\)) and any line through two of the three points would be either horizontal, vertical, or have slope \\(-1\\), all of which are forbidden for a sunny line.  Consequently at least three sunny lines are needed to cover these three points, contradicting \\(k=2\\).\n\n   Hence the assumption \\(k\\ge 2\\) leads to a contradiction in every possible case.\n\n5. **Existence for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Keep all \\(n\\) diagonals (1).  They are distinct, cover \\(T_n\\), and none is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Discard the diagonal \\(D_{2}\\) (which contains the single point \\((1,1)\\)) and replace it by any sunny line passing through \\((1,1)\\) (for instance, the line \\(y=2x\\)).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) diagonals are kept.  The total number of lines is \\( (n-1)+1 = n\\) and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   Both constructions satisfy the required covering condition.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument shows that no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) exists, while explicit constructions exhibit configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generat