**Necessary counting conditions.**  \n\n   *Each sunny line can intersect a discarded diagonal in at most one point.*  \n   Indeed, a sunny line is not parallel to any line of slope \\(-1\\); two\n   distinct points of the same diagonal determine a line of slope \\(-1\\), so a\n   sunny line cannot contain both of them.\n\n   Consequently, for a discarded diagonal \\(D_c\\) (\\(c\\in R^{c}\\)) we need at\n   least \\(|P_c|=c-1\\) distinct sunny lines to cover its points, and a single\n   sunny line can cover at most one point of each discarded diagonal.  \n\n   Denote \\(d:=|R^{c}|=k\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   s_c:=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines that contain a point of }P_c\\}\\qquad(c\\in R^{c}).\n   \\]\n\n   From the discussion above  \n\n   \\[\n   s_c\\ge c-1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n   \\sum_{c\\in R^{c}} s_c\\le k\\cdot d . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,d\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c\\in R^{c}}(c-1). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k=d\\) we obtain the fundamental inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   d^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c\\in R^{c}}(c-1). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sufficiency of the inequality.**  \n\n   Condition (3) is not only necessary but also sufficient.  \n   Consider the bipartite graph whose left part consists of the \\(d\\) sunny\n   lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{d}\\) and whose right part consists of the points\n   \\(\\bigcup_{c\\in R^{c}}P_c\\).  \n   Connect a line \\(L_i\\) with a point \\(p\\in P_c\\) iff \\(L_i\\) passes through\n   \\(p\\).  \n   Because a line can meet at most one point of each diagonal, the degree of\n   each line on the left is at most \\(|R^{c}|=d\\); the degree of a point on the\n   right is exactly \\(1\\) (it must be covered).  \n\n   Hall’s marriage theorem tells us that a perfect matching (i.e. an\n   assignment of each point to a distinct line that contains it) exists iff\n   for every subset \\(X\\) of the right‑hand vertices we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |N(X)|\\ge |X|,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(N(X)\\) is the set of neighbours of \\(X\\).  \n   For any collection of points taken from a set of diagonals \\(S\\subseteq\n   R^{c}\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |X|=\\sum_{c\\in S}(c-1),\\qquad\n   |N(X)|\\le d\\;|S| .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality (3) guarantees that for every possible \\(S\\)\n\n   \\[\n   d\\;|S|\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c\\in S}(c-1)=|X|,\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely Hall’s condition.  Therefore a perfect matching exists,\n   i.e. the \\(d\\) sunny lines can be chosen so that every point of every\n   discarded diagonal is covered, each line meeting at most one point of each\n   diagonal.\n\n5. **Realising every \\(k\\).**  \n\n   We now show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can select a\n   set \\(R^{c}\\) of size \\(k\\) that satisfies (3).  \n   Choose the \\(k\\) *smallest* diagonals, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   R^{c}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c\\in R^{c}}(c-1)=1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\le k^{2},\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(k\\ge 1\\) implies \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\le k^{2}\\).  Hence (3) holds.\n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) diagonals are kept, giving exactly \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines.\n\n   By the argument of §4 we can now construct \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover all\n   points of the discarded diagonals.  An explicit construction is easy:\n   for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = m_i x + b_i ,\n   \\]\n\n   where the slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct and none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)\n   (e.g. \\(m_i=i\\)).  For each diagonal \\(c\\in R^{c}\\) assign its \\((c-1)\\) points\n   cyclically to the \\(k\\) lines; because \\(k\\ge c-1\\) for the chosen set,\n   this assignment is possible and determines the intercepts \\(b_i\\).  The\n   resulting lines are all distinct, sunny, and together with the retained\n   diagonals they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Extremal cases.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Keep all \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_c\\;(c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\); none is sunny.  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  Discard all diagonals.  The construction above with slopes\n   \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) yields \\(n\\) sunny lines that together cover all\n   \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (each line meets exactly one point of\n   each diagonal, so the total number of covered points is \\(n\\cdot n\\ge\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\)).  A simple Hall‑matching argument shows that the\n   assignment can be made without conflict, giving a valid configuration.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary counting condition forces \\(k\\) to satisfy (3), and the\n   construction with the \\(k\\) smallest diagonals shows that every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) meets this condition and can be realised.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
