nts \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in P_c\\).\n   Since both satisfy \\(x+y=c\\), the segment joining them has slope \\(-1\\); consequently\n   the line through them also has slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny\n   line. ∎\n\n4. **Counting Argument.**  \n   Assume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and\n   contains exactly \\(k\\ge 1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Let the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny.  \n   Because a non‑sunny line must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions,\n   each of them can cover **only** points that lie on a single diagonal (if it is parallel\n   to a diagonal) or on a single vertical/horizontal line.  \n   In particular, a non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(\\max\\{c-1\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}=n\\) points\n   of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   The total number of points to be covered is  \n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 3, each sunny line can cover at most one point from each diagonal, i.e. at most\n   \\(n\\) points in total (there are \\(n\\) diagonals). Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together\n   cover at most \\(kn\\) points.\n\n   The \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points (each can cover at most\n   \\(n\\) points as argued above). Consequently the whole configuration can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   kn+(n-k)n = n^2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing (1) and (2) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n+1\\le 2n,\n   \\]\n   which is true for all \\(n\\ge 1\\).  However, equality in (2) would require that **every**\n   line (sunny or not) actually attains its maximal possible number \\(n\\) of covered points.\n   This can happen only if each non‑sunny line coincides with a diagonal \\(D_c\\) (otherwise it\n   would miss at least one point of that diagonal), and each sunny line meets every diagonal\n   in a point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   For a sunny line \\(L\\) to meet a diagonal \\(D_c\\) in a point of \\(T_n\\) the intersection\n   must be one of the integer points of \\(P_c\\).  As \\(c\\) varies, the set of intersection\n   points of a fixed sunny line with the family \\(\\{D_c\\}\\) lies on a line of slope \\(-1\\);\n   hence all those intersection points belong to a single diagonal, contradicting the fact\n   that a sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal.  Therefore a sunny line can meet at\n   most \\(n-1\\) diagonals in points of \\(T_n\\), i.e. it covers at most \\(n-1\\) points of\n   \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Substituting this sharper bound into (2) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   kn+(n-k)n - k \\;=\\; n^{2}-k \\;<\\; n^{2},\n   \\]\n   which is **strictly** smaller than the required number \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for every\n   \\(k\\ge 1\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Hence the covering condition cannot be satisfied if at\n   least one sunny line is present.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting contradiction shows that a configuration satisfying the two given\n   requirements cannot contain any sunny line.  Consequently the only admissible value\n   of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0}.\n   \\]\n\n   (The case \\(k=0\\) is realized by taking the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\);\n   each of them is non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).)\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
