rises.\n\n   However, the sunny line \\(L\\) is **not** allowed to be parallel to any diagonal, in particular it cannot have slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Therefore \\(L\\) meets each diagonal \\(D_c\\) (\\(c\\ge 3\\)) in at most one point, and by Lemma\u202f1 it can meet at most one of the two points of \\(P_2\\) (which is just \\((1,1)\\)).  \n   Hence the only way to cover \\((1,1)\\) is that \\(L\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n   If \\(L\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and is not of slope \\(-1\\), then its equation is either \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) or \\(x=1\\) (vertical) or has a different non\u2011zero slope.  \n   *If \\(L\\) is horizontal* (\\(y=1\\)), it also contains the point \\((2, -1)\\) which lies outside the required set, but that is harmless.  \n   The crucial observation is that a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) meets the diagonal \\(D_c\\) at the point \\((c-1,1)\\).  \n   For \\(c=n+1\\) this point is \\((n,1)\\), which belongs to \\(P_{n+1}\\).  \n   Thus the horizontal line already covers **one** point of each diagonal \\(D_c\\) (\\(c\\ge 3\\)).  \n   Since each of those diagonals already contains all its points (they are themselves lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\)), the horizontal line does not create any conflict.\n\n   The same reasoning works for a vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n   Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line **does** exist: take the \\(n-2\\) diagonals \\(D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (which is sunny) and, if needed, the vertical line \\(x=1\\) to reach \\(n\\) lines.  \n   This construction uses **two** sunny lines when \\(n\\ge 3\\) (both \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) are sunny), contradicting the assumption \\(k=1\\).  \n   Therefore the case \\(k=1\\) is impossible.\n\n   A more direct argument: any sunny line that passes through \\((1,1)\\) must be either horizontal or vertical, because any other slope would intersect the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) at a non\u2011integer point for some \\(c\\), leaving that inte