) can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\) for each fixed value of \\(b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on this line then \\(b=c\\).  \nSince the points of \\(T_n\\) have distinct \\(a\\)-coordinates for a given \\(b\\) (they are \\((1,b),(2,b),\\dots ,(n+1-b,b)\\)), the line \\(y=c\\) can contain **all** those points, not just one.  \nThus the statement of the lemma is false as written.  \n\n*Correction.*  \nWe need a different counting argument.  \n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\) where \\(c\\) is its \\(y\\)-coordinate. Similarly, a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points where \\(c\\) is its \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x-y=d\\). For a fixed value of \\(d\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) on this line satisfy \\(a-b=d\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); there are at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\rfloor\\) such points.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(L\\) be a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). Then the number of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) is at most \\(\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(x-y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\). Points on the line satisfy \\(a=b+d\\). Substituting into the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(b+d)+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;2b\\le n+1-d.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\ge 1\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, using \\(a\\ge 1\\) we get  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge 1-d.\n\\]\n\nHence the admissible integer values of \\(b\\) form an interval of length at most \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\). Therefore the line contains at most \\(\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Counting Argument\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn_1,n_2,n_3\\ge 0,\\qquad n_1+n_2+n_3=n,\n\\]\n\nbe the numbers of lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) parallel respect