ns at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPoints on the line satisfy \\(a=b+d\\). Substituting into the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n(b+d)+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;2b\\le n+1-d.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\ge 1\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}.\n\\]\n\nLikewise \\(a=b+d\\ge 1\\) gives \\(b\\ge 1-d\\). Hence the admissible integer values of \\(b\\) form an interval whose length is at most \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\); therefore the line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### Counting the maximal number of covered points\n\nUsing Lemma 1, the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n_1\\) horizontal lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n_1}(n+1-c_i)\\le n_1\\,(n+1),\n\\]\n\nbecause each constant \\(c_i\\ge 1\\). The same bound holds for the \\(n_2\\) vertical lines.  \nLemma 2 gives the bound \\(n_3\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) for the diagonal lines.\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le n_1(n+1)+n_2(n+1)+n_3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=(n-n_3)(n+1)+n_3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n(n+1)-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge 2,\n\\]\n\nand therefore from (2)\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le n(n+1)-2n_3. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBut by (1)\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=n(n+1)+\\frac{n+1}{2}>n(n+1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInequalities (3) and (4) cannot hold simultaneously because the right‑hand side of (3) is at most \\(n(n+1)\\). This contradiction shows that the assumption “all \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny’’ is false. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge 1}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction showing that every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) has six points.\n\n* \\(k=1\\):  \n  Take the sunny line \\(L_1:\\;y=x\\) (direction \\((1,1)\\)), and the two non‑sunny lines \\(L_2:\\;y=1\\) and \\(L_3:\\;x=1\\). One checks directly that every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of these three lines.\n\n* \\(k=2\\):  \n  Use the sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=2x\\) together with the non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\).\n\n* \\(k=3\\):  \n  Choose three sunny lines, for instance \\(y=x,\\;y=2x,\\;y=3x\\). Their pairwise intersections are distinct, and each point of \\(T_3\\) belongs to at least one of them.\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 4\\) the statement is true for \\(n-1\\): for every integer \\(k'\\) with \\(1\\le k'\\le n-1\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{\\,k'}\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n-1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Inductive step  \n\nFix a target \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\). We shall produce a suitable family \\(\\mathcal F_n^{\\,k}\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(k\\le n-1\\).*  \nTake the configuration \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{\\,k}\\) (which has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines). Translate every line of this configuration by the vector \\((1,0)\\); denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal F'\\). Translation preserves parallelism, distinctness of lines and the number of sunny lines.  \n\nThe translation maps the set \\(T_{n-1}\\) onto  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-1}+ (1,0)=\\{(a+1,b)\\mid a+b\\le n\\}\n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 2\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal F'\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate at least \\(2\\).  \n\nNow add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{new}}:\\;x=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis). This line covers precisely the remaining points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), i.e. all points of \\(T_n\\) having \\(a=1\\).  \n\nThe resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains exactly the original \\(k\\) sunny lines, and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n\\).*  \nStart from the configuration \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{\\,n-1}\\) (which has \\(n-1\\) sunny lines). Translate it by \\((1,0)\\) as above, obtaining a family \\(\\mathcal F'\\) with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n\nNow add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{new}}:\\;y=x,\n\\]\n\nwhich is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, and which passes through all points \\((a,a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\). In particular it covers the point \\((1,1)\\) that is not yet covered by \\(\\mathcal F'\\). Because the other points with \\(a=1\\) are already covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) that belongs to \\(\\mathcal F'\\) (if such a line appears; if not, we may replace one of the non‑sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal F'\\) by \\(x=1\\) without affecting the number of sunny lines). Consequently the final family has \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are sunny, i.e. \\(k=n\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) we have constructed a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=0\\) (Section 2) with the explicit constructions for all \\(k\\) satisfying \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) (Section 3) yields the complete classification:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
