gth at most \\(1\\); therefore they contain at most **two** points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Bounding the number of non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Let the configuration contain \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines.  \n   *Each non‑sunny line contributes at most two points of \\(S_n\\) that are not already covered by another non‑sunny line of the same direction* (parallel non‑sunny lines are disjoint on \\(S_n\\)). Hence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered exclusively by the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines is at most \\(2t\\).  \n   The remaining \\(|S_n|-2t\\) points must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines. A sunny line cannot be parallel to any side of the right‑angled triangle that bounds \\(S_n\\); consequently its intersection with that triangle is a line segment that can contain at most \\(n\\) lattice points. Therefore  \n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|-2t.\n   \\]  \n   Substituting \\(k=n-t\\) and \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) gives  \n   \\[\n   (n-t)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2t\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\le 2.\n   \\]  \n   Hence at most two non‑sunny lines may appear, i.e. \\(k=n-t\\ge n-2\\).\n\n4. **Realising the three admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n   For each integer \\(m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n\\) define the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_m:\\; y=mx+(n+1-m).\n   \\]  \n   The slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct and none is parallel to the forbidden directions. For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b=ma+(n+1-m)\\) with the unique integer \\(m=\\frac{n+1-b}{a}\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_m\\). All points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and \\(k=n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Keep the vertical line \\(\\ell_0:x=1\\) (non‑sunny). It covers the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \n   For the remaining points (those with \\(x\\ge2\\)) use the sunny lines \\(\\ell_m\\) defined above with \\(m=2,\\dots ,n\\). As before each such point belongs to exactly one of these lines. Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n-2\\).*  \n   Use the two non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_0:x=1\\) and \\(\\ell'_0:y=1\\). They cover all points of \\(S_n\\) having a coordinate equal to \\(1\\).  \n   The remaining points satisfy \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\); they form the set  \n   \\[\n   S'_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\ a\\ge2,\\ b\\ge2\\}.\n   \\]  \n   Translate the family \\(\\{\\ell_m\\}_{m=2}^{n}\\) by the vector \\((1,1)\\): for each \\(m=2,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_m':\\; y=m x+(n+1-m)-(m-1).\n   \\]  \n   These lines are sunny, have distinct slopes, and each point \\((a,b)\\in S'_n\\) satisfies  \n   \\[\n   b-1 = m(a-1)+(n+1-m)-(m-1)\n   \\]  \n   with \\(m=\\frac{n+1-b}{a}\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\). Hence every point of \\(S'_n\\) lies on one of the \\(\\ell_m'\\). Together with the two axis‑parallel lines we obtain a configuration with exactly two non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).\n\nThus we have proved the necessary inequality \\(k\\ge n-2\\) and exhibited constructions for the three values \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\). The problem of eliminating the possibility \\(k\\le n-3\\) remains open.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
