"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists **iff** the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines fulfills  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *Sunny*\u202f=\u202fline whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (the integer points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\)).  \n\n2. **Necessary bound.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line meets each diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\;(c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\) in at most one point.  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain at most one point of each diagonal.  \n\n   The two extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on the same diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) and are not collinear with any sunny line (the line through them has slope \\(-1\\)). Hence at least two non\u2011sunny lines are required to cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sufficiency.**  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(i\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   For the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines we take the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   d_j:\\; x+y=j+1\\qquad (j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Every \\(d_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and put \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\n\n   - If \\(s\\ge k+2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(d_{s-1}\\), which is one of the chosen non\u2011sunny lines.\n   - If \\(s\\le k+1\\) then \\(s-1\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\). The sunny line \\(\\ell_{s-1}\\) contains the point \\((a,b)\\) because\n     \\[\n     b = (s-1)a \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+b = a+(s-1)a = s\\le k+1 .\n     \\]\n     Hence every point whose sum of coordinates does not exceed \\(k+1\\) belongs to the appropriate sunny line \\(\\ell_{s-1}\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   *Distinctness.*  \n   The slopes of \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are the distinct integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\); none equals \\(-1\\).  \n   The diagonals \\(d_{k+1},\\dots ,d_n\\) all have slope \\(-1\\) but different intercepts, so they are pairwise distinct and also distinct from the \\(\\ell_i\\)\u2019s. Consequently we have exactly \\(n\\) different lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be realized. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
