ny line can appear in a feasible configuration; i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; x+y = s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   These are precisely the diagonals of slope \\(-1\\); they are distinct because\n   they have different intercepts. Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies\n   \\(a+b=s\\) for some \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), and therefore lies on the\n   line \\(L_s\\). All \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines is\n   \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that \\(k\\le n-2\\) is necessary, while the argument of\n   Section 3 shows that any \\(k\\ge 1\\) leads to a contradiction. The explicit\n   construction of Section 4 demonstrates that \\(k=0\\) is attainable. Hence the\n   set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
