ore each \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.  \n   The lines \\(\\ell_t\\) are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.3 \u2013 verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the\n   constructed lines.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Set \\(s=a+b\\) (so \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\n\n   * If \\(s=2\\) then \\((a,b)=(1,1)\\) and it lies on \\(L_{1}\\).\n   * If \\(s=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{2}\\) by definition.\n   * Otherwise \\(3\\le s\\le n\\). Write \\(s=t+1\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \n     Substituting \\(a\\) into (2) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     b = (t-1)a + (2-t) \\iff a+b = t+1 = s .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_t\\); consequently\n     \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_t\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the \\(n\\) lines\n   \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\}\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3.4 \u2013 counting sunny lines.*  \n   The construction uses the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) and the\n   \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_t\\). Hence the number of sunny lines is exactly\n   \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3.5 \u2013 obtaining any smaller \\(k\\).*  \n   To obtain a configuration with fewer sunny lines, simply replace some of the\n   sunny lines \\(\\ell_t\\) by additional non\u2011sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   For each replacement we keep the set of covered points unchanged because\n   the line \\(x+y=s\\) (with the appropriate \\(s\\)) already contains all points of\n   diagonal \\(D_s\\). Replacing \\(\\ell_t\\) by the diagonal \\(x+y=t+1\\) removes one\n   sunny line and adds one non\u2011sunny line, decreasing \\(k\\) by one while still\n   covering all points. Repeating this operation we can achieve any integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying\n