            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nThe previous answer was incorrect because it incorrectly forced every point on a diagonal that cannot be shared by a single sunny line to be covered by a non‑sunny line. In fact, different sunny lines may each cover a different point on the same diagonal. After correcting this mistake we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ are attainable, and no other }k\\text{ works}.}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line can contain at most one point of any diagonal\n   \\(D_s:=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=s\\}\\) because two points of the same diagonal would give\n   slope \\(-1\\).  \n   The two extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on the same diagonal\n   \\(D_{n+1}\\). Hence at least two *non‑sunny* lines are needed to cover that\n   diagonal. Since we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   We exhibit a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n   ones and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step 3.1 – two non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the two lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}: x+y=2,\\qquad L_{2}: x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Both are non‑sunny and together they contain the points \\((1,1)\\) and\n   \\((1,n),(n,1)\\) respectively.\n\n   *Step 3.2 – the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t:\\; y = (t-1)\\,x + (2-t).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) is \\(t-1\\), which is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\);\n   therefore each \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.  \n   The lines \\(\\ell_t\\) are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n   *Step 3.3 – verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the\n   constructed lines.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Set \\(s=a+b\\) (so \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\n\n   * If \\(s=2\\) then \\((a,b)=(1,1)\\) and it lies on \\(L_{1}\\).\n   * If \\(s=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{2}\\) by definition.\n   * Otherwise \\(3\\le s\\le n\\). Write \\(s=t+1\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \n     Substituting \\(a\\) into (2) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     b = (t-1)a + (2-t) \\iff a+b = t+1 = s .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_t\\); consequently\n     \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_t\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the \\(n\\) lines\n   \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\}\\).\n\n   *Step 3.4 – counting sunny lines.*  \n   The construction uses the two non‑sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) and the\n   \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_t\\). Hence the number of sunny lines is exactly\n   \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n   *Step 3.5 – obtaining any smaller \\(k\\).*  \n   To obtain a configuration with fewer sunny lines, simply replace some of the\n   sunny lines \\(\\ell_t\\) by additional non‑sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   For each replacement we keep the set of covered points unchanged because\n   the line \\(x+y=s\\) (with the appropriate \\(s\\)) already contains all points of\n   diagonal \\(D_s\\). Replacing \\(\\ell_t\\) by the diagonal \\(x+y=t+1\\) removes one\n   sunny line and adds one non‑sunny line, decreasing \\(k\\) by one while still\n   covering all points. Repeating this operation we can achieve any integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be realized. Therefore the set of admissible\n   values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
