n   *Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \n   For each \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   Every \\(L_s\\) has slope \\(-1\\), hence is non‑sunny.  \n   The set \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines and contains\n   every point of \\(T_n\\) because each point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal\n   \\(x+y=a+b\\). Add any extra non‑sunny line (for instance \\(x=0\\)) to obtain a total\n   of \\(n\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny. Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \n   Choose a sunny line that meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) (with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\))\n   in exactly one point. One convenient choice is  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\; y = x + 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(1\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\), so \\(S\\) is sunny.  \n   For a given diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n\\)) the unique integer point of\n   intersection is \\((s-1,\\,1)\\) because substituting \\(y=x+1\\) into \\(x+y=s\\) gives\n   \\(2x+1=s\\Rightarrow x=s-1\\), \\(y=1\\). Hence \\(S\\) covers exactly one point of each\n   diagonal \\(D_s\\) for \\(s=2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n   The remaining diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) still contains \\(n\\) points, none of which\n   lie on \\(S\\). As in the \\(k=0\\) construction we cover this diagonal by the\n   non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{n+1}^{(i)}:\\; x+y=n+1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-1),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. we take \\(n-1\\) distinct copies of the same geometric line but with\n   different “labels’’; geometrically they are the same line, but the problem\n   requires *distinct* lines, so we must use different non‑sunny lines for the\n   other diagonals. A convenient way is to use the family  \n\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\; x = j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\n   \\]\n\n   each of which is vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)) and therefore non‑sunny.\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) of \\(D_{n+1}\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the point with\n   \\(a=j\\) lies on the line