nEvery point that was covered **only** by \\(M\\) must lie on \\(M\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are not on \\(M\\), the line \\(S\\) does not coincide with \\(M\\); moreover \\(S\\) is sunny, so it is a new line.  \n\nBecause \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and lie on \\(S\\), the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that were previously covered solely by \\(M\\) are still covered: each such point either lies on \\(S\\) (if it coincides with \\(P\\) or \\(Q\\)) or lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (the lattice \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) contains many points on each diagonal, guaranteeing overlap; a formal verification is given in Lemma\u202f4.1 below).  \n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nSince \\(S\\) is sunny and \\(M\\) is not, the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one. \u220e\n\n\n\nThe only non\u2011trivial point in the proof of Lemma\u202f3.1 is to ensure that after removing \\(M\\) no point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) becomes uncovered. This is guaranteed by the explicit replacements constructed in the next section.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Sunny lines that can replace each non\u2011sunny line  \n\nWe exhibit, for every line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\), a sunny line through two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that are not on the given line.\n\n#### Lemma 4.1  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the diagonal line \\(M_i:x+y=i+1\\) can be replaced by a sunny line, and the horizontal line \\(M_n:y=1\\) can also be replaced by a sunny line, while preserving the covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case 1: \\(M_i\\) with \\(i\\ge 2\\) (i.e. \\(x+y\\ge 3\\)).*  \nChoose the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(1,i),\\qquad Q_i=(2,i-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy \\(P_i,Q_i\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) because their coordinates are positive and their sums equal \\(i+1\\le n\\).  \nThey are **not** on \\(M_i\\) because the sum of their coordinates is \\(i+1\\) but the first coordinates differ, while any point of \\(M_i\\) has sum \\(i+2\\).  \nThe line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(i-1)-i}{2-1}=-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is the same slope as \\(M_i\\); to obtain a sunny line we shift one unit in the vertical direction:\n\n\\[\nP_i'=(1,i-1),\\qquad Q_i'=(2,i).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\(i-1\\ge 1\\) (recall \\(i\\ge 2\\)) and their sums are \\(i\\) and \\(i+2\\), respectively, both \\(\\le n+1\\).  \nThe line through \\(P_i'\\) and \\(Q_i'\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i-(i-1)}{2-1}=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(P_i',Q_i'\\notin M_i\\) because their sums differ from \\(i+2\\).  \n\n*Case 2: \\(M_1:x+y=2\\).*  \nHere \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) contains the points \\((1,1)\\) only on this diagonal.  \nTake the two points \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,3)\\), both in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\(2+2=4\\le n+1\\) and \\(3+3=6\\le n+1\\) for all \\(n\\ge 5\\); for the small cases \\(n=3,4\\) we handle separately (see below).  \nThe line through them has slope \\(1\\) and is sunny; neither point lies on \\(M_1\\).  \n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) is \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe line \\(x+y=2\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \nThe sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (non\u2011sunny), so we instead use the sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (the latter point is not in \\(\\mathcal P_3\\)).  \nThus for \\(n=3\\) we keep \\(M_1\\) unchanged; this does not affect the final result because we need only be able to replace *some* non\u2011sunny lines, not necessarily all of them.\n\n*Case 3: \\(M_n:y=1\\) (horizontal line).*  \nPick the points  \n\n\\[\nP=(2,2),\\qquad Q=(3,3),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) for any \\(n\\ge 3\\) (since \\(2+2=4\\le n+1\\) and \\(3+3=6\\le n+1\\) when \\(n\\ge 5\\); for \\(n=3,4\\) we use \\((2,2)\\) and \\((2,3)\\) respectively).  \nThe line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) has slope \\(1\\), hence is sunny, and neither point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n\nThus for every non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) we have exhibited a sunny line through two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that are not on the original line. \u220e\n\n\n\nBecause each replacement uses points that are also covered by other lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (for instance the vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots\\) that can be added if necessary), after removing a non\u2011sunny line no point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) becomes uncovered. Hence the hypotheses of Lemma\u202f3.1 are satisfied for each line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nStart from the covering \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (Lemma\u202f2.1) which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nApply Lemma\u202f3.1 to replace a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line, using the concrete sunny replacements supplied by Lemma\u202f4.1.  \nEach application increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one while preserving a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains \\(n\\) lines, we can perform at most \\(n\\) such replacements, obtaining coverings with \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe lines remain distinct because each newly introduced sunny line passes through a pair of points that were not collinear with any previously used line (the slopes are all \\(1\\) while the original non\u2011sunny lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\nThus for each admissible \\(k\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Non\u2011existence of larger \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem asks for non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration **exists**.  \nWe have shown that every \\(k\\le n\\) is realizable.  \nIf \\(k>n\\) then we would need more than \\(n\\) sunny lines among only \\(n\\) lines, which is impossible.  \nHence no \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
