to the \\(x\\)-axis.  \n   – Same \\(a+b\\): the line would have slope \\(-1\\), i.e. be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   In each case the line would not be sunny – a contradiction. ∎  \n\n3. **Counting bound.**  \n   \\(|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \n   If \\(s\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, they cover at most \\(3s\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-s\\) lines are each parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; a line of any of those directions can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) (the largest possible number of points on a horizontal, vertical or diagonal line inside the triangle is \\(n\\)).  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   3s+n\\,(n-s)=n^{2}+(3-n)s .\n   \\]\n\n   Since we must have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}+(3-n)s\\), solving for \\(s\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\ge 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the pigeon‑hole principle imposes **no lower bound** on the number of sunny lines; any \\(s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le n\\) is a priori admissible.  \n\n4. **Explicit construction for every \\(k\\).**  \n   We exhibit, for an arbitrary \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 1 – sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=\\frac23x+\\frac{2i-1}{3}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i,1}=(i,i),\\qquad \n   P_{i,2}=(i+1,i+2),\\qquad \n   P_{i,3}=(i+2,i+1)\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   satisfy (1); they are distinct, belong to \\(\\mathcal P\\) (because\n   \\(i+(i)=2i\\le n+1\\) for \\(i\\le k\\le n\\), and similarly for the other two), and the slope \\(\\frac23\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\). Hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The triples \\(\\{P_{i,1},P_{i,2},P_{i,3}\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, so the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct and together cover exactly \\(3k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining points.*  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal R=\\mathcal P\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{P_{i,1},P_{i,2},P_{i,3}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le k+2\\) or \\(b\\le k+2\\) (otherwise it would belong to one of the triples). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal R|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-3k\\le n\\,(n-k).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, \\(3k\\ge 0\\) and \\(n(n-k)\\) is the maximal number of points that can be placed on \\(n-k\\) lines each parallel to a forbidden direction, because each such line contains at most \\(n\\) points.)\n\n   *Step 3 – covering \\(\\mathcal R\\) with \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.*  \n   We use only lines parallel to the three forbidden directions; they are automatically non‑sunny.\n\n   - **Horizontal lines.** For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-k\\) take the line  \n\n     \\[\n     H_b:\\; y=b .\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     Every point of \\(\\mathcal R\\) whose second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\) lies on the appropriate \\(H_b\\).\n\n   - **Vertical lines.** For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) take  \n\n     \\[\n     V_a:\\; x=a .\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n     If a point of \\(\\mathcal R\\) has \\(b>n-k\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le k\\); hence its first coordinate is at most \\(k\\) and the point lies on the corresponding vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   The total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\;(\\text{horizontal})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{vertical})=n .\n   \\]\n\n   However we already have the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\). To keep the total at exactly \\(n\\) we replace \\(k\\) of the horizontal lines by the \\(k\\) vertical lines; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are the horizontal ones \\(H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_{n}\\). Explicitly, the final family of \\(n\\) lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{L_1,\\dots ,L_k}_{\\text{sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n