\((0,1)\\) and \\((1,-1)\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, its primitive direction vector cannot be any of these three; consequently a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Counting incidences\n\nSumming the contributions of all lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   =|\\mathcal P|\n   \\le 2k + n t\n   = 2k + n(n-k)\n   = n^{2}-(n-2)k .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n(n-2)k \\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n        = \\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge 3\\), the right\u2011hand side is strictly smaller than \\((n-2)n\\); dividing by the positive integer \\(n-2\\) yields\n\n\\[\nk \\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) can actually be realized.\n\n#### 3.1  The two non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{v}: x = c ,\\qquad c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}: y = d ,\\qquad d\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\neq d\\).  Both are non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and the \\(x\\)-axis respectively).\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) having a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) lie on the lines \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\).  By choosing \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) in the range \\(2\\) to \\(n\\) we can shift these two lines so that they pass through **any** subset of points whose first coordinate equals a prescribed integer \\(c\\) or whose second coordinate equals a prescribed integer \\(d\\).  In particular we can make \\(\\ell_{v}\\) contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) with \\(x=c\\) and \\(\\ell_{h}\\) contain every point with \\(y=d\\).\n\nThus the two non\u2011sunny lines can be arranged to cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that have at least one coordinate equal to a chosen value in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n#### 3.2  The sunny lines\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal Q|\n   = |\\mathcal P|-(2n-1)\n   = \\frac{n(n+1)}2-(2n-1)\n   = \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) has both coordinates at least \\(2\\); consequently none of them lies on the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=c\\) or \\(y=d\\) as long as we choose \\(c,d\\ge 2\\) different from the point\u2019s coordinates.\n\nWe now select \\(k\\) disjoint unordered pairs from \\(\\mathcal Q\\).  \nBecause of (2) we have \\(|\\mathcal Q|\\ge 2k\\) for any \\(k\\le n-2\\); therefore such a selection is always possible.\n\nFor each chosen pair \\(\\{(a_{1},b_{1}),\\,(a_{2},b_{2})\\}\\) we draw the unique line \\(\\ell\\) passing through the two points.  \nSince the two points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates and distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates (otherwise they would coincide), the line \\(\\ell\\) is neither vertical nor horizontal.  \nMoreover the sums \\(a_{1}+b_{1}\\) and \\(a_{2}+b_{2}\\) are different because the points belong to \\(\\mathcal Q\\) and have distinct coordinates; consequently \\(\\ell\\) cannot have slope \\(-1\\).  \nHence each such line is sunny.\n\nIf after forming \\(k\\) pairs there remain unpaired points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) (this happens when \\(k<\\frac{|\\mathcal Q|}{2}\\)), we simply let those remaining points be covered by the two non\u2011sunny lines: we adjust the constants \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) so that each leftover point has either \\(x=c\\) or \\(y=d\\).  Because there are at most \\(n-2-k\\) leftover points and we have the freedom to choose any two distinct integers \\(c,d\\) from \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), this adjustment is always feasible.\n\nFinally we have:\n\n* exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the ones through the selected pairs);\n* exactly two non\u2011sunny lines (\\(x=c\\) and \\(y=d\\));\n* a total of \\(k+2=n\\) distinct lines (the constants \\(c,d\\) are chosen different from each other and from the slopes of the sunny lines, guaranteeing distinctness);\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of the lines by construction.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the problem\u2019s requirements exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe incidence argument shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and the explicit construction shows that every number \\(k\\) from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\) can indeed be attained.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
