 say \\(x=1\\), which is not sunny. This extra line does not destroy the covering property because all points are already covered; it merely satisfies the requirement that the total number of lines be exactly \\(n\\).\n\n   - Counting sunny lines we have exactly the prescribed \\(k\\) (the \\(k\\) lines \\(s_b\\) for \\(b\\in B\\)).  \n\n   Therefore for every \\(k\\le n-1\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) together with the explicit construction for each admissible \\(k\\) shows that the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines all of which are sunny and which covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) consider the **column**\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\subset\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belong to exactly one column.  \nIf a line is sunny, it cannot be vertical; consequently it can contain **at most one** point of a given column, because two distinct points of the same column would determine a vertical line.\n\nThus each sunny line contributes at most one point to each column, and with \\(n\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(n\\) points of any fixed column.  \nBut the column \\(C_1\\) contains \\(n\\) points, the column \\(C_2\\) contains \\(n-1\\) points, etc. In particular the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=1}^{n} n = n^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) (the inequality is strict for \\(n\\ge 4\\) and equality holds only for \\(n=3\\)), the required covering cannot be achieved with \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nHence any admissible configuration must contain at least one non‑sunny line, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nWe shall build \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny (vertical) lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad |A|=n-k-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(a\\in A\\) take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\in A\\) lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell_a\\); therefore all columns whose abscissa belongs to \\(A\\) are completely covered.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines  \n\nThe remaining columns are those with abscissae  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{1,2,\\dots ,k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(b\\in B\\) (with \\(b\\ge 2\\)) consider the point  \n\n\\[\nP_b=(b,\\;n+1-b)\\in D_{n+1},\n\\]\n\nthe right‑most point of column \\(b\\).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\ns_b:\\;\\text{the line through }(1,1)\\text{ and }P_b .\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = \\frac{n-b}{b-1}\\,(x-1),\n\\qquad\\text{so its slope }m_b=\\frac{n-b}{b-1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\le k+1\\le n\\), we have \\(0<m_b<\\infty\\) and \\(m_b\\neq -1\\); also \\(m_b\\neq 0\\) because \\(n-b>0\\). Hence each \\(s_b\\) is sunny.\n\nThe line \\(s_b\\) contains the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\(P_b\\); consequently it covers the whole column \\(b\\) at the single point \\(P_b\\).  \nAll points of columns indexed by \\(B\\) except possibly \\((1,1)\\) are now covered.\n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\) itself lies on every line \\(s_b\\) (they all pass through it), so it is certainly covered.\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{s_b\\mid b\\in B\\}\\) together cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose abscissa lies in \\(B\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3  Adding the final line  \n\nSo far we have used  \n\n\\[\n|A|+(|B|)= (n-k-1)+k = n-1\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  \nTo reach the required total of \\(n\\) lines we simply add one more vertical line, for instance \\(x=1\\).  \nThis extra line is not sunny and does not remove any of the already covered points.\n\nConsequently we have produced exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{s_b\\mid b\\in B\\}\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.4  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=0\\) the set \\(A\\) equals \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the construction reduces to the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\), none of which is sunny. This agrees with the general scheme.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* No configuration can have \\(k=n\\) sunny lines (Section 1), hence \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (Section 2).\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]"
