            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   C_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\;(1\\le a\\le n).\n   \\]\n   \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) and \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *A sunny line is never vertical, never horizontal and never of slope \\(-1\\).*  \n   Consequently a sunny line meets any fixed column in **at most one** point.  \n   The same property holds for a horizontal line and for a line of slope \\(-1\\); only a vertical line can contain more than one point of a column.\n\n   Let a configuration contain \\(t\\) vertical lines and \\(s\\) non‑vertical, non‑sunny lines (horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   The remaining \\(k=n-t-s\\) lines are sunny.  \n   All columns whose abscissa is not among the \\(t\\) chosen vertical ones must be covered by the \\(k+s\\) non‑vertical lines, and each such column can receive **at most one** point from each of those lines.  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be supplied to the non‑vertical columns is at most \\((k+s)(n-t)\\).\n\n   The set of points that lie in the non‑vertical columns is\n   \\[\n   N(t)=\\sum_{a=t+1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{(n-t)(n-t+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Feasibility therefore requires\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-t)(n-t+1)}2\\le (k+s)(n-t).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k+s=n-t\\) (the total number of non‑vertical lines), inequality (1) becomes\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n-t+1}{2}\\le n-t\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad n-t\\ge 2 .\n   \\]\n   Thus at least two columns must be made vertical, i.e. \\(t\\le n-2\\).  \n   Since \\(k=n-t-s\\le n-t\\), we obtain the universal bound  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence no configuration can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).**  \n   Fix such a \\(k\\) and put  \n\n   \\[\n   t=n-k\\ (\\ge 2),\\qquad s=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Vertical lines.*  \n   Take the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n   \\tag{V}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover completely the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_t\\).\n\n   *Non‑vertical lines.*  \n   The remaining columns are \\(C_{t+1},\\dots ,C_n\\); there are \\(n-t=k\\) of them.  \n   For each \\(a\\in\\{t+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_a:\\text{ the line through }(a,n+1-a)\\text{ and }(t+1,n-t).\n   \\tag{L}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_a\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_a=\\frac{(n+1-a)-(n-t)}{a-(t+1)}=\\frac{t+1-a}{a-t-1},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a finite non‑zero number different from \\(-1\\); therefore each\n   \\(\\ell_a\\) is **sunny**.\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_a\\) contains exactly the two points\n   \\((a,n+1-a)\\) (the top point of column \\(C_a\\)) and \\((t+1,n-t)\\) (the top\n   point of column \\(C_{t+1}\\)).  Consequently:\n\n   * column \\(C_a\\) receives the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) from \\(\\ell_a\\);\n   * all other points of \\(C_a\\) lie **below** \\((a,n+1-a)\\) and are already\n     covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which belongs to the set (V).\n\n   Thus every point of every column \\(C_{t+1},\\dots ,C_n\\) is covered, and\n   all points of the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_t\\) are covered by the vertical\n   lines.  The family consists of the \\(t\\) vertical lines (non‑sunny) and the\n   \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_a\\); altogether we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n   The construction works for every admissible \\(k\\); the extreme cases are  \n\n   * \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\);  \n   * \\(k=n-2\\): take \\(t=2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2\\) and the sunny lines\n     \\(\\ell_a\\) for \\(a=3,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (2) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny\n   lines, while the explicit construction above shows that every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  Hence the set of all possible\n   numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
