ing condition of the problem and such that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, where \\(k=n\\) or \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is sunny, then \\(L\\) contains **at most one** point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose that a sunny line \\(L\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S\\).  \nBecause the points are distinct, \\((a_1,b_1)\\neq(a_2,b_2)\\).  \nThe slope of \\(L\\) is\n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nBoth numerator and denominator are integers, and\n\n\\[\n|a_2-a_1|+|b_2-b_1|\n   =|(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)|\n   \\le (n+1)-(2)=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(|a_2-a_1|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|b_2-b_1|\\le n-1\\).  \nSince \\(a_i,b_i\\ge 1\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), the only possibilities for the integer ratio \\(\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) are\n\n\\[\n0,\\qquad \\infty\\;(a_2=a_1),\\qquad -1\\;(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)).\n\\]\n\nThus any line passing through two points of \\(S\\) must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nBecause a sunny line is defined precisely as a line **not** parallel to any of these three directions, a sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a line is not sunny (i.e., it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)), then it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(S\\) only for integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); for each such \\(c\\) the admissible \\(a\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), giving at most \\(n\\) points.  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(d\\le n+1\\); for each \\(d\\) there are exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\) integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n### Contradiction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, Lemma 1 implies that each line covers at most one point 