ne \\(x=n-1\\), and one diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\)—covers all of \\(S\\). By replacing any of these non‑sunny lines with a sunny line that passes through a still‑uncovered point we can obtain any prescribed number \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is sunny, then \\(|L\\cap S|\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that a sunny line \\(L\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S\\).  \nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1},\n\\]\n\nwhere the numerator and denominator are integers.  \nBecause \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S\\),\n\n\\[\n1\\le a_i,\\;b_i,\\qquad a_i+b_i\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|a_2-a_1|+|b_2-b_1|\n   =|(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)|\n   \\le (n+1)-2=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(|a_2-a_1|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|b_2-b_1|\\le n-1\\).  \nThe only integer ratios \\(\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) that can arise under these restrictions are  \n\n\\[\n0\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\n\\infty\\;(a_2=a_1,\\text{vertical}),\\qquad\n-1\\;(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1),\\text{diagonal }x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nThus any line through two points of \\(S\\) must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, contradicting the assumption that \\(L\\) is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), then \\(|L\\cap S|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal*: \\(L\\) has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nSince \\(b=c\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a\\le n+1-c\\).  \nThus at most \\(n\\) integer pa