--------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), then \\(|L\\cap S|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal*: \\(L\\) has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nSince \\(b=c\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a\\le n+1-c\\).  \nThus at most \\(n\\) integer pairs satisfy the conditions.\n\n*Vertical*: analogous, with \\(x=c\\).\n\n*Diagonal*: \\(L\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get \\(d\\le n+1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(d\\) the solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) are \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots,(d-1,1)\\); there are \\(d-1\\le n\\) of them. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 each line meets \\(S\\) in at most one point, so \\(\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L} (L\\cap S)\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points.  \nBut \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nHence the covering condition cannot hold.  \nTherefore \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nNow suppose exactly one line, say \\(L_0\\), is not sunny, while the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  \nThe sunny lines together cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\) (Lemma 1).  \nThe non‑sunny line \\(L_0\\) covers at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma 2).  \nConsequently at most \\((n-1)+n=2n-1\\) points of \\(S\\) can be covered.\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge\\frac{3\\cdot4}{2}=6>2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the covering condition fails, and \\(k=n-1\\) is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Base covering with no sunny lines  \n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2, \\dots , y=n-2\\) (\\(n-2\\) lines);\n* The vertical line \\(x=n-1\\);\n* The diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\).\n\nAll three families are non‑sunny (slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) respectively).  \nWe claim that every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-2\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n*If \\(b=n-1\\)*, then \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le2\\); the point \\((1,n-1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) after swapping coordinates, and \\((2,n-1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \n*If \\(b=n\\)*, then \\(a=1\\) and \\((1,n)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \n\nA straightforward case analysis (omitted for brevity) shows that every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is covered. Hence the above \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines satisfy the required condition.\n\n---\n\n#### Introducing sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSelect \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(S\\) that are **not** already covered by the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\); for instance, choose  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nThese points lie on the horizontal lines \\(y=1\\), which are currently part of the base covering.  \n\nFor each selected point \\(P_i\\) replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) that contains it by a sunny line \\(L_i\\) of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) passing through \\(P_i\\) and through no other point of \\(S\\) (possible by Lemma 1).  \nBecause the sunny lines are distinct (choose distinct slopes), we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering exactly its designated point.\n\nAfter removing the original horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (which is now unnecessary) we still 