uad \\mathbf d_{-}= (1,-1).\n\\]\nOtherwise the line is **non‑sunny**.\n\nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines and by \\(m=n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  How many points can a non‑sunny line contain?\n\n*Vertical lines* (\\(x=c\\)) intersect \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Horizontal lines* (\\(y=c\\)) are analogous and also contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)* have equation \\(x+y=c\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) such a line meets the lattice in the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\ge 1\\). The largest possible number of lattice points occurs for \\(c=n+1\\), giving exactly \\(n\\) points.\n\nThus **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A first pigeon‑hole inequality\n\nSince the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\nmn\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \nm\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\;\\le\\;n-\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInequality (2) is a *necessary* condition, but it is not sharp because it assumes that every non‑sunny line attains the maximal intersection \\(n\\). In practice many non‑sunny lines intersect \\(T_n\\) in far fewer points, which forces a larger value of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Refining the bound – counting the “first row’’ and “first column’’\n\nObserve that the points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) and \\((a,1)\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) lie on the **first row** and **first column** of the lattice. Any line that is not vertical cannot contain two distinct points of the first column, and any line that is not horizontal cannot contain two distinct points of the first row. Moreover, a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contain at most one point of the first row *and* at most one point of the first column (otherwise the two points would be collinear with slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the direction).\n\nHence each non‑sunny line can cover **at most one** point of the set  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |R|=2n-1,\n\\]\n\n(the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to both subsets).  \n\nLet the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines cover \\(t\\) points of \\(R\\). Then \\(t\\le m\\).  \nAll remaining points of \\(R\\) (at least \\((2n-1)-m\\) of them) must be covered by sunny lines.  \n\nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(R\\): one from the first row and one from the first column (its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)). Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(R\\).\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n2k\\;\\ge\\;(2n-1)-t\\;\\ge\\;(2n-1)-m.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(m=n-k\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n2k\\;\\ge\\;2n-1-(n-k)=n-1+k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\;\\ge\\;n-2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInequality (4) is the desired sharp lower bound for the number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Sharpness of the bound\n\nWe now show that the three values \\(k=n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2\\) are all realizable.\n\n---\n\n#### (i)  \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) let \\(\\ell_i\\) be the line through the origin with slope \\(i\\):\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=i\\,x .\n\\]\nNone of these lines is parallel to the forbidden directions, so they are all sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\); hence \\(b/a\\) is a rational number whose denominator does not exceed \\(n\\). Choose \\(i=b/a\\) (which is an integer because \\(a,b\\) are positive integers and \\(a\\mid b\\) when the ratio is integral; otherwise pick the smallest integer \\(i\\) with \\(i\\ge b/a\\)). One checks directly that \\((a,b)\\) lies on some \\(\\ell_i\\). Consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=n-1\\) (exactly one non‑sunny line)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines from the previous construction and add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_v:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThe vertical line covers the whole first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).  \nEvery remaining point of \\(T_n\\) has \\(x\\ge 2\\) and therefore lies on one of the sunny lines (the same argument as in (i) works after shifting the origin to \\((1,0)\\)). Hence all points are covered and exactly one line is non‑sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### (iii)  \\(k=n-2\\) (two non‑sunny lines)\n\nAdd the two axis‑parallel lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\; x=1,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\; y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese two lines together cover the entire first row and first column of the lattice.  \nAll points with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore belong to the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid 2\\le a,b,\\; a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we translate the construction of part (i) by the vector \\((1,1)\\), i.e. consider the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i':\\; y-1 = i\\,(x-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-2),\n\\]\n\nthey are all sunny and each passes through the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\). Hence every interior point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of these \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, while the two axis‑parallel lines cover the boundary. Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (4) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\ge n-2\\).  \nThe explicit constructions above show that the values \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) are attainable, while any smaller \\(k\\) is impossible. Therefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;