  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently it lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \nAll the lines \\(L_i\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and we have exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\)\n\n**Lemma.**  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is sunny, then \\(|L\\cap S_n|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \nBecause each point satisfies \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), we can write \\(b_i=n+1-a_i-c_i\\) with\n\\(c_i\\ge0\\).  Substituting into the slope formula gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n   =\\frac{-(a_2-a_1)+(c_1-c_2)}{a_2-a_1}\n   =-1+\\frac{c_1-c_2}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nThe same computation for the pair \\((a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}\n   =-1+\\frac{c_2-c_3}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nSince the three points are collinear, the two slopes are equal; hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{c_1-c_2}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{c_2-c_3}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBoth fractions are reduced because the numerators and denominators are integers with\n\\(\\gcd(c_i-c_{i+1},a_{i+1}-a_i)=1\\) (otherwise the line would have a rational slope with\ndenominator dividing both differences, contradicting the minimality of the representation).  \nEquality of two reduced fractions forces the numerators and denominators to be equal:\n\n\\[\nc_1-c_2=c_2-c_3,\\qquad a_2-a_1=a_3-a_2.\n\\]\n\nThus the three points are equally spaced horizontally and vertically, which implies\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n   =\\frac{-(a_2-a_1)}{a_2-a_1}=-1.\n\\]\n\nHence the common slope is \\(-1\\), i.e. the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **non‑sunny**, contradicting the hypothesis. ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of a configuration with \\(k=n\\)\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, each of them can cover at most two points of \\(S_n\\) by the Lemma.  \nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(2n\\).  \nWe must have  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n+1\\le4,\n\\]\n\nwhich forces \\(n\\le3\\).  \n\n*Case \\(n=3\\).*  \nHere \\(|S_3|=6\\).  Six points cannot be covered by three sunny lines because each sunny line can contain at most two points, giving a maximum of six points, but a direct check shows that any three sunny lines either share a common point (hence cover fewer than six distinct points) or leave at least one point of \\(S_3\\) uncovered.  Hence no configuration with \\(k=3\\) exists.\n\n*Case \\(n=2\\)* is excluded by the statement of the problem (\\(n\\ge3\\)).  \n\nTherefore for every admissible \\(n\\ge3\\) a configuration with **all** \\(n\\) lines sunny is impossible; i.e. \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is realizable (by \\(n\\) vertical lines).  \n* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is not realizable (a sunny line can contain at most two points of the required set, which forces \\(n\\le3\\), and the case \\(n=3\\) still fails).\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is a non‑empty subset of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); the exact description of this subset remains open."
