-----------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).\nSince each point satisfies \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), write \\(b_i=n+1-a_i-c_i\\) with\n\\(c_i\\ge 0\\).  The slope between the first two points is\n\n\\[\nm_{12}= \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n      =\\frac{-(a_2-a_1)+(c_1-c_2)}{a_2-a_1}\n      =-1+\\frac{c_1-c_2}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly\n\n\\[\nm_{23}= -1+\\frac{c_2-c_3}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCollinearity forces \\(m_{12}=m_{23}\\); hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{c_1-c_2}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{c_2-c_3}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBoth fractions are reduced: if a common divisor \\(d>1\\) divided numerator\nand denominator of the left\u2011hand side, then the slope would be\n\\(-1+\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(q\\) divisible by \\(d\\), contradicting the minimal\ninteger representation of the slope of a line through lattice points.\nThus (4) implies equality of the reduced numerators and denominators:\n\n\\[\nc_1-c_2=c_2-c_3,\\qquad a_2-a_1=a_3-a_2.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the three points are equally spaced horizontally and vertically,\nwhich yields\n\n\\[\nm_{12}= \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n      =\\frac{-(a_2-a_1)}{a_2-a_1}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the common slope is \\(-1\\), i.e. the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and\ntherefore **non\u2011sunny**, contradicting the hypothesis. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n*Existence of \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so it lies on the line\n\\(x=a\\).  All these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*Non\u2011existence of \\(k=n\\).*  \nIf all \\(n