d_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf d_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf d_{-}=(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\},\\qquad m=n-k=\\#\\{\\text{non‑sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points that a non‑sunny line can contain\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); at most \\(n\\) points.  \n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) is analogous, also at most \\(n\\) points.  \n\n*Line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)* has equation \\(x+y=c\\). Inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) it contains the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\ge1\\). The maximal number occurs for \\(c=n+1\\) and equals \\(n\\).  \n\nHence **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A refined pigeon‑hole count\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |R|=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Claim 1.* A non‑sunny line contains **at most one** point of \\(R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is vertical, it can meet the first column in at most one point and cannot meet the first row at all.  \nIf the line is horizontal, the situation is symmetric.  \nIf the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), its slope is \\(-1\\); two distinct points of \\(R\\) would have coordinates \\((1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,1)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq1\\) and \\(a_2\\neq1\\). Their difference vector would be \\((a_2-1,1-b_1)\\), whose slope is \\((1-b_1)/(a_2-1)\\neq-1\\). Hence such a line cannot contain both points. ∎\n\n*Claim 2.* A sunny line contains **at most two** points of \\(R\\) (one from the first row and one from the first column).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to either axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\). Therefore it can intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\