ll_v\\) contains every point of the first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).  \n\nFor any remaining point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\ge2\\).  \nAs before, \\(i:=b-a+1\\) lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nTherefore all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Exactly two non‑sunny lines (\\(k=n-2\\))\n\nTake the two axis‑parallel lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\; x=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\; y=1 ,\n\\]\n\nboth non‑sunny, and the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nThe two axis‑parallel lines together contain the whole first row and first column of the lattice, i.e. all points of \\(R\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\). Then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b-a+1\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nSet \\(i:=b-a+1\\); this integer lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and satisfies  \n\n\\[\ni\\,a-(i-1)=b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\). Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and precisely two lines are non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nInequality (3) shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\ge n-2\\).  \nThe three explicit constructions demonstrate that the values  \n\n\\[\nk=n-2,\\qquad k=n-1,\\qquad k=n\n\\]\n\nare indeed attainable. No smaller value of \\(k\\) is possible, and larger values are impossible because \\(k\\le n\\) by definition.\n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
