ntains the \\(n\\) points \\((a,1),\\,1\\le a\\le n\\) and never meets the first column.\n\n* **Parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)**: equation \\(x+y=c\\).  \n  Among the points of \\(R\\) the only possibilities with sum \\(c\\) are \\((1,c-1)\\) or \\((c-1,1)\\); at most one of them belongs to \\(R\\). Hence such a line contains **at most one** point of \\(R\\).\n\nConsequently **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(R\\)**, and the bound \\(n\\) is attained only by the two axis‑parallel families.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points of \\(R\\) that a sunny line can contain\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to either axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\). Therefore it can intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point. Hence a sunny line contains **at most two** points of \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Counting argument\n\nThe \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(R\\).  \nThus at least  \n\n\\[\n|R|-mn=(2n-1)-mn\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(R\\) must be covered by sunny lines.  \nSince each sunny line can cover at most two points of \\(R\\),\n\n\\[\n2k\\;\\ge\\;(2n-1)-mn .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(m=n-k\\) into (4) yields\n\n\\[\n2k\\;\\ge\\;2n-1-(n-k)n\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2k\\;\\ge\\;n-1+k\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\;\\ge\\;n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus **any admissible set of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions attaining the bound\n\nWe now exhibit configurations for the three possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n#### 5.1  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(i\\) is different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); hence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny and the lines are distinct.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\),\n\n\\[\n1\\le b-a+1\\le n .\n\\]\n\nSet \\(i:=b-a+1\\). Then\n\n\\[\nb = i a-(i-1),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\). Therefore the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 5.2  Exactly one non‑sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nUse the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) from the previous paragraph and add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_v:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_v\\) is non‑sunny and contains the whole first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).  \nAny remaining point \\((a,b)\\) has \\(a\\ge2\\); for such a point \\(i=b-a+1\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\). Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered with exactly one non‑sunny line.\n\n#### 5.3  Exactly two non‑sunny lines (\\(k=n-2\\))\n\nTake the two axis‑parallel lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\; x=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\; y=1 ,\n\\]\n\nboth non‑sunny, and the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nThe two axis‑parallel lines together contain the whole set \\(R\\).  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b-a+1\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nSetting \\(i=b-a+1\\) gives \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\). Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and precisely two lines are non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (5) shows that no configuration can have fewer than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines. The three explicit constructions demonstrate that the values  \n\n\\[\nk=n-2,\\qquad k=n-1,\\qquad k=n\n\\]\n\nare indeed attainable. Consequently these are the only possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution 