ifferent from both \\(c_1\\) and \\(c_2\\); such a point is not on any of the three non‑sunny lines.  \n   As in Case 1, covering all such points would require more sunny lines than are available, a contradiction.\n\n   Hence three non‑sunny lines cannot exist; at most two are possible. ∎\n\n   From Lemma 5.1 we obtain the **lower bound**\n\n   \\[\n   k = n-\\text{(# non‑sunny lines)} \\;\\ge\\; n-2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Sharpness – explicit constructions for the three admissible values.**  \n\n   *All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(i\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\); thus every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) set \\(i=b-a+1\\) (which lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\)). Then \\(b=i a-(i-1)\\), so \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\). Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points.\n\n   *Exactly one non‑sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\)).*  \n   Use the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) above and add the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_v:\\; x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\ell_v\\) is non‑sunny and covers the whole first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).  \n   Any remaining point has \\(a\\ge2\\); for it \\(i=b-a+1\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and the point lies on \\(\\ell_i\\). Thus all points are covered with exactly one non‑sunny line.\n\n   *Exactly two non‑sunny lines (\\(k=n-2\\)).*  \n   Take the two axis‑parallel lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_x:\\; x=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\; y=1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   both non‑sunny, and the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-2).\n   \\]\n\n   The two axis‑parallel lines together contain the whole set \\(R\\).  \n   For any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(1\\le b-a+1\\le n-2\\); setting \\(i=b-a+1\\) yields \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and precisely two lines are non‑sunny.\n\n   These three constructions realise the values \\(k=n\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n   Lemma 5.1 gives the necessary lower bound \\(k\\ge n-2\\); inequality (2) gives the obvious upper bound \\(k\\le n\\). The explicit configurations show that the three intermediate values are attainable. Therefore the only possible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf d_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf d_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf d_{-}=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\},\\qquad m=n-k=\\#\\{\\text{non‑sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The set \\(R\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |R|=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(R\\subset T_n\\), every admissible family of lines must cover all points of \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points of \\(R\\) on a single line\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*\n\n*   Vertical line \\(x=c\\): it meets the first column only when \\(c=1\\), in which case it contains exactly the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\); otherwise it meets \\(R\\) in at most one point.\n*   Horizontal line \\(y=c\\): analogous, at most \\(n\\) points of \\(R\\) (the case \\(c=1\\) gives the \\(n\\) points \\((a,1)\\)).\n*   Diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\): among the points of \\(R\\) the only ones with sum \\(c\\) are \\((1,c-1)\\) or \\((c-1,1)\\); at most one of them belongs to \\(R\\).\n\nHence a non‑sunny line contains **at most \\(n\\) points of \\(R\\)**, and the bound \\(n\\) is attained only by the two axis‑parallel families.\n\n*Sunny lines.*\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to either axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it can intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point. Consequently a sunny line contains **at most two points of \\(R\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nThe \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(R\\).  \nThe remaining points, at least \\((2n-1)-mn\\), must be covered by sunny lines, each contributing at most two points. Thus\n\n\\[\n2k\\ge (2n-1)-mn .\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(m=n-k\\) gives\n\n\\[\n2k\\ge (2n-1)-n(n-k)\n\\Longrightarrow k(n-2)\\le (n-1)^2 .\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\((n-1)^2=n(n-2)+1\\), (3.2) yields the trivial inequality \\(k\\le n\\).  \nTherefore (3.1) does **not** provide a lower bound; a different argument is required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  At most two non‑sunny lines (lower bound)\n\n**Lemma 4.1.** In any admissible configuration at most two of the \\(n\\) lines can be non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume three non‑sunny lines exist. Each belongs to one of the families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy the pigeonhole principle, at least two of them lie in the same family.\n\n*If two are vertical* (say \\(x=c_1\\) and \\(x=c_2\\) with \\(c_1\\neq c_2\\)), the third line is either horizontal or diagonal.  \nConsider the point  \n\n\\[\nP=(\\max\\{c_1,c_2\\}+1,\\,2)\\in T_n .\n\\]\n\n\\(P\\) is not on either vertical line (its \\(x\\)-coordinate differs) and is not on a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\neq2\\) nor on a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\neq \\max\\{c_1,c_2\\}+3\\). Hence \\(P\\) is not covered by any of the three non‑sunny lines.  \n\nA sunny line can contain at most one point of the set  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge \\max\\{c_1,c_2\\}+1,\\; b\\ge 2\\},\n\\]\n\nbecause distinct points of \\(S\\) have distinct sums \\(a+b\\).  \nSince \\(|S|\\ge 2\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), covering all of \\(S\\) would require at least two sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that only \\(n-3\\) sunny lines are available.  \n\n*If two are horizontal* the argument is symmetric.\n\n*If two are diagonal* (say \\(x+y=c_1\\) and \\(x+y=c_2\\) with \\(c_1\\neq c_2\\)), the third line is vertical or horizontal.  \nChoose a point \\(Q\\in T_n\\) whose sum is different from both \\(c_1\\) and \\(c_2\\) and whose coordinates are larger than 1; such a point exists because the possible sums in \\(T_n\\) range from \\(2\\) up to \\(n+1\\).  \nAgain \\(Q\\) is not on any of the three non‑sunny lines, and the same counting argument as above shows that the remaining sunny lines are insufficient to cover all points of the region where both coordinates exceed 1.  \n\nAll possibilities lead to a contradiction; therefore three non‑sunny lines cannot occur. ∎\n\nFrom Lemma 4.1 we obtain the **lower bound**\n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\ge n-2 .\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions attaining the bounds\n\n*All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define \\(\\ell_i:\\; y=i x-(i-1)\\).  \nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(i\\neq0,-1\\), hence is sunny.  \nGiven \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), set \\(i=b-a+1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); then \\(b=i a-(i-1)\\), so \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\). Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Exactly one non‑sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\)).*  \nTake the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) above and add the vertical line \\(\\ell_v:x=1\\).  \n\\(\\ell_v\\) covers the whole first column; any point with \\(a\\ge2\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{b-a+1}\\). Hence all points are covered with precisely one non‑sunny line.\n\n*Exactly two non‑sunny lines (\\(k=n-2\\)).*  \nUse the two axis‑parallel lines \\(\\ell_x:x=1\\) and \\(\\ell_y:y=1\\) (both non‑sunny) together with the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \nThe two axis‑parallel lines contain the whole set \\(R\\).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(1\\le b-a+1\\le n-2\\); setting \\(i=b-a+1\\) gives \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\). Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly two lines are non‑sunny.\n\nThese three families show that the values \\(k=n\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n-2\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nCombining the lower bound \\(k\\ge n-2\\) (Lemma 4.1) with the obvious upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) and the explicit constructions, we conclude that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\;}.\n\\]"
