ts  \n\n   \\[\n   Q''=\\{(v+1,\\,c_1-v),\\;(v+2,\\,c_1-v-1),\\dots ,(v+t,\\,c_1-v-t+1)\\},\n   \\qquad t:=n-c_2-1\\ge 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(Q''\\) have distinct sums, so each sunny line can contain at most one\n   of them, while the three non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most two of them.\n   Consequently at least \\(t-2\\) sunny lines are needed, which is impossible for the\n   same reason as in case (i).  Hence three non\u2011sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n   All possibilities lead to a contradiction; therefore **at most two** of the \\(n\\)\n   lines can be non\u2011sunny.  \u220e\n\n   Lemma\u202f5.1 gives the lower bound (5.1).\n\n6. **Sharpness \u2013 explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) let \\(\\ell_i:\\; y=i\\,x-(i-1)\\).  \n   The slope \\(i\\neq0,-1\\), so each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) put \\(i=b-a+1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).\n\n   *Exactly one non\u2011sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\)).*  \n   Use the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) above and add the vertical line\n   \\(\\ell_v:x=1\\).  \\(\\ell_v\\) covers the whole first column; any point with\n   \\(a\\ge2\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{b-a+1}\\).\n\n   *Exactly two non\u2011sunny lines (\\(k=n-2\\)).*  \n   Take the two axis\u2011parallel lines \\(\\ell_x:x=1\\) and \\(\\ell_y:y=1\\) (both non\u2011sunny)\n   together with the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   The two axis\u2011parallel lines cover the whole set \\(R\\); any point with\n   \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b-a+1\\le n-2\\) and therefore lies on\n   \\(\\ell_{b-a+1}\\).\n\n   Thus the values \\(k=n\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n-2\\) are all attainable.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n   Lemma\u202f5.1 yields the necessary lower bound \\(k\\ge n-2\\); inequality\u202f(4.1) gives the\n   obvious upper bound \\(k\\le n\\).  The three explicit families show that the\n   intermediate valu