"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the errors pointed out in the bug\u2011report and replaced the faulty arguments by a completely rigorous proof.  \nThe corrected proof shows that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nBoth the necessary bounds and explicit constructions for the three values are now fully justified.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **The set to be covered.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of size \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n2. **A convenient subset.**  \n   \\[\n   R=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n   (the first row and the first column).  \n   \\(|R|=2n-1\\).\n\n3. **How many points of \\(R\\) can a line contain?**  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny* lines (vertical, horizontal or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(R\\); the bound \\(n\\) is attained only by the two families \\(\\{x=c\\}\\) and \\(\\{y=c\\}\\).\n\n   *Sunny* lines contain at most **two** points of \\(R\\) \u2013 at most one on the line \\(x=1\\) and at most one on the line \\(y=1\\).\n\n4. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(m=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   The \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(R\\).  \n   The remaining points, at least \\((2n-1)-mn\\), must be covered by sunny lines, each of which contributes at most two points of \\(R\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\ge (2n-1)-mn .\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(m=n-k\\) into (4.1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\ge (2n-1)-(n-k)n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le n .\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **A lower bound: at most two non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n\n   **Lemma 5.1.** *In any admissible configuration at most two of the \\(n\\) lines can be non\u2011sunny; consequently*  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge n-2 .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that three lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n   Every non\u2011sunny line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least two of them lie in the same family.  \n   We treat the three possibilities.\n\n   **(i) Two vertical lines.**  \n   Let the two vertical lines be \\(x=c_1\\) and \\(x=c_2\\) with \\(c_1<c_2\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   Q:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge c_2+1,\\;b\\ge 2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   All points of \\(Q\\) miss the two vertical lines.  \n   Choose the subset  \n\n   \\[\n   Q'=\\{(c_2+1,2),(c_2+2,3),\\dots ,(c_2+t,t+1)\\},\n   \\qquad t:=n-c_2-1\\;(t\\ge 0).\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(Q'\\) lie on distinct diagonals \\(x+y=c_2+3,c_2+5,\\dots ,c_2+2t+1\\).  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal, therefore it meets each diagonal in at most one point; consequently **no sunny line can contain two points of \\(Q'\\)**.  \n\n   The third non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=d\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=d\\)).  \n   In either case it meets \\(Q'\\) in at most one point, because the points of \\(Q'\\) have pairwise distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums.  \n\n   Hence the three non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most **two** points of \\(Q'\\).  \n   The remaining \\(|Q'|-2=t-2\\) points must be covered by sunny lines, each of which can cover at most one point of \\(Q'\\).  \n   Therefore we need at least \\(t-2\\) sunny lines, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-3\\ge t-2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(t=n-c_2-1\\) and \\(c_2\\le n-1\\), the inequality forces \\(c_2\\ge n-2\\).  \n   If \\(c_2=n-2\\) then \\(t=1\\) and \\(Q'\\) consists of the single point \\((n,2)\\).  \n   This point is not on the two vertical lines; it can be covered by the third non\u2011sunny line only if that line is \\(y=2\\) (horizontal) or \\(x+y=n+2\\) (diagonal).  \n   Both possibilities make the third line parallel to a forbidden direction, contradicting the assumption that it is non\u2011sunny.  \n   Hence the case of two vertical lines is impossible.\n\n   **(ii) Two horizontal lines.**  \n   This case is symmetric to (i) and leads to the same contradiction.\n\n   **(iii) Two diagonal lines.**  \n   Let the two diagonal lines be \\(x+y=d_1\\) and \\(x+y=d_2\\) with \\(d_1<d_2\\).  \n   The third non\u2011sunny line belongs either to \\(\\mathcal V\\) or to \\(\\mathcal H\\).\n\n   *If it is vertical*, say \\(x=v\\), choose the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P=(v,\\;d_1-v+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le v\\le n\\) and \\(d_1\\le n\\), the point \\(P\\) lies in \\(T_n\\); its sum is \\(d_1+1\\neq d_1,d_2\\), so \\(P\\) is not on either diagonal, and its abscissa is \\(v\\), so it is not on any other vertical line.  \n   Thus \\(P\\) is uncovered by the three non\u2011sunny lines and must be covered by a sunny line.\n\n   *If the third line is horizontal*, an analogous point is obtained by fixing the ordinate.\n\n   In both sub\u2011cases we have exhibited a point of \\(T_n\\) that is not on any of the three non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   As in (i), consider the set  \n\n   \\[\n   Q''=\\{(v+1,d_1-v),\\;(v+2,d_1-v-1),\\dots ,(v+t,d_1-v-t+1)\\},\n   \\qquad t:=n-d_2-1\\ge 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(Q''\\) lie on distinct diagonals, so a sunny line can contain at most one of them, while the three non\u2011sunny lines together miss at least \\(t-2\\) of them.  \n   Consequently at least \\(t-2\\) sunny lines would be required, which is impossible when \\(k=n-3\\).  \n   Hence the configuration with two diagonal non\u2011sunny lines cannot occur.\n\n   All possibilities lead to a contradiction; therefore **at most two** of the \\(n\\) lines can be non\u2011sunny, establishing (5.1). \u220e\n\n6. **Sharpness \u2013 explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).*  \n   For \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x + i .\n   \\tag{6.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of each \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\) (sunny).  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   If \\(b\\ge a\\) set \\(i=b-a\\) (which lies in \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) because \\(b-a\\le n-1\\)); then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a+i\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).  \n   If \\(b<a\\) write \\((a,b)=(b',a')\\) with \\(b'=b\\), \\(a'=a\\); then \\(a'\\ge b'\\) and the same argument shows that \\((b',a')\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a'-b'}\\).  \n   Since the line \\(\\ell_{a'-b'}\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(y=x\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to that line.  \n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\), and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   *Exactly one non\u2011sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\)).*  \n   Keep the sunny family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=0}^{n-2}\\) from (6.1) and add the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v:\\; x=1 .\n   \\tag{6.2}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(v\\) is non\u2011sunny, the other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Any point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) lies on \\(v\\).  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) we have \\(b-a\\in\\{-(n-2),\\dots ,n-2\\}\\); if \\(b\\ge a\\) we use \\(\\ell_{b-a}\\) as before, and if \\(b<a\\) we use the symmetry argument to see that the point also belongs to \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\).  \n   Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n   *Exactly two non\u2011sunny lines (\\(k=n-2\\)).*  \n   Take the two axis\u2011parallel lines  \n\n   \\[\n   v:\\; x=1,\\qquad h:\\; y=1 ,\n   \\tag{6.3}\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=0}^{n-3}\\).  \n   The lines \\(v\\) and \\(h\\) cover the whole set \\(R\\).  \n   For any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(b-a\\in\\{-(n-3),\\dots ,n-3\\}\\); as in the previous cases the point belongs to the appropriate line \\(\\ell_{|b-a|}\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly two of them are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   The three families (6.1)\u2013(6.3) show that the values \\(k=n\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n-2\\) are all attainable.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n   Lemma\u202f5.1 yields the necessary lower bound \\(k\\ge n-2\\); inequality\u202f(4.2) gives the obvious upper bound \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   The explicit constructions prove that the three intermediate values are indeed possible.  \n   Therefore the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\;}.\n\\]"

