 if that line is \\(y=2\\) (horizontal) or \\(x+y=n+2\\) (diagonal).  \n   Both possibilities make the third line parallel to a forbidden direction, contradicting the assumption that it is non‑sunny.  \n   Hence the case of two vertical lines is impossible.\n\n   **(ii) Two horizontal lines.**  \n   This case is symmetric to (i) and leads to the same contradiction.\n\n   **(iii) Two diagonal lines.**  \n   Let the two diagonal lines be \\(x+y=d_1\\) and \\(x+y=d_2\\) with \\(d_1<d_2\\).  \n   The third non‑sunny line belongs either to \\(\\mathcal V\\) or to \\(\\mathcal H\\).\n\n   *If it is vertical*, say \\(x=v\\), choose the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P=(v,\\;d_1-v+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le v\\le n\\) and \\(d_1\\le n\\), the point \\(P\\) lies in \\(T_n\\); its sum is \\(d_1+1\\neq d_1,d_2\\), so \\(P\\) is not on either diagonal, and its abscissa is \\(v\\), so it is not on any other vertical line.  \n   Thus \\(P\\) is uncovered by the three non‑sunny lines and must be covered by a sunny line.\n\n   *If the third line is horizontal*, an analogous point is obtained by fixing the ordinate.\n\n   In both sub‑cases we have exhibited a point of \\(T_n\\) that is not on any of the three non‑sunny lines.  \n   As in (i), consider the set  \n\n   \\[\n   Q''=\\{(v+1,d_1-v),\\;(v+2,d_1-v-1),\\dots ,(v+t,d_1-v-t+1)\\},\n   \\qquad t:=n-d_2-1\\ge 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(Q''\\) lie on distinct diagonals, so a sunny line can contain at most one of them, while the three non‑sunny lines together miss at least \\(t-2\\) of them.  \n   Consequently at least \\(t-2\\) sunny lines would be required, which is impossible when \\(k=n-3\\).  \n   Hence the configuration with two diagonal non‑sunny lines cannot occur.\n\n   All possibilities lead to a contradiction; therefore **at most two** of the \\(n\\) lines can be non‑sunny, establishing (5.1). ∎\n\n6. **Sharpness – explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).*  \n   For \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\)