ot contain two points of \\(R\\) that lie on the same axis\u2011parallel line, because that would force the line to be parallel to that axis\u2011parallel line, contradicting the definition of \u201csunny\u201d.\n\n3. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(m=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   The \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(R\\).  \n   Hence at least \\((2n-1)-mn\\) points of \\(R\\) must be covered by sunny lines.  \n   Each sunny line contributes at most two points of \\(R\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\;\\ge\\;(2n-1)-mn. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(m=n-k\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\ge (2n-1)-(n-k)n\n   \\Longrightarrow 2k\\ge 2n-1-n^{2}+kn\n   \\Longrightarrow kn-2k\\le n^{2}-2n+1\n   \\Longrightarrow k(n-2)\\le (n-1)^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(n-2>0\\), and dividing yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le \\frac{(n-1)^{2}}{\\,n-2\\,}=n+\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}\\le n+1.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\) is an integer and \\(k\\le n\\) is already forced by the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines), we obtain the clean upper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma (at most two non\u2011sunny lines).**  \n\n   **Lemma 4.1.** *In any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) there are at most two non\u2011sunny lines; equivalently \\(k\\ge n-2\\).*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose, to the contrary, that three lines are non\u2011sunny.  Each non\u2011sunny line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least two of them lie in the same family.  We treat the three possibilities.\n\n   **(i) Two vertical lines.**  \n   Let the two vertical lines be \\(x=c_{1}\\) and \\(x=c_{2}\\) with \\(c_{1}<c_{2}\\).  \n   Consider 