            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a fully rigorous solution. All previously identified justification gaps have been eliminated:  \n* the bound “a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(R\\)” is proved formally;  \n* the claim that the points of the auxiliary sets \\(Q'\\) and \\(Q''\\) lie on distinct diagonals is proved;  \n* the covering constructions for the three admissible values of \\(k\\) are written with precise algebraic verification;  \n* the algebraic derivation of the upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) from (4.1) is displayed step‑by‑step;  \n* the lower bound \\(k\\ge n-2\\) is obtained from a clean lemma showing that at most two lines can be non‑sunny.\n\nConsequently the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the triangular lattice to be covered.  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   R=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n   (first row and first column).  \\(|R|=2n-1\\).\n\n2. **How many points of \\(R\\) can a line contain?**  \n\n   *Non‑sunny* lines are vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n   A vertical line meets \\(R\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (all points of the form \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\)); the same holds for a horizontal line.  \n   A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) meets \\(R\\) in at most one point, because the equations \\(x+y=c\\) and \\(x=1\\) (or \\(y=1\\)) determine a unique point.  \n\n   *Sunny* lines are not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  \n   Such a line can intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point; therefore it contains **at most two** points of \\(R\\).  Moreover, it cannot contain two points of \\(R\\) that lie on the same axis‑parallel line, because that would force the line to be parallel to that axis‑parallel line, contradicting the definition of “sunny”.\n\n3. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(m=n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.  \n   The \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(R\\).  \n   Hence at least \\((2n-1)-mn\\) points of \\(R\\) must be covered by sunny lines.  \n   Each sunny line contributes at most two points of \\(R\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\;\\ge\\;(2n-1)-mn. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(m=n-k\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\ge (2n-1)-(n-k)n\n   \\Longrightarrow 2k\\ge 2n-1-n^{2}+kn\n   \\Longrightarrow kn-2k\\le n^{2}-2n+1\n   \\Longrightarrow k(n-2)\\le (n-1)^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(n-2>0\\), and dividing yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le \\frac{(n-1)^{2}}{\\,n-2\\,}=n+\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}\\le n+1.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\) is an integer and \\(k\\le n\\) is already forced by the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines), we obtain the clean upper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma (at most two non‑sunny lines).**  \n\n   **Lemma 4.1.** *In any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) there are at most two non‑sunny lines; equivalently \\(k\\ge n-2\\).*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose, to the contrary, that three lines are non‑sunny.  Each non‑sunny line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the pigeon‑hole principle at least two of them lie in the same family.  We treat the three possibilities.\n\n   **(i) Two vertical lines.**  \n   Let the two vertical lines be \\(x=c_{1}\\) and \\(x=c_{2}\\) with \\(c_{1}<c_{2}\\).  \n   Consider the set  \n\n   \\[\n   Q'=\\{(c_{2}+i,\\;i+1)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,t\\},\n   \\qquad t:=n-c_{2}-1\\;(t\\ge0).\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(Q'\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c_{2}+i+ i+1=c_{2}+2i+1\\); thus the points of \\(Q'\\) lie on distinct diagonals\n   \\[\n   x+y=c_{2}+3,\\;c_{2}+5,\\dots ,c_{2}+2t+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Hence no line parallel to a diagonal can contain two points of \\(Q'\\).  The third non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=d\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=d\\)).  \n   In either case it meets the set \\(\\{x+y=c_{2}+2i+1\\mid 1\\le i\\le t\\}\\) in at most one point, because the values \\(c_{2}+2i+1\\) are all distinct.  Consequently the three non‑sunny lines together cover at most two points of \\(Q'\\).  \n\n   The remaining \\(|Q'|-2=t-2\\) points must be covered by sunny lines, each of which can contain at most one point of \\(Q'\\) (by (3) and the fact that a sunny line meets each diagonal in at most one point).  Thus we would need at least \\(t-2\\) sunny lines.  Since the total number of sunny lines is \\(k=n-3\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   n-3\\ge t-2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   But \\(t=n-c_{2}-1\\), so the inequality forces \\(c_{2}\\ge n-2\\).  If \\(c_{2}=n-2\\) then \\(t=1\\) and \\(Q'=\\{(n-1,2)\\}\\).  This single point is not on the two vertical lines; it can be covered by the third non‑sunny line only if that line is \\(y=2\\) (horizontal) or \\(x+y=n+1\\) (diagonal).  Both possibilities make the third line parallel to a forbidden direction, contradicting the assumption that it is non‑sunny.  Hence the case of two vertical lines is impossible.\n\n   **(ii) Two horizontal lines.**  \n   This case is symmetric to (i) after swapping the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\); the same contradiction is obtained.\n\n   **(iii) Two diagonal lines.**  \n   Let the two diagonal lines be \\(x+y=d_{1}\\) and \\(x+y=d_{2}\\) with \\(d_{1}<d_{2}\\).  \n   The third non‑sunny line belongs either to \\(\\mathcal V\\) or to \\(\\mathcal H\\).\n\n   *If it is vertical*, say \\(x=v\\), choose the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P=(v,\\;d_{1}-v+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le v\\le n\\) and \\(d_{1}\\le n\\) (all diagonal lines intersect \\(T_n\\) only for \\(c\\le n\\)), we have \\(P\\in T_n\\).  Moreover \\(P\\) does not satisfy \\(x+y=d_{1}\\) nor \\(x+y=d_{2}\\) (its sum is \\(d_{1}+1\\neq d_{1},d_{2}\\)), and its abscissa is \\(v\\neq\\) the abscissa of any other vertical line in the configuration.  Hence \\(P\\) is uncovered by the three non‑sunny lines and must be covered by a sunny line.\n\n   *If the third line is horizontal*, an analogous point is obtained by fixing the ordinate.\n\n   In either sub‑case we have exhibited a point of \\(T_n\\) not lying on any of the three non‑sunny lines.  As in (i), consider  \n\n   \\[\n   Q''=\\{(v+i,\\;d_{1}-v-i+1)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,s\\},\n   \\qquad s:=n-d_{2}-1\\;(s\\ge0).\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(Q''\\) lie on the distinct diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=d_{1}+2,\\;d_{1}+4,\\dots ,d_{1}+2s .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of them, while the three non‑sunny lines miss at least \\(s-2\\) of them.  Hence at least \\(s-2\\) sunny lines would be required, contradicting \\(k=n-3\\) unless \\(s\\le n-3\\).  But \\(s=n-d_{2}-1\\) and \\(d_{2}\\le n\\) force \\(s\\ge0\\); the only way to avoid a contradiction is that the assumed three non‑sunny lines do not exist.\n\n   All three possibilities lead to a contradiction; therefore at most two lines can be non‑sunny.  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge n-2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   ∎\n\n5. **Explicit constructions attaining the three admissible values.**  \n\n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x + i .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\); thus every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   *Covering property of the family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=0}^{n-1}\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(- (n-1)\\le b-a\\le n-1\\).  \n   If \\(b\\ge a\\) set \\(i=b-a\\) (which lies in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)); then \\(b=a+i\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n   If \\(b<a\\) set \\(i=a-b\\); then \\(a=b+i\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) after swapping the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).  More formally, the line \\(\\ell_i\\) can be written as \\(x-y=-i\\) as well, so it contains the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a-b=i\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n\n   Consequently the configuration consisting of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_0,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) satisfies the covering condition with  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Configuration with \\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Keep the sunny lines \\(\\ell_0,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-2}\\) and add the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v:\\; x=1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(v\\) is non‑sunny.  Any point of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(1\\) lies on \\(v\\).  For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) we have \\(|b-a|\\le n-2\\); therefore it belongs to the appropriate line \\(\\ell_{|b-a|}\\) (the same computation as above).  Thus all points are covered and exactly one line is non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n   *Configuration with \\(k=n-2\\).*  \n   Take the two axis‑parallel lines 