longs to the family.\nIf it is absent we replace any line of slope \\(0\\) (which is always present\nin the constructions of Lemma 2 and is preserved by the induction) by\n\\(x=1\\); this does not change the count of sunny lines.\n\nNow the point \\(P=(1,n)\\in L_n\\) is already covered by the line \\(x=1\\).\n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\text{s}}:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac{n+2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(-\\frac12\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{\\text{s}}\\)\nis sunny.  For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a,\\; -\\tfrac12 a + \\tfrac{n+2}{2}\\bigr)\n   = (a,\\; n+1-a)\\in L_n .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_{\\text{s}}\\) contains all points of \\(L_n\\) except the point\n\\(P=(1,n)\\), which is already covered by \\(x=1\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n^{(k+1)}:=\\mathcal L_{n-1}^{(k)}\\cup\\{\\ell_{\\text{s}}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family covers \\(T_n\\); the added line is distinct from all previous ones\n(because its slope \\(-\\frac12\\) does not appear among the slopes of\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}^{(k)}\\)), and the number of sunny lines is exactly\n\\(k+1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Completion of the induction\n\nFrom a configuration for \\(n-1\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines we have produced\nconfigurations for \\(n\\) with **both** \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\nStarting with the base families for \\(n=3\\) (Lemma 2), which realise every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\), repeated application of the two constructions yields,\nfor any \\(n\\ge 3\\), families with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every\ninteger \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nLemma 1 shows that no covering family of \\(n\\) lines can contain more than\n\\(n\\) sunny lines. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
