ring by the three non‑sunny families)\n\nDefine the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\), the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\). Similarly the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\). Moreover \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) and also contains \\((a,b)\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Maximum number of points a single non‑sunny line can cover)\n\nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) contains exactly \\(n-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\); a vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains exactly \\(n-c\\) points; a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(y=c\\) we need \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+c\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(1\\le a\\le n-c\\); thus there are \\(n-c\\) admissible \\(a\\). The other two families are analogous. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Upper bound for the case \\(k=n-1\\))\n\nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines. Then the remaining line must be non‑sunny. Such a configuration cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the unique non‑sunny line be \\(L\\). By Lemma 2 it covers at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Hence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together must cover the remaining  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints. Each sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(