construction exactly \\(k\\) of the chosen lines are sunny, the remaining \\(n-k\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) inclusive except possibly \\(k=n-1\\).  \nLemma 3 shows that the case \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Consequently the complete answer is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,\\;n\\; } .\n\\]"
