) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   - \\(k=0\\): take the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;x=1\\).  \n   - \\(k=1\\): take one sunny line, e.g. the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(0\\) \u2013 not sunny) is forbidden, so choose a sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)), together with the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\).  \n   Both choices satisfy the requirements, and \\(k=2\\) is excluded by the impossibility result.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\). Let \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) with \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n\n   - **Step 1 (border covering).**  \n     Include the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     L_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n     \\]\n     They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).\n\n   - **Step 2 (reduction to a smaller triangle).**  \n     The remaining uncovered points are those with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Subtracting \\(1\\) from each coordinate gives the set\n     \\[\n     T_{n-2}=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le (n-2)+1\\},\n     \\]\n     which is exactly the triangular lattice for the integer \\(n-2\\).\n\n   - **Step 3 (apply the induction hypothesis).**  \n     We need to cover \\(T_{n-2}\\) with the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines, of which exactly \\(k\\) must be sunny (the two border lines are non\u2011sunny). By the induction hypothesis, this is possible because \\(k\\le (n-2)-2\\) holds (since \\(k\\le n-2\\)). Hence we obtain a set \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n-2\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n-2}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   - **Step 4 (lift back to the original triangle).**  \n     Each line of \\(\\mathcal L'\\) can be written in the form\n     \\[\n     \\ell:\\;a'x+b'y=c\\quad (a',b',c\\in\\mathbb Z),\n     \\]\n     and the corresponding line\n     \\[\n     \\ell^{*}:\\;a'(x-1)+b'(y-1)=c\n     \\]\n     passes through the points \\((x,y)=(a'+1,b'+1)\\) of the original set \\(T_n\\). The collection\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L=\\{L_h,L_v\\}\\cup\\{\\ell^{*}\\mid \\ell\\in\\mathcal L'\\}\n     \\]\n     consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny (the sunny/non\u2011sunny nature is preserved under the translation \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x-1,y-1)\\)). By construction \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   This completes the inductive construction.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The impossibility lemmas rule out \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\); the inductive construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realized. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\ny=\\text{const},\\qquad x=\\text{const},\\qquad x+y=\\text{const},\n\\]\n\nand **sunny** otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Size of a non\u2011sunny line inside \\(T_n\\))\n\nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) contains exactly \\(n-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\); a vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains exactly \\(n-c\\) points; a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(y=c\\) we need \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+c\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(1\\le a\\le n-c\\); thus there are \\(n-c\\) admissible \\(a\\). The other two families are analogous. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\) then such a family does not exist.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWith \\(k=n-1\\) the remaining line is non\u2011sunny, hence by Lemma\u202f1 it covers at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Consequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-(n-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}2-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints. Each sunny line meets the triangle in at most \\(n-1\\) lattice points (a line of any other slope can intersect the triangle in at most one point per column, giving at most \\(n-1\\) points). Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most \\((n-1)(n-1)\\). We would need  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\\le (n-1)^2,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(n\\le 2\\), contradicting \\(n\\ge 3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*If \\(k=n\\) then such a family does not exist.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf all lines are sunny, none of them can be horizontal, vertical or diagonal. In particular no line can be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so the points with \\(b=1\\) (the bottom row of the triangle) must each lie on a distinct line. There are \\(n-1\\) such points, requiring at least \\(n-1\\) distinct lines. The remaining point \\((1,1)\\) would need a further line, giving at least \\(n\\) lines, but then the points with \\(a=1\\) (the leftmost column) would also each need a distinct line, forcing more than \\(n\\) lines. Hence a covering with only sunny lines is impossible. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Inductive construction)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) such that exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n\n- \\(k=0\\): take the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;x=1\\).  \n- \\(k=1\\): take the sunny line \\(L:\\;y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)), together with the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\).  \n\nBoth families consist of three distinct lines and cover all three points, establishing the claim for \\(n=3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\). Let \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. Include the two non\u2011sunny border lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).\n\n2. The uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\}\n   =\\{(a'+1,b'+1)\\mid (a',b')\\in T_{\\,n-2}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, after translating by \\((-1,-1)\\), we obtain exactly the set \\(T_{\\,n-2}\\).\n\n3. By the induction hypothesis applied to the integer \\(n-2\\), there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n-2\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{\\,n-2}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (note that \\(k\\le n-2-2\\) because \\(k\\le n-2\\)).\n\n4. Translate each line of \\(\\mathcal L'\\) back by the vector \\((1,1)\\).  \n   If a line of \\(\\mathcal L'\\) has equation \\(a'x+b'y=c\\), the translated line is  \n\n   \\[\n   a'(x-1)+b'(y-1)=c,\n   \\]\n\n   which we denote by \\(\\ell^{*}\\). The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_h,L_v\\}\\cup\\{\\ell^{*}\\mid \\ell\\in\\mathcal L'\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines (the translation does not create coincidences because the original lines were distinct). The sunny/non\u2011sunny nature of a line is preserved under translation, so exactly \\(k\\) of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n5. By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on \\(L_h\\), on \\(L_v\\), or on some \\(\\ell^{*}\\); hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n\\), completing the induction. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Result\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we