            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### Notation  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \n*The \\(n\\) lines*\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\frac{2i-1}{2}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n*are sunny, distinct, and their union contains \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nAll \\(S_i\\) have slope \\(\\frac12\\), which is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nDistinctness follows from the different \\(y\\)-intercepts \\(\\frac{2i-1}{2}\\).\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) satisfies \\(b=\\frac12 a+\\frac{2i-1}{2}\\) for some integer \\(i\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n2b = a + 2i-1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad i = \\frac{2b-a+1}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\) are positive integers, the right‑hand side of (1) is an integer exactly when \\(a\\) and \\(2b+1\\) have the same parity; in either case the value given by (1) lies in the interval \\([1,n]\\) because\n\n\\[\n1\\le i = \\frac{2b-a+1}{2}\\le \\frac{2b+(n+1-b)+1}{2}=n .\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) there exists an \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) satisfying (1), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in S_i\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \n*The \\(n\\) horizontal lines*\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n*are not sunny, distinct, and their union also contains \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nEach \\(H_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so its slope is \\(0\\); consequently it is not sunny.  \nDistinctness is obvious because the intercepts differ.\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)). Hence \\(b=j\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H_j\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine the family of lines\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = \\{\\,S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_k\\,\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{\\,H_1,H_2,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).*\n\n1. **Cardinality.**  \n   The two sub‑collections are disjoint (sunny lines have slope \\(\\frac12\\) whereas horizontal lines have slope \\(0\\)), so \\(|\\mathcal L_k| = k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n2. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   Exactly the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny; the horizontal lines are not. Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Covering condition.**  \n   By Lemma 1 the union \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k} S_i\\) already contains every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\). Adding the horizontal lines cannot remove points, therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_n \\subseteq \\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L_k} L .\n   \\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies both requirements of the problem.\n\nSince the construction works for every admissible \\(k\\), we have exhibited, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nThe definition of a sunny line is independent of the particular configuration; a line is either sunny or not. Consequently, in any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative. Hence the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) contains **all** possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
