issible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer satisfying (6) and put \\(m=n-k\\) (so \\(m\\ge\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\)).\n\n**Step\u202f1 \u2013 horizontal lines.**  \nTake the \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=m . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and \\(b\\le m\\) then the line \\(y=b\\) from (7) contains the point.\n\n**Step\u202f2 \u2013 vertical lines for the remaining points.**  \nIf \\(b>m\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(m+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence such a point lies on one of the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=k . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe lines in (7) and (8) are all distinct; together they give exactly \\(m+k=n\\) lines and they cover every point of \\(S_n\\).  \nAll of them are non\u2011sunny, so this construction shows that \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n**Step\u202f3 \u2013 inserting sunny lines.**  \nTo obtain a configuration with a prescribed positive \\(k\\) (still respecting (6)), replace the \\(k\\) vertical lines in (8) by any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that pass through the same sets of lattice points.  \nFor instance, for each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) replace the line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ny=i\\,x \\qquad(\\text{slope }i\\neq0,\\infty,-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause the original vertical line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\), the new line \\(y=i x\\) contains the same integer points (they satisfy \\(b=i a\\) with \\(a=i\\)). Thus the covering property is preserved, the total number of lines remains \\(n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nTherefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (6) and the explicit construction together prove that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\