"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution to the problem. However, I have proved a necessary condition on the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines that can appear in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \nThe proof proceeds by a combinatorial counting argument.  \n\n* I first observe that a line which is not sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) of the required lattice points, because such a line lies entirely on a single row, column, or anti\u2011diagonal of the triangular lattice \\(\\{(a,b):a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n\n* A sunny line, having a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), can intersect each of the \\(n\\) rows, each of the \\(n\\) columns and each of the \\(n\\) anti\u2011diagonals at most once; consequently a sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) of the required points as well, but the pattern of its intersections is more flexible.  \n\n* Let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines. Counting the total number of incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) lattice points gives the inequality  \n  \\[\n  s\\cdot n + t\\cdot n \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n  \\]\n  i.e. \\(n^{2}\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), which is always true.  \n\n* A sharper bound is obtained by noting that each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one whole \u201clayer\u2019\u2019 of the triangle (a set of points with constant \\(a+b\\)). There are exactly \\(n\\) such layers, so at most \\(t\\) of them can be completely covered by non\u2011sunny lines. The remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-t\\) points must be covered by sunny lines, each of which can contribute at most one point from each uncovered layer. Hence we need  \n  \\[\n  s\\ge \\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-t}{n}= \\frac{n+1}{2}-\\frac{t}{n}.\n  \\]  \n  Since \\(s\\) and \\(t\\) are integers with \\(s+t=n\\), this inequality forces  \n  \\[\n  s\\ge \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n  \\]  \n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) sunny lines. Consequently the set of possible values of \\(k\\) (the number of sunny lines) is contained in  \n\\[\n\\Big\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\,\\Big\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe construction of configurations attaining each integer \\(k\\) in this interval remains open; therefore the problem is not completely solved.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nDenote by  \n\n* **non\u2011sunny lines** \u2013 lines parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\);  \n* **sunny lines** \u2013 all other lines.\n\nSuppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, and let  \n\n\\[\ns=\\text{number of sunny lines},\\qquad t=n-s=\\text{number of non\u2011sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Incidence bound for a single line\n\nFor any line \\(\\ell\\) (sunny or not) consider its intersection with the triangular region \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is non\u2011sunny.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) it meets exactly the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points.  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is vertical (\\(x=c\\)) the same bound holds.  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)) then the points on \\(\\ell\\) inside \\(T_n\\) are those with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\); again at most \\(n\\) points occur.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.*  \nA sunny line has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). Hence it meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) in at most one point, each vertical line \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) in at most one point, and each anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) in at most one point. Consequently a sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) as well.\n\nThus **every line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences** with the set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Counting incidences\n\nThe total number of points in \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(I\\) be the total number of incidences (point\u2013line pairs) in the configuration.  \nSince each point must lie on at least one line, we have  \n\n\\[\nI\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, each of the \\(n\\) lines contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences, so  \n\n\\[\nI\\le n\\cdot n=n^{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInequalities (1) and (2) are compatible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\); they do not yet restrict \\(s\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Using the special structure of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nThe non\u2011sunny lines are aligned with the three families of parallel lines that partition the triangle \\(T_n\\):\n\n* horizontal families \\(\\{y=b\\}\\) for \\(b=1,\\dots ,n\\);\n* vertical families \\(\\{x=a\\}\\) for \\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\);\n* anti\u2011diagonal families \\(\\{x+y=c\\}\\) for \\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\).\n\nEach such family consists of a *layer* of points having a constant value of \\(b\\), \\(a\\), or \\(a+b\\).  \nA non\u2011sunny line can cover **all** points of at most one layer, because all points of a given layer share the same direction.\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) distinct layers (the values \\(a+b=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  \nHence at most \\(t\\) layers can be completely covered by the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-t\\cdot n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-tn\n\\]\n\npoints remain uncovered by non\u2011sunny lines and must be covered by sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Capacity of a sunny line across uncovered layers\n\nConsider a sunny line \\(\\ell\\). Because its slope is different from \\(-1\\), \\(\\ell\\) meets each anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point. Therefore, from each *uncovered* layer \\(\\{x+y=c\\}\\) the line can contribute at most one new point. Since there are \\(n\\) layers in total, a sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of the uncovered set, but more precisely **at most one point per uncovered layer**.\n\nThus, to cover the \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-tn\\) uncovered points we need at least  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge \\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-tn}{n}= \\frac{n+1}{2}-t.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(s+t=n\\). Substituting \\(t=n-s\\) into (3) yields  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge \\frac{n+1}{2}-(n-s)=\\frac{n+1}{2}-n+s,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge \\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer, we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;s\\ge \\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\; }.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Consequence for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nIn the statement of the problem the integer \\(k\\) denotes the number of sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=s\\).  \nFrom (4) we conclude that any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\big\\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\; }.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is contained in the interval\n\\(\\{\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil,\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Remarks on sufficiency\n\nThe argument above shows that **no configuration can have fewer than \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) sunny lines**.  \nWhether every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil,n]\\) can actually be realized by an explicit arrangement of \\(n\\) distinct lines remains open. Constructing such arrangements (or proving that some values are impossible) would complete the solution of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nWe have proved a necessary lower bound on the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration:\n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nHence the only candidates for \\(k\\) are the integers between \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) and \\(n\\). Determining which of these candidates are actually attainable is left as a further problem."
