            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three directions\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{anti‑diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}),\n\\]\nand **sunny** otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Incidence bound)\n\nEvery line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the points of \\(T_n\\) on it are \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  \nThe vertical and anti‑diagonal cases are analogous.  \n\nIf the line is sunny, its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it intersects each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) in at most one point, each vertical line \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) in at most one point, and each anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) in at most one point. Hence it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Necessary number of sunny lines)\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf \\(s\\) of the lines are sunny and \\(t=n-s\\) are non‑sunny, then  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑sunny line lies entirely in a single family of parallel lines, thus it can cover **all** points of at most one “layer’’ of \\(T_n\\), where a layer is a set of points with a fixed value of \\(a+b\\).  \nThere are exactly \\(n\\) layers (the values \\(a+b=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)). Consequently at most \\(t\\) layers can be completely covered by the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n\nHence at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-t\\cdot n\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-t\\,n\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints remain uncovered by non‑sunny lines.  \n\nA sunny line can meet each layer in at most one point (different slopes guarantee at most one intersection with a given anti‑diagonal). Therefore the number of sunny lines needed to cover the points in (3) satisfies  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge\\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}2-t\\,n}{n}= \\frac{n+1}{2}-t .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t=n-s\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}-(n-s)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ns\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\) is integral,  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPut \\(t=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines).  \n\n**Non‑sunny part.**  \nChoose the \\(t\\) anti‑diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; x+y = 2+i-1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,t).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) consists precisely of the points of the layer \\(a+b= i+1\\).  \nThus the first \\(t\\) layers of \\(T_n\\) are completely covered.\n\n**Remaining region.**  \nAfter deleting those layers, the uncovered points form  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-t}= \