gned}\n\\]\n\n(The first inequality uses \\(a\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\); the second uses \\(a\\ge\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) when necessary. A direct case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\) shows that the bounds are exactly the endpoints of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\).)  \nThus \\(d\\in\\mathcal D_n\\), and the point satisfies \\(b = a + d\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(S_d\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A non‑sunny covering family  \n\nConsider the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence not sunny, and the lines are distinct.\n\n#### Lemma 2  \n*The union \\(\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n} V_j\\) contains \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) because \\(a\\le n\\) follows from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\). Hence the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall construct a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones and still covering \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n#### 4.1  Choice of the sunny lines  \n\nWrite \\(m:=n-k\\).  \nWe will keep the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\) (these are not sunny).  \nFor the remaining \\(k\\) values of \\(x\\) (namely \\(x=m+1,\\dots ,n\\)) we need sunny lines that cover all points whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is in this range.\n\nFor each integer \\(x\\) with \\(m+1\\le x\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nd_x := (n+1-2x).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nObserve that \\(d_x\\) is an integer and, because \\(x\\ge m+1 = n-k+1\\),\n\n\\[\nd_x \\ge n+1-2n = - (n-1) \\ge -\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\qquad\nd_x \\le n+1-2(m+1)= n+1-2(n-k+1)=2k-n-1\\le\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nHence each \\(d_x\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D_n\\).  \nMoreover the numbers \\(d_{m+1},\\dots ,d_n\\) are **pairwise distinct** because the map \\(x\\mapsto n+1-2x\\) is strictly decreasing.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_k:=\\{\\,S_{d_x}\\mid x=m+1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal S_k|=k\\) and all \\(S_{d_x}\\) are sunny, \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) supplies exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.2  Definition of the final family  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\mathcal L_k \\;:=\\; \\bigl\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\bigr\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\mathcal S_k } .\n\\]\n\nThe two sub‑collections are disjoint (vertical lines have undefined slope, sunny lines have slope \\(1\\)), so \\(|\\mathcal L_k| = m + k = n\\).  \nExactly the lines in \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) are sunny, giving precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.3  Verification of the covering condition  \n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le m\\)*, then the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge m+1\\)*, then by definition of \\(d_a\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb \\le n+1-a \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b-a \\le n+1-2a = d_a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a\\ge m+1\\), the only possible value of \\(b-a\\) that can occur for a point with this \\(a\\) is exactly \\(d_a\\); indeed, the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b = n+1-a\\). Consequently \\(b-a = d_a\\), and the point satisfies \\(b = a + d_a\\), i.e. it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{d_a}\\in\\mathcal S_k\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Exhaustiveness of the range of \\(k\\)\n\nIn any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  \nThe construction above shows that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  \nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
