vertical line \\(x=1\\) by the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\{\\,y=x\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=2,\\,x=3,\\dots ,x=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe only point of column\u202f1 in \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) is \\((1,1)\\), which lies on \\(y=x\\); all other points lie on the unchanged vertical lines.  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) covers \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n#### Lemma\u202f4 (\\(k=2\\))\n\nReplace the vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\) by the sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=x+1\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_2=\\{\\,y=x,\\;y=x+1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=3,x=4,\\dots ,x=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of the two smallest columns are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\qquad (2,1),\\;(2,2).\n\\]\n\nWe have \\((1,1)\\in y=x\\), \\((2,1)\\in y=x+1\\) and \\((2,2)\\in y=x\\); all remaining columns are still covered by their vertical lines.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_2\\) covers \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1 proves that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain more than two sunny lines while covering all points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  \nLemmas\u202f2,\u202f3 and\u202f4 provide explicit configurations with \\(k=0,1,2\\) sunny lines, respectively.  \n\nTherefore the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\; } .\n\\]"

