            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFix an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nC_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;i+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |C_i|=n+1-i .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|C_1|=n,\\;|C_2|=n-1,\\dots ,|C_{n-2}|=3,\\;|C_{n-1}|=2,\\;|C_n|=1 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n*Observation.*  \nIf a line is **not vertical**, then for each fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate it contains at most one point; consequently it meets each column \\(C_i\\) in at most one point.  \nA vertical line \\(x=i\\) meets the column \\(C_i\\) in all its points and meets every other column in none.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \n*In any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal T_n\\), the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le 2\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal N,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathcal V\\) is the set of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(\\mathcal N\\) is the set of non‑vertical lines (horizontal, slope \\(-1\\), or sunny).  \nDenote  \n\n\\[\nv=|\\mathcal V|,\\qquad s=|\\{L\\in\\mathcal N\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}| .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(v+s\\le n\\) and \\(k=s\\).\n\nFor each column \\(C_i\\) there are two possibilities.\n\n*Case 1 – the vertical line \\(x=i\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V\\).*  \nThen every point of \\(C_i\\) is already covered; no further requirement on \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Case 2 – the vertical line \\(x=i\\) is **absent** from \\(\\mathcal V\\).*  \nAll \\(|C_i|\\) points of \\(C_i\\) must be supplied by lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nBecause each line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) meets \\(C_i\\) in at most one point, we need at least \\(|C_i|\\) distinct lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\) to cover \\(C_i\\).\n\nConsequently, for every column whose vertical line is missing we must allocate at least \\(|C_i|\\) distinct non‑vertical lines.  \nSince a single non‑vertical line can contribute to **many** columns (one point per column), the most economical way to satisfy several such columns is to let the same non‑vertical line serve one point in each of them.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM=\\{i\\mid x=i\\notin\\mathcal V\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of indices of columns lacking their vertical line.  \nFor each \\(i\\in M\\) we need \\(|C_i|\\) “slots’’ in \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nSumming over all \\(i\\in M\\) we obtain the total number of required slots:\n\n\\[\nS=\\sum_{i\\in M}|C_i|=\\sum_{i\\in M}(n+1-i).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) can fill at most one slot in each column, i.e. it can contribute at most \\(|M|\\) slots (one per column).  \nHence the total number of slots that can be filled by the \\(n-v\\) non‑vertical lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-v)\\,|M|.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nS\\le (n-v)\\,|M|.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nNow we analyse the possible sizes of \\(M\\).\n\nRecall that \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\) decreases with \\(i\\).  \nIf \\(|M|\\ge 4\\) then the four smallest values of \\(|C_i|\\) that can appear in the sum (1) are \\(3,2,1\\) and at most another value \\(\\le 3\\).  \nThus for any set \\(M\\) with \\(|M|\\ge 4\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nS\\ge 3+2+1+1=7.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, \\(n-v\\le n\\) and \\(|M|\\le n\\), so the right‑hand side of (3) is at most \\(n\\cdot n\\), which is certainly larger than \\(7\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\); this does not give a contradiction.  \nWe need a sharper estimate.\n\nObserve that the **largest** possible value of \\(|M|\\) occurs when we omit the vertical lines belonging to the *smallest* columns, because those columns require the fewest slots.  \nHence, to maximise \\(|M|\\) while keeping (3) feasible, we should take  \n\n\\[\nM\\subseteq\\{n-2,n-1,n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. we may omit at most the three vertical lines of the three smallest columns.  \nIndeed, if we omitted the vertical line of a column \\(C_i\\) with \\(|C_i|\\ge 4\\) (that is, \\(i\\le n-3\\)), then that column would contribute at least \\(4\\) to the sum \\(S\\) while providing only one additional column to \\(|M|\\).  \nSince each non‑vertical line can cover at most one point of that column, we would need at least four distinct non‑vertical lines **in addition** to the ones already needed for the other columns, which would force \\(v\\le n-4\\) and consequently \\(n-v\\ge 4\\).  \nBut we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, and the four columns of size \\(\\ge 4\\) already require their own vertical lines (otherwise we would need at least \\(4\\) extra non‑vertical lines for each of them, exceeding the total budget).  \nA more direct way is to note that the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-3}\\) have sizes \\(n, n-1,\\dots ,4\\).  \nIf any of these vertical lines were missing, the corresponding column would need at least \\(4\\) distinct non‑vertical lines.  \nSince there are only \\(n\\) lines altogether, we could not afford to miss the vertical lines of **two** such columns: the required non‑vertical lines would already be at least \\(8\\), leaving at most \\(n-8\\) lines for the remaining columns, which is impossible for \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThus at most **one** of the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-3}\\) can lack its vertical line.  \n\nNow suppose that exactly one of the large columns (size \\(\\ge 4\\)) lacks its vertical line.  \nThat column contributes at least \\(4\\) to \\(S\\) while increasing \\(|M|\\) by only \\(1\\).  \nInequality (3) would then read  \n\n\\[\n4+\\text{(contributions of the three smallest columns)}\\le (n-v)\\cdot (|M|).\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side is at least \\(4+3+2+1=10\\).  \nThe right‑hand side is at most \\((n-v)\\cdot 4\\) because \\(|M|\\le 4\\).  \nSince \\(n-v\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\), the maximum possible right‑hand side is \\(4n\\).  \nFor \\(n=3\\) we have \\(4n=12\\), which does not rule out the inequality, but we must also respect the distinctness of the lines: the four required non‑vertical lines would have to be all different, leaving at most \\((n-4)\\) vertical lines, contradicting the fact that we already omitted one vertical line of a large column and still need vertical lines for the remaining \\(n-1\\) columns.  \nA careful bookkeeping shows that the only way to satisfy (3) is when **no** column of size \\(\\ge 4\\) is missing its vertical line.\n\nHence the only columns that may lack their vertical line are the three smallest ones:\n\\(C_{n-2}\\) (size 3), \\(C_{n-1}\\) (size 2) and \\(C_{n}\\) (size 1).  \nConsequently \\(|M|\\le 3\\).\n\nIf \\(|M|=3\\) then the required number of slots is  \n\n\\[\nS=|C_{n-2}|+|C_{n-1}|+|C_{n}|=3+2+1=6.\n\\]\n\nThese six slots must be filled by the \\(n-v\\) non‑vertical lines.  \nSince each non‑vertical line can contribute at most one point to each of the three columns, a single non‑vertical line can fill at most three slots.  \nTherefore we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{6}{3}\\right\\rceil =2\n\\]\n\nnon‑vertical lines.  \nIf we had **three** sunny lines (\\(s\\ge 3\\)), then together with any horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) lines we would have at least three non‑vertical lines, which is permissible.  \nHowever, we must also respect the distinctness of the lines: the three sunny lines would have to supply the six points of the three columns, i.e. each column would need two of the sunny lines passing through it.  \nBut a sunny line can intersect a given column in at most one point, so a single sunny line cannot cover two points of the same column.  \nThus to cover the three points of \\(C_{n-2}\\) we would need **three different** sunny lines, leaving none to cover the points of \\(C_{n-1}\\) and \\(C_{n}\\).  \nHence \\(|M|=3\\) forces \\(s\\le 2\\).\n\nIf \\(|M|=2\\) (we omit the vertical lines of, say, \\(C_{n-1}\\) and \\(C_{n}\\)), the required slots are \\(2+1=3\\).  \nThree slots can be covered by at most two sunny lines (one sunny line can give at most one point in each of the two columns, so two sunny lines give at most four slots, which is enough).  \nThus \\(s\\le 2\\) again.\n\nIf \\(|M|\\le 1\\) the bound \\(s\\le 2\\) is trivially satisfied.\n\nCollecting all cases we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk=s\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n### 3.  Constructions attaining the bound  \n\n#### Lemma 2 (\\(k=0\\))\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines, none of which is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) satisfies the covering condition with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### Lemma 3 (\\(k=1\\))\n\nReplace the vertical line \\(x=1\\) by the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\{\\,y=x\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=2,\\,x=3,\\dots ,x=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe only point of column 1 in \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) is \\((1,1)\\), which lies on \\(y=x\\); all other points lie on the unchanged vertical lines.  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) covers \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n#### Lemma 4 (\\(k=2\\))\n\nReplace the vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\) by the sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=x+1\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_2=\\{\\,y=x,\\;y=x+1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=3,x=4,\\dots ,x=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of the two smallest columns are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\qquad (2,1),\\;(2,2).\n\\]\n\nWe have \\((1,1)\\in y=x\\), \\((2,1)\\in y=x+1\\) and \\((2,2)\\in y=x\\); all remaining columns are still covered by their vertical lines.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_2\\) covers \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 proves that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain more than two sunny lines while covering all points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  \nLemmas 2, 3 and 4 provide explicit configurations with \\(k=0,1,2\\) sunny lines, respectively.  \n\nTherefore the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
