lel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(\\lambda\\neq-1\\)); hence \\(\\ell\\) is sunny and, by construction, has a slope distinct from every line in \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n| \\(k\\) | Construction of 3 distinct lines |\n|------|-----------------------------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2\\) (all non‑sunny) |\n| 1 | Sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\) (through \\((1,1)\\)), plus \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) |\n| 2 | Sunny lines \\(y=2x-1\\) (through \\((1,1)\\)) and \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\) (through \\((2,1)\\)), plus non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\) |\n| 3 | Three distinct sunny lines, e.g. \\(\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=3x-2\\). |\n\nEach family covers all points of \\(S_3\\) and contains exactly the indicated number of sunny lines. Hence every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) the following statement holds:\n\n> (**Inductive hypothesis**)  \n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal F_{n,k}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n> \\]\n> of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> 1. each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\);  \n> 2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nWe prove the analogous statement for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### The new points\n\nFrom Lemma 1,\n\\[\nS_{n+1}=S_n\\;\\cup\\;T_{n+2},\n\\qquad\nT_{n+2}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b=n+2\\},\n\\]\nand all points of \\(T_{n+2}\\) lie on the line \\(\\Delta_{n+2}:x+y=n+2\\).\n\nThus, to extend a configuration for \\(n\\) to one for \\(n+1\\) it suffices to add **one** line that contains every point of \\(T_{n+2}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Adding a non‑sunny line  \n\nTake the line \\(\\Delta_{n+2}\\) itself. It is parallel to the forbidden diagonal direction, hence **not** sunny.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n+1,k}^{\\text{(non‑sunny)}}:=\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\Delta_{n+2}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe new family has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines (the added line is different from all lines of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) because none of those lines passes through any point with \\(a+b=n+2\\)).  \nAll points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) are covered, and the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Adding a sunny line  \n\nChoose any point \\(P\\in T_{n+2}\\) (for instance \\(P=(1,n+1)\\)).  \nApply Lemma 2 with the set \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) to obtain a sunny line \\(L\\) through \\(P\\) whose slope is distinct from the slopes of all lines already present.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n+1,k}^{\\text{(sunny)}}:=\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\cup\\{L\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain we have \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, all points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) are covered (the new line contains the whole set \\(T_{n+2}\\)), and the number of sunny lines is now \\(k+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Obtaining every \\(k'\\) for \\(n+1\\)\n\nLet \\(k'\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k'\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(k'\\le n\\): start from the configuration \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k'}\\) (inductive hypothesis) and add the non‑sunny line \\(\\Delta_{n+2}\\). The resulting family has exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n* If \\(k'=n+1\\): start from \\(\\mathcal F_{n,n}\\) (which exists by the hypothesis) and add a sunny line through a point of \\(T_{n+2}\\) as described above. The new family has \\(n+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k'\\) we have constructed a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\nThis completes the induction.\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion  \n\nBy the base case \\(n=3\\) and the inductive step, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince we cannot have more