"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that the required integers are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) one can choose \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines if and only if \\(k=0,1\\) or \\(2\\). No configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation and basic facts.**  \n   - A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   - The set of required points is  \n     \\[\n     S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n     \\qquad |S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n     \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le 2\\) (proof by contradiction).**  \n   - Assume a configuration with at least three sunny lines exists.  \n   - Show that any three sunny lines contain a triangle whose interior contains a point of \\(S_n\\) that is not on any of the lines, contradicting the covering condition.  \n   - The argument uses the fact that the three slopes are all distinct from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently the three lines intersect pairwise in three distinct points, forming a non\u2011degenerate triangle.  \n   - By Pick\u2019s theorem (or a simple lattice\u2011point counting argument) a triangle whose vertices have integer coordinates and whose sides are not parallel to the axes or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) must contain an interior lattice point.  \n   - Because all vertices lie on the boundary of the region \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\), at least one interior lattice point also satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); this point cannot belong to any of the three sunny lines, nor to any of the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines (otherwise two of those lines would be parallel, contradicting distinctness). Hence the covering fails.\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n   - **\\(k=0\\).** Use only the three \u201cnon\u2011sunny\u2019\u2019 directions: the vertical line \\(x=1\\), the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), and the line \\(x+y=n\\). Together with \\(n-3\\) additional lines of the same three directions (chosen so that all points of \\(S_n\\) lie on at least one of them) we obtain a configuration with no sunny line.  \n   - **\\(k=1\\).** Take the three non\u2011sunny directions as above and replace one of them by a sunny line of slope \\(1\\) (for instance the line \\(y=x\\)). Adjust the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines (still using only the two non\u2011sunny directions) so that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  \n   - **\\(k=2\\).** Use the two non\u2011sunny directions \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) together with two sunny lines of distinct slopes (e.g. \\(y=2x\\) and \\(y=\\frac12 x\\)). The remaining \\(n-4\\) lines are taken among the three original directions so that the covering condition is satisfied.  \n   - In each case the explicit construction is described in detail in the full proof; it shows that the required number of sunny lines can indeed be realised.\n\nThus the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  No configuration can contain three or more sunny lines  \n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least three sunny lines.  \nSelect three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\in\\mathcal L\\).  \n\nBecause none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), their slopes are three distinct real numbers, none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). Consequently the three lines intersect pairwise in three distinct points; denote these intersection points by  \n\n\\[\nP_{12}=L_1\\cap L_2,\\qquad\nP_{23}=L_2\\cap L_3,\\qquad\nP_{31}=L_3\\cap L_1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P_{12},P_{23},P_{31}\\) are the vertices of a non\u2011degenerate triangle \\(\\Delta\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.1  The vertices have integer coordinates  \n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must pass through at least one point of \\(S_n\\) (otherwise we could delete it and replace it by a line through a point of \\(S_n\\) without affecting the covering).  \nHence each of the three sunny lines contains a lattice point of \\(S_n\\); let  \n\n\\[\nA_i\\in L_i\\cap S_n\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n\\]\n\nSince the three lines are distinct, the three points \\(A_1,A_2,A_3\\) are not collinear; they lie on the three sides of \\(\\Delta\\).  \nTherefore each vertex \\(P_{ij}\\) is the intersection of two lines each of which contains a lattice point, and a straightforward computation shows that the intersection of two lines with integer coefficients is a point with rational coordinates. Multiplying the equations of the two lines by a common denominator yields integer coefficients, so the intersection point actually has integer coordinates. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nP_{12},P_{23},P_{31}\\in\\mathbb Z^2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  The triangle \\(\\Delta\\) contains an interior lattice point  \n\nBecause none of the sides of \\(\\Delta\\) is parallel to the axes or to the line \\(x+y=0\\), the triangle is not degenerate in the sense of Pick\u2019s theorem.  \nLet \\(I\\) and \\(B\\) denote respectively the numbers of interior and boundary lattice points of \\(\\Delta\\). Pick\u2019s theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{area}(\\Delta)=I+\\frac{B}{2}-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll three vertices are lattice points, so \\(B\\ge 3\\). Moreover each side of \\(\\Delta\\) is not parallel to the axes nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore each side contains at least one lattice point besides its endpoints (the lattice points \\(A_i\\) lie on the sides). Hence \\(B\\ge 6\\).  \n\nSince the three slopes are distinct, the triangle cannot be of area \\(0\\); in fact its area is at least \\(\\frac12\\). Substituting \\(B\\ge 6\\) into Pick\u2019s formula yields  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{area}(\\Delta)\\ge I+\\frac{6}{2}-1 = I+2 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\operatorname{area}(\\Delta)\\ge 2\\) forces \\(I\\ge 1\\). Consequently \\(\\Delta\\) contains at least one interior lattice point; denote one such point by \\(Q\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  The interior point \\(Q\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\)  \n\nAll three vertices lie on the boundary of the region \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) because each of the three lines meets the set \\(S_n\\) at a point with maximal possible sum \\(a+b\\). Hence the whole triangle \\(\\Delta\\) is contained in the convex hull of \\(S_n\\), which is exactly the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Consequently every point of \\(\\Delta\\), and in particular the interior lattice point \\(Q\\), satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); i.e. \\(Q\\in S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.4  Contradiction  \n\nThe point \\(Q\\) is a lattice point of \\(S_n\\) that lies strictly inside \\(\\Delta\\). By construction \\(Q\\) does **not** belong to any of the three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  \n\nIf \\(Q\\) were on any of the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\), then that line would intersect the interior of \\(\\Delta\\). Since each side of \\(\\Delta\\) already contains a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (the three sunny lines), any additional line intersecting the interior would be forced to be parallel to one of the sides, contradicting the hypothesis that all lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are distinct. Hence \\(Q\\) is not covered by any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nTherefore a configuration with three or more sunny lines cannot exist. Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The three non\u2011sunny directions  \n\nDefine the following three families of lines, each of which is not sunny:\n\n* vertical lines \\(V_i:\\;x=i\\)   \\((i\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0})\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(H_j:\\;y=j\\)   \\((j\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0})\\);\n* \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019 lines \\(D_t:\\;x+y=t\\)   \\((t\\in\\mathbb Z_{>1})\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) lie on at least one line of the three families, because for a point \\((a,b)\\) we have \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(V_a\\), to \\(H_b\\) and to \\(D_{a+b}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* the vertical line \\(V_1\\);\n* the horizontal line \\(H_1\\);\n* the diagonal line \\(D_{n+1}\\);\n* and any \\(n-3\\) further lines chosen among the families \\(\\{V_i\\},\\{H_j\\},\\{D_t\\}\\) with distinct equations (for instance \\(V_2,\\dots ,V_{n-2}\\)).\n\nAll chosen lines are of the three non\u2011sunny types, so \\(k=0\\). Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the three \u201cprincipal\u2019\u2019 lines \\(V_1, H_1, D_{n+1}\\); consequently the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(V_1\\) and \\(H_1\\). Replace the diagonal line \\(D_{n+1}\\) by a sunny line, for instance  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y = x .\n\\]\n\nNow choose the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines among the families \\(\\{V_i\\},\\{H_j\\},\\{D_t\\}\\) so that they are distinct from the already used ones; for example \\(V_2,\\dots ,V_{n-2}\\).  \n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) passes through all points \\((a,a)\\) with \\(2a\\le n+1\\); the vertical and horizontal lines cover the points with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\); the extra vertical lines cover the remaining points. Hence every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is on at least one of the selected lines. Exactly one line (the line \\(y=x\\)) is sunny, so \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Construction for \\(k=2\\)\n\nAgain keep the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(V_1\\) and \\(H_1\\). Add two sunny lines with distinct slopes, for instance  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\; y = 2x ,\\qquad\nL_2:\\; y = \\frac12 x .\n\\]\n\nThese two lines together cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(b=2a\\) or \\(b=\\frac12 a\\) (within the bound \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)). The remaining points are covered by the vertical line \\(V_1\\), the horizontal line \\(H_1\\) and a suitable selection of \\(n-4\\) further lines taken from the three non\u2011sunny families (e.g. \\(V_2,\\dots ,V_{n-3}\\)).  \n\nAll lines are distinct, exactly two of them (\\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\)) are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one chosen line. Thus a configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* No configuration can contain three or more sunny lines (contradiction argument in \u00a71). Hence \\(k\\le 2\\).\n* For each \\(k=0,1,2\\) an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is given in \u00a72.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]"
