            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines if and only if \\(k=0,1\\) or \\(2\\). No configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines can satisfy the covering condition.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is sunny* ⇔ its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le 2\\) (proof by contradiction).**  \n   * Assume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least three sunny lines.  \n   * Choose three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\). Because none is parallel to the axes or to \\(x+y=0\\), the three slopes are pairwise distinct; consequently the three lines intersect pairwise in three distinct points \\(P_{12},P_{23},P_{31}\\) forming a non‑degenerate triangle \\(\\Delta\\).  \n   * Each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) meets \\(S_n\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) contains a lattice point of \\(S_n\\). From the equations of two lines with integer coefficients one obtains that their intersection point has integer coordinates, so the three vertices of \\(\\Delta\\) are lattice points.  \n   * The sides of \\(\\Delta\\) are not parallel to the axes nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore each side contains at least one lattice point of \\(S_n\\) besides its endpoints. Hence the boundary of \\(\\Delta\\) contains at least six lattice points.  \n   * By Pick’s theorem,\n     \\[\n     \\operatorname{area}(\\Delta)=I+\\frac{B}{2}-1,\n     \\]\n     where \\(I\\) (resp. \\(B\\)) is the number of interior (resp. boundary) lattice points. Since \\(B\\ge 6\\) we obtain \\(\\operatorname{area}(\\Delta)\\ge I+2\\). The area of \\(\\Delta\\) is positive, so \\(I\\ge 1\\); thus \\(\\Delta\\) contains an interior lattice point \\(Q\\).  \n   * All three vertices lie on the boundary of the convex region \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) (they are intersections of lines that already contain points of \\(S_n\\) with maximal possible sum \\(a+b\\)). Consequently the whole triangle \\(\\Delta\\) is contained in that convex region, and therefore the interior point \\(Q\\) also satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); i.e. \\(Q\\in S_n\\).  \n   * By construction \\(Q\\) is not on any of the three sunny lines. If it were on any of the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines, that line would intersect the interior of \\(\\Delta\\); but each side of \\(\\Delta\\) already contains a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), and any additional line intersecting the interior would have to be parallel to one of the sides, contradicting the distinctness of the lines. Hence \\(Q\\) is uncovered, contradicting the covering requirement.  \n   * Therefore a configuration cannot contain three sunny lines; thus \\(k\\le 2\\).\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n   *Define three families of non‑sunny lines* (all have slopes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)):\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\;(i\\ge1),\\qquad\n   H_j:\\;y=j\\;(j\\ge1),\\qquad\n   D_t:\\;x+y=t\\;(t\\ge2).\n   \\]\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs simultaneously to \\(V_a\\), \\(H_b\\) and \\(D_{a+b}\\).\n\n   - **\\(k=0\\).**  \n     Choose the three lines \\(V_1,\\;H_1,\\;D_{n+1}\\) and any additional \\(n-3\\) distinct lines from the three families (for instance \\(V_2,\\dots ,V_{n-2}\\)). All chosen lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\). Since each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the three “principal’’ lines, the covering condition holds.\n\n   - **\\(k=1\\).**  \n     Keep the non‑sunny lines \\(V_1\\) and \\(H_1\\). Add the sunny line\n     \\[\n     L_{\\text{s}}:\\;y=x .\n     \\]\n     The line \\(y=x\\) contains all points \\((a,a)\\) with \\(2a\\le n+1\\). The remaining points of \\(S_n\\) have either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) or \\(a\\neq b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); those with \\(a=1\\) (resp. \\(b=1\\)) are covered by \\(V_1\\) (resp. \\(H_1\\)). For the still uncovered points we add \\(n-3\\) further lines taken from the families \\(V_i\\) (e.g. \\(V_2,\\dots ,V_{n-2}\\)). Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one selected line and exactly one line (\\(y=x\\)) is sunny, giving \\(k=1\\).\n\n   - **\\(k=2\\).**  \n     Again keep \\(V_1\\) and \\(H_1\\). Add two sunny lines with distinct slopes, for instance\n     \\[\n     L_1:\\;y=2x,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=\\tfrac12 x .\n     \\]\n     The line \\(y=2x\\) contains all points \\((a,2a)\\) with \\(3a\\le n+1\\); the line \\(y=\\frac12 x\\) contains all points \\((2b,b)\\) with \\(3b\\le n+1\\). Together with \\(V_1\\) and \\(H_1\\) they cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates are equal or differ by a factor \\(2\\). Any remaining points have either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) and are already covered; the rest can be covered by adding \\(n-4\\) further lines from the three non‑sunny families (e.g. \\(V_2,\\dots ,V_{n-3}\\)). Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly two sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=2\\).\n\n   In each case the lines are distinct, the covering condition is satisfied, and the number of sunny lines equals the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k\\ge3\\) with the explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
