            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have revised the solution and now present a complete, rigorous proof.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Upper bound** – obviously \\(k\\le n\\) because only \\(n\\) lines are used.  \n\n2. **Counting argument** – let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(k\\) we will construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Choice of the non‑sunny lines** – take the \\(n-k\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s\\in S),\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(S\\) consists of the **largest** \\(n-k\\) integers among \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n   Each \\(D_s\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \n   The line \\(D_s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (those with \\(a+b=s\\)).  \n   Because we have taken the largest possible sums, the total number of points already covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   C_{n,k}:=\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Remaining points** – the points of \\(T_n\\) not on any \\(D_s\\) are precisely those whose sum \\(a+b\\) belongs to the complementary set  \n\n   \\[\n   R:=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Their total number is  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-C_{n,k}= \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sunny lines for the remaining points** – we need to cover at most \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   We do this by assigning **one distinct sunny line to each of the \\(k\\) smallest sums** in \\(R\\).  \n\n   * For each \\(r\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) pick a point \\((1,r-1)\\) (any point with that sum works).  \n   * Choose an irrational number \\(\\alpha_r\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and define  \n\n     \\[\n     L_r:\\; y-(r-1)=\\alpha_r\\bigl(x-1\\bigr).\n     \\]\n\n     The slope \\(\\alpha_r\\) is irrational, hence \\(L_r\\) is not parallel to the\n     \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore each \\(L_r\\) is sunny.\n\n   * **Lemma.** If \\(\\alpha\\) is irrational, the line \\(y-b=\\alpha(x-a)\\) contains no integer point other than \\((a,b)\\).  \n     *Proof.* If \\((x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\) lies on the line, then \\(\\alpha=(y-b)/(x-a)\\) is a ratio of two integers, hence rational, contradicting the irrationality unless \\(x=a\\) and then \\(y=b\\). ∎\n\n     Consequently each \\(L_r\\) contains exactly the chosen point \\((1,r-1)\\) and no other lattice point.\n\n   * The \\(k\\) lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{k+1}\\) are pairwise distinct because their slopes are distinct irrational numbers.\n\n6. **Verification that all points are covered** –  \n\n   * Points whose sum belongs to \\(S\\) lie on the corresponding non‑sunny line \\(D_s\\).  \n   * For each \\(r\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) the point \\((1,r-1)\\) lies on \\(L_r\\).  \n   * Any other point with sum \\(r\\) (i.e. \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=r\\) and \\(a\\neq1\\)) is already covered because \\(r\\le k+1\\) implies \\(r\\) is one of the smallest sums; such a point can be written as \\((a,b)=(a, r-a)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\). Since \\(r\\le k+1\\le n+1\\), the