\ge 1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Among those points we keep only the ones that satisfy the original\n   restriction \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Because \\(a+b = a + (a-i+1)=2a-i+1\\), the inequality \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) (the\n   region still uncovered after step 4) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   2a \\le k+i \\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\n   i\\le a \\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(L_i\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\, (a,\\,a-i+1)\\;\\big|\\; i\\le a\\le\\lfloor (k+i)/2\\rfloor \\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The number of such points is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor - i + 1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   A short case check shows that (7) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\dfrac{i+1}{2}, &\\text{if }i\\text{ is odd},\\\\[4pt]\n   \\dfrac{i}{2},   &\\text{if }i\\text{ is even},\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. each line \\(L_i\\) contributes exactly \\(\\lceil i/2\\rceil\\) new points.\n   Summing (7) over \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor - i + 1\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 ,\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   which coincides with the number of uncovered points in (2).  \n   Moreover, the sets (6) for different \\(i\\) are disjoint, because two distinct\n   lines of slope \\(1\\) have different intercepts.\n\n6. **Verification of coverage** –  \n\n   * Points with sum \\(s\\ge k+2\\) lie on the non‑sunny line \\(D_s\\).  \n   * Points with sum \\(\\le k+1\\) lie on exactly one of the sunny lines\n     \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) by the description (6).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed\n   lines. Because the families \\(\\{D_s\\}\\) and \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint,\n   each point is covered exactly once.\n\n7. **Distinctness of all lines** –  \n\n   * The \\(n-k\\) lines \\(D_s\\) have distinct constants \\(s\\), so they are distinct.  \n   * The \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) have distinct intercepts \\(-i+1\\), so they are distinct\n     from each other and also from any \\(D_s\\) (the latter have slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   Thus we have obtained \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n8. **Conclusion** – the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\), this proves that\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{All }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are attainable}.}\n   \\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny lines\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it is **not**\nsunny.  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) that satisfy \\(a+b=s\\) are exactly the \\(s-1\\) lattice\npoints  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence the collection \\(\\{D_s\\}_{s=k+2}^{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-k\\) of them)\ncovers  \n\n\\[\nC_{n,k}:=\\sum_{s=k+2}^{n+1}(s-1)\n        =\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points that remain uncovered\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on any \\(D_s\\) are precisely those with\n\\(a+b\\le k+1\\). Their number is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-C_{n,k}= \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sunny lines covering the remaining points\n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(L_i\\) is **sunny** (it is not\nparallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nA lattice point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb = a - i + 1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a,b\\ge 1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the original restriction \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na + (a-i+1) \\le k+1\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n2a \\le k+i\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\ni\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the points of \\(T_n\\) that belong to \\(L_i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_i:=\\Bigl\\{\\, (a,\\,a-i+1)\\;\\Big|\\;\ni\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal{P}_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_i|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor - i + 1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_i|=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{i+1}{2}, & i\\text{ odd},\\\\[6pt]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{i}{2},   & i\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSumming (7) over all \\(i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} |\\mathcal{P}_i|\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines \\(L_i\\) have distinct intercepts, the sets \\(\\mathcal{P}_i\\)\nare pairwise disjoint; consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together contain\nexactly the \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points counted in (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completeness of the covering\n\n*If \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\), which is\none of the non‑sunny lines.\n\n*If \\(a+b\\le k+1\\)*, then by (5) the integer \\(a\\) satisfies the bounds for a\nunique index \\(i\\) (namely \\(i = a - b + 1\\)), and consequently \\((a,b)\\in\n\\mathcal{P}_i\\subset L_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\nSince the families \\(\\{D_s\\}\\) and \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) are disjoint, each point is\ncovered exactly once.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Distinctness of the lines\n\nThe \\(n-k\\) lines \\(D_s\\) have different constants \\(s\\), so they are distinct.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) have different intercepts \\(-i+1\\); therefore they are\npairwise distinct and none coincides with a line \\(D_s\\) (the latter have slope\n\\(-1\\)). Hence we have obtained \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain all points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nConversely, obviously \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
