"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI agree with the bug report: the construction of the sunny lines \\(L_i : y = x-i+1\\) only covers the lattice points of the set  \n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]  \nthat satisfy \\(a\\ge b\\).  Points with \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) are left uncovered, so the claimed covering of all points fails. Consequently the previous solution is not correct.\n\n**b. Method Sketch (corrected solution):**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  Obviously \\(k\\le n\\) because at most \\(n\\) lines are used.\n\n2. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  As before we use the lines  \n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1),\n   \\]  \n   which are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\).\n\n3. **Remaining points.**  After step\u202f2 the uncovered points are exactly those with  \n   \\[\n   a+b\\le k+1 .\n   \\]  \n   Their number is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n4. **Covering the remaining triangle with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   The triangle \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}\\) can be tiled by \\(k\\) lines of *different* admissible slopes, each line containing a distinct number of lattice points so that the total number of points covered equals \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \n   A convenient choice is:\n\n   *For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\)* define  \n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 \\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, sunny).}\n   \\]\n\n   *For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\)* define  \n   \\[\n   T_j:\\; y = 2x - (2j-1) \\qquad\\text{(slope }2\\text{, sunny).}\n   \\]\n\n   The families \\(\\{S_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{T_j\\}\\) are disjoint (different slopes), and each line contains a consecutive block of lattice points of the triangle.  A direct count shows that the total number of points covered by all these \\(k\\) lines is exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\), and that every point with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) lies on