\n\\]\n\nTheir number is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-C_{n,k}= \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDenote this set by  \n\n\\[\nU_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sunny lines covering \\(U_k\\)\n\nWe shall cover \\(U_k\\) with **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines**.  \nWrite \\(k=2q\\) or \\(k=2q+1\\) with \\(q=\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n*For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,q\\)* define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese lines have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny).  \nA lattice point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb = a-i+1,\\qquad a\\ge i,\\;b\\ge1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the condition \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a-i+1\\le k+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\ni\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the points of \\(U_k\\) that belong to \\(S_i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{S}_i:=\\Bigl\\{\\, (a,\\,a-i+1)\\;\\Big|\\;\ni\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\Bigr\\},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nand the cardinality of \\(\\mathcal{S}_i\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{S}_i|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-i+1\n   =\\begin{cases}\n      \\dfrac{i}{2}, & i\\text{ even},\\\\[4pt]\n      \\dfrac{i+1}{2}, & i\\text{ odd}.\n     \\end{cases}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd* (\\(k=2q+1\\)), we need one additional sunny line; define  \n\n\\[\nT_{q+1}:\\; y = 2x-(2q+1) .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(2\\) (also sunny).  A point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(T_{q+1}\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb = 2a-(2q+1),\\qquad a\\ge q+1,\\;b\\ge1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nTogether with \\(a+b\\le k+1=2q+2\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a-(2q+1)+a\\le 2q+2\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n3a\\le 4q+3\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\na\\le q+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the only admissible integer \\(a\\) is \\(a=q+1\\), and the corresponding point is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(q+1,\\;2(q+1)-(2q+1)\\bigr)=(q+1,\\,1).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(T_{q+1}\\) contributes exactly one new point.\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even (\\(k=2q\\)), we do **not** need a line of slope \\(2\\); the set of lines \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{q}\\) already supplies  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{q}|\\mathcal{S}_i|\n   =\\frac{q(2q+1)}2\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\npoints, which equals \\(|U_k|\\) by (2).\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd, the total number of points covered by the \\(q\\) lines \\(S_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{q}|\\mathcal{S}_i|\n   =\\frac{q(2q+1)}2\n   =\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAdding the single point contributed by \\(T_{q+1}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k-1)}2+1=\\frac{k(k+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nagain matching \\(|U_k|\\).\n\nTherefore, in both parity cases the \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(q\\) lines \\(S_i\\) together with, when \\(k\\) is odd, the extra line \\(T_{q+1}\\)) cover **all** points of \\(U_k\\), and each point of \\(U_k\\) lies on exactly one of these lines because the lines have distinct slopes or distinct intercepts.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completeness of the covering\n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\), then it lies on the non\u2011sunny line \\(D_{a+b}\\).\n* If \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), then by the analysis of Section\u202f3 it lies on a unique sunny line among the constructed \\(k\\) lines.\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Distinctness of all lines\n\n* The \\(n-k\\) lines \\(D_s\\) have slope \\(-1\\) and distinct constants \\(s\\); they are pairwise distinct.\n* The sunny lines \\(S_i\\) have slope \\(1\\) and distinct intercepts \\(-i+1\\); they are distinct from each other and from any \\(D_s\\) (different slopes).\n* When \\(k\\) is odd, the extra line \\(T_{q+1}\\) has slope \\(2\\), different from both \\(-1\\) and \\(1\\); thus it is distinct from all previously listed lines.\n\nConsequently we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that together contain all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  The obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]"

