            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny lines covering the points with large sum\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) satisfying \\(a+b=s\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nso the family \\(\\{D_s\\}_{s=k+2}^{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-k\\) lines) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) with  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge k+2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The uncovered triangle\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy (6) the points of \\(U_k\\) are exactly those of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered.  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|U_k|=\\sum_{t=2}^{k+1}(t-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sunny lines that cover \\(U_k\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,m\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y = x-i+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\nA lattice point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb=a-i+1,\\qquad a\\ge i,\\;b\\ge1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the defining inequality of \\(U_k\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\na+(a-i+1)\\le k+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\ni\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence the set of points of \\(U_k\\) belonging to \\(S_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_i:=\\Bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(a,\\,a-i+1\\bigr)\\;\\Big|\\;\n          i\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n\\]\nand its cardinality equals  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S_i|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-i+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow we sum (11) over all \\(i\\).\n\n*If \\(k=2q\\) is even*, then \\(m=q\\) and for each \\(i\\) we have\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2q+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =q+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\nA short computation yields\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{q}\\bigl(q+\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor-i+1\\bigr)\n   =\\frac{2q(2q+1)}4\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(k=2q+1\\) is odd*, then \\(m=q+1\\).  Splitting the sum into the first \\(q\\) indices and the last one gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{q}\\bigl(q+\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor-i+1\\bigr)\n   +\\bigl(q+1+\\lfloor (q+1)/2\\rfloor-(q+1)+1\\bigr)\n   =\\frac{(2q+1)(2q+2)}4\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus in **both** parity cases\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{m}|\\mathcal S_i|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|U_k|.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\) cover **all** points of \\(U_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nIf \\(k=m\\) (which happens when \\(k\\) is odd) we already have the required number of sunny lines.  \nIf \\(k>m\\) (the even case, where \\(k=2m\\)), we need \\(k-m=m\\) additional sunny lines.  \nChoose any \\(k-m\\) distinct sunny lines that avoid the region \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); for example,\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = 2x + C_j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,k-m),\n\\]\nwith constants \\(C_j\\) taken so large that every point of \\(L_j\\) satisfies \\(x+y>n+1\\).  \nThese extra lines are sunny, distinct from one another and from all previously constructed lines, and they do not affect the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Counting the total number of lines\n\nWe have assembled\n\n* \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines \\(D_s\\) (slope \\(-1\\));\n* \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(S_i\\) (slope \\(1\\));\n* \\(k-m\\) dummy sunny lines \\(L_j\\) (slope \\(2\\), say).\n\nAll are pairwise distinct because they have different slopes or, when the slope coincides, different intercepts.  \nThe total number of lines is\n\\[\n(n-k)+m+(k-m)=n,\n\\]\nand exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Necessity of the bound \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nWe are required to use precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines; therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines:\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\nNo further restriction is needed, as the construction above works for every \\(k\\) in this range.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, such that each lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder