he covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Counting the total number of lines\n\nWe have assembled  \n\n* \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines \\(D_s\\) (slope \\(-1\\));\n* \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(S_i\\) (slope \\(1\\));\n* \\(k-m\\) dummy sunny lines \\(L_j\\) (slope \\(2\\)).\n\nAll are pairwise distinct, so the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)+m+(k-m)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(m\\) lines \\(S_i\\) together with the \\(k-m\\) lines \\(L_j\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Necessity of the bound \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nSince we are required to use precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines; therefore \\(k\\le n\\). No further restriction on \\(k\\) is needed, because the construction above works for every integer in this range.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Final conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, such that each lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]"
