            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Non‑sunny lines covering the points with large sum\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) satisfying \\(a+b=s\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nso the family \\(\\{D_s\\}_{s=k+2}^{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-k\\) lines) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) with  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge k+2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The uncovered triangle\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy (2) the points of \\(U_k\\) are exactly those of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered.  \nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|U_k|=\\sum_{t=2}^{k+1}(t-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Sunny lines that cover \\(U_k\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,m\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y = x-i+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\nA lattice point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb=a-i+1,\\qquad a\\ge i,\\;b\\ge1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the defining inequality of \\(U_k\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\na+(a-i+1)\\le k+1\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\ni\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence the set of points of \\(U_k\\) belonging to \\(S_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_i:=\\Bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(a,\\,a-i+1\\bigr)\\;\\Big|\\;\n          i\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nand its cardinality equals  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S_i|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-i+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Exact counting of the covered points\n\nWe now compute \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{m}|\\mathcal S_i|\\) and show that it equals \\(|U_k|\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case 1: \\(k=2q\\) (even)\n\nThen \\(m=\\lceil k/2\\rceil = q\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2q+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   = q+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nHence, using (8),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S_i|\n   =\\bigl(q+\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor\\bigr)-i+1\n   = q -\\bigl\\lceil i/2\\rceil +1 .\n\\]\n\nNow split the sum according to the parity of \\(i\\).\n\n*If \\(i=2r\\) (even, \\(1\\le r\\le\\lfloor q/2\\rfloor\\)):*  \n\\(\\lceil i/2\\rceil = r\\), so \\(|\\mathcal S_{2r}| = q-r+1\\).\n\n*If \\(i=2r-1\\) (odd, \\(1\\le r\\le\\lceil q/2\\rceil\\)):*  \n\\(\\lceil i/2\\rceil = r\\), so \\(|\\mathcal S_{2r-1}| = q-r+1\\).\n\nThus the multiset of values \\(|\\mathcal S_i|\\) consists of the numbers  \n\n\\[\nq,\\,q-1,\\,q-2,\\dots ,1\n\\]\n\neach appearing exactly twice, except that when \\(q\\) is odd the smallest number \\(1\\) appears only once. Summing gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{q}|\\mathcal S_i|\n   =\\begin{cases}\n      2\\bigl(1+2+\\dots +q\\bigr)-q\n        = q(q+1)-q = q^2 , & q\\text{ odd},\\\\[4pt]\n      2\\bigl(1+2+\\dots +q\\bigr)\n        = q(q+1), & q\\text{ even}.\n     \\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIn both sub‑cases this equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(2q)(2q+1)}{2}= \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Case 2: \\(k=2q+1\\) (odd)\n\nNow \\(m=\\lceil k/2\\rceil = q+1\\).  \nFor \\(i\\le q\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2q+1+i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   = q+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{i+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nHence for \\(1\\le i\\le q\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S_i|\n   = q+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{i+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-i+1\n   = q-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{i-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+2 .\n\\]\n\nA short inspection shows that the sequence \\(|\\mathcal S_i|\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,q\\) is  \n\n\\[\nq+1,\\,q,\\,q-1,\\,\\dots ,2 .\n\\]\n\nFor the last index \\(i=q+1\\) we compute directly from (8):\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S_{q+1}|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2q+1+q+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(q+1)+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{3q+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-q\n   = q+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the multiset of values \\(|\\mathcal S_i|\\) is  \n\n\\[\nq+1,\\,q+1,\\,q,\\,q-1,\\dots ,2 .\n\\]\n\nSumming,\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{q+1}|\\mathcal S_i|\n   = (q+1)+(q+1)+\\bigl(q+(q-1)+\\dots +2\\bigr)\n   = 2(q+1)+\\frac{q(q+1)}2-1 .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward simplification yields\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{q+1}|\\mathcal S_i|\n   = \\frac{(2q+1)(2q+2)}2 = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\nFrom (9) and (10) we have proved that for **both** parity cases\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{m}|\\mathcal S_i|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 =|U_k|.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\) cover **all** points of \\(U_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Adding dummy sunny lines when \\(k>m\\)\n\nIf \\(k=m\\) (which occurs when \\(k\\) is odd) we already have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nAssume now that \\(k\\) is even, so \\(k=2m\\) and we need \\(k-m=m\\) additional sunny lines.\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m\\) choose a constant \\(C_j\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\nC_j > n .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = 2x + C_j .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\pm1\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\nWe claim that no lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on any \\(L_j\\).\n\nIndeed, if \\((a,b)\\) satisfied (13) then \\(b=2a+C_j\\). Adding the two coordinates gives  \n\n\\[\na+b = a+(2a+C_j)=3a+C_j .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(C_j>n\\),\n\n\\[\na+b = 3a+C_j \\ge 3\\cdot1 + (n+1) = n+4 > n+1 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the requirement \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence the region \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is disjoint from every line \\(L_j\\).\n\nThe lines \\(L_j\\) are distinct because the constants \\(C_j\\) are distinct, and they are also distinct from all previously constructed lines (the latter have slopes \\(-1\\) or \\(1\\)). Thus we have added exactly \\(k-m\\) extra sunny lines without affecting the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Counting the total number of lines\n\nWe have assembled  \n\n* \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines \\(D_s\\) (slope \\(-1\\));\n* \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(S_i\\) (slope \\(1\\));\n* \\(k-m\\) dummy sunny lines \\(L_j\\) (slope \\(2\\)).\n\nAll are pairwise distinct, so the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)+m+(k-m)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(m\\) lines \\(S_i\\) together with the \\(k-m\\) lines \\(L_j\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Necessity of the bound \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nSince we are required to use precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines; therefore \\(k\\le n\\). No further restriction on \\(k\\) is needed, because the construction above works for every integer in this range.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Final conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, such that each lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
