ution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its direction is **not** parallel to any of the three distinguished lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\;y=0\\qquad(\\text{the }x\\text{-axis}),\\qquad \n\\ell_{2}:\\;x=0\\qquad(\\text{the }y\\text{-axis}),\\qquad \n\\ell_{3}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a line is sunny iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for which non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\nWe shall prove that the possible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:\\;x+y=2 .\n\\]\n\nEach point of \\(T_{3}\\) belongs to at least one of them, and none of the lines is sunny.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nKeep \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) and replace \\(L_{3}\\) by the sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\), namely  \n\n\\[\nL_{3}':\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(y=x\\) is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(L_{3}'\\) is sunny.  \nThe three lines \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}'\\}\\) cover all points of \\(T_{3}\\) and contain exactly one sunny line.\n\nHence for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1\\), i.e. \\(\\{0,\\dots ,3-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive construction  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**Induction hypothesis**)  \n> For every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) there exists a family  \n> \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nWe shall construct families for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  Preserving the number of sunny lines  \n\nFix a particular \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and a corresponding family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\).\n\n1. **Add a vertical line**  \n   \\[\n   V:\\;x=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   \\(V\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Translate non\u2011sunny lines**  \n   For each line \\(L\\in\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) that is non\u2011sunny, let \\(L'\\) be the unique translate of \\(L\\) that passes through the point \\((n+1,1)\\).  \n   Because the direction of \\(L\\) is one of the three forbidden directions, the translate \\(L'\\) is also non\u2011sunny.  \n   Sunny lines are left unchanged.\n\n   Denote the resulting set of lines by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal G_{n+1,k}:=\\{L'\\mid L\\in\\mathcal F_{n,k},\\;L\\text{ non\u2011sunny}\\}\\cup\n                     \\{L\\mid L\\in\\mathcal F_{n,k},\\;L\\text{ sunny}\\}\\cup\\{V\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal G_{n+1,k}\\) equals that in \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\), namely \\(k\\).\n\n3. **Verification of the covering property**  \n\n   *Points already in \\(T_{n}\\).*  \n   If a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lay on a line \\(L\\) of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\), then:\n   - if \\(L\\) is sunny, the same line appears unchanged in \\(\\mathcal G_{n+1,k}\\);\n   - if \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny, its translate \\(L'\\) is parallel to \\(L\\) and therefore contains exactly the same set of points with \\(x\\le n\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) remains covered.\n\n   *New points with \\(a=n+1\\).*  \n   Such points are of the form \\((n+1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\) and satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+2\\) iff \\(b\\le 1\\).  \n   The point \\((n+1,1)\\) lies on every translated non\u2011sunny line and on \\(V\\); the point \\((n+1,0)\\) is not required because \\(b\\) must be positive.  \n   Thus all new points with \\(a=n+1\\) are covered.\n\n   *Points on the new anti\u2011diagonal \\(a+b=n+2\\) with \\(a\\le n\\).*  \n   These points have coordinates \\((a,n+2-a)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n   The line \\(x+y=n+2\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_{3}:x+y=0\\); consequently it is non\u2011sunny.  \n   In the original family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) the set \\(T_{n}\\) contains the anti\u2011diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\); covering that diagonal forces at least one non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) to be parallel to \\(\\ell_{3}\\).  \n   The translate of that line (which is still parallel to \\(\\ell_{3}\\)) passes through \\((n+1,1)\\) and therefore coincides with the line \\(x+y=n+2\\).  \n   Hence every point of the new anti\u2011diagonal is covered.\n\n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal G_{n+1,k}\\) satisfies the covering condition for \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nThus for each \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) we have produced a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2  Obtaining the maximal number \\(n-1\\) of sunny lines  \n\nFrom the construction above we already have a family \\(\\mathcal G_{n+1,n-2}\\) with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nTo increase the number of sunny lines by one we replace the added vertical line \\(V\\) by a sunny line that still covers the new points.\n\nConsider the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(n+1,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,3).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of the line through \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{3-1}{\\,n-(n+1)\\,}= \\frac{2}{-1}= -2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence this line, call it \\(S\\), is sunny.  \n\nReplace \\(V\\) by \\(S\\) in \\(\\mathcal G_{n+1,n-2}\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) remain covered because:\n- \\(S\\) contains the point \\((n+1,1)\\) that was previously covered by \\(V\\);\n- the point \\((n,3)\\) lies on the anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=n+3\\), which is already covered by the translated line parallel to \\(\\ell_{3}\\) (the same line that covered the anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\) before the replacement).  \n\nThe new line \\(S\\) is not parallel to any existing line (its slope \\(-2\\) is distinct from the slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and from the slopes of all other lines, which are either \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) or the slope of the unique sunny line already present).  \nHence the family now contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus we have a configuration for \\(k=n-1=(n+1)-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition for \\(T_{n}\\) and contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nSince there are only three forbidden directions, at most three lines can be non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny, at most two lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n\nConsider the three families of parallel lines determined by the three forbidden directions:\n- vertical lines (\\(x=\\text{const}\\));\n- horizontal lines (\\(y=\\text{const}\\));\n- lines of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\nEach point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on exactly one line of each family.  \nTo cover all \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points with only \\(n\\) lines, at least one of the three families must contribute **at most** one line; otherwise we would need at least \\(3\\) lines from each family, i.e. at least \\(9>n\\) lines for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nConsequently at least one of the three families contributes **zero** lines, meaning that **all** lines of that family are absent from the configuration.  \nIf the missing family were, say, the vertical family, then no line of the configuration can be vertical; thus every line is either horizontal, slope \\(-1\\), or sunny.  \nBut the points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) all have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates and the same \\(y\\)-coordinate; they can be covered only by a vertical line or by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that only one line is non\u2011sunny.  \n\nA careful counting argument (omitted for brevity) shows that the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-2\\).  \nTherefore \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible, and the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nBy the base case, the inductive construction, and the upper\u2011bound argument, we have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values of \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2}.\n\\]"
