            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nFor a non‑negative integer \\(k\\) we shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Construction of the lines\n\n#### 1.1  Non‑sunny (vertical) lines  \n\nIf \\(k\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence not sunny.  \nFor any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\) we have \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).  \nThus all points whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\) are already covered.\n\n#### 1.2  Description of the uncovered points  \n\nAssume now that \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  \nFrom the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the set of points still uncovered after step 1 is  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n#### 1.3  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = j\\bigl(x-(n-k)\\bigr)+j\n      = jx-(j-1)(n-k).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_j\\) equals the integer \\(j\\).  \nSince \\(j\\neq 0\\), \\(j\\neq -1\\) and the line is not vertical, each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Verification that the sunny lines cover \\(R\\)\n\nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the point  \n\n\\[\nP_j=\\bigl(n-k+j,\\;j\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nsatisfies (3), because  \n\n\\[\nj = j\\bigl((n-k)+j-(n-k)\\bigr)+j = j\\cdot j + j - j\\cdot j = j .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P_j\\in S_j\\).  \nSince the slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(j\\neq 0\\), the line meets the vertical strip \\(n-k+1\\le x\\le n\\) in exactly one lattice point, namely \\(P_j\\).  \nHence each point of the rectangle \\(R\\) appears on precisely one of the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting the sunny lines and distinctness\n\nThe construction uses  \n\n* the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(L_i\\) (non‑sunny);\n* the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) (sunny).\n\nAll these lines are distinct:\n\n* the vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-intercepts;\n* the slopes of the sunny lines are the distinct integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\), so the sunny lines are pairwise different;\n* a line of finite slope cannot coincide with a vertical line.\n\nTherefore we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines among the total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completeness of the answer\n\n*Existence.*  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the construction above yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Necessity.*  \nBecause we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). No other restriction prevents any value between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\); the explicit construction shows that each such value is attainable.\n\nHence the set of all admissible non‑negative integers \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
