\\) through \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{(n+1-2j)-(n+2-2j)}{(j+1)-j}=-1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is forbidden.  To obtain a sunny line we perturb \\(Q_j\\) upward by\n   one unit, i.e. replace \\(Q_j\\) with \\(Q'_j=(j+1,\\;n+2-2j)\\).  The new line\n   \\(\\ell'_j\\) passes through \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q'_j\\); its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m'_j=\\frac{(n+2-2j)-(n+2-2j)}{(j+1)-j}=0,\n   \\]\n\n   which is also forbidden.  Finally we perturb \\(Q_j\\) **downward** by one\n   unit, i.e. take \\(Q''_j=(j+1,\\;n-2j)\\).  Then  \n\n   \\[\n   m''_j=\\frac{(n-2j)-(n+2-2j)}{1}= -2,\n   \\]\n\n   which is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); thus \\(\\ell''_j\\) is sunny.\n   Moreover \\(\\ell''_j\\) still contains the original point \\(P_j\\) and the\n   perturbed point \\(Q''_j\\), both of which belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  The\n   perturbation is performed for each \\(j\\) independently, guaranteeing that\n   the slopes \\(\\{m''_j\\}\\) are all distinct and that the sunny lines are\n   pairwise distinct.\n\n   Consequently we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\n   which are sunny, covering every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) with the explicit constructions for\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) yields the final answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\n(vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  No configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be sunny, with equation \\(y=mx+c\\) where\n\\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) finite.  Suppose three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) (\\(i=1,2,3\\)) lie on \\(\\ell\\) with\n\\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  Because they are collinear,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n(b_2-b_1)(a_3-a_2)=(b_3-b_2)(a_2-a_1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince the \\(a_i\\) are integers, the differences \\(a_2-a_1\\) and\n\\(a_3-a_2\\) are positive integers.  Equation (3) forces the ratios\n\\(\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) and \\(\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}\\) to be equal\nrational numbers.  Because \\(m\\neq -1\\), the differences\n\\(b_i-a_i\\) cannot all be equal; consequently at least one of the three\npoints would satisfy \\(a_i+b_i>n+1\\), contradicting the definition of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Hence no sunny line can contain three distinct points\nof \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of points that could be\ncovered would be at most \\(2n\\).  By (1),\n\n\\[\n2n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThus the set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) could not be covered, a contradiction.\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  We shall produce \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point\nof \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Non\u2011sunny (vertical) lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  The line\n\\(v_i\\) contains precisely the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\);\nall such points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) consider the anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_j=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2-j\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(j\\le k\\le n-1\\), the diagonal \\(D_j\\) lies entirely inside the\ntriangle defining \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Choose the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j,\\;n+2-2j),\\qquad\nQ_j=(j+1,\\;n-2j). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy \\(a+b=n+2-j\\) and have positive integer coordinates, so\n\\(P_j,Q_j\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and they are not vertically aligned\n(\\(x\\)-coordinates differ by \\(1\\)).\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_j\\) through \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{(n-2j)-(n+2-2j)}{(j+1)-j}= -2 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(-2\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the line is not vertical, \\(\\ell_j\\) is\nsunny.  Moreover \\(\\ell_j\\) contains exactly the two points \\(P_j\\) and\n\\(Q_j\\); any other point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the same anti\u2011diagonal\nhas a different \\(x\\)-coordinate and would give a different slope, so it\ncannot lie on \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\nFor distinct indices \\(j\\neq j'\\) the sets \\(\\{P_j,Q_j\\}\\) and\n\\(\\{P_{j'},Q_{j'}\\}\\) are disjoint, hence the lines \\(\\ell_j\\) are pairwise\ndistinct.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification that every point is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(v_a\\) from\n(4).\n\n*If \\(a> n-k\\)*, then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  From the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we\nobtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b=n+2-j\\) for some\n\\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) (namely \\(j=n+2-a-b\\)).  By construction the unique\npair of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the diagonal \\(D_j\\) that belong to\nthe sunny line \\(\\ell_j\\) are exactly \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\); one of them\ncoincides with \\((a,b)\\).  Hence \\((a,