\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\nConsequently a **non\u2011sunny** line is parallel to one of the three directions:\n\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* diagonal: \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,c)\\in\\mathcal P\\) then \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+c\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n+1-c\\).\nThus the admissible integer values of \\(a\\) are at most \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Symmetric to Lemma\u202f1. \u220e\n\n### Lemma 3  \nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{c-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of\n\\(\\mathcal P\\); in particular it contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nPoints of \\(\\mathcal P\\) on the line satisfy \\(a+b=c\\) together with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\(c\\le n+1\\).  Writing \\(a\\) determines \\(b=c-a\\); the conditions\n\\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) give \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\).  The number of integer choices for \\(a\\) is\n\\(\\lfloor (c-1)/2\\rfloor\\).  Since \\(c\\le n+1\\), this quantity never exceeds\n\\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). \u220e\n\nFrom Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 we obtain the uniform bound\n\n\\[\n\\text{any non\u2011sunny line contains at most }M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\text{ points of }\\mathcal P. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\)\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\), and let\n\\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.  The remaining \\(t=n-k\\) lines are\nnon\u2011sunny, and by (2) they together cover at most \\(tM\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\nHence\n\n\\[\ntM\\ge |\\mathcal P| \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (n-k)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k<\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) then \\(n-k>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), and the left\u2011hand side is at most\n\\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\cdot\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor < \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) (a direct check for all\n\\(n\\ge3\\)).  This contradicts the covering requirement.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\) (so \\(0\\le m\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).\n\n#### 1. Sunny lines\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x + \\frac{i}{k+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{k+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); thus each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nThe lines are distinct because they have different slopes.  Moreover the point\n\\((i,1)\\) satisfies \\(1 = \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,i + \\frac{i}{k+1}\\), i.e. \\((i,1)\\in L_i\\).\nAll points \\((i,1)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal P\\) (they satisfy\n\\(i+1\\le n+1\\) because \\(k\\le n\\)).  Hence the first \\(k\\) points of the first row of the\ntriangle are covered.\n\n#### 2. Non\u2011sunny diagonal lines\nConsider the remaining points  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P'=\\mathcal P\\setminus\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\n          =\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_j:\\; x+y = n+2-j .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_j\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is non\u2011sunny.\nThe constants \\(n+2-j\\) are distinct, so the \\(D_j\\) are distinct lines.\n\nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P'\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{n+1,n,\\dots ,n+2-m\\}\\);\nconversely, for each integer \\(s\\) in this range the set\n\\(\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P'\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) is exactly the intersection of \\(\\mathcal P'\\) with\nthe diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\).  Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P'\\) lies on exactly one of the\nlines \\(D_1,\\dots ,D_m\\).  Consequently the \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover all points of\n\\(\\mathcal P'\\).\n\n#### 3. Verification\nThe total family consists of the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) and the\n\\(m=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(D_1,\\dots ,D_m\\); all are distinct.\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is either of the form \\((i,1)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) (covered by\n\\(L_i\\)) or belongs to \\(\\mathcal P'\\) (covered by the appropriate \\(D_j\\)).\nThus the covering condition is satisfied and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\),\nthe condition (3) is also sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\Bigl\\lceil \\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\; \\Bigl\\lceil \\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\;\n\\ldots ,\\; n \\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) satisfying \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\). \u220e"
