            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence of families with any prescribed \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Boundary points.**  \n   The points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,n+1-i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   all belong to \\(T_n\\) and lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n2. **Selection of sunny lines.**  \n   Choose a subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\in I\\) define a line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr),\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(\\alpha_i\\) is an irrational number (e.g. \\(\\alpha_i=\\sqrt{2}+i\\)).  \n   Because the slope is irrational, the line \\(S_i\\) contains no other lattice point; in particular it contains no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny and covers exactly the single point \\(P_i\\).\n\n3. **Selection of non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   J=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus I,\\qquad |J|=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(j\\in J\\) take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore they are not sunny.  \n   They are distinct because the \\(x\\)-coordinates differ.\n\n4. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   *If \\(a\\le n-k\\)*, then \\(a\\in J\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n   *If \\(a> n-k\\)*, then \\(a\\in I\\); moreover \\(a+b=n+1\\) (by definition of \\(T_n\\)), so \\((a,b)=P_a\\) and it lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\mid j\\in J\\}\\).\n\nThe constructed family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all distinct, and it covers all required points.  Hence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a family exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A trivial necessary condition\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) satisfy the covering condition and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.\n\n*Each line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).*  \nIndeed, the projection of \\(T_n\\) onto the \\(x\\)-axis (or onto the \\(y\\)-axis) is the interval \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); therefore a line, whether vertical, horizontal, of slope \\(-1\\) or of any other slope, can meet the integer lattice in at most one point for each integer \\(x\\)-coordinate, giving at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n\\) lines is at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\).  \nSince \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) (see (1)), this inequality is always satisfied and does not impose a restriction on \\(k\\).\n\nThe only obvious restriction is that not all \\(n\\) lines can be sunny: a sunny line can pass through at most one point of the hypotenuse \\(x+y=n+1\\), while the triangle contains points off that hypotenuse that must be covered.  Hence at least one line must be non‑sunny, giving\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Current status\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines (construction above).\n* Any suitable family must satisfy the very weak necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) is known to contain \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and to be contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nWhether the value \\(k=n-1\\) can ever be attained, or whether a stronger universal upper bound (e.g. \\(k\\le n-2\\)) holds for all \\(n\\), remains an open question within the scope of this answer.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
