+ c\n        = \\frac{p+q}{q}\\,u + (i-1)(p+q) + c . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll three points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore each satisfies\n\\(x_i+y_i\\le n+1\\).  From (7) we obtain the two inequalities\n\n\\[\n\\frac{p+q}{q}\\,u + c \\le n+1 ,\\qquad\n\\frac{p+q}{q}\\,u + (p+q) + c \\le n+1 . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first inequality from the second yields  \n\n\\[\np+q \\le 0 . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(p+q = q(m+1)\\) and \\(q>0\\); because \\(m\\neq -1\\) we have \\(m+1\\neq 0\\),\nhence \\(p+q\\neq 0\\).  Moreover \\(m\\) is not \\(-1\\) and not \\(0\\), so\n\\(m+1>0\\) and consequently \\(p+q>0\\), contradicting (9).\n\nThus a sunny line cannot contain three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\);\nit can contain at most two. \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of points that could be\ncovered would be at most \\(2n\\).  By (1),\n\n\\[\n2n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nHence the set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) could not be covered, a contradiction.\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist; the\nvalue \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  We shall produce \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point\nof \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Non\u2011sunny (vertical) lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  The line\n\\(v_i\\) contains precisely the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\);\nall such points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) consider the anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_j=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2-j\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(j\\le k\\le n-1\\), the diagonal \\(D_j\\) lies entirely inside the\ntriangle defining \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Choose the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j,\\;n+2-2j),\\qquad\nQ_j=(j+1,\\;n-2j). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy \\(a+b=n+2-j\\) and have positive integer coordinates, so\n\\(P_j,Q_j\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and they are not vertically aligned\n(\\(x\\)-coordinates differ by \\(1\\)).\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_j\\) through \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{(n-2j)-(n+2-2j)}{(j+1)-j}= -2 . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(-2\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the line is not vertical, \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\nMoreover \\(\\ell_j\\) contains exactly the two points \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\); any\nother point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the same anti\u2011diagonal has a different\n\\(x\\)-coordinate and would give a different slope, so it cannot lie on\n\\(\\ell_j\\).\n\nFor distinct indices \\(j\\neq j'\\) the sets \\(\\{P_j,Q_j\\}\\) and\n\\(\\{P_{j'},Q_{j'}\\}\\) are disjoint, hence the lines \\(\\ell_j\\) are pairwise\ndistinct.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification that every point is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(v_a\\) from\n(9).\n\n*If \\(a> n-k\\)*, then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  From the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we\nobtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b=n+2-j\\) for a unique\n\\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) (namely \\(j=n+2-a-b\\)).  By construction the two\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the diagonal \\(D_j\\) that belong to the sunny\nline \\(\\ell_j\\) are exactly \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\); one of them coincides with\n\\((a,b)\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the\n\\(n\\) lines \\(\\{v_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-k}\\cup\\{\\ell_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\);