"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the flaw in the original proof and now present a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe argument proceeds in two stages.\n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1* (re\u2011proved) shows that a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) (triangular number), covering all points with only sunny lines would require at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Big\\lceil\\frac{|\\mathcal P_n|}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n   =\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\Big\\rceil>n\\qquad (n\\ge 3)\n   \\]\n\n   lines, which is impossible when only \\(n\\) lines are allowed. Hence a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist; therefore \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n2. **Existence for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   For a given \\(k\\) we construct explicitly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines*: the vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) cover all points whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines*: for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) we take the two lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(j,\\;n+2-2j),\\qquad Q_j=(j+1,\\;n-2j)\n   \\]\n\n   lying on the anti\u2011diagonal \\(a+b=n+2-j\\).  \n   The line through \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\) has slope \\(-2\\), which is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence it is sunny. The \\(k\\) such lines are distinct and together with the vertical lines cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nThus the values \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) are realizable, while \\(k=n\\) is not, giving the final answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(\\ell\\) in the form  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c ,\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Q,\\; m\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n(the slope is rational because \\(\\ell\\) contains at least one lattice point; the line is not vertical).\n\nFor any integer \\(s\\ge 2\\) consider the anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of a fixed \\(D_s\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to a line of slope \\(-1\\), it can intersect each\n\\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nIf two of them had the same sum \\(a_i+b_i\\), they would lie on the same\nanti\u2011diagonal, forcing \\(\\ell\\) to have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the\ndefinition of a sunny line.  Hence the three sums  \n\n\\[\ns_i:=a_i+b_i\\qquad(i=1,2,3)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct.\n\nBecause each \\((a_i,b_i)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), we have\n\\(2\\le s_i\\le n+1\\).  Consequently the three distinct integers \\(s_1,s_2,s_3\\)\nmust satisfy  \n\n\\[\n2\\le s_{\\min}\\le s_{\\max}\\le n+1,\\qquad s_{\\max}-s_{\\min}\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the differences of the coordinates of two of the points,\nsay \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\).  From (2),\n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq 0\\), the equality (4) implies \\(a_2\\neq a_1\\) and\n\\(b_2\\neq b_1\\).  Moreover, adding the two equalities \\(a_i+b_i=s_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)=s_2-s_1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (4) into (5) yields  \n\n\\[\n(a_2-a_1)+m(a_2-a_1)=s_2-s_1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n(a_2-a_1)(1+m)=s_2-s_1 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq -1\\), the factor \\(1+m\\) is a non\u2011zero rational number.\nHence \\(|a_2-a_1|=|s_2-s_1|/|1+m|\\).  The right\u2011hand side is a positive\nrational number, while the left\u2011hand side is a positive integer.\nConsequently \\(|s_2-s_1|\\) must be a multiple of \\(|1+m|\\).\n\nApply the same reasoning to the pair \\((a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) and obtain that\n\\(|s_3-s_2|\\) is also a multiple of \\(|1+m|\\).  Since the three sums are\ndistinct integers, the smallest possible positive multiple of \\(|1+m|\\) is\n\\(1\\).  Therefore \\(|1+m|\\ge 1\\), i.e. \\(|m+1|\\ge 1\\).\n\nBut from (3) we have \\(|s_3-s_1|\\ge 2\\).  Using (6) with the pair\n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_3,b_3)\\) we get  \n\n\\[\n|a_3-a_1|\\,|1+m| = |s_3-s_1|\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|a_3-a_1|\\) is a positive integer, the left\u2011hand side is at least\n\\(2|1+m|\\).  Hence \\(2|1+m|\\le |s_3-s_1|\\).  The only way this can hold with\n\\(|s_3-s_1|\\le n+1\\) and \\(|1+m|\\ge 1\\) is when \\(|a_3-a_1|=1\\) and\n\\(|s_3-s_1|=2\\).  That forces \\(|1+m|=1\\), i.e. \\(m=0\\) or \\(m=-2\\).\n\nThe case \\(m=0\\) is excluded because a sunny line cannot be horizontal.\nThus we would have \\(m=-2\\).  Substituting \\(m=-2\\) into (6) gives  \n\n\\[\n(a_2-a_1)(-1)=s_2-s_1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a_1-a_2=s_2-s_1 .\n\\]\n\nSince both sides are integers, this forces \\(a_1-a_2\\) and \\(s_2-s_1\\) to have\nthe same sign, contradicting the fact that \\(a_2>a_1\\) (otherwise the line\nwould be vertical).  Hence our assumption that three points lie on a sunny\nline leads to a contradiction.\n\nTherefore any sunny line meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most two points. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Consequence: \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each could cover at most two points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by Lemma\u202f1.  Hence at most \\(2n\\) points could be covered.\nFrom (1),\n\n\\[\n2n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThus the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) cannot be covered with only sunny lines,\nso a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist.  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Non\u2011sunny (vertical) lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  The line \\(v_i\\)\ncontains precisely the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); all such\npoints belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) consider the anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_j=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2-j\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(j\\le k\\le n-1\\), the diagonal \\(D_j\\) lies inside the triangle\ndefining \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Choose the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j,\\;n+2-2j),\\qquad Q_j=(j+1,\\;n-2j) . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy \\(a+b=n+2-j\\) and have positive integer coordinates, so\n\\(P_j,Q_j\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and they are not vertically aligned.\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_j\\) through \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{(n-2j)-(n+2-2j)}{(j+1)-j}= -2 . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(-2\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the line is not vertical, \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\nMoreover \\(\\ell_j\\) contains exactly the two points \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\); any\nother point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the same anti\u2011diagonal would give a\ndifferent slope and therefore cannot lie on \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\nFor distinct indices \\(j\\neq j'\\) the sets \\(\\{P_j,Q_j\\}\\) and\n\\(\\{P_{j'},Q_{j'}\\}\\) are disjoint, so the lines \\(\\ell_j\\) are pairwise\ndistinct.\n\n#### 3.3  Verification that every point is covered  \n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(v_a\\) from (8).\n\n*If \\(a> n-k\\)*, then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  From the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a+b=n+2-j\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) (namely\n\\(j=n+2-a-b\\)).  By construction the two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the\nanti\u2011diagonal \\(D_j\\) that belong to the sunny line \\(\\ell_j\\) are exactly\n\\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\); one of them coincides with \\((a,b)\\).  Consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the\n\\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\{v_i\\}_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}\\cup\\{\\ell_j\\}_{j=1}^{\\,k}.\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\); exactly the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(\\ell_j\\) are sunny, while the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are non\u2011sunny.\nAll lines are distinct, completing the construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* No configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny exists, so \\(k=n\\) is\n  impossible.\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a\n  configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition\n  and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]"

