 of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny (vertical) lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\nThe line \\(L_i\\) contains precisely the points \\((i,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); all such points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines for the remaining points  \n\nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a>n-k\\), then from\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le k . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus there are at most \\(k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate\nexceeds \\(n-k\\).  List all of them (some may be absent) as\n\\((a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_k,b_k)\\).\n\nFor each existing point \\((a_j,b_j)\\) choose the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y=\\sqrt{2}\\,(x-a_j)+b_j . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is irrational; consequently the line is not parallel\nto the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence each\n\\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\nBecause the slope is irrational, a line of the form (8) cannot contain any\nother lattice point: if \\((x',y')\\) with integer coordinates satisfied the\nequation, then \\((y'-b_j)/(x'-a_j)=\\sqrt{2}\\) would be rational, a\ncontradiction.  Therefore each \\(S_j\\) contains exactly the single point\n\\((a_j,b_j)\\).\n\nThe lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-k}\\cup\\{S_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) are distinct\n(the \\(L_i\\) are vertical, the \\(S_j\\) have irrational slope), their total\nnumber is \\((n-k)+k=n\\), and precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny.\n\nFinally, every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of these\nlines: points with \\(x\\le n-k\\) lie on a vertical line \\(L_i\\); points with\n\\(x>n-k\\) are among the \\((a_j,b_j)\\) and lie on the corresponding sunny\nline \\(S_j\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have exhibited a configuration satisfying both\nrequirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists (inequality (5)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration of \\(n\\)\n  distinct lines containing all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and having exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines does exist (construction in §3).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
