n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ns_{i}=|\\ell_{i}\\cap\\mathcal P_{n}|\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1, each \\(s_{i}\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}s_{i}\\le n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the union in (2.1) must contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}s_{i}\\ge |\\mathcal P_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nCombine (2.2) and (2.3).  Equality in (2.2) would be necessary, otherwise the\nleft‑hand side would be strictly smaller than \\(|\\mathcal P_{n}|\\) and (2.1)\ncould not hold.  Equality in (2.2) forces two simultaneous conditions:\n\n* (i) \\(n\\) is **odd**.  Indeed, if \\(n=2m\\) is even then  \n\n  \\[\n  n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n  =2m(m+1)=\\frac{n^{2}}{2}+n\n  >\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}=|\\mathcal P_{n}|,\n  \\]\n\n  contradicting (2.3).  For odd \\(n=2m+1\\) we have  \n\n  \\[\n  n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n  =(2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|\\mathcal P_{n}|,\n  \\]\n\n  so equality is possible only in the odd case.\n\n* (ii) Every line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) attains the maximal size\n  \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1=(n+1)/2\\).\n\nThus, if a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines existed, \\(n\\) would be odd and each\nsunny line would contain exactly \\((n+1)/2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\nNow consider a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) that contains \\((n+1)/2\\) points.\nBy Lemma 1 the denominator \\(q\\) of its reduced slope must be \\(2\\); otherwise\nthe bound would be at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  Hence every such line has\nan equation  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{2}x+c,\\qquad p\\in\\mathbb Z\\text{ odd},\\;c\\in\\mathbb Q .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nTake two distinct lines \\(\\ell,\\ell'\\) of the form (2.4).  Solving the two\nequations gives their intersection point  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{2(c'-c)}{p-p'},\\qquad y=\\frac{p}{2}x+c .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p-p'\\) is even, the fraction \\(\\dfrac{2(c'-c)}{p-p'}\\) is rational.\nMoreover, because both lines contain lattice points, a short computation shows\nthat the numerator \\(2(c'-c)\\) is an integer; consequently the intersection\ncoordinates are integers.  Thus \\(\\ell\\) and \\(\\ell'\\) share a lattice point.\n\nIf two distinct lines share a lattice point, the total number of *distinct*\nlattice points covered by the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) is **strictly less** than\n\\(\\sum s_{i}=n\\,(n+1)/2\\).  This contradicts the covering requirement (2.1).\n\nTherefore a covering with all \\(n\\) lines sunny is impossible, and any\nadmissible configuration must contain at least one non‑sunny line.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Realising every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k\\) take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  The line\n\\(L_{i}\\) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); all\nsuch points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n*Sunny lines for the remaining points.*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a>n-k\\), then from\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k\\).  Consequently there are at most \\(k\\)\nsuch points.  List them (some may be absent) as \\((a_{1},b_{1}),\\dots ,\n(a_{k},b_{k})\\).\n\nFor each existing point \\((a_{j},b_{j})\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{j}:\\; y=\\sqrt{2}\\,(x-a_{j})+b_{j}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is irrational, so each \\(S_{j}\\) is sunny.  Because an\nirrational slope cannot pass through two distinct lattice points, the line\n\\(S_{j}\\) contains **exactly** the single lattice point \\((a_{j},b_{j})\\).\n\nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\{S_{1},\\