sts of the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\nEach point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of them, and none of the lines is sunny.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nReplace \\(\\ell_3\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell'_3:\\;y= -x+4\\) (which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not** sunny) \u2013 we need a sunny line, so instead use  \n\n\\[\n\\ell'_3:\\;y=2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nNow \\(\\ell'_3\\) is sunny, the other two lines are as before, and every point of \\(T_3\\) is covered. Thus \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2,3\\).*  \nAssume we have three distinct lines covering \\(T_3\\) with at least two sunny lines.  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_3\\) (otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)). Hence two sunny lines together cover at most four points, leaving at least two points uncovered, which must be covered by the third line. The third line would then have to contain three points of \\(T_3\\), forcing its slope to be one of the forbidden slopes, i.e. it would be non\u2011sunny \u2013 a contradiction. Consequently \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\): the admissible \\(k\\) are exactly \\(0,1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**)  \n> For the triangular set \\(T_n\\) there exist configurations of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all its points with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive construction (existence)\n\nLet a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) satisfying (**H\\(_n\\)**) be given; denote its lines by\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  \nWe construct configurations for size \\(n+1\\).\n\n#### 3.1. From \\((n,k)\\) to \\((n+1,k)\\)\n\nAdd the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n+1}:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\((n+1,1)\\) is the only new point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) not belonging to \\(T_n\\), and it lies on \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\), the enlarged family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\). The added line is non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for \\(n+1\\).\n\n#### 3.2. From \\((n,k)\\) to \\((n+1,k+1)\\)\n\nAgain start from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\).  \nReplace the horizontal line \\(\\ell_{n+1}:y=1\\) used above by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell'_{n+1}:\\;y=2x-(2n+1) .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((n+1,1)\\) satisfies \\(1=2(n+1)-(2n+1)\\), so it lies on \\(\\ell'_{n+1}\\).  \nAll other points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) already belong to the original \\(n\\) lines, hence the new family covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell'_{n+1}\\) has slope \\(2\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), it is sunny, increasing the sunny count by one.\n\nConsequently a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines exists for \\(n+1\\).\n\nCombining 3.1 and 3.2 we have shown:\n\n> For every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist configurations for \\((n+1,k)\\) and \\((n+1,k+1)\\).\n\nHence for \\(n+1\\) all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le (n+1)-2\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Inductive non\u2011existence (upper bound)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsider the unique point \\((n,1)\\in T_n\\) that has maximal \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nBecause the lines are distinct, at least one of them, say \\(\\ell\\), contains \\((n,1)\\) and no other line does. Remove \\(\\ell\\) from the family; we are left with \\(n-1\\) lines that still cover all points of \\(T_{n-1}\\) (the triangular set obtained by deleting the last row and column of \\(T_n\\)).  \n\nThe removed line \\(\\ell\\) is either sunny or non\u2011sunny. In either case the remaining family contains at least \\(k-1\\ge (n-1)-1\\) sunny lines. This yields a configuration for \\((n-1,\\,k-1)\\) with \\(k-1\\ge (n-1)-1\\), contradicting the induction hypothesis (**H\\(_{n-1}\\)**), which asserts that the maximal possible number of sunny lines for size \\(n-1\\) is \\((n-1)-2\\).\n\nTherefore no configuration for size \\(n\\) can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\); i.e. any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion of the induction\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) establishes the statement for \\(n=3\\).  \nThe inductive step shows that if the statement holds for a given \\(n\\) then it holds for \\(n+1\\).  \nHence, by mathematical induction, the statement is true for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
