            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation\n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nFor a given \\(n\\) we denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C(n,k)\n\\]\n\nthe statement “there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny”.\n\nOur goal is to determine, for each \\(n\\ge3\\), the set of \\(k\\) for which \\(\\mathcal C(n,k)\\) holds.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains six points.\n\n#### 2.1  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of them, and none of the lines is sunny. Hence \\(\\mathcal C(3,0)\\) holds.\n\n#### 2.2  Existence for \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) as above and replace \\(\\ell_3\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell'_3:\\;y=2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_3\\) are covered as follows:\n\n* \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\);\n* \\((1,2),(1,3)\\) lie on \\(\\ell_2\\);\n* \\((2,1),(3,1)\\) lie on \\(\\ell_1\\);\n* \\((2,2)\\) satisfies \\(2=2\\cdot2-1\\) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell'_3\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal C(3,1)\\) holds.\n\n#### 2.3  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\n*Lemma 2.3.1.*  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contains three distinct points of \\(T_3\\), those points have integer coordinates and satisfy \\(a+b\\le4\\). The differences of the coordinates of any two of them are integers belonging to \\(\\{-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3\\}\\). Computing the slope from any pair yields a rational number whose numerator and denominator are integers of absolute value at most \\(3\\). The only slopes that can be realised by three points of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le4\\) are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=4\\)). Hence a line with a different slope (i.e. a sunny line) can meet \\(T_3\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\nAssume a configuration of three distinct lines covering \\(T_3\\) has at least two sunny lines. By the lemma each sunny line covers at most two points, so together they cover at most four points. The remaining (at least) two points must be covered by the third line, which therefore contains at least three points of \\(T_3\\). By the same reasoning as in the lemma, such a line must have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), i.e. it is not sunny – a contradiction. Consequently \\(\\mathcal C(3,k)\\) is false for \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are exactly \\(k=0,1\\), i.e. \\(0\\le k\\le 3-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive hypothesis\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) we assume\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H_n:\\ \\mathcal C(n,k)\\text{ holds }\\Longleftrightarrow\\ 0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nOur aim is to prove \\(H_{n+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Inductive constructions (existence)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies \\(\\mathcal C(n,k)\\) for some \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nRecall that  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}=T_n\\cup\\{(n+1,1)\\}\\cup\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2,\\ a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe only points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that are not already in \\(T_n\\) are those on the new “border’’ \\(a+b=n+2\\). In particular the point \\((n+1,1)\\) is new.\n\n#### 4.1  Obtaining \\(\\mathcal C(n+1,k)\\)\n\nAdd the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n+1}: y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((n+1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\); every other new point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) and \\(a\\le n\\) satisfies \\(b\\ge2\\) and therefore already belongs to some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (because those points were already in \\(T_n\\)). Hence the enlarged family \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{\\ell_{n+1}\\}\\) covers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\). The added line has slope \\(0\\), so it is not sunny; the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal C(n+1,k)\\) holds.\n\n#### 4.2  Obtaining \\(\\mathcal C(n+1,k+1)\\)\n\nReplace the line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) introduced above by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell'_{n+1}: y=2x-(2n+1) .\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n1 = 2(n+1)-(2n+1),\n\\]\nso \\((n+1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell'_{n+1}\\). The slope of \\(\\ell'_{n+1}\\) is \\(2\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell'_{n+1}\\) is sunny. As before, all other points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\). Consequently the family \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{\\ell'_{n+1}\\}\\) satisfies \\(\\mathcal C(n+1,k+1)\\).\n\nFrom 4.1 and 4.2 we have proved:\n\n> **Lemma 4.3.**  \n> If \\(\\mathcal C(n,k)\\) holds for some \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), then both \\(\\mathcal C(n+1,k)\\) and \\(\\mathcal C(n+1,k+1)\\) hold.\n\nIterating this lemma starting from the base case \\(H_3\\) yields that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) the statement \\(\\mathcal C(n,k)\\) is true. This gives the **existence** part of the theorem.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Upper bound (non‑existence)\n\nWe now show that for any \\(n\\ge3\\) it is impossible to have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsider the point \\((n,1)\\in T_n\\) (the rightmost point of the bottom row). Because the lines are distinct, at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\); denote such a line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\nRemove \\(\\ell\\) from the family and denote the remaining set by \\(\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\). We claim that \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers all points of \\(T_{n-1}\\).\n\nIndeed, any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n-1}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\). Since \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to \\(T_n\\), it was covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). If that covering line were \\(\\ell\\), then \\((a,b)\\) would lie on \\(\\ell\\) together with \\((n,1)\\). The line \\(\\ell\\) therefore contains two points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum of coordinates differ by at most \\(n-1\\). A direct inspection shows that the only lines that can contain both \\((n,1)\\) and another point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) are the three non‑sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)). Consequently, if \\(\\ell\\) were sunny, it could not pass through any point of \\(T_{n-1}\\); all points of \\(T_{n-1}\\) would then be covered by lines of \\(\\mathcal L'\\). If \\(\\ell\\) is non‑sunny, the same conclusion holds because the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines already cover \\(T_{n-1}\\) (the points of the new bottom row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid a\\le n-1\\}\\) are covered by the vertical line \\(x=n\\) or by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), both of which belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and are not \\(\\ell\\)). Hence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) indeed covers \\(T_{n-1}\\).\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) lines and contains at least \\((n-1)-1=n-2\\) sunny lines (because we removed at most one sunny line). This contradicts the induction hypothesis \\(H_{n-1}\\), which asserts that a configuration for size \\(n-1\\) can have at most \\((n-1)-2=n-3\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines exists for \\(T_n\\). Equivalently,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) has been settled in Section 2, establishing \\(H_3\\).  \nLemma 4.3 provides the inductive step from \\(H_n\\) to \\(H_{n+1}\\) for the existence part, while Section 5 gives the necessary upper bound. Consequently \\(H_n\\) holds for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
