{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the sunny line be given by \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) on this line, then  \n\n\\[\nb-ma=c=b'-ma'.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nb-b'=m(a-a').\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,a',b,b'\\) are integers, the right–hand side is an integer multiple of \\(m\\).  \nIf \\(m\\) were rational, write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms; then \\(q\\mid (a-a')\\) and \\(p\\mid (b-b')\\).  \nBecause \\(a,a',b,b'\\) all lie in the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the differences \\(|a-a'|\\) and \\(|b-b'|\\) are strictly less than \\(n\\).  \nThe only way for a non‑zero rational \\(m\\) with denominator \\(q\\ge 2\\) to satisfy the divisibility conditions is for \\(|a-a'|\\ge q\\), contradicting the bound \\(|a-a'|<n\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\nIf \\(m\\) is irrational, the equality \\(b-b'=m(a-a')\\) cannot hold for two distinct integer pairs.  \n\nThus a sunny line cannot pass through two distinct points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nAny line that is **not** sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b_0\\) with \\(1\\le b_0\\le n\\), then the admissible points on it are \\((a,b_0)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_0\\); there are exactly \\(n+1-b_0\\le n\\) such points.  \nThe vertical case is symmetric.  \n\nIf the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Main Argument (Proof by Contradiction)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing **at least** \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk:=|\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal S\\) the set of sunny lines and by \\(\\mathcal N\\) the set of non‑sunny lines; thus  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S|=k,\\qquad |\\mathcal N|=n-k\\le 1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most one point of \\(T_n\\); consequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by all sunny lines is at most \\(k\\).  \n\nAll remaining points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered by the lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nIf \\(\\mathcal N\\) is empty, then at most \\(k\\le n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, contradicting (1) because \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal N|=1\\); let \\(L_0\\) be the unique non‑sunny line.  \nBy Lemma 2, \\(L_0\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\nk + n \\le (n-1)+n = 2n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBut from (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\ge \\frac{3\\cdot4}{2}=6\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-1\\quad\\text{for all }n\\ge 4.\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(2n-1)=\\frac{n^2-n+2}{2}>0\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \n\nThe contradiction shows that our assumption \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible. Consequently any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\nSince \