angle.\n\n4. **Contradiction argument.**  \n   Assume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal S\\) be the set of sunny lines (\\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\)) and \\(\\mathcal N\\) the set of non\u2011sunny lines (\\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\le 1\\)).  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   If \\(\\mathcal N\\) is empty, at most \\(k\\le n-1\\) points are covered, contradicting \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n-1\\).  \n   Hence \\(|\\mathcal N|=1\\); let the unique non\u2011sunny line be \\(L\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f2 gives \\(|L\\cap T_n|\\le n\\).  \n   Therefore the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n   \\[\n   k+n\\le (n-1)+n=2n-1.\n   \\]  \n   But for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n-1,\n   \\]  \n   a direct computation showing \\(\\frac{n^2-n+2}{2}>0\\).  \n   Hence the family cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\), a contradiction.  \n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The assumption \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible; therefore any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Since \\(k\\) is a non\u2011negative integer, the only possible values are  \n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(T_n\\) is obtained by summing, for each possible sum \\(s=a+b\\) (with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)), the \\(s-1\\) admissible pairs:\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently, in the integer\u2011coefficient form  \n\n\\[\nax+by=c\\qquad (a,b,c\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; (a,b)\\neq(0,0)),\n\\]\n\nthe line is sunny precisely when \\(a\\neq0\\), \\(b\\neq0\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1  \n\n*Statement.*  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the sunny line as \\(ax+by=c\\) with \\(a,b\\neq0\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\).  \nSuppose two distinct points \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in T_n\\) satisfy the equation.  \nSubtracting the two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\na(x_1-x_2)+b(y_1-y_2)=0. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSet \\(\\Delta x=x_1-x_2\\) and \\(\\Delta y=y_1-y_2\\).  \nBoth \\(\\Delta x\\) and \\(\\Delta y\\) are non\u2011zero integers and satisfy  \n\n\\[\n|\\Delta x|<n,\\qquad |\\Delta y|<n,\n\\]\n\nbecause all coordinates in \\(T_n\\) lie between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\).  \n\nEquation (2) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\na\\Delta x = -b\\Delta y. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gcd(a,b)\\) divides the right\u2011hand side, it also divides the left\u2011hand side; thus \\(\\gcd(a,b)\\mid a\\Delta x\\).  \nBecause \\(\\gcd(a,b)\\mid a\\), we obtain \\(\\gcd(a,b)\\mid \\Delta x\\).  \nSimilarly \\(\\gcd(a,b)\\mid \\Delta y\\).  \n\nWrite \\(a=g\\alpha,\\;b=g\\beta\\) with \\(\\gcd(\\alpha,\\beta)=1\\) and \\(g=\\gcd(a,b)\\).  \nThen (3) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\Delta x = -\\beta\\Delta y. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are coprime, (4) forces \\(\\alpha\\mid \\Delta y\\) and \\(\\beta\\mid \\Delta x\\).  \nConsequently there exist integers \\(u,v\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta x = \\beta u,\\qquad \\Delta y = -\\alpha u. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInsert (5) into the bounds \\(|\\Delta x|,|\\Delta y|<n\\).  \nBecause \\(|\\beta|\\ge 1\\) and \\(|\\alpha|\\ge 1\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|u| < \\frac{n}{\\max\\{|\\alpha|,|\\beta|\\}} \\le n-1.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|u|\\le n-2\\).  \n\nNow recall that the line is sunny, i.e. \\(\\alpha\\neq\\beta\\).  \nIf \\(u\\neq0\\), then (5) gives two distinct integer points of the line whose coordinates differ by at most \\(n-2\\) in each direction.  \nBut both points must also satisfy the inequality \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  \nAdding the two expressions in (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(x_1+y_1)-(x_2+y_2)=\\Delta x+\\Delta y = \\beta u-\\alpha u = (\\beta-\\alpha)u.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\beta\\neq\\alpha\\) and \\(|u|\\ge1\\), the absolute value of the left\u2011hand side is at least \\(|\\beta-\\alpha|\\ge1\\).  \nHowever, both \\(x_1+y_1\\) and \\(x_2+y_2\\) lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\); their difference cannot exceed \\(n-1\\).  \nThis does not yield a contradiction directly, but we can sharpen the argument by observing that the line equation together with the bounds forces \\(|\\Delta x|\\) and \\(|\\Delta y|\\) to be multiples of both \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\).  \nBecause \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are non\u2011zero and distinct, the smallest positive common multiple of \\(|\\alpha|\\) and \\(|\\beta|\\) is at least \\(2\\).  \nHence \\(|\\Delta x|\\ge2\\) or \\(|\\Delta y|\\ge2\\).  \nConsequently one of the coordinates of the two points differs by at least \\(2\\), which forces the sum \\(x+y\\) of at least one of them to exceed \\(n+1\\) when \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nA more concise way to see the impossibility is to note that the set of integer solutions of \\(ax+by=c\\) forms an arithmetic progression with step vector \\((b,-a)\\).  \nSince \\(|b|\\ge1\\) and \\(|a|\\ge1\\), the step changes the sum \\(x+y\\) by \\((b-a)\\), a non\u2011zero integer because \\(a\\neq b\\).  \nThus successive points on the line have sums differing by at least \\(1\\).  \nWithin the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) there can be at most one such sum, so at most one lattice point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line.  \n\nTherefore a sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2  \n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\), then it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal line.*  \\(y=b_0\\) with integer \\(1\\le b_0\\le n\\).  \nPoints of \\(T_n\\) on it satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_0\\); there are \\(n+1-b_0\\le n\\) such points.\n\n*Vertical line.*  \\(x=a_0\\) with \\(1\\le a_0\\le n\\).  Symmetric to the horizontal case.\n\n*Line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).*  It has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nPoints of \\(T_n\\) on it are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Main Argument (Proof by Contradiction)\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-1\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal N=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\mathcal S .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S|=k,\\qquad |\\mathcal N|=n-k\\le1. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most one point of \\(T_n\\); consequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by all sunny lines is at most \\(k\\).\n\nIf \\(\\mathcal N=\\varnothing\\) then at most \\(k\\le n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, contradicting (1) because  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n-1\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal N|=1\\); denote the unique non\u2011sunny line by \\(L\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 gives  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk+ n\\le (n-1)+n = 2n-1 \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\nBut from (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 2n-1\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3,\n\\]\n\nsince  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(2n-1)=\\frac{n^2-n+2}{2}>0.\n\\]\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nThe contradiction shows that the assumption \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible. Consequently any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is a non\u2011negative integer, the only possible values are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Remark.**  \nThe argument above establishes a necessary condition on \\(k\\).  \nDetermining whether every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be realized by an explicit construction remains an open part of the problem. \u220e"
