\\) has been constructed.  \nThe new points that appear in \\(T_n\\) but not in \\(T_{n-1}\\) are  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) lie on the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d(n+1):x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWe now define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n^{(0)}=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}^{(0)}\\cup\\{\\ell_v(n)\\}, &\\text{if }\\ell_d(n+1)\\in\\mathcal L_{n-1}^{(0)},\\\\[4pt]\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}^{(0)}\\cup\\{\\ell_v(n),\\;\\ell_d(n+1)\\}\\setminus\\{\\ell_h(1)\\},\n &\\text{if }\\ell_d(n+1)\\notin\\mathcal L_{n-1}^{(0)} .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nExplanation of the two cases:\n\n1. **Diagonal already present.**  \n   The line \\(\\ell_d(n+1)\\) already covers every point of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Adding the new vertical line \\(\\ell_v(n)\\) (which is distinct from all previous vertical lines because its abscissa is larger) gives a total of \\((n-1)+1=n\\) lines.\n\n2. **Diagonal missing.**  \n   We need the diagonal \\(\\ell_d(n+1)\\) to cover the points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Adding both \\(\\ell_v(n)\\) and \\(\\ell_d(n+1)\\) would give \\(n+1\\) lines, so we delete one previously used line that is *not* needed any more.  \n   The horizontal line \\(\\ell_h(1)\\) (i.e. \\(y=1\\)) is never needed after the first step, because every point with \\(b=1\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\) lies on a vertical line \\(x=a\\) that will appear later, and the point \\((1,1)\\) is already covered by the diagonal \\(\\ell_d(2)\\) which is present from the base case.  \n   Hence removing \\(\\ell_h(1)\\) keeps the covering property while restoring the total number of lines to \\(n\\).\n\nIn both sub‑cases the lines added are non‑sunny (vertical or diagonal), all lines remain distinct, and every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered:\n\n* Points already in \\(T_{n-1}\\) stay covered because we never delete a line that is the *only* line covering any of them (the only line ever removed, \\(\\ell_h(1)\\), covered only \\((1,1)\\), which is also on \\(\\ell_d(2)\\)).  \n* Points of \\(S_n\\) are covered either by the already present diagonal \\(\\ell_d(n+1)\\) (case 1) or by the newly added diagonal \\(\\ell_d(n+1)\\) (case 2).  \n\nThus the inductive construction succeeds, and by induction we obtain for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) a family \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(0)}\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma 2 – adding sunny lines without destroying the covering  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any finite family of lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nBecause \\(T_n\\) is a finite set, the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\bigcup_{P\\in T_n}\\{\\,\\text{all lines passing through }P\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nis a finite union of one‑parameter families; consequently its complement in the plane is dense and infinite.\n\nChoose a line \\(\\ell\\) satisfying the three conditions:\n\n1. \\(\\ell\\) does **not** pass through any point of \\(T_n\\) (so \\(\\ell\\notin\\mathcal P\\));\n2. \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to any line already in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n3. \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to any of the three non‑sunny directions (otherwise it would be non‑sunny).\n\nSuch a line exists because there are infinitely many directions and, for each direction, only finitely many positions that would make the line intersect \\(T_n\\).  \n\nAdding \\(\\ell\\) to \\(\\mathcal L\\) does not affect the covering of \\(T_n\\) (condition 1) and makes \\(\\ell\\) a sunny line (condition 3). Moreover, condition 2 guarantees that all lines in the enlarged family remain distinct. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* Start with the family \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(0)}\\) given by Lemma 1. It contains \\(0\\) sunny lines and \\(n\\) lines in total.\n* Apply Lemma 2 repeatedly \\(k\\) times. At each step we add a new sunny line that avoids all points of \\(T_n\\) and is not parallel to any line already present. After the \\(k\\)-th addition we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) consisting of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and which still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nBecause the process never removes lines, the total number of lines stays equal to \\(n\\). The construction works for every \\(k\\) in the allowed range.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Exhaustiveness of the answer  \n\n*The lower bound.*  \nBy Lemma 1 the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n*The upper bound.*  \nA family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\n*All intermediate values.*  \nSection 4 shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a suitable family.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. ∎"
