that the right\u2011hand side is an integer for every\n   admissible pair \\((a,b)\\) is possible: take\n   \\[\n   m_i=2\\quad\\text{for all }i .\n   \\]\n   Then \\(\\ell_i\\) is the line \\(y-1=2(x-i)\\), i.e. \\(y=2x-2i+1\\).  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have\n   \\[\n   b=2a-2i+1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad i=a-\\frac{b-1}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\), the integer\n   \\[\n   i:=a-\\frac{b-1}{2}\n   \\]\n   satisfies \\(1\\le i\\le n\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\),\n   and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. Thus \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3 (Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one).**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \n   Suppose \\(\\ell\\) is a sunny line that does **not** intersect \\(T_n\\) and is not\n   parallel to any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   Then there exists a line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) such that\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\n   \\]\n   still covers \\(T_n\\). Moreover the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is\n   exactly one larger than in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Because \\(\\ell\\) avoids \\(T_n\\), every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by\n   \\(\\mathcal L\\) alone. Consider the set\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C=\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ contains no point of }T_n\\}.\n   \\]\n   If \\(\\mathcal C\\neq\\varnothing\\) we may take any \\(L\\in\\mathcal C\\); removing it does\n   not affect the covering, and adding \\(\\ell\\) yields the desired family.\n\n   It remains to show that \\(\\mathcal C\\neq\\varnothing\\) for the families we shall\n   construct. In the constructions below we will always keep at least one line\n   (a horizontal line \\(y=n+2\\), for instance) that lies completely above the\n   triangle \\(T_n\\); such a line belongs to \\(\\mathcal C\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n\n   *Base families.*  \n   - \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}:=\\mathcal V_n\\) (Lemma\u202f1) contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \n   - \\(\\mathcal L^{(n)}:=\\mathcal S_n\\) (Lemma\u202f2) contains \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume we have a covering \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\) (\\(0\\le t<n\\)) that contains exactly\n   \\(t\\) sunny lines.  \n   1. Add the horizontal line\n      \\[\n      h:=\\{y=n+2\\},\n      \\]\n      which does not meet \\(T_n\\) and is not parallel to any line already present.\n   2. Choose a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) that also avoids \\(T_n\\) and is not parallel to any\n      line of the enlarged family \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\cup\\{h\\}\\). For instance,\n      \\(\\ell\\) can be the line \\(y=2x+(n+3)\\); its slope \\(2\\) is sunny and the\n      intercept is large enough to keep the line above the triangle.\n   3. Apply Lemma\u202f3 to replace the horizontal line \\(h\\) by \\(\\ell\\).  \n      The resulting family \\(\\mathcal L^{(t+1)}\\) still consists of \\(n\\) distinct\n      lines, now contains \\(t+1\\) sunny lines, and still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Repeating the step \\(k\\) times transforms \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}\\) into a family\n   \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Because the step can be\n   performed as long as \\(t<n\\), we obtain families for every integer\n   \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n6. **Upper bound.**  \n   A collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so\n   no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f1, Lemma\u202f2 and the inductive construction of Lemma\u202f5 we have shown\nthat **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized, and that no\nlarger \\(k\\) can occur. Hence the set of admissible values is exactly\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). \u220e"
