vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, which *is* a forbidden direction, so they are **non‑sunny** as intended). The statement is correct and fully justified.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2 (Covering with all sunny lines)\n\nThe lemma attempts to construct \\(n\\) sunny lines that together cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### Step 1 – Definition of the lines\n> “For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) choose a distinct slope \\(m_i\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\). Define the line \\(\\ell_i:\\; y-1=m_i(x-i)\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe definition is clear; each \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\((i,1)\\) and, by choice of slope, is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(m_i\\neq0\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(m_i\\neq\\infty\\)), nor the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(m_i\\neq-1\\)). Thus each \\(\\ell_i\\) is indeed sunny. No problem here.\n\n#### Step 2 – Claim of coverage\n> “For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\); the line \\(\\ell_a\\) passes through the point \\((a,1)\\) and, because its slope is non‑zero, also through the point \\((a,b)\\): indeed,\n> \\[\n> b-1=m_a(a-a)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b=1 .\n> \\]”\n\n**Critical Error:**  \nThe algebraic manipulation is wrong. Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_a\\) yields \\(y-1=m_a(a-a)=0\\), which forces **\\(y=1\\)**. Hence \\(\\ell_a\\) contains only the point \\((a,1)\\) among points with abscissa \\(a\\). It does **not** automatically contain \\((a,b)\\) for arbitrary \\(b\\). The conclusion “the line also passes through \\((a,b)\\)” is false unless \\(b=1\\). Therefore the claim that the family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\) is unsupported. This error invalidates the entire Lemma 2.\n\n#### Step 3 – Attempted fix with a specific slope\n> “Choosing the slopes \\(m_i\\) so that the right‑hand side is an integer for every admissible pair \\((a,b)\\) is possible: take \\(m_i=2\\) for all \\(i\\). Then \\(\\ell_i\\) is the line \\(y=2x-2i+1\\). For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have\n> \\[\n> b=2a-2i+1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad i=a-\\frac{b-1}{2}.\n> \\]”\n\n**Critical Error:**  \nEven with \\(m_i=2\\), the equation \\(b=2a-2i+1\\) must hold for some integer \\(i\\) between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\). The solution asserts that for every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) the quantity \\(i=a-\\frac{b-1}{2}\\) is an integer in \\([1,n]\\). However, \\(a-\\frac{b-1}{2}\\) need not be integral (e.g., \\((a,b)=(2,2)\\) gives \\(i=2-\\frac{1}{2}=1.5\\)). Moreover, even when it is integral, there is no guarantee that the resulting line \\(\\ell_i\\) actually passes through \\((a,b)\\) because the derivation assumes the existence of such an \\(i\\) a priori. Hence the construction does **not** cover all points of \\(T_n\\). This reinforces the failure of Lemma 2.\n\n**Conclusion for Lemma 2:** Because the core claim of coverage is false, Lemma 2 is invalid. This is a **Critical Error** that undermines any later argument relying on the existence of an all‑sunny covering.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 3 (Replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny one)\n\n> “Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\). Suppose \\(\\ell\\) is a sunny line that does **not** intersect \\(T_n\\) and is not parallel to any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). Then there exists a line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) such that \\(\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).”\n\n**Justification Gap:**  \nThe proof proceeds by defining \\(\\mathcal C=\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ contains no point of }T_n\\}\\) and claims \\(\\mathcal C\\neq\\varnothing\\). However, for the two base families introduced earlier:\n\n* In \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) (all vertical lines), each line \\(x=i\\) contains the whole column of points \\(\\{(i,b):1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\), so **every** line meets \\(T_n\\); thus \\(\\mathcal C=\\varnothing\\).\n* In the (flawed) sunny family \\(\\mathcal S_n\\), each line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\((i,1)\\) and, as shown, does not necessarily meet other points of \\(T_n\\); nevertheless, the construction does not guarantee the existence of a line completely disjoint from \\(T_n\\).\n\nTherefore the lemma’s prerequisite that such an \\(L\\) exists is not justified for the families under consideration. The statement “It remains to show that \\(\\mathcal C\\neq\\varnothing\\) for the families we shall construct. In the constructions below we will always keep at least one line (a horizontal line \\(y=n+2\\), for instance) that lies completely above the triangle \\(T_n\\); such a line belongs to \\(\\mathcal C\\).” merely **postpones** the problem: the horizontal line is introduced **after** the lemma, not part of the original \\(\\mathcal L\\). Hence the lemma, as used, rests on an unproven assumption—a justification gap.\n\n---\n\n### Inductive Construction (Step 5)\n\nThe solution attempts to start from the all‑vertical (0 sunny) family and, by repeatedly adding a sunny line that avoids \\(T_n\\) and removing a line that also avoids \\(T_n\\), increase the number of sunny lines.\n\n* The process first **adds** a horizontal line \\(h: y=n+2\\) to the current family, creating a family of size \\(n+1\\).  \n* Then it **chooses** a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) also avoiding \\(T_n\\) and applies Lemma 3 to replace \\(h\\) by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n**Justification Gaps:**\n1. Adding \\(h\\) temporarily violates the requirement that the family contain exactly \\(n\\) lines; the subsequent removal relies on Lemma 3, which, as noted, requires the existence of a line in the *original* family that does not meet \\(T_n\\). In the base case \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) there is none, so Lemma 3 cannot be invoked directly.\n2. The argument that “we will always keep at least one line (a horizontal line \\(y=n+2\\)) that lies completely above the triangle” is circular: this line is precisely the one being added in the current step, not a pre‑existing member of the family.\n3. Even assuming Lemma 3 could be applied, the construction never guarantees that the resulting family after each replacement still covers all points of \\(T_n\\); the proof that coverage is preserved hinges on the faulty Lemma 3.\n\nThus the inductive step is not rigorously established.\n\n---\n\n### Upper Bound\n\n> “A collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no \\(k>n\\) is possible.”\n\n**Verification:**  \nTrivially, with \\(n\\) lines you cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. This statement is correct, though it adds nothing substantive to the problem.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Conclusion\n\nThe solution’s core construction for the case \\(k=n\\) (Lemma 2) is incorrect, and the mechanism for moving from a covering with \\(t\\) sunny lines to one with \\(t+1\\) sunny lines (Lemma 3 and the inductive step) contains unjustified assumptions. Because the existence of a covering with *all* lines sunny is not established, the subsequent claim that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable lacks a valid foundation.\n\n**Therefore, the solution is invalid due to critical errors.**"
