 \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \n\nThe construction never yields \\(k=n\\) because the two extreme points \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\) force at least two lines to be parallel to the forbidden directions. Hence the admissible set of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and definitions  \n\nA line is **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf d_1=(1,0)\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad \n\\mathbf d_2=(0,1)\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad \n\\mathbf d_3=(1,-1)\\quad(\\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nFor an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define the triangular set of lattice points  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal S\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 \u2013 covering \\(T_n\\) with non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad \n\\forall n\\ge 3\\;\\exists\\;\\mathcal L_n\\subset\\{\\text{horizontal, vertical, slope }-1\\text{ lines}\\},\n\\;|\\mathcal L_n|=n,\n\\]\nsuch that \\(\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L_n}L\\supseteq T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(n=2q\\) or \\(n=2q+1\\) with \\(q\\ge 1\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2q\\) (even).*  \nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{horizontal lines } y=j,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,q,\\\\\n&\\text{vertical   lines } x=i,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,q-1,\\\\\n&\\text{diagonal   lines } x+y=m,\\qquad m=2,3,\\dots ,q .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(q+(q-1)+q=2q=n\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2q+1\\) (odd).*  \nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{horizontal lines } y=j,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,q,\\\\\n&\\text{vertical   lines } x=i,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,q,\\\\\n&\\text{diagonal   lines } x+y=m,\\qquad m=2,3,\\dots ,q .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow we have \\(q+q+q=3q=n-1\\) lines; add the diagonal line \\(x+y=q+1\\) to obtain \\(n\\) lines.\n\nIn both parity cases we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, each parallel to a forbidden direction.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le q\\) (resp. \\(b\\le q\\)) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (resp. horizontal line \\(y=b\\)) contains the point.  \nIf both \\(a>q\\) and \\(b>q\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1=2q+2\\) (or \\(2q+3\\) in the odd case), hence \\(a+b\\) equals one of the numbers \\(2,\\dots ,q+1\\); the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma 2 \u2013 extending the covering by a non\u2011sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad \n\\text{If }\\mathcal L\\text{ is a family of }m\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_m,\n\\text{ then for any line }L\\text{ parallel to a forbidden direction}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{with }L\\notin\\mathcal L,\\;\n\\mathcal L\\cup\\{L\\}\\text{ covers }T_{m+1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{and the number of sunny lines does not change.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe new points in \\(T_{m+1}\\setminus T_m\\) are exactly those with \\(a+b=m+2\\).  \nAll such points lie on the diagonal line \\(x+y=m+2\\), which is parallel to the forbidden direction \\(\\mathbf d_3\\).  \nChoosing \\(L\\) to be this diagonal line (or any horizontal/vertical line not already present) guarantees that every new point belongs to \\(L\\).  \nSince \\(L\\) is parallel to a forbidden direction it is not sunny, so the sunny\u2011line count is unchanged. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): use the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=3\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): take \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and the sunny line \\(x-y=0\\) (which passes through \\((2,1)\\)).  \n\n\\(k=2\\) is impossible: the three points are not collinear, so at least one of the three lines must be parallel to a forbidden direction to hit the two points sharing a coordinate; consequently we cannot have two sunny lines.  \n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\) and all admissible \\(k\\) (namely \\(0,1\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following **induction hypothesis** holds:\n\n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S_n(k)\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe prove the statement for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.1  Obtaining \\(k\\le n-2\\) for \\(n+1\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nBy the hypothesis we have \\(\\mathcal S_n(k)\\).  \nApply Lemma\u202f2 with a line \\(L\\) parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (for instance \\(y=\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{2}\\rceil\\)) that is not already in \\(\\mathcal S_n(k)\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{n+1}(k):=\\mathcal S_n(k)\\cup\\{L\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal S_{n+1}(k)\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers \\(T_{n+1}\\) by Lemma\u202f2, and the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).  \nHence every \\(k\\le n-2\\) is realizable for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.2  Obtaining \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n+1\\)\n\nNow let \\(k=n-1\\).  \nStart from the configuration \\(\\mathcal S_n(n-2)\\) given by the hypothesis; it has exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines and therefore at least two non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nChoose one non\u2011sunny line \\(M\\) from \\(\\mathcal S_n(n-2)\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) is parallel to a forbidden direction, it contains many points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSelect a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that lies on \\(M\\) but **not** on any sunny line of \\(\\mathcal S_n(n-2)\\) (such a point exists because the sunny lines are at most \\(n-2\\) and each sunny line can contain at most one point of each row/column, while \\(M\\) contains at least two points).  \n\nReplace \\(M\\) by a new sunny line \\(M'\\) of a direction different from the three forbidden ones, chosen so that it passes through \\(P\\) (for example the line of slope \\(2\\) through \\(P\\)).  \nNow the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S'_n:=\\bigl(\\mathcal S_n(n-2)\\setminus\\{M\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{M'\\}\n\\]\n\nhas \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny.  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) except possibly \\(P\\) are still covered; \\(P\\) is covered by the new sunny line \\(M'\\).\n\nFinally add a fresh non\u2011sunny line \\(N\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, say \\(y=\\) the second coordinate of \\(P\\)) that passes through \\(P\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{n+1}(n-1):=\\mathcal S'_n\\cup\\{N\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow we have \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of them sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered (by construction).  \nThe new points of \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\\) have sum of coordinates \\(n+2\\); they all lie on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+2\\), which is parallel to a forbidden direction.  \nIf this diagonal line is not already present, we may choose \\(N\\) to be that diagonal line; otherwise we keep the previously added horizontal line \\(N\\) and note that the diagonal line is already among the non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal S'_n\\).  \nConsequently all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) are covered.\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines exists for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.3  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) contains \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nSince the two extreme points \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\) have respectively \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(1\\) and \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(1\\), any line covering \\((1,n+1)\\) must be either vertical (\\(x=1\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\); similarly any line covering \\((n+1,1)\\) must be horizontal (\\(y=1\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\).  \nThus at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are parallel to forbidden directions, contradicting the assumption that only one line is non\u2011sunny.  \nHence \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nBy the base case and the inductive step we have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
