* Hence at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must belong to the three families of non‑sunny lines (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)). Therefore the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n     \\[\n     k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   * **Step 1 – two fixed non‑sunny lines.**  \n     Take  \n     \\[\n     L_{1}:x=n ,\\qquad L_{2}:y=n .\n     \\]\n     These two lines cover the two extreme points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) and are non‑sunny.  \n\n   * **Step 2 – a family of lines that cover all remaining points.**  \n     For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) define the line  \n\n     \\[\n     M_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{i}{\\,n-i\\,}\\,x .\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     The slope \\(\\frac{i}{n-i}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(M_{i}\\) is a sunny line.  \n     The line \\(M_{i}\\) passes through the lattice points  \n\n     \\[\n     (n-i,i)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (0,0),\n     \\]\n\n     and therefore contains **all** points \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge n-i\\) (i.e. the points on the diagonal from \\((n-i,i)\\) down to \\((1,n-1)\\)).  \n     Moreover, for any interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) we can choose \\(i=n-a\\); then \\(a=n-i\\) and \\(b\\le i\\), so \\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(M_{i}\\).  \n     Hence the collection \\(\\{M_{1},\\dots ,M_{n-2}\\}\\) together with \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) covers **every** point of \\(S\\).\n\n   * **Step 3 – adjusting the number of sunny lines.**  \n     The lines \\(M_{1},\\dots ,M_{n-2}\\) are all sunny, giving \\(k=n-2\\).  \n     To obtain a smaller number of sunny lines we simply replace some of the \\(M_{i}\\) by non‑sunny lines while preserving the covering property.  \n     For any chosen index \\(j\\) (with \\(1\\le j\\le n-2\\)) replace \\(M_{j}\\) by the diagonal line  \n\n     \\[\n     D:\\; x+y=n+1,\n     \\]\n\n     which is non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n     The diagonal line already contains every point of \\(S\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(n+1\\); the points that were covered exclusively by \\(M_{j}\\) and have sum \\(<n+1\\) are still covered because each such point \\((a,b)\\) also lies on another line \\(M_{i}\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) (take \\(i=n-a\\neq j\\)).  \n     Thus each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by exactly one and does not destroy the covering condition.\n\n   * By performing the replacement for exactly \\(r\\) indices we obtain a configuration with  \n\n     \\[\n     k=(n-2)-r\\qquad (0\\le r\\le n-2)\n     \\]\n\n     sunny lines. Since \\(r\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary bound (1) and the explicit construction together show that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
