le configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}\\!:\\ y=c,\\qquad \n\\ell_{y}\\!:\\ x=c,\\qquad \n\\ell_{d}\\!:\\ x+y=c,\n\\]\nwhere \\(c\\) is a real constant.  \nA line is **sunny** otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) having a given\n\\(x\\)-coordinate, a given \\(y\\)-coordinate, or a given sum \\(a+b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, its equation is \\(y=c\\); all points on it have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, so two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) on this line would have the same \\(y\\)-value, contradicting the definition of \\(\\mathcal P\\) (different points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates). The same reasoning applies to lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and to lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\). By Lemma 1 it can contain at most one point of each of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\text{ fixed}\\},\\qquad\n\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\text{ fixed}\\},\\qquad\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\text{ fixed}\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contained three distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\) of \\(\\mathcal P\\). The three points determine the slope of \\(\\ell\\). Because the three points have integer coordinates, the slope must be either \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), or \\(-1\\) (the slope of the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)). In each of these three cases \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions, contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting argument\n\nLet the configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which \\(k\\) are sunny and \\(s=n-k\\) are non‑sunny.\n\n* From Lemma 2, the \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(3s\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n* From Lemma 3, the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must lie on at least one of the lines, we have  \n\n\\[\n3s+2k\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(s=n-k\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n3(n-k)+2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n3n-3k+2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n3n-k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(k\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3n\n      =\\frac{n^{2}+n-6n}{2}\n      =\\frac{n^{2}-5n}{2}\n      =\\frac{n(n-5)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) cannot be negative, we take the ceiling of the right‑hand side after simplifying the inequality more directly. A cleaner manipulation is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le 3(n-k)+2k = 3n- k,\n\\]\nso  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge 3n-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   =\\frac{6n-n^{2}-n}{2}\n   =\\frac{-(n^{2}-5n)}{2}\n   =\\frac{5n-n^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince the right‑hand side is decreasing for \\(n\\ge5\\), the strongest bound is obtained for the smallest admissible \\(n\\). A uniform bound that works for all \\(n\\ge3\\) is obtained by observing t