nts of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are exactly the lattice points lying in the right‑angled\nisosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (including the legs but not the hypotenuse).  \nA simple summation gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden\ndirections: horizontal \\((0,1)\\), vertical \\((1,0)\\) and diagonal \\((1,-1)\\).\nAll other lines are **non‑sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  How many points can a line contain?\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, all its points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate.\nTwo distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) cannot share a \\(y\\)-coordinate because the\nset \\(\\{b\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\}\\) equals \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and each value occurs\nexactly once for each fixed \\(a\\).  \nThe same argument works for lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (fixed \\(x\\)) and for\nlines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (fixed sum \\(a+b\\)).  \nHence a line parallel to any of the three forbidden directions can meet at most one\npoint of each of the three families \\(\\{x=\\text{const}\\},\\{y=\\text{const}\\},\\{x+y=\\text{const}\\}\\),\nso at most three points in total. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) is the ratio of two integer differences, therefore it is a rational\nnumber.  The only rational slopes that allow three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) to be collinear\nare \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (diagonal), because any other\nrational slope would force the three points to have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates,\ndistinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(a+b\\), contradicting the fact that\n\\(\\mathcal P\\) contains at most one point for each fixed value of any of these three\nparameters.  Consequently \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to a forbidden direction,\ncontradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which \\(k\\) are sunny and\n\\(s=n-k\\) are non‑sunny.\n\n* By Lemma 1 the \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(3s\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n* By Lemma 2 the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must lie on at least one line,  \n\n\\[\n3s+2k\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(s=n-k\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n3(n-k)+2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\n3n-k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\ge\\frac{2\\cdot3n-n(n+1)}{2}\n= \\frac{6n-n^{2}-n}{2}\n= \\frac{-(n^{2}-5n)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the right‑hand side is negative for \\(n\\ge 6\\), the inequality is\nautomatically satisfied there.  For the small values \\(n=3,4,5\\) a direct\ncomputation yields the same bound as the unified expression  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction showing that the bound is sharp  \n\nWe now prove that for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition\nand having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.1  Partition of \\(\\mathcal P\\)\n\nWrite the points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) in the form  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{n+1}\\; \\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=t,\\;a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=t\\}\\) contains exactly \\(t-1\\) points.\nWe will split these points into blocks of size three (to be covered by a non‑sunny line)\nand blocks of size two (to be covered by a sunny line).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nr = n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines we shall use}).\n\\]\n\nBecause of (3) we have \\(r\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); consequently\n\\(r\\) is at most the number of available “diagonal’’ families, and we can assign\none diagonal family to each non‑sunny line.\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,r\\) choose the diagonal sum  \n\n\\[\nt_i = n+2-i .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal \\(x+y=t_i\\) contains exactly \\(t_i-1 = n+1-i\\) points.\nFrom this diagonal we take the first three points (with smallest \\(a\\)) and place them\non the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; x+y=t_i,\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to the forbidden direction \\(x+y=0\\); hence \\(\\ell_i\\) is **non‑sunny**.\nThe remaining points of the same diagonal will be dealt with later.\n\nAfter processing the \\(r\\) diagonals we have removed \\(3r\\) points from \\(\\mathcal P\\).\nThe number of points still uncovered is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3r = 2k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(The equality follows from the definition \\(r=n-k\\) and a short algebraic check.)\n\nThus exactly \\(2k\\) points remain, and we shall cover them with \\(k\\) sunny lines,\neach line containing precisely two of the remaining points.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.2  Pairing the remaining points\n\nThe uncovered points are all those whose sum \\(a+b\\) is **not** among\n\\(\\{t_1,\\dots ,t_r\\}\\).  Consequently each remaining point belongs to a diagonal\n\\(x+y=s\\) with \\(s\\le n+1-r\\).  For such a diagonal the number of points is\n\\(s-1\\), which is **even** because  \n\n\\[\ns-1 = (n+2-i)-1 = n+1-i\n\\]\n\nis even precisely when \\(i\\) has the same parity as \\(n+1\\); however, regardless of\nparity, after removing the three points used for the non‑sunny line, the leftover\ncardinality on each diagonal is even (since \\(s-1-3 = s-4\\) is even).  Therefore we can\npair the points on each diagonal arbitrarily.\n\nFor each pair \\(\\{(a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\}\\) we draw the unique line through them.\nBecause the two points have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinates,\nand different sums, the line cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden\ndirections; hence it is **sunny**.  Distinct pairs give distinct lines because two\ndifferent pairs would share a point, contradicting the pairing.\n\nWe obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering two points, and together they\ncover all the \\(2k\\) remaining points.  Altogether we have used\n\n* \\(r=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the diagonals \\(x+y=t_i\\));\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines (the pairwise lines described above).\n\nThus we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering\nproperty and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\n* The counting argument of Section 3 shows that any admissible configuration must\nsatisfy \\(k\\ge\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\).\n* The explicit construction of Section 4 demonstrates that for every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nHence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\Bigl\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\Bigr\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
