the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence at most \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points lie\non it.  The same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nIf the line is diagonal, i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), then the points on it\nare \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most **two** points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\) of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\nBecause the three points are collinear, the differences\n\\((a_{i}-a_{j},\\,b_{i}-b_{j})\\) are all proportional.\nConsequently the ratios \\(\\dfrac{b_{i}-b_{j}}{a_{i}-a_{j}}\\) are equal for the three\npairs \\(\\{i,j\\}\\).  Since all coordinates are integers, this common ratio is a rational\nnumber \\(p/q\\) with integers \\(p,q\\).  The only rational slopes that allow three\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P\\) to be collinear are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical)\nand \\(-1\\) (diagonal); any other slope would force at least two of the three points\nto have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate or the same sum\n\\(x+y\\), which is impossible because each of those three quantities takes each\nvalue at most once among the points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  Hence \\(\\ell\\) would be\nparallel to a forbidden direction, contradicting the definition of a sunny line.\nTherefore a sunny line can meet \\(\\mathcal P\\) in at most two points. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which \\(k\\) are sunny\nand \\(s=n-k\\) are non\u2011sunny.\n\n* By Lemma\u202f1 the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(sn\\) points of\n  \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n* By Lemma\u202f2 the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of\n  \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must lie on at least one line, we have  \n\n\\[\nsn+2k\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(s=n-k\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\nn^{2}-kn+2k\\ge\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\n2n^{2}-2kn+4k\\ge n^{2}+n\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nn^{2}-2kn+4k\\ge n\n\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n2k\\ge 2kn-n^{2}+n\n\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\ge\\frac{2kn-n^{2}+n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(k\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must contain at least\n\\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition\nand having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\ns=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non\u2011sunny lines we shall use}).\n\\]\n\nBecause of (3) we have \\(s\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); consequently we can\nchoose \\(s\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines, one from each of the three families\n(horizontal, vertical, diagonal), in such a way that each of them contains **exactly\nthree** points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  The concrete choice is as follows.\n\n*For \\(i=1,\\dots ,s\\) define*  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i^{\\text{h}}:\\; y=i,\\qquad\n\\ell_i^{\\text{v}}:\\; x=i,\\qquad\n\\ell_i^{\\text{d}}:\\; x+y=n+2-i .\n\\]\n\nAmong these three families we select the first \\(s\\) lines in the order  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1^{\\text{d}},\\;\\ell_2^{\\text{d}},\\dots ,\\ell_{s}^{\\text{d}}\n\\]\n\n(if \\(s\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) this is possible because the diagonal\n\\(x+y=n+2-i\\) contains at least three points for every \\(i\\le s\\)).  \nEach chosen 