\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor \\,\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The point set  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(\\mathcal P\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right‑angled\nisosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (including the legs).\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=N=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three\nforbidden directions: horizontal \\((0,1)\\), vertical \\((1,0)\\) and diagonal\n\\((1,-1)\\).  All other lines are **non‑sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  How many points can a line contain?\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence at most \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points\nlie on it.  The same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nIf the line is diagonal, i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), then the points on\nit are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) of\nthem. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most **two** points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\) of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\nBecause the three points are collinear, the vectors\n\\((a_{i}-a_{j},\\,b_{i}-b_{j})\\) are all proportional; consequently the three\nslopes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{i}-b_{j}}{a_{i}-a_{j}}\\qquad (1\\le i<j\\le 3)\n\\]\n\nare equal (the denominators are non‑zero because the points have distinct\n\\(x\\)-coordinates – otherwise two points would share the same \\(x\\) and the line\nwould be