    =\\frac{n(n-1)}{\\,n-2\\,}\n      =\\frac{2\\,n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the **necessary upper bound**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNo other restriction follows from (★); in particular \\(k=0\\) is not ruled out.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying (1) and put \\(s=n-k\\).\n\nBecause of (1) we have \\(s\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nConsequently the three families\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=s\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=s\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n+2-i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,s\\}\n\\]\n\nare well defined and consist of pairwise distinct lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.1  Points covered by the non‑sunny lines  \n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nThe line \\(y=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le s\\)) contains the points \\((a,j)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\); therefore it contains exactly \\(n+1-j\\) points of\n\\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nThe line \\(x=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le s\\)) contains the points \\((j,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\); again exactly \\(n+1-j\\) points.\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nThe line \\(x+y=n+2-i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le s\\)) contains the points\n\\((1,n+1-i),(2,n-i),\\dots ,(i,n+1-i-i)\\); there are exactly \\(n+1-i\\) points.\n\nSumming over the three families we obtain the total number of points covered\nby the \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{\\text{ns}}\n&=\\sum_{j=1}^{s}\\bigl[(n+1-j)+(n+1-j)+(n+1-j)\\bigr] \\\\\n&=3\\sum_{j=1}^{s}(n+1-j)\n =3\\Bigl(s(n+1)-\\frac{s(s+1)}2\\Bigr) .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.2  Remaining points  \n\nUsing (3) and (4),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{\\text{rem}}\n&=N-N_{\\text{ns}}\\\\[2pt]\n&=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   -3\\Bigl(s(n+1)-\\frac{s(s+1)}2\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n&=\\frac{n(n+1)-6s(n+1)+3s(s+1)}2\\\\[2pt]\n&=\\frac{(n+1)(n-6s)+3s^{2}+3s}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(s=n-k\\).  Substituting and simplifying (a straightforward algebraic\ncomputation) yields\n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{rem}}=2k . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus exactly \\(2k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are still uncovered.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.3  Sunny lines  \n\nPair the \\(2k\\) uncovered points arbitrarily; each pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\) determines a\nunique line \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\).  For any two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) the\nthree numbers \\(x\\), \\(y\\) and \\(x+y\\) are all different, because each of these\nthree quantities assumes each admissible integer value at most once in \\(\\mathcal\nP\\).  Consequently the line through a pair cannot be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); hence every \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) is sunny.\n\nIf two different pairs produced the same line, that line would contain at least\nthree points of \\(\\mathcal P\\), contradicting Lemma 2.  Therefore the \\(k\\) lines\nobtained from the \\(k\\) pairs are pairwise distinct.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.4  Summary of the construction  \n\n* we have \\(s=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the \\(s\\) horizontals, \\(s\\) verticals and\n  \\(s\\) diagonals – altogether \\(3s\\) lines, but we keep only the first \\(s\\) of\n  them, e.g. the \\(s\\) horizontals; any choice of \\(s\\) distinct non‑sunny lines\n  works);\n* we have \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained from the \\(k\\) pairs of the remaining\n  points;\n* the total number of lines is \\(s+k=n\\);\n* by (5) all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are covered.\n\nThus a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final answer  \n\nThe inequality (1) is necessary, and the construction above shows it is also\nsufficient.  Hence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\n0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor \\,\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
