nstruction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\ns=n-k .\n\\]\n\n#### 3.1  Non\u2011sunny (horizontal) lines  \n\nTake the \\(s\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=s . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on one of the lines (3) exactly when \\(b\\le s\\). Consequently all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\le s\\) are already covered.\n\n#### 3.2  Points still uncovered  \n\nThe remaining points are those with  \n\n\\[\nb\\in\\{s+1,s+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) the set of points having \\(b=s+t\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal Q_{t}\n&=\\{(a,s+t)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;a+(s+t)\\le n+1\\}\\\\\n&=\\{(1,s+t),\\;(2,s+t-1),\\dots ,(t,s+1)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the total number of uncovered points equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=1}^{k}t=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.3  Sunny lines covering the uncovered points  \n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{j}:\\; y = -\\frac{j}{j+1}\\,x + \\Bigl(s+1+\\frac{j}{j+1}\\Bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Why \\(L_{j}\\) is sunny.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_{j}\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\). This number is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\) (the slope of the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence each \\(L_{j}\\) is sunny. Distinct values of \\(j\\) give distinct slopes, so the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are pairwise different.\n\n*Points of \\(\\mathcal Q_{j}\\) lie on \\(L_{j}\\).*  \nTake a point \\((m,s+j+1-m)\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le j\\) (these are exactly the points of \\(\\mathcal Q_{j}\\)). Substituting into (6) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny\n&=s+j+1-m\\\\\n&= -\\frac{j}{j+1}\\,m +\\Bigl(s+1+\\frac{j}{j+1}\\Bigr),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhich is an identity; therefore every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_{j}\\) belongs to \\(L_{j}\\). Conversely, if \\((x,y)\\) satisfies (6) with integer coordinates and \\(1\\le x\\le j\\), then \\(y=s+j+1-x\\), so the integer points of \\(L_{j}\\) inside the triangle \\(\\mathcal P\\) are precisely the set \\(\\mathcal Q_{j}\\).\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) together cover all points of the sets \\(\\mathcal Q_{1},\\dots ,\\mathcal Q_{k}\\), i.e. all points that were not covered by the horizontal lines (3). The families \\(\\mathcal Q_{j}\\) are disjoint, so no point is covered twice.\n\n#### 3.4  The whole family of lines  \n\nThe complete collection consists of  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=s}_{n-k\\ \\text{horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines}}\n\\quad\\cup\\quad\n\\underbrace{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{k}}_{k\\ \\text{sunny lines}} .\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of the lines. Hence for the chosen \\(k\\) the required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Any feasible configuration must satisfy the obvious necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (equation (2)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have constructed an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
