us the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\setminus\\{\\ell_{0}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell^{*},\\;x+y=n+2\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, satisfies the covering condition for \\(S_{n+1}\\), and has exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines (the sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) plus \\(\\ell^{*}\\); the added line \\(x+y=n+2\\) is not sunny). Hence a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case supplies configurations for \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) when \\(n=3\\).  \nAssume the claim holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nUsing the constructions of Sections\u202f3 and\u202f4 we obtain configurations for \\(n+1\\) with\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the statement is true for \\(n+1\\). By induction it holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Non\u2011existence of the case \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (C) and in which **all** \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\nConsider the family of diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_m:\\; x+y=m,\\qquad m=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nEach sunny line has slope different from \\(-1\\); therefore it can intersect a given diagonal \\(D_m\\) in at most one point. Moreover, if a sunny line intersected the same diagonal \\(D_m\\) in two distinct points, the line would be forced to be parallel to \\(D_m\\), i.e. have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting sunniness. Consequently a sunny line meets **each** diagonal \\(D_m\\) in at most one point, and hence the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) can cover at most \\(n\\) points of the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{m=2}^{n+1} D_m\\cap\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_n|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{m=2}^{n+1}(m-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThus the covering condition cannot be fulfilled with \\(k=n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two given conditions is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]"
