her value is possible because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny line  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_i:\\;x+y=i+1 .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_i\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique diagonal \\(D_{a+b-1}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{D_1,\\dots ,D_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line that covers a whole diagonal  \n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(m_t\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nm_t\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y=m_t x + (t+1-m_t).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_t\\) passes through the two points \\((1,t)\\) and \\((t,1)\\); consequently it contains every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\).  \nIndeed, for such a point we have  \n\n\\[\nb = (t+1)-a = m_t a + (t+1-m_t),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (1).  \nBecause the slope \\(m_t\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), the line \\(S_t\\) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing diagonals by sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(K\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be any subset with \\(|K|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in K\\) replace the diagonal \\(D_i\\) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\) defined in (1) with a slope \\(m_i\\) chosen so that  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\neq m_j\\quad\\text{for }i\\neq j,\\qquad \nm_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1