 sunny line can contain at most $n$ points of $S$.\n\n*Proof of Lemma.*  \nLet $L$ be a sunny line with slope $m\\neq0,\\infty,-1$.  \nSuppose $L$ contains $n+1$ distinct points of $S$, say  \n\n\\[\n(a_{1},b_{1}),\\dots ,(a_{n+1},b_{n+1})\\in S\\cap L .\n\\]\n\nBecause the $a$‑coordinates are positive integers not exceeding $n$, the pigeon‑hole principle forces two of them, say $a_{p}$ and $a_{q}$, to be equal.  \nThen the two corresponding points have the same $x$‑coordinate, so the line through them is vertical, contradicting $m\\neq\\infty$.  \nSimilarly, if two points share the same $y$‑coordinate, the line would be horizontal, contradicting $m\\neq0$.  \nFinally, if two points satisfy $a_{r}+b_{r}=a_{s}+b_{s}$, then the line through them is parallel to $x+y=0$, contradicting $m\\neq-1$.  \nSince among $n+1$ points at least one of these three coincidences must occur, $L$ cannot contain $n+1$ points of $S$. ∎\n\n---\n\nFrom the lemma each line $L_{i}$ covers at most $n$ points of $S$.  \nHence the total number of points of $S$ that can be covered by the $n$ lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot n=n^{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBut by (2) we have  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the requirement that all points of $S$ be covered.  \n\nTherefore our initial assumption that all $n$ lines are sunny is false.  \nConsequently, in any admissible configuration at least one line is non‑sunny, i.e. the number $k$ of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1.\n\\]\n\nThus the integer $k=n$ is impossible. ∎"
