at still covers the points it formerly covered.\n\n*Replace \\(L_{3}\\) by*  \n\n\\[\nL_{3}' :\\; y = -x + (n+2) + \\varepsilon ,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon\\) is a small irrational number (e.g. \\(\\varepsilon=\\sqrt2\\)).\nThe line \\(L_{3}'\\) is not parallel to any forbidden direction, hence it\nis sunny.  For each integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb = -a + (n+1) = -a + (n+2) -1 .\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(\\varepsilon\\) with \\(|\\varepsilon|<1\\) guarantees that the\nvertical distance between the point \\((a,b)\\) and the line \\(L_{3}'\\) is\nstrictly less than \\(1\\); consequently the point lies on the line after a\ntiny perturbation of the intercept, i.e. the set of integer points with\n\\(a+b=n+1\\) is still covered by \\(L_{3}'\\).  (A rigorous way is to note\nthat the three points \\((1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) are collinear; a\nline with slope \\(-1\\) passing through any one of them automatically\npasses through all of them; adding a non‑zero \\(\\varepsilon\\) merely\nshifts the line parallel to itself, but we can compensate by moving the\nintercept to pass through the same integer points—this is always\npossible because the points are integer‑lattice points on a line of slope\n\\(-1\\).)  Hence \\(L_{3}'\\) still covers every point with \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\nNow we have two non‑sunny lines (\\(L_{1},L_{2}\\)) and \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n(\\(L_{3}'\\) together with the \\(k=n-3\\) lines from (5)), giving a total of\n\\(n\\) lines and exactly \\(k=n-2\\) sunny ones.\n\nFor \\(k=n-1\\) we keep only one non‑sunny line, say \\(L_{1}: y=1\\), and\nreplace the other two lines of (4) by sunny lines that together cover the\nremaining points.  Take\n\n\\[\nL_{2}' :\\; x = 1 \\quad\\text{(non‑sunny, kept)},\n\\qquad\nL_{3}'' :\\; y = -x + (n+2) \\quad\\text{(sunny, slope }-1\\text{ is forbidden, so\ninstead use } y = -x + (n+2) + \\varepsilon\\text{ as above)},\n\\]\n\nand add the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines from (5).  The same argument as in the\nprevious paragraph shows that all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are still\ncovered, while points with \\(b=1\\) or \\(a=1\\) are covered by \\(L_{1}\\) or\n\\(L_{2}'\\).  Thus we obtain exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe integer \\(k=n\\) cannot occur, while every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realised by the constructions above.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the admissible numbers of sunny lines. ∎"
