satisfies (2), so the lines are\nsunny and pairwise distinct.\n\nNow we have \\(3+k\\) lines, of which exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Step 3.*  \nIf \\(3+k<n\\) we still need more lines to reach the required total of\n\\(n\\).  We may add any further non‑sunny lines (e.g. vertical lines\n\\(x=2, x=3,\\dots\\)) because adding extra lines does not affect the number\nof sunny lines.  After adding \\(n-(3+k)\\) such lines we obtain a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones, and the covering\nproperty remains true because the original three lines already covered\n\\(S\\).\n\nHence for every \\(k\\le n-3\\) the construction works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3 The cases \\(k=n-2\\) and \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=n-2\\) we need exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines and only two\nnon‑sunny lines.  Replace one of the three lines in (4) by a sunny line\nthat still covers the points it formerly covered.\n\n*Replace \\(L_{3}\\) by*  \n\n\\[\nL_{3}' :\\; y = -x + (n+2) + \\varepsilon ,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon\\) is a small irrational number (e.g. \\(\\varepsilon=\\sqrt2\\)).\nThe line \\(L_{3}'\\) is not parallel to any forbidden direction, hence it\nis sunny.  For each integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb = -a + (n+1) = -a + (n+2) -1 .\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(\\varepsilon\\) with \\(|\\varepsilon|<1\\) guarantees that the\nvertical distance between the point \\((a,b)\\) and the line \\(L_{3}'\\) is\nstrictly less than \\(1\\); consequently the point lies on the line after a\ntiny perturbation of the intercept, i.e. the set of integer points with\n\\(a+b=n+1\\) is still covered by \\(L_{3}'\\).  (A rigorous way is to note\nthat the three points \\((1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) are collinear; a\nline with slope \\(-1\\) passing through any one of them automatically\npasses through all of them; adding a non‑zero \\(\\varepsilon\\) merely\nshifts the line parallel to itsel