----------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most three points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line meets each of the three families \\(\\{y=b\\},\\{x=a\\},\\{x+y=c\\}\\) in at most one\npoint, because being parallel to one of them would contradict the definition of sunny.\nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to exactly one line of each family, a sunny line can\ncontain at most one point from each family, i.e. at most three points. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA line parallel to one of the directions in (5) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the admissible abscissas are\n\\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n+1-b\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  \nThe vertical case \\(x=a\\) is symmetric.  \nIf the line is diagonal, say \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), then the admissible points are\n\\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and also \\(c-a\\le n+1-a\\); again the number of solutions does\nnot exceed \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most three points,\nand by Lemma\u202f2 each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.\nHence\n\n\\[\n3k+n\\,(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (6),\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-3\\,}\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-3)} . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause we cannot have more sunny lines than total lines, we also need \\(k\\le n\\).\nFor \\(n=3\\) the denominator in (7) vanishes; directly, \\(|S_3|=6\\) and each line can\ncontain at most three points, so \\(k\\le3=n\\).\n\nThus a **necessary** condition is  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\min\\!\\left\\{\\,n,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-3)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\right\\},\n& n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases} \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficient construction\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the bound (8).  We construct \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### Sunny lines\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=x+(i-1). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is not parallel to any of the three directions in (5), hence it is sunny.\nMoreover \\(L_i\\) passes through the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,i),\\;(2,i+1),\\;(3,i+2), \\tag{10}\n\\]\nall of which belong to \\(S_n\\) because \\(i\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover exactly \\(3k\\) distinct points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### Remaining points\nSet \\(R=S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\\).  From (6) we have  \n\n\\[\n|R|=|S_n|-3k\\le n(n-k). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nPartition \\(R\\) into at most \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines.  For each integer \\(b\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains at most \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  Because the total number of points to be placed is at most\n\\(n(n-k)\\), a greedy assignment of the points of \\(R\\) to distinct horizontal lines\nuses no more than \\(n-k\\) such lines.  These horizontal lines are parallel to \\(\\ell_1\\),\nhence they are non\u2011sunny.\n\nThus we obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n(the chosen horizontal lines), altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (8) is also sufficient, so the admissible values of \\(k\\) are\nexactly those described in (8).  In compact form:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\qqu