h the two points; denote it by \\(m_j\\).\n\nThe slope of \\(m_j\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), nor \\(-1\\) (because \\(b_2-b_1\\neq -(a_2-a_1)\\); otherwise the two points would have the same sum \\(a+b\\), contradicting the definition of the rows).  \nHence each \\(m_j\\) is a sunny line.\n\nThe number of such lines equals the number of pairs, which is at most \\(k\\).  \nIf fewer than \\(k\\) sunny lines have been produced, we may add arbitrary sunny lines that pass through any already covered point; this does not affect the covering property.\n\nThus we have constructed exactly \\(t\\) horizontal (non‑sunny) lines and at most \\(k\\) sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, covering every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nBecause the construction works for every admissible \\(k\\), the bound (4) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the integers  \n\n\\[\n0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFor the small case \\(n=3\\) a direct inspection shows that all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) can be realized (e.g., three sunny lines each passing through two of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\)).\n\nHence the answer is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
