            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The required points lie on the diagonals \\(D_s\\)\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) then \\(s:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (1); conversely every lattice point on a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal T_n\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}\\bigl(D_s\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\bigr) ,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nand the union is disjoint.  \nFor a fixed \\(s\\) the diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\ns-1\\quad\\text{points }(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\mathcal T_n\\bigr|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Intersection capacity of a non‑diagonal line\n\nA line is **non‑diagonal** if it is not equal to any \\(D_s\\).  \nSuch a line is either\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* or has equation \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq -1\\).\n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_s\\). Solving the system with a vertical line gives the unique point \\((c,s-c)\\); with a horizontal line we obtain \\((s-c,c)\\); with a non‑vertical, non‑horizontal line we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\tfrac{s-c}{1+m},\\tfrac{ms+c}{1+m}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nIn every case there is **at most one** solution, so a non‑diagonal line meets each diagonal in at most one point. Consequently a non‑diagonal line can contribute at most one point to each diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points in total (one per diagonal).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting with a family of \\(n\\) lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  \nDenote  \n\n\\[\nM=\\{s\\mid D_s\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad m=|M|.\n\\]\n\nThe \\(m\\) diagonal lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) already cover  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1) \\tag{5}\n\\]\npoints (by (3)).  \n\nThe remaining \\(n-m\\) lines are non‑diagonal; each of them can meet each diagonal in at most one point, hence together they can cover at most \\((n-m)n\\) additional points (there are \\(n\\) diagonals).  \n\nThus the total number of covered points satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n=|\\mathcal T_n|\n\\le \\underbrace{\\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1)}_{\\text{points on chosen diagonals}}\n   + (n-m)n . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessity of having all diagonals\n\nRewrite (6) as  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1)\\le (n-m)n . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side is the sum of the numbers \\(s-1\\) for the \\(n-m\\) diagonals that are **not** in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe smallest possible value of this sum occurs when the missing diagonals are the first \\(n-m\\) ones, i.e. \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n-m+1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1)\\ge\n\\sum_{t=1}^{\\,n-m} t = \\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (7) and (8) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}\\le (n-m)n . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n-m>0\\) we can divide by \\(n-m\\) and obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n-m+1}{2}\\le n . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nInequality (10) is always true, so it does not by itself force \\(m=n\\).  \nWe must use the fact that equality must hold in (6), because every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) is indeed covered.\n\nEquality in (6) forces equality in both (7) and (8).  \nEquality in (8) can occur only when the missing diagonals are exactly the first \\(n-m\\) diagonals; otherwise the sum would be larger.  \nAssume this is the case. Then each of the \\(n-m\\) non‑diagonal lines must contribute **one point on every diagonal**, otherwise the right‑hand side of (6) would be strictly smaller than \\(|\\mathcal T_n|\\).\n\nBut a non‑diagonal line cannot provide a lattice point on more than one diagonal.  \nIndeed, for a line \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq -1\\) the intersection with \\(D_s\\) is \\(\\bigl(\\frac{s-c}{1+m},\\frac{ms+c}{1+m}\\bigr)\\). As \\(s\\) varies over integers, the first coordinate is an integer for at most one integer \\(s\\) (otherwise the difference of two such integers would be a multiple of \\(1+m\\), forcing \\(1+m\\) to be an integer divisor of 0, i.e. \\(m=-1\\), which is excluded). The same argument applies to vertical and horizontal lines. Hence a non‑diagonal line can lie on a lattice point of **at most one** diagonal.\n\nTherefore the requirement that each non‑diagonal line supplies a point on every diagonal is impossible unless there are **no** non‑diagonal lines, i.e. \\(n-m=0\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nm=n .\n\\]\n\nThus every diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Determination of \\(k\\)\n\nAll the lines \\(D_s\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); by definition a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) is **not** sunny.  \nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists exactly of the \\(n\\) diagonals, the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration is  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) indeed covers every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\), so the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
