upply is at most \\((n-m)n\\).  \n\n   Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|\\mathcal T_n|\n   \\le\n   \\underbrace{\\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1)}_{\\text{points on chosen diagonals}}\n   +(n-m)n .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Deriving \\(m=n\\).**  \n   Rearranging (7) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1)\\le (n-m)n .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The left‑hand side is the sum of the numbers \\(s-1\\) for the \\(n-m\\) diagonals that are **missing** from \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   Its smallest possible value occurs when the missing diagonals are the first \\(n-m\\) ones, i.e. \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n-m+1\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1)\\ge\n   \\sum_{t=1}^{\\,n-m}t\n   =\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (8) and (9) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}\\le (n-m)n .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(n-m>0\\) we may divide by \\(n-m\\) and get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n-m+1}{2}\\le n ,\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   which is always true.  However, equality must hold in (7) because every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) is indeed covered.  \n   Equality in (7) forces equality in both (8) and (9).  \n\n   *Equality in (9)* can occur only when the missing diagonals are exactly the first \\(n-m\\) diagonals; otherwise the sum would be larger.  \n\n   *Equality in (8)* then requires that the \\(n-m\\) non‑diagonal lines together supply **exactly** \\((n-m)n\\) points, i.e. each non‑diagonal line must meet **every** diagonal in a lattice point.  \n\n   Lemma 2 shows that a non‑diagonal line can meet at most **one** diagonal in a lattice point, so the only way to achieve \\((n-m)n\\) points is when \\(n-m=0\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   m=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **all** the \\(n\\) lines must be the diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\n\n5. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   Every diagonal \\(D_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); by definition such a line is **not** sunny.  \n   Therefore the configuration consisting