 \\(D_s\\), and on that diagonal at most one point.  \n   *Proof.* Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(ax+by=c\\) with integers \\(a,b,c\\) such that \\(\\gcd(a,b)=1\\), \\(a\\neq0\\), \\(b\\neq0\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\) (the three forbidden parallels).  \n   Solving together with \\(x+y=s\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   x=\\frac{c-b s}{a-b},\\qquad y=\\frac{a s-c}{a-b}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\gcd(a-b,b)=\\gcd(a,b)=1\\), the denominator \\(a-b\\) is coprime to the coefficient of \\(s\\) in the numerator.  \n   Hence \\((x,y)\\) is integral **iff**  \n\n   \\[\n   a-b\\mid c-b s\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b s\\equiv c\\pmod{a-b}.\n   \\]\n\n   The congruence has exactly one solution modulo \\(|a-b|\\); therefore there is at most one integer \\(s\\) for which a lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) lies on a diagonal.  \n   If such an \\(s\\) exists, the corresponding point is unique because the two linear equations determine a single point. ∎\n\n3. **Notation for the chosen lines.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  \n   Denote by  \n\n   \\[\n   M:=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid D_s\\in\\mathcal L\\},\n   \\qquad m:=|M|\n   \\]\n\n   the set (and its cardinality) of diagonals that are **exactly** among the chosen lines.  \n   The remaining \\(n-m\\) lines are non‑diagonal.\n\n4. **Counting the covered points.**  \n   *Points supplied by the diagonal lines.*  \n   Each diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\); therefore the diagonals in \\(\\mathcal L\\) cover  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{\\text{diag}}:=\\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1)\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points.\n\n   *Points supplied by the non‑diagonal lines.*  \n   By Lemma 2 a non‑diagonal line can contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\). Consequently the \\(n-m\\) non‑diagonal lines together contribute at most  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{\\text{non}}\\le n-m .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Total number of points.*  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) has  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   points (the triangular numbers).  \n   Since every point must be covered, we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le P_{\\text{diag}}+P_{\\text{non}}\n   \\le \\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1)+(n-m).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Deriving a contradiction unless \\(m=n\\).**  \n   Rearranging (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}- (n-m)\\le \\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The right‑hand side is the sum of \\(m\\) distinct integers taken from the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (because \\(s-1\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\)).  \n   Its **maximum** occurs when we take the largest \\(m\\) of them, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   \\max\\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1)=\\sum_{t=n-m+1}^{n} t\n   =\\frac{m(2n-m+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (5) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-m)\\le \\frac{m(2n-m+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying (7):\n\n   \\[\n   n(n+1)-2(n-m)\\le m(2n-m+1)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   n^{2}+n-2n+2m\\le 2mn-m^{2}+m\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   n^{2}-n+2m\\le 2mn-m^{2}+m\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   n^{2}-n\\le 2mn-m^{2}-m .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Bring all terms to one side:\n\n   \\[\n   0\\le 2mn-m^{2}-m-n^{2}+n\n   =-(m^{2}-2mn)+(n-m)-n^{2}+n\n   =-(m-n)^{2}+ (n-m)+n .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\((m-n)^{2}\\ge 0\\), the right‑hand side is maximised when \\(m=n\\).  \n   If \\(m\\le n-1\\) then \\((m-n)^{2}\\ge 1\\) and we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   0\\le -(m-n)^{2}+ (n-m)+n\\le -1+(n-m)+n =2n-m-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) the inequality \\(2n-m-1\\ge 0\\) is always true, so we need a sharper argument.  \n   Instead of the above algebraic manipulation we use a direct counting contradiction.\n\n   **Direct contradiction.**  \n   The total number of points not covered by the diagonal lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal T_n|-P_{\\text{diag}}\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1)\n   =\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\{s-1\\mid s\\notin M\\}\\) consists of exactly \\(n-m\\) distinct positive integers taken from \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Its **minimum** occurs when the missing diagonals are the smallest ones, i.e. when  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{s\\notin M\\}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-m+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   giving  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1)\\ge \\sum_{t=1}^{\\,n-m} t\n   =\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, by Lemma 2 each of the \\(n-m\\) non‑diagonal lines can supply **at most one** point, so the right‑hand side of (9) cannot exceed \\(n-m\\). Hence\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}\\le n-m .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(n-m>0\\) we can divide (11) by \\(n-m\\) and obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n-m+1}{2}\\le 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n-m+1\\le 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n-m\\le 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(n-m\\) can be only \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).\n\n   *Case \\(n-m=1\\).*  \n   Then (10) gives \\(\\frac{2\\cdot3}{2}=3\\le 1\\), a contradiction.  \n   Hence this case is impossible.\n\n   Therefore we must have \\(n-m=0\\), i.e. \\(m=n\\).\n\n   Consequently **all** the \\(n\\) lines are the diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\n\n6. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   Every diagonal \\(D_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); by definition such a line is **not** sunny.  \n   Hence the configuration consisting of the \\(n\\) diagonals contains **no** sunny lines.  \n   The value  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\n   \\]\n\n   is therefore attainable, and no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n**Conclusion** – for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps