und is valid.\n\n6. **Total number of points in \\(\\mathcal T_n\\)**  \n   > \u201c\\(|\\mathcal T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\u201d\n\n   *Verification*: The set \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) consists of the integer points in a right\u2011angled triangle of side length \\(n\\); the count is the \\(n\\)-th triangular number, which is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\). Correct.\n\n7. **Inequality linking the counts**  \n   > \u201c\\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le P_{\\text{diag}}+P_{\\text{non}}\\le \\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1)+(n-m).\\)\u201d\n\n   *Verification*: Since every point must be covered, the total number of points cannot exceed the sum of points contributed by diagonal and non\u2011diagonal lines. The right\u2011hand inequality uses the bounds from steps\u202f4 and\u202f5. Correct.\n\n8. **Deriving a lower bound for \\(\\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1)\\)**  \n   > \u201c\\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-m)\\le \\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1).\\)\u201d\n\n   *Verification*: Simple algebraic rearrangement of the previous inequality; correct.\n\n9. **Maximum possible sum of \\(m\\) distinct integers from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)**  \n   > \u201c\\(\\max\\sum_{s\\in M}(s-1)=\\sum_{t=n-m+1}^{n} t =\\frac{m(2n-m+1)}{2}.\\)\u201d\n\n   *Verification*: The sum is maximised by taking the largest \\(m\\) numbers; the formula follows from the arithmetic series sum. Correct.\n\n10. **Inequality (7) and its simplification**  \n    The solution manipulates the inequality to obtain  \n    \\[\n    n^{2}-n\\le 2mn-m^{2}-m .\n    \\]\n    *Verification*: The algebraic steps are straightforward and correct.\n\n11. **Argument that the inequality forces \\(m=n\\)**  \n    The author notes that the previous algebraic manipulation does not yield a contradiction for \\(m\\le n-1\\) and therefore proceeds to a *direct counting contradiction*.\n\n12. **Direct counting contradiction**  \n    - Compute the number of points not covered by diagonal lines:\n      \\[\n      |\\mathcal T_n|-P_{\\text{diag}}=\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1).\n      \\]\n    - Observ