t), or  \n   * **Case B:** \\(n-m=1\\) (exactly one diagonal is missing).\n\n6. **Analysis of Case B.**  \n\n   If the single missing diagonal is \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge 3\\), then (3) would read \\(1\\ge s-1\\), impossible.  \n   Hence the only admissible missing diagonal is \\(D_2\\), which contains the single point \\((1,1)\\).\n\n7. **Existence of a sunny line through \\((1,1)\\).**  \n\n   A line through \\((1,1)\\) is sunny provided its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   Choosing slope \\(2\\) gives the line  \n\n   \\[\n   y=2x-1,\n   \\]\n\n   which clearly is not parallel to any of the forbidden directions; thus it is sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n8. **Construction of admissible families.**\n\n   * **All diagonals (Case A).**  \n     The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n     \\]\n\n     cover \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and none is sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   * **All diagonals except \\(x+y=2\\) plus one sunny line (Case B).**  \n     Use the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x+y=3,\\;x+y=4,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n     \\]\n\n     together with the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\).  \n     These \\(n\\) distinct lines cover every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and contain exactly one sunny line. Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n9. **No other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   From the bound \\(n-m\\le1\\) we have at most one non‑diagonal line; if there is none we obtain \\(k=0\\), and if there is one it can be taken sunny, giving \\(k=1\\). No configuration can yield \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1\\}}\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid x+y=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_s\\) are pairwise disjoint, \\(|D_s|=s-1\\), and  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1} D_s. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen for each integer \\(s\\) the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap D_s\\) contains at most one lattice point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with real numbers \\(m,b\\) and \\(m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq-1\\).  \nIntersecting with \\(x+y=s\\) yields the unique solution (1) displayed above:\n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{s-b}{m+1},\\qquad y=\\frac{ms+b}{m+1}.\n\\]\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(s\\) both coordinates are integers.  \nMultiplying by the denominator \\(m+1\\) gives (2):\n\n\\[\n(m+1)x = s-b,\\qquad (m+1)y = ms+b.\n\\]\n\nSince the left‑hand sides are integers, the right‑hand sides are integers; in particular \\(b\\) must be rational.  \nWrite \\(b=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q>0\\).  \nFrom the first equality we obtain  \n\n\\[\nq(m+1)x = qs-p.\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side is an integer, therefore \\(qs-p\\) is an integer, i.e.  \n\n\\[\np\\equiv qs\\pmod{q}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are coprime, (6) determines at most one residue class for \\(s\\) modulo \\(q\\).  \nConsequently there can be at most one integer \\(s\\) for which (1) yields a lattice point. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nNow let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nM=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid x+y=s\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad m=|M|.\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(m\\) lines indexed by \\(M\\) are exactly the diagonal lines.  \nThe remaining \\(n-m\\) lines are non‑diagonal; among them let \\(t\\) be the number of sunny lines (so \\(0\\le t\\le n-m\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting points on each diagonal\n\nIf \\(s\\in M\\) the line \\(x+y=s\\) supplies all \\(s-1\\) points of \\(D_s\\).  \nIf \\(s\\notin M\\), Lemma 1 shows that each non‑diagonal line can contribute at most one point of \\(D_s\\). Hence at least \\(s-1\\) distinct non‑diagonal lines are needed to cover \\(D_s\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nn-m\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequences of (7)\n\nThe numbers \\(\\{s-1\\mid s\\notin M\\}\\) are distinct elements of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and there are exactly \\(n-m\\) of them.  \nTheir smallest possible sum is obtained by taking the smallest \\(n-m\\) integers:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInsert (8) into (7):\n\n\\[\nn-m\\ge\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n-m>0\\) we divide by \\(n-m\\) and obtain  \n\n\\[\n1\\ge\\frac{n-m+1}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n-m\\le1.\n\\]\n\nThus either\n\n* **Case A:** \\(n-m=0\\) (all diagonals are present), or  \n* **Case B:** \\(n-m=1\\) (exactly one diagonal is missing).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Analysis of Case B\n\nIf the missing diagonal is \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge3\\), then (7) would read \\(1\\ge s-1\\), impossible.  \nHence the only possible missing diagonal is \\(D_2\\), which consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of a sunny line through \\((1,1)\\)\n\nA line through \\((1,1)\\) is sunny provided its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nChoosing slope \\(2\\) gives the line  \n\n\\[\ny=2x-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is not parallel to any of the forbidden directions; therefore it is sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  They cover \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and none is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=3,\\;x+y=4,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n  \\]\n\n  together with the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\).  \n  These \\(n\\) distinct lines cover every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and contain exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nFrom the bound \\(n-m\\le1\\) we have at most one non‑diagonal line; consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed one. Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1\\}} .\n\\]"
