ct lines that together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   M:=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid x+y=s\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad m:=|M|.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the \\(m\\) lines indexed by \\(M\\) are exactly the *diagonal* lines \\(x+y=s\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-m\\) lines are non\u2011diagonal; among them let \\(t\\) be the number of sunny lines (clearly \\(0\\le t\\le n-m\\)).\n\n4. **Counting points on each diagonal.**  \n\n   *If a diagonal \\(D_s\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\)*, it supplies all its \\(s-1\\) points.\n\n   *If a diagonal \\(D_s\\) is absent*, Lemma\u202f1 shows that each non\u2011diagonal line can contribute **at most one** point of \\(D_s\\). Consequently at least \\(s-1\\) distinct non\u2011diagonal lines are needed to cover all points of that diagonal. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   n-m\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Deriving a bound from (1).**  \n\n   The numbers \\(\\{s-1\\mid s\\notin M\\}\\) are distinct integers taken from \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and there are exactly \\(n-m\\) of them.  \n   Their **minimum possible sum** occurs when they are the smallest \\(n-m\\) integers, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1)\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-m} i\n   =\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (1) and (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n-m\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(n-m>0\\) we may divide by \\(n-m\\) and obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n-m+1}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n-m\\le 1.\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore either  \n\n   * **Case\u202fA:** \\(n-m=0\\) (all diagonals are present), or  \n   * **Case\u202fB:** \\(n-m=1\\) (exactly one diagonal is missing).\n\n6. **Analysis of Case\u202fB.**  \n\n   If the missing diagonal is \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge 3\\), then (1) would read \\(1\\ge s-1\\), impossible.  \n   Hence the only admissible missing diagonal is \\(D_2\\), which consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\).\n\n7. **Existence of a su