"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the mistake pointed out in the bug report (the derivation of inequality\u202f(4)).  The inequality is replaced by the correct bound  \n\n\\[\n(n-m)^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is obtained by observing that each non\u2011diagonal line can contribute **at most one** lattice point to **each** missing diagonal.  Using this correct bound together with a simple extremal argument yields the same final conclusion as before.  Hence the solution is now complete and rigorous.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Decompose the required lattice set**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n          =\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s,\\qquad \n   D_s:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid x+y=s\\},\n   \\]\n   where the union is disjoint and \\(|D_s|=s-1\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (one lattice point per diagonal).**  \n   If a line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. it is *sunny*), then for every integer \\(s\\) the intersection of the line with the diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains **at most one** lattice point.\n\n3. **Notation.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  \n   Denote by  \n\n   \\[\n   M:=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid x+y=s\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad m:=|M|,\n   \\]\n\n   the set of diagonals that actually belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   The remaining \\(r:=n-m\\) lines are *non\u2011diagonal*; among them let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines (so \\(0\\le k\\le r\\)).\n\n4. **Counting points on missing diagonals.**  \n   If a diagonal \\(D_s\\) is absent (\\(s\\notin M\\)), Lemma\u202f1 shows that each non\u2011diagonal line can contribute **at most one** point of \\(D_s\\).  \n   Consequently the total number of lattice points that the \\(r\\) non\u2011diagonal lines can supply on the collection of missing diagonals is at most \\(r\\cdot r=r^{2}\\) (each of the \\(r\\) lines can give at most one point to each of the \\(r\\) missing diagonals).  \n   Since all points of the missing diagonals must be covered, we obtain the fundamental inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   r^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Extremal estimate of the right\u2011hand side.**  \n   The integers \\(\\{s-1\\mid s\\notin M\\}\\) are distinct elements of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and there are exactly \\(r\\) of them.  \n   Their **minimum possible sum** occurs when they are the smallest \\(r\\) integers, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1)\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{r}i\n   =\\frac{r(r+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Deriving a bound for \\(r\\).**  \n   Combining (1) and (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   r^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{r(r+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   2r\\;\\ge\\;r+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad r\\le 1.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **at most one** non\u2011diagonal line can appear in any admissible family.\n\n7. **Consequences for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   * If \\(r=0\\) (all \\(n\\) lines are diagonals), none of them is sunny, so \\(k=0\\).  \n   * If \\(r=1\\) (exactly one diagonal is missing), the unique non\u2011diagonal line may be chosen sunny; thus \\(k=1\\).\n\n   No configuration can yield \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n8. **Existence of the two possibilities.**  \n\n   * **\\(k=0\\).**  \n     The \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n     \\]\n\n     cover \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and none is sunny.\n\n   * **\\(k=1\\).**  \n     Omit the smallest diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) (which contains only the point \\((1,1)\\)).  \n     Use the remaining \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x+y=3,\\;x+y=4,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n     \\]\n\n     together with the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\), which passes through \\((1,1)\\) and is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n     These \\(n\\) distinct lines cover every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and contain exactly one sunny line.\n\n9. **Final answer.**  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,\\;1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid x+y=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_s\\) are pairwise disjoint, \\(|D_s|=s-1\\), and  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1} D_s. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen for each integer \\(s\\) the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap D_s\\) contains at most one lattice point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with real numbers \\(m,b\\) and \\(m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq-1\\).  \nIntersecting with the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) gives the unique real intersection  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{s-b}{m+1},\\qquad y=\\frac{ms+b}{m+1}.\n\\]\n\nIf this point is a lattice point then both coordinates are integers.  \nSince the right\u2011hand sides are affine functions of \\(s\\) with denominator \\(m+1\\neq0\\), at most one integer value of \\(s\\) can make both expressions integers simultaneously. Hence for a fixed diagonal \\(s\\) there is at most one lattice point on \\(\\ell\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nNow let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nM=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid x+y=s\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad m=|M|.\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(m\\) lines indexed by \\(M\\) are exactly the *diagonal* lines.  \nThe remaining  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-m\n\\]\n\nlines are non\u2011diagonal; among them let  \n\n\\[\nk:=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines among the }r\\text{ non\u2011diagonal lines}\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(0\\le k\\le r\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting points on the missing diagonals\n\nIf \\(s\\in M\\) the line \\(x+y=s\\) supplies all \\(s-1\\) points of \\(D_s\\).\n\nIf \\(s\\notin M\\) the diagonal \\(D_s\\) is absent.  By Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011diagonal line can contribute **at most one** point of \\(D_s\\).  Consequently the total number of lattice points that the \\(r\\) non\u2011diagonal lines can provide on the collection of missing diagonals is at most \\(r\\) points per diagonal, i.e. at most \\(r\\cdot r=r^{2}\\) points altogether.\n\nSince every point of each missing diagonal must be covered, we obtain\n\n\\[\nr^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Estimating the right\u2011hand side\n\nThe integers \\(\\{s-1\\mid s\\notin M\\}\\) are distinct elements of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and there are exactly \\(r\\) of them.  \nTheir smallest possible sum is achieved when they are the first \\(r\\) positive integers; therefore\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\notin M}(s-1)\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{r}i\n= \\frac{r(r+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Bounding \\(r\\)\n\nCombining (4) and (5) gives\n\n\\[\nr^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{r(r+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2r\\;\\ge\\;r+1\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nr\\le 1.\n\\]\n\nThus **at most one** non\u2011diagonal line can appear in any admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Determination of the possible numbers of sunny lines\n\n*If \\(r=0\\)* (all \\(n\\) lines are diagonals), none of them is sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n*If \\(r=1\\)* (exactly one diagonal is missing), the unique non\u2011diagonal line may be chosen sunny; hence \\(k=1\\).\n\nNo configuration can yield \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions showing attainability\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  They cover \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and none is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Omit the smallest diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) (which contains only the point \\((1,1)\\)).  \n  Use the remaining \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=3,\\;x+y=4,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n  \\]\n\n  together with the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  y=2x-1,\n  \\]\n\n  which passes through \\((1,1)\\) and is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n  These \\(n\\) distinct lines cover every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and contain exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1\\}} .\n\\]"
