e; picking any slope different from those finitely many forbidden slopes yields a sunny line with the required property.\n\nNow use the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\n\\]\n\nto cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\).  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) either has \\(x\\le n-k\\) (hence is covered by one of the vertical lines) or belongs to \\(D_k\\) (hence is covered by the corresponding sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\)).  \n\nThus we have a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) are sunny.  Hence a covering with any prescribed \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Necessity of at least one non‑sunny line (impossibility of \\(k=n\\)).\n\nWe prove the following statement by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n> **Claim.**  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) contains at least one line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line that contains two of these three points has either slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), slope \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or slope \\(-1\\).  Consequently, among any three lines covering the three points at least one line is non‑sunny.  The claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n#### Inductive step.\n\nAssume the claim holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Consider a covering of \\(S_{n+1}\\) by \\(n+1\\) distinct lines and let \\(\\ell\\) be the line that contains the point \\((1,1)\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is non‑sunny*, the claim is already satisfied.\n\n*Suppose \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.*  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the axes nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\), two distinct points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) cannot lie on \\(\\ell\\) while sharing the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate or the same sum of coordinates.  Hence the only point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) that can belong to \\(\\ell\\) is \\((1,1)\\) itself.  Removing \\(\\ell\\) and the point \\((1,1)\\) leaves the set  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}=S_n .\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n\\) lines still cover \\(S_n\\).  By the induction hypothesis, among these \\(n\\) lines there is at least one non‑sunny line.  Consequently the original covering of \\(S_{n+1}\\) also contains a non‑sunny line.\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(n+1\\), completing the induction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\nFrom Section 1 we have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nFrom Section 2 we know that a covering by \\(n\\) lines cannot consist solely of sunny lines; at least one line must be non‑sunny.  Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]"
