e or more sunny lines  \n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) covering all points of \\(T\\) and that at least three\nof them are sunny.  Relabel so that \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) are sunny.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) contains at most \\(n-1\\) points\nof \\(T\\); by Lemma\u202f2 each of the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines contains at most \\(n\\)\npoints of \\(T\\).  Hence the total number of points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|T|\\le 3\\,(n-1)+(n-3)\\,n\n      =3n-3+n^{2}-3n\n      =n^{2}-3. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(|T|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}-3,\n\\]\n\nbecause  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n^{2}-3)=\\frac{-n^{2}+n+6}{2}\n                         =\\frac{-(n-3)(n+2)}{2}\\ge 0,\n\\]\n\nwith equality only when \\(n=3\\).  When \\(n=3\\) the right\u2011hand side of (2) equals\n\\(6\\), which coincides with \\(|T|=6\\); however a direct inspection shows that\nthree distinct sunny lines cannot cover the six points of \\(T\\) (any three\nnon\u2011parallel lines miss at least one vertex of the triangle).  Thus the\nassumption \\(k\\ge 3\\) leads to a contradiction for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Sufficiency: constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncover \\(T\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for each admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2\\);\n* the two lines of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y= n\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nAll these lines are parallel either to the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\); hence none\nis sunny.  Every point \\((a,b)\\in T\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-2\\) (so it lies\non the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a\\ge n-1\\).  If \\(a\\ge n-1\\) then\n\\(b\\le n+1-a\\le 2\\), and the point belongs to one of the two lines\n\\(x+y=n\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Thus the whole set \\(T\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the construction for \\(k=0\\) and replace the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) by the\nsunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence \\(\\ell\\) is\nsunny.  For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-2\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(b=n+1-a\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\) because  \n\n\\[\nb = n+1-a = -\\frac12 a + \\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus all points that were previously covered by the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) are still\ncovered, now by the sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are the\nsame as in the \\(k=0\\) construction, so every point of \\(T\\) is covered and\nexactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  The case \\(k=2\\)\n\nAgain start from the \\(k=0\\) family and replace the two lines of slope \\(-1\\) by\nthe two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac{n+1}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}:\\; y = -\\frac13 x + \\frac{2n+2}{3}.\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes \\(-\\frac12\\) and \\(-\\frac13\\) avoid the forbidden values, so the\nlines are sunny and distinct.  For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-2\\)\nthe point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b=n+1-a\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{1}\\) (as shown above);\nthe point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b=n-a\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{2}\\) because  \n\n\\[\nb = n-a = -\\frac13 a + \\frac{2n+2}{3}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the two sunny lines together cover all points of \\(T\\) that were\npreviously covered by the two lines of slope \\(-1\\).  The vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) remain unchanged, covering the remaining points.  Hence the\nwhole set \\(T\\) is covered and exactly two lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that a configuration with three or more sunny lines is\nimpossible, and we have exhibited explicit configurations with \\(0\\), \\(1\\) and\n\\(2\\) sunny lines for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Therefore the set of admissible values\nof \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
