d b\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\quad(\\text{rows}),\\qquad\n   \\mathcal C=\\{x=a\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\quad(\\text{columns}),\\qquad\n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\quad(\\text{diagonals}).\n   \\]\n\n   Every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on exactly one line of each family:\n   \\[\n   (a,b)\\in y=b\\;\\cap\\;x=a\\;\\cap\\;x+y=a+b .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Pigeonhole principle for the non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have only \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   By the pigeonhole principle at least one of the three families contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\Big\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   of those lines.  \n   If \\(k\\ge 1\\) then \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\big\\rfloor < n\\); consequently there exists a whole row,\n   a whole column or a whole diagonal that does **not** appear among the chosen non‑sunny lines.\n\n3. **Existence of an uncovered lattice point.**  \n   Assume, without loss of generality, that the missing family is the rows.  \n   Then there is a number \\(b_{0}\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that no line \\(y=b_{0}\\) is present.  \n   Choose any \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_{0}\\); the point \\((a,b_{0})\\) belongs to the required triangular\n   set, but it cannot lie on any of the non‑sunny lines (it would need a row \\(y=b_{0}\\), a column\n   \\(x=a\\) or a diagonal \\(x+y=a+b_{0}\\)).  \n   Hence at least one point of the set remains uncovered by the non‑sunny lines.\n\n4. **Insufficiency of a single sunny line.**  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so it can contain at most one\n   point from each row, each column and each diagonal.  \n   In particular it can contain at most two points of the triangular set (any two distinct points already\n   determine the line).  \n   Since the uncovered set contains at least \\(n\\) points (there are \\(n\\) rows altogether), a single\n   sunny line cannot cover all of them.  \n\n5. **Consequences.**  \n   *   **\\(k=0\\) is feasible.**  \n       Take the \\(n\\) distinct rows \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); they are all non‑sunny and together they contain\n       every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   *   **\\(k\\ge 1\\) is not forced to be possible** by the pigeonhole argument alone, because the\n       uncovered points would require more than one sunny line.  \n       The argument therefore yields the necessary condition that any admissible configuration must\n       have \\(k=0\\); whether a configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) can be constructed by a more clever choice of\n       sunny lines is not settled here.\n\nThus the rigorous result obtained is that **\\(k=0\\) is always attainable, while the existence of\nconfigurations with \\(k\\ge 1\\) remains an open question** under the present analysis.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
