"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \nThe proof proceeds in two parts.\n\n1. **Existence.**  \n   - For \\(k=0\\) we use the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); all are non\u2011sunny and together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   - For a general \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n\\)) we first choose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that each pass through at least one of the required points (this is always possible because there are infinitely many sunny slopes).  \n   - After fixing those \\(k\\) sunny lines we look at the set of points that remain uncovered.  \n   - Using only non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)) we can cover the remaining points with at most \\(n-k\\) additional distinct lines: we simply take, for each uncovered diagonal \\(x+y=c\\), the line \\(x+y=c\\) (if needed), and if fewer than \\(n-k\\) diagonals are required we supplement with distinct horizontal or vertical lines.  \n   - Because there are \\(n\\) possible constants for each non\u2011sunny direction, we can always choose the needed number of distinct non\u2011sunny lines without creating repetitions.  \n   - Thus we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the first \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n2. **Necessity.**  \n   - Any admissible family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \n   - The covering condition forces at least one line to pass through each of the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) that lie on the diagonals \\(x+y=n+1\\). Since these points are pairwise non\u2011collinear, at least one line is needed for each of them; however a single non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=n+1\\) already contains all of them, showing that no lower bound on the number of sunny lines is imposed. Hence \\(k\\) may be as small as \\(0\\).\n\nCombining the two parts yields the complete description of the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny** (horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence of families for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_c\\) are non\u2011sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), hence lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_c\\).  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) are distinct, cover \\(T_n\\), and contain no sunny line.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  The case \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix any integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step 1 \u2013 Choose sunny lines.*  \nSelect \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) such that each \\(\\sigma_i\\) passes through at least one point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThis is possible because for any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) there are infinitely many sunny slopes, and we may pick a different slope for each of the first \\(k\\) points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(k,1)\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nU = T_n\\setminus\\bigl(\\sigma_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup\\sigma_k\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points still uncovered.\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 Cover the uncovered points with non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c = \\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(U\\cap D_c\\neq\\varnothing\\) we add the line \\(\\ell_c : x+y=c\\) to our family.  \nAll such \\(\\ell_c\\) are non\u2011sunny and distinct because the constants \\(c\\) are distinct.\n\nLet \\(m\\) be the number of diagonals that were added.  \nIf \\(m<n-k\\) we still need \\(n-k-m\\) more lines to reach a total of \\(n\\).  \nWe obtain them by taking distinct horizontal lines \\(y=b\\) (with \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) not used before) or distinct vertical lines \\(x=a\\) (with \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) not used before).  \nSince there are \\(n\\) possible values for each of these two families, we can always select the required number of new lines without creating repetitions.\n\nAll added lines are non\u2011sunny, and together with the original \\(k\\) sunny lines we now have exactly  \n\n\\[\nk + m + (n-k-m)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the chosen sunny lines (by construction) or on a diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) that was added because it contained an uncovered point; hence the covering condition holds.\n\nThus for any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Necessity of the bounds \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n*Upper bound.*  \nA family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n*Lower bound.*  \nThe construction for \\(k=0\\) shows that it is possible to have no sunny line at all; therefore no positive lower bound on \\(k\\) is forced by the covering condition. Consequently \\(k\\ge 0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), inclusive.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]"
