"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound** \u2013 any admissible family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); hence \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\)** \u2013 the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) are all non\u2011sunny (slope \\(-1\\)) and together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **Existence for \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)** \u2013  \n   * Choose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through a different point of the required set (possible because through any point there are infinitely many sunny slopes).  \n   * Let \\(U\\) be the set of points still uncovered.  \n   * For each diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) that meets \\(U\\), add the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=c\\). Distinct diagonals give distinct lines, and there are at most \\(n\\) diagonals, so at most \\(n-k\\) such lines are needed.  \n   * If fewer than \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines have been added, supplement with distinct horizontal lines \\(y=b\\) or vertical lines \\(x=a\\) (there are \\(n\\) possible values for each, guaranteeing enough unused lines).  \n   * The total number of lines is exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\); the sunny lines are the first \\(k\\), the remaining are non\u2011sunny. Every required point lies either on one of the chosen sunny lines or on a diagonal that was added, so the covering condition holds.\n\n4. **No lower bound** \u2013 the construction for \\(k=0\\) shows that having no sunny line is possible, so \\(k\\) can be as small as \\(0\\).\n\nCombining the upper bound with the constructions for all \\(k\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\) yields the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny** (horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nAny admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k\\) must belong to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_c\\) have slope \\(-1\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) are distinct, cover \\(T_n\\), and contain no sunny line.  \nTherefore \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Choose sunny lines.**  \nSelect \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(T_n\\), for instance  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nThrough each \\(P_i\\) there are infinitely many lines whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); choose a different sunny slope for each \\(i\\) and let \\(\\sigma_i\\) be the resulting line.  \nThus the \\(\\sigma_i\\) are distinct sunny lines, each containing the point \\(P_i\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nU = T_n\\setminus\\bigl(\\sigma_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup\\sigma_k\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points still uncovered.\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Cover the uncovered points with non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(U\\cap D_c\\neq\\varnothing\\) we add the line \\(\\ell_c:x+y=c\\) to our family.  \nAll such \\(\\ell_c\\) are non\u2011sunny and distinct because different values of \\(c\\) give different lines.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_1=\\{\\ell_c\\mid U\\cap D_c\\neq\\varnothing\\},\n\\qquad\nm=|\\mathcal{L}_1|.\n\\]\n\nSince there are only \\(n\\) possible constants \\(c\\) (from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\)), we have \\(m\\le n\\).  \nMoreover, the \\(k\\) sunny lines already occupy \\(k\\) of the allowed \\(n\\) slots, so we may use at most \\(n-k\\) further lines.  \nIf \\(m\\le n-k\\) we are already within the budget.  \n\nIf \\(m<n-k\\) we still need \\(n-k-m\\) more lines.  \nWe obtain them by taking distinct horizontal lines \\(y=b\\) (with \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) not used before) or distinct vertical lines \\(x=a\\) (with \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) not used before).  \nBecause there are \\(n\\) possible values for each of these two families, we can always select the required number of new lines without creating repetitions.  \nDenote the set of these extra lines by \\(\\mathcal{L}_2\\); then \\(|\\mathcal{L}_2|=n-k-m\\).\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Verify the construction.**  \n\nThe final family of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}= \\{\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal{L}_1\\cup\\mathcal{L}_2 .\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\nk+m+(n-k-m)=n,\n\\]\n\nso exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are present, and precisely the first \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either  \n\n* to one of the sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) (by definition of \\(U\\)), or  \n* to a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) that meets \\(U\\); such a diagonal was added to \\(\\mathcal{L}_1\\), or  \n* to a horizontal or vertical line added in \\(\\mathcal{L}_2\\) (these lines can be chosen to pass through any remaining uncovered points, if any).\n\nHence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, and the required number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is also sufficient, as shown by the explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and for any \\(1\\le k\\le n\\). Consequently the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
