tains exactly the single point \\((n-1,1)\\) of \\(S_n\\).  \n   - Choose \\(\\sigma_{2}\\) analogously to pass through \\((n,1)\\) with a slope \\(\\beta\\) distinct from \\(0,-1,\\alpha\\) and from the finitely many forbidden slopes; again \\(\\sigma_{2}\\) meets \\(S_n\\) only at \\((n,1)\\).  \n\n   Thus we have a covering by \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly two sunny lines.  \n\n   To obtain fewer sunny lines, simply replace some of the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) by additional sunny lines that each cover a single uncovered point of the last two columns. Because each such sunny line can be chosen to avoid all other points of \\(S_n\\) (the same \u201cavoid the finitely many forbidden slopes\u2019\u2019 argument), we can replace any subset of the vertical lines, one by one, and thereby obtain coverings with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Excluding \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists. Then only one line would be non\u2011sunny, i.e. parallel to a forbidden direction. By Lemma\u202f1 a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most the whole *first* column (all points with \\(x=1\\)) or the whole *first* row (all points with \\(y=1\\)) or the whole diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). In each of these three possibilities the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2- (n)\\) points would have to be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Since a sunny line can meet \\(S_n\\) in at most two points, the total number of incidences contributed by the sunny lines is at most \\(2(n-1)\\). Adding the at most \\(n\\) incidences contributed by the single non\u2011sunny line gives at most \\(2n-2+n =3n-2\\) incidences, whereas \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) exceeds \\(3n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 4\\). For \\(n=3\\) a direct inspection shows that no covering with two sunny lines exists (the six points of \\(S_3\\) cannot be covered by three lines with two of them sunny). Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nCombining the upper bound and the explicit constructions, the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line has a finite slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Incidence bound)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThen at most \\(n-2\\) of the lines can be sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by \\(\\nu(L)=|L\\cap S_n|\\) the number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) is sunny, then its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\). Consequently two distinct points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) cannot have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, nor the same sum of coordinates; otherwise the line would be respectively vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\). Hence a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{if }L\\text{ is sunny, then }\\nu(L)\\le 2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny (i.e. parallel to one of the forbidden directions) it may contain many points of \\(S_n\\); we only need the trivial bound \\(\\nu(L)\\le |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\nSumming \\(\\nu(L)\\) over all lines gives the total number of incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the points of \\(S_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\nu(L_i)=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines among the \\(L_i\\). Using (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\nu(L_i)\\le 2s+\\Bigl(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\Bigr)(n-s) .\n\\]\n\nSince the right\u2011hand side is certainly larger than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) when \\(s\\ge n-1\\), we obtain a sharper bound by observing that a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the whole first column, the whole first row, or the whole diagonal). Thus\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\nu(L_i)\\le 2s + n\\,(n-s). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2) and (3) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le 2s + n\\,(n-s)= n^2 - (n-2)s .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives\n\n\\[\n(n-2)s \\le n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = \\frac{n(2n-(n+1))}2 = \\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\ns \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} = \\frac{n}{2}\\,\\frac{n-1}{n-2}< n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer, we obtain \\(s\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe first exhibit a covering with exactly two sunny lines; then we show how to reduce the number of sunny lines down to zero.\n\n#### 2.1  Two sunny lines\n\nConsider the following set of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) the vertical line  \n\n  \\[\n  V_i:\\;x=i .\n  \\]\n\n  These lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n* A sunny line \\(\\sigma_{1}\\) passing through the point \\((n-1,1)\\) with a slope \\(\\alpha\\) chosen as follows.  \n  The set of slopes that would make \\(\\sigma_{1}\\) pass through any other point of \\(S_n\\) is finite (it consists of the slopes of the lines joining \\((n-1,1)\\) to each of the other \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-1\\) points). Choose \\(\\alpha\\) different from \\(0,-1\\) and from all those finitely many forbidden slopes. Then \\(\\sigma_{1}\\) meets \\(S_n\\) only at \\((n-1,1)\\).\n\n* A sunny line \\(\\sigma_{2}\\) passing through \\((n,1)\\) with a slope \\(\\beta\\) chosen analogously, distinct from \\(0,-1,\\alpha\\) and from the finitely many slopes that would create an extra intersection with \\(S_n\\). Consequently \\(\\sigma_{2}\\) meets \\(S_n\\) only at \\((n,1)\\).\n\nAll together we have \\( (n-2)+2 = n\\) distinct lines.  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to one of them:\n\n* If its \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(i\\le n-2\\) it lies on \\(V_i\\);\n* If its \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(n-1\\) the only point of \\(S_n\\) with that abscissa is \\((n-1,1)\\), which lies on \\(\\sigma_{1}\\);\n* If its \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(n\\) the only point of \\(S_n\\) with that abscissa is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(\\sigma_{2}\\).\n\nThus we have a covering with exactly two sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.2  Reducing the number of sunny lines\n\nTo obtain a covering with \\(k\\) sunny lines where \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), start from the construction above (which has two sunny lines) and, if \\(k<2\\), replace some of the vertical lines \\(V_i\\) by additional sunny lines that each cover a single point of the set \\(\\{(n-1,1),(n,1)\\}\\) already covered, or by a sunny line covering a point of the first column that is not yet covered.  \n\nMore systematically, for any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) we can produce a covering with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines as follows:\n\n* Keep the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-2-t}\\) (these are non\u2011sunny).\n* For each of the remaining \\(t\\) columns \\(x=n-1-t+1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) introduce a sunny line \\(\\sigma_j\\) that passes through the unique point of \\(S_n\\) lying in that column (the point \\((x,1)\\)) and whose slope avoids all forbidden slopes, exactly as in the construction of \\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\) above.\n\nBecause each such sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in precisely one point, the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(n-2-t)+t = n-2,\n\\]\n\nand we then add the two sunny lines \\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\) from \u00a72.1 to reach a total of \\(n\\) lines with exactly  \n\n\\[\nt+2 = k\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  By choosing \\(t=k-2\\) (with the convention that \\(t=0\\) when \\(k=0,1\\)) we obtain a covering for every \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nHence for each admissible \\(k\\) a concrete covering exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Then there is precisely one non\u2011sunny line, say \\(L\\).  \n\nA non\u2011sunny line can be of three types:\n\n* vertical (\\(x=c\\));\n* horizontal (\\(y=c\\));\n* slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nIn each case the line can intersect \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the whole first column, the whole first row, or the whole diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)). Consequently the total number of incidences contributed by the non\u2011sunny line is at most \\(n\\).\n\nEach sunny line contributes at most two incidences (Lemma\u202f1). With \\(n-1\\) sunny lines we obtain at most \\(2(n-1)\\) further incidences. Hence the total number of incidences is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\nn+2(n-1)=3n-2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). For every \\(n\\ge 4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 3n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the incidence bound is violated, a contradiction.  \n\nFor the remaining case \\(n=3\\) we can check directly. The set  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains three points. Any line through two of them is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); therefore any covering by three lines must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, so \\(k=2\\) (i.e. \\(n-1\\)) is impossible.\n\nFinally, a covering with all \\(n\\) lines sunny would in particular have \\(k=n-1\\), which we have just shown impossible; therefore \\(k=n\\) is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n- Lemma\u202f1 shows that at most \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n- The explicit construction in \u00a72 produces, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n- The cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are ruled out by the incidence argument of \u00a73.\n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"

