tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny.*  \nThen \\(L\\) is either vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\).  \nThe intersection of such a line with the region \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) consists of at most \\(n\\) lattice points (the whole first column, the whole first row, or the whole diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\nu(L)\\le n\\qquad\\text{if }L\\text{ is non\u2011sunny}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSumming \\(\\nu(L)\\) over all lines gives the total number of incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the points of \\(S_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\nu(L_i)=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines. Using (1) and (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\nu(L_i)\\le 2s + n\\,(n-s)=n^2-(n-2)s .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n(n-2)s\\le n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2 =\\frac{n(n-1)}2 ,\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\ns\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}<n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer, \\(s\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\n#### 2.1  A covering with exactly two sunny lines\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines*  \n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\n*Sunny line \\(\\sigma_{1}\\).*  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) that is **different from** the slopes of the \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-1\\) lines joining \\((n-1,1)\\) to any other point of \\(S_n\\). Such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because the forbidden set is finite. Let  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{1}:\\; y-1=\\alpha\\,(x-(n-1)).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\sigma_{1}\\) passes through \\((n-1,1)\\) and through no other point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Sunny line \\(\\sigma_{2}\\).*  \nChoose \\(\\beta\\notin\\{0,-1,\\alpha\\}\\) distinct from the slopes of the lines joining \\((n,1)\\) to any other point of \\(S_n\\). Define  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{2}:\\; y-1=\\beta\\,(x-n).\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(\\sigma_{2}\\) meets \\(S_n\\) only at \\((n,1)\\).\n\nNow check that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of these lines.\n\n* If the \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(i\\le n-2\\) the point lies on \\(V_i\\).\n* The column \\(x=n-1\\) contains the points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\); both are covered by the **non\u2011sunny** line \\(V_{\\,n-1}\\) (which we keep among the vertical lines, i.e. we actually use the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) instead of a sunny line for this column).\n* The column \\(x=n\\) contains only \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(\\sigma_{2}\\).\n\nThus we have a covering by \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly **two** sunny lines (\\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\)).\n\n#### 2.2  Obtaining any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be given, \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nt = n-2-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of vertical lines we will keep}).\n\\]\n\nConstruction:\n\n1. Keep the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\) (these are non\u2011sunny).\n2. For each integer \\(x\\) with \\(t+1\\le x\\le n-2\\) introduce a sunny line \\(\\tau_x\\) that passes through the single point \\((x,1)\\) of \\(S_n\\) having that abscissa. Choose its slope so that it avoids the finitely many forbidden slopes (the same argument as for \\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\)). Each \\(\\tau_x\\) therefore meets \\(S_n\\) only at \\((x,1)\\).\n3. Keep the two lines \\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\) from the base covering.\n\nCounting lines: we have \\(t\\) vertical lines, \\((n-2-t)\\) new sunny lines \\(\\tau_x\\), and the two original sunny lines, for a total of  \n\n\\[\nt+(n-2-t)+2 = n .\n\\]\n\nCounting sunny lines: the sunny lines are the \\((n-2-t)\\) lines \\(\\tau_x\\) together with \\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\); their number is  \n\n\\[\n(n-2-t)+2 = k .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) are covered:\n\n* Points with \\(x\\le t\\) are on the kept vertical lines.\n* Points with \\(t+1\\le x\\le n-2\\) are on the corresponding \\(\\tau_x\\) (the only point with that \\(x\\) is \\((x,1)\\)).\n* Points with \\(x=n-1\\) are on the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (non\u2011sunny).\n* The point \\((n,1)\\) is on \\(\\sigma_{2}\\).\n\nHence for every \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists. Then there is precisely one non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\).\n\n* By the discussion in Lemma\u202f1, a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n* Each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nConsequently the total number of incidences is at most  \n\n\\[\nn + 2\\,(n-1)=3n-2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). For every \\(n\\ge 4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 3n-2 ,\n\\]\n\na contradiction. For \\(n=3\\) one checks directly that the six points of \\(S_3\\) cannot be covered by three lines with two of them sunny. Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, then in particular a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would exist (by discarding any one line), which we have shown impossible. Therefore \\(k=n\\) is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n- Lemma\u202f1 gives the universal upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n- The explicit construction of \u00a72 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n- The incidence argument of \u00a73 excludes the remaining possibilities \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"

