s indeed present because the list \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-2}\\) includes it when \\(n-2\\ge n-1\\), i.e. for all \\(n\\ge3\\); explicitly we may add it as \\(V_{n-1}\\) if desired – it does not affect the count of sunny lines.)\n\n   *Increasing the number of sunny lines.*  \n   Let the desired \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Write  \n\n   \\[\n   t=n-2-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of vertical lines we shall keep}).\n   \\]\n\n   - Keep the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\) (these are non‑sunny).\n   - For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(t+1\\le i\\le n-2\\) construct a sunny line \\(\\tau_i\\) that passes through the unique point \\((i,1)\\) of its column and avoids every other point of \\(S_n\\).  This is possible because only finitely many slopes would cause an extra intersection, and we may also avoid the forbidden slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\n   - Retain the two sunny lines \\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\) from the base covering.\n\n   Counting lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{t}_{\\text{vertical}}+\n   \\underbrace{(n-2-t)}_{\\text{new sunny } \\tau_i}+\n   \\underbrace{2}_{\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}}=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Counting sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   (n-2-t)+2 = k .\n   \\]\n\n   Coverage of \\(S_n\\) is immediate:\n   * columns \\(1,\\dots ,t\\) are covered by the retained vertical lines;\n   * for each \\(i\\in\\{t+1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) is covered by \\(\\tau_i\\); all other points of those columns have already been covered by the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\) or belong to columns \\(n-1,n\\) which are handled as in the base covering.\n   * column \\(n-1\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\);\n   * column \\(n\\) is covered by \\(\\sigma_{2}\\).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists.\n\n4. **Excluding \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines existed.  Then exactly one line, say \\(L\\), is non‑sunny.