umn \\(C_i\\) is not covered by a vertical line, it must be intersected by a distinct non‑vertical line.  Since there are \\(n\\) columns, the number of columns that are not covered by a vertical line is at most the number of non‑vertical lines.\n\nLet \\(v\\) be the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Then the number of non‑vertical lines equals \\(n-v\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\nn-v\\;\\ge\\; \\#\\{\\text{columns not covered by a vertical line}\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAmong the non‑vertical lines the only ones that are **not** sunny are the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) and the line \\(x+y=0\\).  At most one of them can appear in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (otherwise two lines would be parallel, contradicting distinctness).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{non‑vertical lines}\\}=n-v\\le k+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\nn-v\\le k+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v\\ge n-(k+1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(v\\) is a non‑negative integer, we must have \\(k\\le n-2\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n#### 2.1  A base covering with exactly two sunny lines\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) set  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical (non‑sunny) lines.\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\alpha\\neq\\frac{b-1}{a-(n-1)}\\;\\text{for every }(a,b)\\in S_n\\setminus\\{(n-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set of forbidden values is finite, so such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists.  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{1}:\\; y-1=\\alpha\\,(x-(n-1)).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\sigma_{1}\\) passes through \\((n-1,1)\\) and through no other point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nSimilarly choose \\(\\beta\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\beta\\notin\\{0,-1,\\alpha\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\beta\\neq\\frac{b-1}{a-n}\\;\\text{for every }(a,b)\\in S_n\\setminus\\{(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nand set  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{2}:\\; y-1=\\beta\\,(x-n).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\sigma_{2}\\) meets \\(S_n\\) only at \\((n,1)\\).\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-2},\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, contain exactly two sunny lines (\\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\)), and cover all points of \\(S_n\\):\n* For \\(i\\le n-2\\) the whole column \\(C_i\\) is covered by \\(V_i\\).\n* Column \\(C_{n-1}\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (which is \\(V_{n-1}\\) when \\(n-1\\le n-2\\); for \\(n=3\\) the column consists of the single point \\((2,1)\\) already covered by \\(\\sigma_{1}\\)).\n* Column \\(C_n\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\) lying on \\(\\sigma_{2}\\).\n\nHence a covering with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n#### 2.2  Obtaining any prescribed \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nt=n-2-k\\qquad(\\text{number of vertical lines we keep}).\n\\]\n\n* Keep the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\).  \n* For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(t+1\\le i\\le n-2\\) construct a sunny line \\(\\tau_i\\) through the point \\((i,1)\\) that avoids all other points of \\(S_n\\).  This is possible because only finitely many slopes would cause an extra intersection, and we may also avoid the forbidden slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\n* Retain the two sunny lines \\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\) from the base covering.\n\nThe resulting family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\}\\cup\\{\\tau_i\\mid t+1\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\nt+(n-2-t)+2=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, of which  \n\n\\[\n(n-2-t)+2=k\n\\]\n\nare sunny.\n\nCoverage:\n* Columns \\(1,\\dots ,t\\) are covered by the retained vertical lines.\n* For each \\(i\\in\\{t+1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) is covered by \\(\\tau_i\\); all other points of those columns have already been covered by the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\) or belong to columns \\(n-1,n\\) which are handled as before.\n* Column \\(n-1\\) is covered by the vertical line