n   (The vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) is indeed present because the list \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-2}\\) includes it when \\(n-2\\ge n-1\\), i.e. for all \\(n\\ge3\\); explicitly we may add it as \\(V_{n-1}\\) if desired \u2013 it does not affect the count of sunny lines.)\n\n   *Increasing the number of sunny lines.*  \n   Let the desired \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Write  \n\n   \\[\n   t=n-2-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of vertical lines we shall keep}).\n   \\]\n\n   - Keep the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\) (these are non\u2011sunny).\n   - For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(t+1\\le i\\le n-2\\) construct a sunny line \\(\\tau_i\\) that passes through the unique point \\((i,1)\\) of its column and avoids every other point of \\(S_n\\).  This is possible because only finitely many slopes would cause an extra intersection, and we may also avoid the forbidden slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\n   - Retain the two sunny lines \\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\) from the base covering.\n\n   Counting lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{t}_{\\text{vertical}}+\n   \\underbrace{(n-2-t)}_{\\text{new sunny } \\tau_i}+\n   \\underbrace{2}_{\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}}=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Counting sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   (n-2-t)+2 = k .\n   \\]\n\n   Coverage of \\(S_n\\) is immediate:\n   * columns \\(1,\\dots ,t\\) are covered by the retained vertical lines;\n   * for each \\(i\\in\\{t+1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) is covered by \\(\\tau_i\\); all other points of those columns have already been covered by the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\) or belong to columns \\(n-1,n\\) which are handled as in the base covering.\n   * column \\(n-1\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\);\n   * column \\(n\\) is covered by \\(\\sigma_{2}\\).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists.\n\n4. **Excluding \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines existed.  Then exactly one line, say \\(L\\), is non\u2011sunny.  The only possibilities for a non\u2011sunny line are:\n   * a vertical line (covers an entire column, at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\));\n   * a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (covers at most one point of each column, hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\));\n   * the line \\(x+y=0\\) (does not intersect \\(S_n\\) at all because all points of \\(S_n\\) have positive coordinates).  \n   In any case \\(|L\\cap S_n|\\le n\\).\n\n   Each sunny line meets a given column in at most one point, so a sunny line can contribute at most one point per column, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points overall.  With \\(n-1\\) sunny lines we obtain the crude bound  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le n+(n-1)\\,n = n^2 .\n   \\]\n\n   However \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  For \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n\\) and the bound does not give a contradiction.  A sharper argument is needed.\n\n   Observe that a sunny line cannot contain two points that lie in the same **row** (same \\(y\\)-coordinate), because two points with equal \\(y\\) would give slope \\(0\\).  Likewise it cannot contain two points that lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  Therefore each sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the three families:\n   * the set of vertical columns,\n   * the set of horizontal rows,\n   * the set of points on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   The diagonal contains exactly \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); the rows together contain \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) points, and the columns contain the same number.  Counting incidences of sunny lines with these three families shows that with only one non\u2011sunny line we cannot cover all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points when \\(n\\ge3\\).  A concise way is to use Lemma\u202f1: if only one line is non\u2011vertical, then at most one column is covered completely; the remaining \\(n-1\\) columns would each need a distinct non\u2011vertical line, but we have only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, and each sunny line can meet a column in at most one point, so the points of those columns beyond the first row would remain uncovered.  Hence a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, removing any one of them would give a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, which we have just shown cannot exist.  Therefore \\(k=n\\) is also impossible.\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable; and the impossibility of \\(k=n-1,n\\) completes the classification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (column covering)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines among them. Then \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the **column**\n\n\\[\nC_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\subset S_n .\n\\]\n\nThe columns are pairwise disjoint and \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}C_i=S_n\\).\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nIf a line is vertical, say \\(x=c\\), then it coincides with exactly one column \\(C_c\\) and therefore covers all points of that column.\n\n*Non\u2011vertical lines.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be a line that is not vertical.  Fix a column \\(C_i\\).  If \\(L\\) met \\(C_i\\) in two distinct points, those two points would have the same abscissa \\(i\\); the line through them would have to be vertical, contradicting the assumption.  Hence a non\u2011vertical line meets a given column in **at most one** point.\n\nConsequently, if a column \\(C_i\\) is not covered by a vertical line, it must be intersected by a distinct non\u2011vertical line.  Since there are \\(n\\) columns, the number of columns that are not covered by a vertical line is at most the number of non\u2011vertical lines.\n\nLet \\(v\\) be the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Then the number of non\u2011vertical lines equals \\(n-v\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\nn-v\\;\\ge\\; \\#\\{\\text{columns not covered by a vertical line}\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAmong the non\u2011vertical lines the only ones that are **not** sunny are the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) and the line \\(x+y=0\\).  At most one of them can appear in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (otherwise two lines would be parallel, contradicting distinctness).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{non\u2011vertical lines}\\}=n-v\\le k+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\nn-v\\le k+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v\\ge n-(k+1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(v\\) is a non\u2011negative integer, we must have \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n#### 2.1  A base covering with exactly two sunny lines\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) set  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines.\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\alpha\\neq\\frac{b-1}{a-(n-1)}\\;\\text{for every }(a,b)\\in S_n\\setminus\\{(n-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set of forbidden values is finite, so such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists.  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{1}:\\; y-1=\\alpha\\,(x-(n-1)).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\sigma_{1}\\) passes through \\((n-1,1)\\) and through no other point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nSimilarly choose \\(\\beta\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\beta\\notin\\{0,-1,\\alpha\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\beta\\neq\\frac{b-1}{a-n}\\;\\text{for every }(a,b)\\in S_n\\setminus\\{(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nand set  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{2}:\\; y-1=\\beta\\,(x-n).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\sigma_{2}\\) meets \\(S_n\\) only at \\((n,1)\\).\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-2},\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, contain exactly two sunny lines (\\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\)), and cover all points of \\(S_n\\):\n* For \\(i\\le n-2\\) the whole column \\(C_i\\) is covered by \\(V_i\\).\n* Column \\(C_{n-1}\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (which is \\(V_{n-1}\\) when \\(n-1\\le n-2\\); for \\(n=3\\) the column consists of the single point \\((2,1)\\) already covered by \\(\\sigma_{1}\\)).\n* Column \\(C_n\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\) lying on \\(\\sigma_{2}\\).\n\nHence a covering with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n#### 2.2  Obtaining any prescribed \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nt=n-2-k\\qquad(\\text{number of vertical lines we keep}).\n\\]\n\n* Keep the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\).  \n* For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(t+1\\le i\\le n-2\\) construct a sunny line \\(\\tau_i\\) through the point \\((i,1)\\) that avoids all other points of \\(S_n\\).  This is possible because only finitely many slopes would cause an extra intersection, and we may also avoid the forbidden slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\n* Retain the two sunny lines \\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\) from the base covering.\n\nThe resulting family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\}\\cup\\{\\tau_i\\mid t+1\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\nt+(n-2-t)+2=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, of which  \n\n\\[\n(n-2-t)+2=k\n\\]\n\nare sunny.\n\nCoverage:\n* Columns \\(1,\\dots ,t\\) are covered by the retained vertical lines.\n* For each \\(i\\in\\{t+1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) is covered by \\(\\tau_i\\); all other points of those columns have already been covered by the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_t\\) or belong to columns \\(n-1,n\\) which are handled as before.\n* Column \\(n-1\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\).\n* Column \\(n\\) is covered by \\(\\sigma_{2}\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Then exactly one line, say \\(L\\), is non\u2011sunny.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can be only of three types:\n\n* **Vertical** \\(x=c\\): it coincides with a column and therefore contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n* **Horizontal** \\(y=c\\): because all points of \\(S_n\\) have positive coordinates and \\(c\\ge1\\), such a line meets each column in at most one point, hence contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n* **The line \\(x+y=0\\)**: it does not intersect \\(S_n\\) at all.\n\nThus \\(|L\\cap S_n|\\le n\\).\n\nEach sunny line meets a fixed column in at most one point (Lemma\u202f1).  Consequently a sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) columns, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  With \\(n-1\\) sunny lines we obtain the crude bound  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le n+(n-1)n = n^2 .\n\\]\n\nWhile this bound does not contradict \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), a sharper observation rules out the configuration.\n\nConsider the **first row** of \\(S_n\\), i.e. the set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(R\\) lie in distinct columns.  A vertical line can cover at most one of them (the one belonging to its column).  A sunny line can also cover at most one point of \\(R\\), because two points of \\(R\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate and a line through them would have slope \\(0\\), which is forbidden for sunny lines.  Hence each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers at most one point of \\(R\\).  Since \\(|R|=n\\) and we have only \\(n\\) lines, every line must contain **exactly one** point of \\(R\\).\n\nNow look at the **second row**  \n\n\\[\nR'=\\{(a,2)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA vertical line that already contains a point of \\(R\\) cannot contain any point of \\(R'\\) (different \\(y\\)-coordinates).  A sunny line that contains a point of \\(R\\) cannot contain a point of \\(R'\\) either, because the slope between a point of \\(R\\) and a point of \\(R'\\) is \\(\\frac{1}{\\Delta x}\\neq 0,-1\\) but would be forced to be the same for all points of \\(R\\) that the line meets, which is impossible since the \\(\\Delta x\\) values are distinct.  Therefore **no line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can contain a point of \\(R'\\)**, contradicting the requirement that all points of \\(S_n\\) be covered.\n\nThus a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, removing any one would give a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, which we have just shown impossible.  Hence \\(k=n\\) is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition from Lemma\u202f1 is \\(k\\le n-2\\); the construction of \u00a72 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable; and the impossibility proof of \u00a73 excludes the remaining values \\(k=n-1,n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}}\n\\]"
