e on the straight line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n+1}:\\; x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, adding the line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) to any configuration that already covers \\(T_n\\) yields a configuration covering \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Controlling the sunny count\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) is **not** sunny because it is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nHence, if we start from a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) (which has \\(k\\) sunny lines) and add \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\), we obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) lines with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\nTo obtain a configuration with **\\(k+1\\)** sunny lines, we replace \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) by a different line that still passes through all points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) but is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  \n\nTake any real number \\(\\lambda\\neq 0,-1\\) and consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n+1}^{\\lambda}:\\; y = -x + (n+2) + \\lambda (x-1).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(\\lambda\\neq 0,-1\\) the line \\(\\ell_{n+1}^{\\lambda}\\)\n\n* passes through \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\), hence through every point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) (the two extreme points determine the line, and all intermediate points lie on it);\n* has slope \\(-1+\\lambda\\), which is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), nor \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThus \\(\\ell_{n+1}^{\\lambda}\\) is sunny.\n\nTherefore, starting from \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) and adding the sunny line \\(\\ell_{n+1}^{\\lambda}\\) yields a configuration for \\(n+1\\) lines with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.4  Distinctness of the lines\n\nThe added line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) (or \\(\\ell_{n+1}^{\\lambda}\\)) is not equal to any line already present in \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(\\cdot)}\\): every line of \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(\\cdot)}\\) meets the set \\(T_n\\) but does **not** contain the point \\((n+1,1)\\) (or \\((1,n+1)\\)), whereas both \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{n+1}^{\\lambda}\\) contain these points. Hence the total number of lines is indeed \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the induction\n\nFrom the inductive hypothesis we have configurations for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThe construction of §3 shows that from a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines we can obtain configurations for \\(n+1\\) lines with\n\n* the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines (by adding the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\));\n* the number \\(k+1\\) of sunny lines (by adding the sunny line \\(\\ell_{n+1}^{\\lambda}\\)).\n\nConsequently, for \\(n+1\\) we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nBy the base case \\(n=3\\) and the inductive step, the claim is true for all integers \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ and every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ there exists a set of }n\\text{ distinct lines satisfying the two conditions and containing exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines.}}\n\\]"
