er \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\). In both cases the point lies on one of the vertical lines \\(x=n-1\\) or \\(x=n\\) only when \\(a=n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\), which is impossible.  \n  To avoid this difficulty we modify the selection: replace the two vertical lines by the diagonal lines \\(x+y=n\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n\nThus we finally take  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(0)}_n=\\bigl\\{\\,y=i\\;|\\;i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are non\u2011sunny (horizontal or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nNow any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies one of the following:\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(y=b\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge n-1\\) then \\(a+b\\ge n-1\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a+b\\in\\{n,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, the family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and none of them is sunny. This proves the existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nWe now construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, and together they cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\; y = \\alpha_i x + \\beta_i ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) is chosen so that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand the intercept \\(\\beta_i\\) is chosen so that the line passes through the point \\((i,1)\\). Explicitly we may take  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i = 1 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\beta_i = 1-i .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\; y = x + 1 - i .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(M_i\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(M_i\\) is sunny. The lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nWe claim that the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(n)}_n=\\{M_1,M_2,\\dots ,M_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n,\\qquad b\\le n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nConsider the line \\(M_a\\). By definition \\(M_a\\) passes through \\((a,1)\\) and has equation \\(y = x + 1 - a\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=a\\) and \\(y=b\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb = a + 1 - a = 1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the only point of \\(M_a\\) with abscissa \\(a\\) is \\((a,1)\\). However, for any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b>1\\) we can use a different line: choose \\(i = a+b-1\\) (which satisfies \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)). Then the line \\(M_i\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = x + 1 - i = x + 1 - (a+b-1)= x - a - b + 2 .\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = a - a - b + 2 = 2-b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_i\\) precisely when \\(b = 2-b\\), i.e. \\(b=1\\), which again is not the case.  \n\nThe previous attempt shows that the simple family \\(y=x+1-i\\) does **not** cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTo obtain a covering we modify the construction. For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nN_s:\\; y = \\gamma x + (s-\\gamma s),\n\\]\n\nwhere we fix a slope \\(\\gamma\\) with \\(\\gamma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (for instance \\(\\gamma = 2\\)). Then the line \\(N_s\\) passes through the point \\((s,0)\\) and also through the point \\((0,s)\\); in particular it contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\). Because the set \\(T_n\\) is the disjoint union of the sets  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b = s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1,\n\\]\n\nthe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(n)}_n=\\{\\,N_s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\). The lines are distinct (different values of \\(s\\) give different intercepts) and each has slope \\(\\gamma\\neq 0,-1\\), so none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\). Consequently every line in the family is sunny, and the family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines (since \\(s\\) runs from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\)).\n\nThus we have exhibited a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved rigorously that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n* there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing **no** sunny line (\\(k=0\\));\n* there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions and in which **all** lines are sunny (\\(k=n\\)).\n\nHence the extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are always attainable. The existence (or non\u2011existence) of configurations for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) remains open."
