0\\), i.e. must be of the form  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_c:\\; x+y=c\\qquad (c\\in\\mathbb R).\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction with only non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Choose the \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_c\\;(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   For any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the above list, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_c\\).  \n   All chosen lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \n   This shows that the covering condition can be satisfied with \\(k=0\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of any sunny line.**  \n   Suppose a configuration with at least one sunny line existed.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line would contribute at most one point from each set \\(S_s\\).  \n   Even if the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines were all of the form \\(x+y=c\\) (which can cover an entire \\(S_s\\)), the total number of points that could be covered would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\cdot (n) + (n-1) = n^2-1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\text{for }n\\ge 3,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Hence no sunny line can appear in a feasible configuration.\n\nFrom 5 and 6 we conclude that the only possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(T_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}S_s\\) and the sets \\(S_s\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery \\(S_s\\) lies on the line  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).  \nThen \\(\\ell\\) meets each line \\(L_s\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) contained two distinct points of \\(L_s\\), those two points would determine the line \\(L_s\\) itself, forcing \\(\\ell\\) to have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 2  \nA sunny line can contain at most one point of each set \\(S_s\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nA vertical line (\\(x=c\\)) or a horizontal line (\\(y=c\\)) also meets each \\(L_s\\) in at most one point; consequently such a line can contain at most one point of each \\(S_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a vertical (resp. horizontal) line contained two points of \\(L_s\\), those points would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate (resp. \\(y\\)-coordinate) but different \\(y\\)-coordinates (resp. \\(x\\)-coordinates), which is impossible because points of \\(L_s\\) satisfy \\(y=s-x\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting argument\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}|S_s|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAssume that among the required \\(n\\) distinct lines at least one is sunny.  \nBy Corollary\u202f2 each sunny line contributes at most one point from each \\(S_s\\); hence a single sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points (one for each \\(s\\)).  \n\nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines, even if they are all of the form \\(x+y=c\\) and therefore cover an entire set \\(S_s\\), can together cover at most \\((n-1)\\cdot (n)\\) points (each of the \\(n-1\\) lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points because \\(|S_{n+1}|=n\\)).  \n\nThus the total number of points that could be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)n + n = n^2 < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (1).  Hence **no sunny line can appear** in any admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)\n\nSince vertical, horizontal and sunny lines are all impossible, every line in a feasible configuration must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. each line has the form  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct such lines correspond to different constants \\(c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c\\;(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the above list, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_c\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  \nThus the required covering is achieved with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe covering condition forces every line to be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently the number of sunny lines must be zero.  The construction above shows that this value is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]"
