n\\), those two points would share either the same horizontal line, the same vertical line, or the same diagonal line (because any two points of \\(T_n\\) differ by a vector of the form \\((\\pm1,0)\\), \\((0,\\pm1)\\) or \\((\\pm1,\\mp1)\\)).  \nIn each case \\(L\\) would be forced to be parallel to the corresponding family, contradicting the definition of sunny.  \nHence a sunny line meets at most one point of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nThe (possibly) non‑sunny line \\(L_n\\) can contain **at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three families.\n\n* If \\(L_n\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\). Hence the points of \\(T_n\\) on this line are \\((1,c),(2,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n* If \\(L_n\\) is vertical, the same bound follows by symmetry.\n\n* If \\(L_n\\) is diagonal, i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\), then the admissible points are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); again there are at most \\(n\\) of them (the maximum occurs for \\(c=n\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Deriving the contradiction  \n\nBy Lemma 1 each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines covers at most one point of \\(T_n\\); together they cover at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \nBy Lemma 2 the remaining line \\(L_n\\) covers at most \\(n\\) points.  \nTherefore the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+n = 2n-1\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\nOn the other hand  \n\n\\[\n|T_n| = 1+2+\\dots + n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} > 2n-1,\n\\]\n\nso the required set \\(T_n\\) contains strictly more points than can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThis contradicts the hypothesis that the \\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition cannot contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Consequently any admissible number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n*The problem of exhibiting, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines remains open.  Providing such constructions would complete the determination of all possible values of \\(k\\).*\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
