ment are \\((1,c),(2,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them because \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \n\nThe vertical case \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.  \n\nFor a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (again with integer \\(c\\)) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le c-1,\\qquad b=c-a .\n\\]\n\nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\).  \nHence the admissible integer pairs are  \n\n\\[\n(1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1),\n\\]\n\nagain at most \\(n\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nThus \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, so the total number of points that can be covered by all non\u2011sunny lines does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, therefore the sunny lines together must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-(n-k)n\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-k)n\n= \\frac{n(2k-n+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line can always be chosen to pass through at least one point of \\(T_n\\); consequently the right\u2011hand side of (3) cannot be negative. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(2k-n+1)}{2}\\ge0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 2k-n+1\\ge0 .\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, the sharper integer bound is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction showing sufficiency  \n\nWe now prove that for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements.\n\n#### 4.1  Choice of the non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad\nv=n-k-h .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(h+v=n-k\\) and \\(h,v\\ge0\\).  \nTake the following non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* the horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots , y=h\\);\n* the vertical lines   \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots , x=v\\).\n\nAll these lines are distinct and clearly non\u2011sunny.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le v\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\);  \nevery point with \\(b\\le h\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \nConsequently all points of \\(T_n\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\na\\le v\\quad\\text{or}\\quad b\\le h\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nare already covered.\n\n#### 4.2  The uncovered remainder  \n\nCondition (5) fails precisely for points with  \n\n\\[\na\\ge v+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\ge h+1 .\n\\]\n\nTogether with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) this describes a (possibly empty) right\u2011isosceles triangle whose side length is  \n\n\\[\ns=n-(h+v)=n-(n-k)=k .\n\\]\n\nBecause of the lower bound (4) we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; s=k\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered set consists of at most the two rows  \n\n\\[\n\\{(v+1,\\,h+1),\\;(v+2,\\,h+1),\\dots ,(v+s,\\,h+1)\\},\n\\qquad\n\\{(v+1,\\,h+2),\\dots ,(v+s,\\,h+2)\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(s\\le2\\). In particular it contains at most two points.\n\n#### 4.3  Covering the remainder with sunny lines  \n\nIf the remainder is empty we are done.  \nOtherwise it contains one or two points. Choose a line of irrational slope passing through each of those points; such a line is automatically sunny because its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \nIf the remainder consists of a single point we use one sunny line; if it consists of two points we use two distinct sunny lines (different slopes guarantee distinctness).\n\nThus we have used exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\;(\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\;+\\;r\\;(\\text{sunny}),\n\\qquad r\\in\\{0,1,2\\},\n\\]\n\nlines, where \\(r\\le k\\).  \nIf \\(r<k\\) we still need to reach the prescribed total of \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nWe simply add \\(k-r\\) further sunny lines that pass through any already\u2011covered lattice point (for instance the line through \\((1,1)\\) with slope \\(\\sqrt{2}+m\\) for distinct integers \\(m\\)).  \nThese extra lines do not affect the covering property and remain distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nIn total we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe pigeon\u2011hole argument yields the necessary condition (4).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) can indeed be realised.  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\Bigl\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor the given integer \\(n\\ge3\\). \u220e"
