e \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)). Hence all points are covered with \\(n\\) sunny lines, so \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Interpolation for intermediate \\(k\\):**  \n   Starting from the construction for \\(k=n\\), replace successively any sunny line by a non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)) that still contains all points previously covered by that line. Because each non‑sunny line can cover an entire row, column, or diagonal of the triangular array, such a replacement never destroys the covering property. Performing exactly \\(n-k\\) replacements yields a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. Since the replacement can be carried out for any \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), every integer in this interval is realizable.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nA line of any slope can intersect \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points:\nindeed, fixing a slope, the intersection of the line with the triangle\n\\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge0,\\;y\\ge0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) is a segment whose projection on the\n\\(x\\)-axis (or on the \\(y\\)-axis) has length at most \\(n\\), and the lattice points\non that segment are therefore at most \\(n\\). Consequently a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines can cover at most \\(n\\cdot n\\) points, while by (1)\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2 .\n\\]\n\nHence there is no combinatorial obstruction to using any number\n\\(k\\le n\\) of sunny lines; the only possible restriction would be a lower\nbound on \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n\\(\\ell_b\\). Thus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) and\ncontain no sunny line. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=i\\,x+(1-i).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0,-1\\) and the line is not\nvertical, every \\(L_i\\) is sunny. Moreover \\(L_i\\) passes through the point\n\\((1,i)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\), so the \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\). Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-a+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le n\\). Substituting\n\\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=i a+(1-i)=a(b-a+1)+(1-b+a-1)=b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\). Consequently the family\n\\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) and consists\nentirely of sunny lines. Hence \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Interpolation between the two extremes\n\nStarting from the configuration of §3 (all \\(n\\) lines sunny), we replace\nsome sunny lines by non‑sunny ones while preserving the covering property.\n\n*Horizontal replacement.*  \nReplace a sunny line \\(L\\) by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), where \\(b\\) is the\n\\(y\\)-coordinate of any point