e point\n\\((a,b)\\) and any other point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that was on \\(L\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) coincides with a line already present in the family, we simply\nchoose a different point of \\(L\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\) (such a point exists because\nevery \\(L_i\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at least one lattice point) and perform\nthe replacement with the corresponding row; distinct rows are distinct lines,\nso (D) is preserved.\n\n*Lemma 4.2 (Column replacement).*  \nAnalogously, replacing \\(L\\) by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (through a point\n\\((a,b)\\in L\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\)) preserves (C) and (D).\n\n*Lemma 4.3 (Diagonal replacement).*  \nReplacing \\(L\\) by the line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c=a+b\\) (through a point\n\\((a,b)\\in L\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\)) also preserves (C) and (D), because the whole\nanti\u2011diagonal of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with constant sum \\(c\\) is contained in that\nline.\n\nThus any sunny line can be swapped for a non\u2011sunny line of one of the three\nallowed slopes without losing coverage or creating a duplicate line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nBegin with \\(\\mathcal L^{(n)}\\) (all \\(n\\) lines sunny).  \nPerform exactly \\(n-k\\) replacements, each time applying Lemma\u202f4.1,\nLemma\u202f4.2 or Lemma\u202f4.3 to a sunny line that has not yet been replaced.\nBecause after each step the family still contains at least one sunny line,\nthe process can continue until precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines remain.\nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\ncovers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by (C), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines by construction.\n\nHence for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a configuration satisfying\nthe two conditions of the problem.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------