n\nFix \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge 2).\n\\]\n\n1. **Diagonal lines.**  \n   For each \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,m+1\\) take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(m\\) lines are distinct, none of them is sunny, and they cover exactly the sets \\(S_2,\\dots ,S_{m+1}\\).\n\n2. **Points that remain uncovered.**  \n   The uncovered sets are \\(S_{m+2},S_{m+3},\\dots ,S_{n+1}\\).  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) consider the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i:=\\bigl(i,\\;n+2-i\\bigr)\\in S_{n+2-i}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(n+2-i\\ge m+2\\) (the inequality follows from \\(k\\le n-2\\)), each \\(P_i\\) indeed belongs to one of the uncovered sets.\n\n3. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_i:\\; y = -\\frac12\\,x + \\Bigl(n+2-\\frac{i}{2}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=n+2-i\\), so \\(\\sigma_i\\) passes through the point \\(P_i\\).  \n   Distinct values of \\(i\\) give distinct intercepts, hence the \\(\\sigma_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n   Each \\(\\sigma_i\\) meets every diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) (including those not covered by a diagonal line) in exactly one point. Consequently the family \\(\\{\\sigma_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) supplies one point for each uncovered set \\(S_s\\) with \\(s\\ge m+2\\). Because there are exactly \\(k\\) such lines, all points of the uncovered sets are covered.\n\nThus we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every required point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nInequality (7) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can b