\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Existence of configurations for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) actually occurs.\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 4.1 (Construction).}\n\\]\nFor each \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=k\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*  \n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe possibilities are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n*   \\(k=0\\): take the three lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=2\\). All three are non\u2011sunny and they cover the three points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n*   \\(k=1\\): keep the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) and add a sunny line, for instance the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, so we choose the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\), slope \\(0\\) is also forbidden; instead take the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,3)\\), slope \\(1\\)). This line is sunny and together the three lines cover \\(T_3\\).\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the lemma holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nFix a \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (note that for the next size we need \\(k\\le (n+1)-2=n-1\\)).  \n\n*If \\(k\\le n-2\\)*, we already have by the induction hypothesis a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nAdd to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\).  \nThe new line is non\u2011sunny, it contains the new extreme point \\((n+1,1)\\) and does not coincide with any existing line because all previous lines intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\) at distinct points.  \nConsequently the enlarged family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{x=n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and now covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) (the added line 