required \\(n\\) distinct lines there are \\(k\\) sunny lines. By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(S\\); by Lemma\u202f2 each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le k\\cdot 1+(n-k)\\cdot n = kn+n-k. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le kn+n-k.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n\\le k(n-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le k(n-1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(n-1>0\\) and can divide by it:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{2}\\le k.\n\\]\n\nBut (2) is an *upper* bound, not a lower bound; the correct manipulation is to observe that the right\u2011hand side of (2) is maximised when \\(k\\) is as large as possible. Plugging \\(k=3\\) gives\n\n\\[\nkn+n-k = 3n+n-3 = 4n-3.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>4n-3,\n\\]\n\nbecause\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(4n-3)=\\frac{n^{2}+n-8n+6}{2}\n=\\frac{n^{2}-7n+6}{2}\n=\\frac{(n-1)(n-6)}{2}\\ge0,\n\\]\n\nwith strict inequality for \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence (2) cannot hold when \\(k\\ge3\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le 2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1 Construction for \\(k=0\\) (no sunny lines)\n\nUse only the three allowed directions.\n\n*Horizontal lines*: for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) take the line \\(y=c\\).  \n*Vertical lines*: for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) take the line \\(x=c\\).  \n*Diagonal lines*: for each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n\\) take the line \\(x+y=d\\).\n\nThere are \\((n-1)+(n-1)+(n-1)=3n-3\\) lines, which is more than \\(n\\). We need exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so we select a suitable subset.\n\nObserve that every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies at least one of the three equations:\n\n* If \\(a=b\\) then \\(x+y=2a\\) (diagonal).  \n* If \\(a<b\\) then \\(y=b\\) (horizontal).  \n* If \\(a>b\\) then \\(x=a\\) (vertical).\n\nThus the **set**\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1,\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\,x+y=n\\,\\}\n\\]\ncovers all points of \\(S\\). \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains exactly \\(2(n-1)+1=2n-1\\) lines. To reduce the number to \\(n\\) we simply discard any \\(n-1\\) lines that are redundant. For instance, keep the \\(n\\) lines\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y_{\\,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor},\\;\nx=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x_{\\,\\lceil n/2\\rceil}\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n(the precise split of the count is irrelevant; any \\(n\\) distinct lines among the above family work). None of these lines is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on at least one of them, because either its \\(y\\)-coordinate or its \\(x\\)-coordinate is among the kept values. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2 Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nStart from the \\(k=0\\) configuration and modify it as follows.\n\nChoose a point \\((a_0,b_0)\\in S\\) that is **not** on any of the \\(n\\) lines kept in the \\(k=0\\) construction (such a point exists because the \\(k=0\\) construction uses only \\(n\\) of the \\(2n-1\\) possible non\u2011sunny lines). Replace one of the existing non\u2011sunny lines by the sunny line passing through \\((a_0,b_0)\\) and having slope, say, \\(2\\) (any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) works). The new line is sunny, covers the chosen point, and does not coincide with any of the remaining lines. All other points of \\(S\\) are still covered because we removed only a line that was not essential for covering any point other than possibly \\((a_0,b_0)\\). Thus we obtain a family of \