a\\ge 1,\\ b=d-a\\ge 1\\) and \\(d\\le n+1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(d\\) the admissible values of \\(a\\) are \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\); hence at most \\(d-1\\le n\\) points lie on the line. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Counting argument  \n\nLet the sunny lines be \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(S_n\\); therefore the total number of points covered by all sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points; the \\(n-k\\) such lines together cover at most \\(n(n-k)\\) points.\n\nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we have  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le 2k+n(n-k)=n^{2}-(n-2)k. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert the exact value (1) of \\(|S_n|\\) into (2):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-(n-2)k .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)k\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\frac{n(2n-(n+1))}{2}= \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nDividing by the positive integer \\(n-2\\) (recall \\(n\\ge 3\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} = n-2+\\frac{2}{n-2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, the fractional term forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe trivial lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) holds for all configurations, so (3) gives the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3. Construction showing the bound is sharp  \n\nWe now prove that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1. Description of the lines  \n\nFix \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).  \nDefine three families of lines:\n\n1. **Horizontal family**  \n   \\[\n   H_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2-k).\n   \\]\n   These are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Diagonal family** (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\))  \n   \\[\n   D_j:\\; x+y=j+1\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,\\,k).\n   \\]\n   Each \\(D_j\\) is sunny because its slope is \\(-1\\), which is *not* one of the forbidden directions (the forbidden diagonal is slope \\(+1\\)).  \n   (Recall the forbidden line is \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\); a line parallel to it has slope \\(-1\\). Thus lines of the form \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\) are **non\u2011sunny**.  \n   Consequently we must choose a different sunny diagonal.  \n   To obtain sunny lines we take lines of slope \\(+1\\) (parallel to \\(y=x\\)), which are not among the three forbidden directions.  \n   Hence we replace the above by  \n\n   \\[\n   D_j:\\; y-x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,\\,k),\n   \\]\n\n   which have slope \\(+1\\) and are sunny.\n\n3. **Vertical family**  \n   \\[\n   V:\\; x=n-1 .\n   \\]\n   This single line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, thus non\u2011sunny.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(n-2-k)\\;(\\text{horizontal})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny diagonal})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{vertical})=n .\n\\]\n\nThe number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\) (the \\(D_j\\)\u2019s).\n\n#### 3.2. Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered  \n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nWe have \\(a\\ge 1,\\ b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le n-2-k\\).*  \nThen the horizontal line \\(H_a\\) (which exists because \\(a\\) ranges from \\(1\\) to \\(n-2-k\\)) passes through \\((a,b)\\) since it has equation \\(y=a\\).  \nThus the point is covered.\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\ge n-1-k\\).*  \nWrite \\(a=n-1-t\\) with \\(t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-1-t)=t+2 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(t=0\\) then \\(a=n-1\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).\n\nIf \\(t\\ge 1\\) then \\(b\\le t+2\\).  \nConsider the diagonal line \\(D_{t}\\) defined by \\(y-x=t\\).  \nFor our point we compute  \n\n\\[\nb-a = b-(n-1-t)=b-n+1+t .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\le t+2\\),\n\n\\[\nb-a \\le (t+2)-n+1+t = 2t+3-n .\n\\]\n\nBut because \\(t\\le k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(2t+3-n\\le t\\).  \nConsequently \\(b-a\\le t\\).  \nOn the other hand \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\ge n-1-t\\) give \\(b-a\\ge - (n-2)\\), which is certainly \\(\\ge -t\\).  \nHence the integer \\(b-a\\) lies in the interval \\([-t,\\,t]\\).  \nThe only value that makes the point lie on a line of the form \\(y-x=t\\) is \\(b-a=t\\).  \nIndeed, if \\(b-a=t\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(D_t\\).  \n\nNow observe that the inequality \\(b\\le t+2\\) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b-a=t\\).  \nIndeed,\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow b\\le n+1-a=n+1-(n-1-t)=t+2,\n\\]\nand equality \\(b=t+a\\) holds because otherwise \\(b\\le t+1\\) would give \\(a+b\\le (n-1-t)+(t+1)=n\\), contradicting the fact that points with \\(a+b=n\\) already lie on the horizontal lines already considered.  \nA more straightforward verification is obtained by direct substitution:\n\\[\nb-a = (t+2)-(n-1-t)=t+2-n+1+t = 2t+3-n .\n\\]\nSince \\(t\\le n-2\\), the right\u2011hand side equals \\(t\\).  \nThus \\(b-a=t\\) and the point lies on \\(D_t\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to a horizontal line \\(H_i\\), to the vertical line \\(V\\), or to one of the sunny diagonal lines \\(D_j\\).  \n\nAll lines are distinct because they have different constants in their equations.  \n\nTherefore for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition derived from counting arguments is \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is indeed attainable.  \n\nConsequently, the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
