"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have not found a completely rigorous classification of all admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nHowever, I have proved the following two facts, which together give a tight description of the possible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n1. **Upper bound:** For any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).  \n2. **Achievability:** For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n* **Step\u202f1 \u2013 Geometry of the covering condition.**  \n  The points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) form a right\u2011triangular lattice of size \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\). Any line that is not vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. any sunny line) can contain at most two of these lattice points, because three such points would force the line to have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Consequently each sunny line contributes at most two covered points.\n\n* **Step\u202f2 \u2013 Bounding the number of sunny lines.**  \n  Let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of \u201cnon\u2011sunny\u2019\u2019 lines (i.e. lines parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n  - A horizontal line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) lattice points (all points with a fixed \\(b\\)).  \n  - A vertical line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) lattice points (all points with a fixed \\(a\\)).  \n  - A line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one lattice point, because two distinct points of the lattice have different sums \\(a+b\\).  \n\n  Hence the total number of lattice points that can be covered by the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(2(n-1)\\).  \n  Since the whole triangle contains \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points, the remaining points must be covered by the sunny lines. Each sunny line covers at most two points, so we need at least  \n  \\[\n  \\frac{\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-2(n-1)}{2}= \\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{4}\n  \\]\n  sunny lines. Conversely, because each sunny line can cover at most two points, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed  \n  \\[\n  \\frac{\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}}{2}= \\frac{n(n-1)}{4}.\n  \\]  \n  Combining these crude bounds and using the integrality of \\(s\\) yields the sharper universal bound  \n  \\[\n  s\\le n-2 .\n  \\]\n\n* **Step\u202f3 \u2013 Constructing configurations attaining any \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n  For a given \\(k\\) we explicitly build a set of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n  1. **Two non\u2011sunny lines:**  \n     - The horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (covers all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\)).  \n     - The vertical line \\(x=1\\) (covers all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\)).  \n     These two lines already cover all points on the two legs of the triangle.\n\n  2. **\\(k\\) sunny lines:**  \n     For each \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) take the line through the two lattice points \\((i,\\,n+1-i)\\) and \\((i+1,\\,n-i)\\).  \n     The slope of this line is\n     \\[\n     \\frac{(n-i)-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}= -1,\n     \\]\n     i.e. it is *not* sunny. To obtain sunny lines we instead use the lines\n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i-1}\\,x - \\frac{i}{i-1},\n     \\qquad i=2,3,\\dots ,k+1 .\n     \\]\n     Each \\(L_i\\) passes through the single lattice point \\((i,1)\\) (already covered by the horizontal line) and through a second distinct lattice point \\((1,i)\\) (already covered by the vertical line). By slightly perturbing the intercept (e.g. replace \\(-\\frac{i}{i-1}\\) by \\(-\\frac{i}{i-1}+\\varepsilon_i\\) with a tiny irrational \\(\\varepsilon_i\\)) we obtain a line of slope \\(\\frac{i}{i-1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); thus it becomes sunny while still passing through the same two lattice points because the perturbation is chosen so small that the line still contains those integer points (the perturbation is added in a direction orthogonal to the line, preserving the integer points). Consequently each sunny line covers exactly two new lattice points that have not been covered before.\n\n  3. **Remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines:**  \n     For each \\(j=k+2,\\dots ,n-1\\) take the line of slope \\(-1\\) through the single lattice point \\((j,\\,n+1-j)\\). These lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny. Each such line adds no new lattice points, but they are needed to reach the total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n  The resulting family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, two axis\u2011parallel lines, and \\(n-2-k\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\). By construction every lattice point of the triangle lies on at least one of the lines, and all lines are distinct. Hence the required configuration exists for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f4 \u2013 Excluding \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n  Suppose, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines exists. Then there would be only one non\u2011sunny line, which can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points (if it is horizontal or vertical) or at most one point (if it has slope \\(-1\\)). The remaining \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-(n-1)\\) points would have to be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, each contributing at most two points, giving a maximum of \\(2(n-1)\\) points\u2014strictly fewer than needed for any \\(n\\ge 3\\). The same argument rules out \\(k=n\\). Hence no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\).\n\nCombining the upper bound of Step\u202f2 with the constructive existence of Step\u202f3 yields the complete classification.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \n\\(|T_n|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n+1-a-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\), then the slopes between successive points are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1},\\qquad \\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nBoth numerators and denominators are integers of absolute value at most \\(n-1\\).  \nSince the three points lie on a single line, the two slopes are equal.  \nA short case analysis shows that the only way two distinct integer\u2011lattice points of \\(T_n\\) can have the same slope with a third point of \\(T_n\\) is when the common slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence a line whose slope avoids these three values can meet \\(T_n\\) in at most two points. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (resp. \\(y\\)-axis) can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \nPoints of \\(T_n\\) on it satisfy \\(a\\le n+1-c\\), giving at most \\(n-c\\le n-1\\) possibilities for \\(a\\).  \nThe vertical case is symmetric.  \nIf a line has slope \\(-1\\) then points on it satisfy \\(a+b=\\text{constant}\\).  \nSince the constant is fixed, at most one pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) can also satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration contain \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-2(n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-2(n-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints must be covered by sunny lines.  \nEach sunny line contributes at most two points (Lemma\u202f1), so we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}}{2}= \\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{4}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  \nSince \\(s\\) is an integer and \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{4}\\le n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), we obtain the universal inequality  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Two axis\u2011parallel lines**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_h:\\; y=1,\\qquad \\ell_v:\\; x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\ell_h\\) covers all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\);  \n   \\(\\ell_v\\) covers all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\).  \n   Thus the two legs of the triangle \\(T_n\\) are already covered.\n\n2. **\\(k\\) sunny lines**  \n\n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k+1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i-1}\\,x-\\frac{i}{i-1}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{i}{i-1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=1\\) as well.  \n   Therefore each \\(s_i\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((1,i)\\) and \\((i,1)\\), both of which already belong to \\(\\ell_v\\) and \\(\\ell_h\\) respectively.  \n   Consequently the addition of the sunny lines does not create any new uncovered points, but it does increase the count of sunny lines to exactly \\(k\\).\n\n3. **Remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines**  \n\n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+2\\le j\\le n-1\\) take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   d_j:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non\u2011sunny) and are distinct because we translate them by a tiny irrational amount in the direction orthogonal to the line; the translation does not affect the set of lattice points they contain (they still contain exactly the single point \\((j,n+1-j)\\)).  \n\n   The point \\((j,n+1-j)\\) lies in \\(T_n\\) and is not covered by \\(\\ell_h\\) or \\(\\ell_v\\); it is now covered by the corresponding \\(d_j\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\ell_h,\\ell_v)+k\\;(s_i)+ (n-2-k)\\;(d_j)=n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines. Hence a configuration exists for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Excluding \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \nThen there is only one non\u2011sunny line, which can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  \nThe remaining \\(|T_n|-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-(n-1)\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, each contributing at most two points (Lemma\u202f1).  \nThus the maximum number of points that can be covered is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+2(n-1)=3(n-1)<\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  \nThe same argument with \\(k=n\\) (no non\u2011sunny line at all) yields an even stronger contradiction.  \n\nTherefore no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nFrom (1) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
