  \\]\n\n   In order to cover all interior points we need  \n\n   \\[\n   n-2+k\\;\\ge\\;|I_n|\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-5)(n-2)}2 .\n   \\tag{D}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer, the necessary lower bound is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge k_{\\min}:=\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{(n-5)(n-2)}2\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{E}\n   \\]\n\n   Together with (A) we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n   \\[\n   k_{\\min}\\le k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{F}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Sufficiency** – for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (F) we construct a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *The two axis‑parallel lines*  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad \\ell_v:\\;x=1\n   \\]\n   are always present.\n\n   *The \\(k\\) sunny lines* – enumerate the interior points of \\(I_n\\) in any order\n   \\[\n   P_1,P_2,\\dots ,P_{|I_n|}.\n   \\]\n   Pair the first \\(2k\\) points as \\((P_{2i-1},P_{2i})\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).\n   For each pair define the line through the two points; its slope is a rational number\n   different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (otherwise the two points would lie on a horizontal,\n   vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line, which is impossible because all points of a pair have\n   distinct \\(a\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(b\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(a+b\\)).\n   Hence each of these \\(k\\) lines is sunny and covers exactly the two paired points.\n\n   *The remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny lines* – the still‑uncovered interior points are\n   precisely the set\n   \\[\n   \\{P_{2k+1},P_{2k+2},\\dots ,P_{|I_n|}\\},\n   \\]\n   whose cardinality equals\n   \\[\n   |I_n|-2k = \\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2-2k = n-2-k\n   \\]\n   by virtue of the equality in (D).  \n   For each such point \\(Q=(a,b)\\) we take the line\n   \\[\n   d_Q:\\;x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n   i.e. the line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) that passes through \\(Q\\).  \n   Because all points of \\(I_n\\) have distinct sums \\(a+b\\), each line \\(d_Q\\) contains\n   exactly