e n+1\\) are \\(m=0\\) (horizontal), \\(m=\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(m=-1\\)\n(diagonal).  Hence a line with any other slope can contain at most two points of\n\\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \n* (i)  A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}\\)) contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n* (ii) A vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n* (iii) A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor (i) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(b=c\\) gives \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus\n\\(a\\) can take at most \\(n-c\\le n-1\\) integer values.  (ii) is symmetric.  \nFor (iii) points on a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) satisfy \\(a+b=s\\) for a fixed integer\n\\(s\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(s\\le n+1\\); for a given \\(s\\) there is\nat most one pair \\((a,b)\\) with both coordinates \\(\\ge1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A necessary upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nEvery configuration must contain a horizontal line (to cover the points\n\\((a,1),\\;a=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) and a vertical line (to cover \\((1,b),\\;b=2,\\dots ,n-1\\)).\nBoth are non‑sunny.  Hence at least two lines are non‑sunny, so the number \\(k\\) of\nsunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Counting the interior points  \n\nThe two axis‑parallel lines cover exactly  \n\n\\[\n( n-1 )\\;(\\text{points on }y=1) + ( n-2 )\\;(\\text{new points on }x=1)=2n-3\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  The set of points that remain uncovered is  \n\n\\[\nI_n:=T_n\\setminus\\{(a,1),(1,b)\\},\n\\qquad |I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A necessary lower bound for \\(k\\)\n\nBesides the two axis‑parallel lines we may use  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines – each can meet \\(I_n\\) in at most two points (Lemma 1);  \n* \\(n-2-k\\) further non‑sunny lines, which we shall choose parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n  By Lemma 2 each of them meets \\(I_n\\) in at most one point.\n\nHence the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2k+(n-2-k)=n-2+k . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTo cover all interior points we need \\(n-2+k\\ge|I_n|\\).  Using (3) this gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{(n-5)(n-2)}2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral, the necessary lower bound is  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge k_{\\min}:=\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{(n-5)(n-2)}2\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2) and (6) we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}\\le k\\le n-2 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n*Two axis‑parallel lines*  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad \\ell_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThey cover all points of the two legs of the triangle.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nList the interior points \\(I_n\\) arbitrarily as\n\\(P_1,P_2,\\dots ,P_{|I_n|}\\).\nTake the first \\(2k\\) points and pair them:\n\\((P_{2i-1},P_{2i})\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).\nFor each pair define the line \\(S_i\\) through the two points.\nBecause the two points of a pair have different \\(a\\)-coordinates,\ndifferent \\(b\\)-coordinates and different sums \\(a+b\\), the slope of \\(S_i\\) is\nneither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) cover exactly the \\(2k\\) paired interior points.\n\n*Remaining non‑sunny lines.*  \nThe still uncovered interior points are\n\\[\nQ_j:=P_{2k+j},\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,|I_n|-2k .\n\\]\nBy (5) we have \\(|I_n|-2k=n-2-k\\).  \nFor each \\(Q_j=(a_j,b_j)\\) we take the line  \n\n\\[\nD_j:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(I_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) with equality **only** for the points\n\\(Q_j\\); consequently each line \\(D_j\\) contains exactly the single point \\(Q_j\\).\nAll the \\(D_j\\) are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), hence non‑sunny, and they are distinct\nbecause they pass through distinct points.\n\n*Counting.*  \nWe have  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\ell_h,\\ell_v)+k\\;(S_i)+(n-2-k)\\;(D_j)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on\nat least one of them.  Thus a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) satisfying (7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer  \n\nThe necessary condition (7) is also sufficient.  Therefore the set of all\nnon‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\Bigl\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\;\\big|\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{(n-5)(n-2)}2\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2\\Bigr\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n(in particular, for \\(n=3,4,5\\) the lower bound equals \\(0\\), so all\n\\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) are possible). ∎"
