2\\) and \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) (the points have different sums), the slope \\((b_2-b_1)/(a_2-a_1)\\) is finite and non\u2011zero. If it were \\(-1\\) we would have \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\), i.e. \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\), contradicting the hypothesis that the points lie on different diagonals. \u220e\n\n5. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Base non\u2011sunny lines* \u2013 always use  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_h:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_v:\\;x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Choosing points for sunny lines.*  \n   Because there are \\(n-2\\) diagonals (1), we can select \\(2k\\) interior points, each taken from a distinct diagonal; denote them  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1,P_2,\\dots ,P_{2k},\\qquad P_i\\in d_{s_i},\\; s_i\\text{ all different}.\n   \\]\n\n   Pair them as \\((P_1,P_{k+1}), (P_2,P_{k+2}),\\dots ,(P_k,P_{2k})\\) and for each pair define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:=\\text{line through }P_i\\text{ and }P_{k+i}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1 each \\(S_i\\) is sunny, contains exactly the two chosen points, and is distinct from every other \\(S_j\\) because the pairs are disjoint.\n\n   *Remaining interior points.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=I_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_{2k}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(U\\) still lies on its diagonal \\(d_s\\) from (1).  \n   For each diagonal that contains at least one point of \\(U\\) we keep the whole line \\(d_s\\).  \n   Since the \\(2k\\) removed points occupy \\(2k\\) distinct diagonals, at most \\(k\\) diagonals become empty; consequently at least \\((n-2)-k\\) diagonals still contain points of \\(U\\).  \n   We therefore keep exactly \\((n-2)-k\\) diagonal lines, all of which are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *Counting the lines.*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{2}_{\\ell_h,\\ell_v}\n   +\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny }S_i}\n   +\\underbrace{(n-2)-k}_{\\text{remaining diagonals}}\n   = n .\n   \\]\n\n   All listed lines are distinct: the two leg lines are horizontal/vertical; each sunny line contains points from two different diagonals, so it cannot coincide with any diagonal line; and the sunny lines are pairwise distinct because their defining point\u2011pairs are disjoint.\n\n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered:\n   * points on the legs are on \\(\\ell_h\\) or \\(\\ell_v\\);\n   * each interior point either is one of the selected \\(P_i\\) (covered by a sunny line) or belongs to a diagonal kept in the last step.\n\n   Thus we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (A) \\(k\\le n-2\\) is also sufficient, and there is no lower restriction (the case \\(k=0\\) is obtained by taking the two leg lines and all \\(n-2\\) diagonals). Hence for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\; } .\n   \\] \u220e"
