            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and repaired them.  \nThe complete, rigorous answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\;}\n\\qquad\\text{for every integer }n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nIn words, the number of sunny lines that can appear among the \\(n\\) required lines is any non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Required point set**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two non‑sunny “leg’’ lines are forced** – the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (covers all points with \\(b=1\\)) and the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (covers all points with \\(a=1\\)). Hence any admissible family contains at least these two lines, so  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{A}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Interior points and diagonal lines**  \n   Remove the leg points  \n\n   \\[\n   L_h=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\},\\qquad   \n   L_v=\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and set  \n\n   \\[\n   I_n:=T_n\\setminus(L_h\\cup L_v)\n        =\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   d_s:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   The collection \\(\\{d_s\\mid 4\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (n+1)-4+1 = n-2\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, and every point of \\(I_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.  \n   Moreover each diagonal \\(d_s\\) contains the interior point \\((2,s-2)\\) (both coordinates are \\(\\ge2\\) because \\(s\\ge4\\)).\n\n4. **Lemma 1 (sunny line from two different diagonals).**  \n   Let \\(P\\in d_{s_1}\\) and \\(Q\\in d_{s_2}\\) with \\(s_1\\neq s_2\\).  \n   Then the line \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is sunny; i.e. its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Because the two points have different sums, \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) and \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\); hence the slope \\(\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) is finite and non‑zero. If the slope were \\(-1\\) we would have \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\), which rewrites as \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\), contradicting \\(s_1\\neq s_2\\). ∎\n\n5. **Necessary upper bound for sunny lines.**  \n   Each sunny line must contain at least two interior points (otherwise it would be one of the leg lines, which are not sunny). By Lemma 1 the two interior points on a sunny line must belong to *different* diagonals. Consequently each sunny line consumes two distinct diagonals. Since there are only \\(n-2\\) diagonals, we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\le n-2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{B}\n   \\]\n\n   Together with (A) this yields the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *Leg lines* – always include  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_h:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_v:\\;x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Choosing interior points.*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,2k\\) pick the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i:=(2,\\,4+i-1)=\\bigl(2,\\,3+i\\bigr)\n   \\]\n\n   which lies on diagonal \\(d_{s_i}\\) with \\(s_i=4+i-1\\).  \n   The indices \\(s_i\\) are distinct because they run through the consecutive integers  \n\n   \\[\n   4,5,\\dots ,4+2k-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(2k\\le n-2\\), all these diagonals belong to the set \\(\\{d_s\\mid 4\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   Pair the chosen points as \\((P_1,P_{k+1}), (P_2,P_{k+2}),\\dots ,(P_k,P_{2k})\\).  \n   For each pair define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:=\\text{line through }P_j\\text{ and }P_{k+j}\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1 each \\(S_j\\) is sunny, contains exactly the two selected points, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because the point‑pairs are disjoint.\n\n   *Remaining diagonals.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   D:=\\{d_s\\mid 4\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\setminus\\{d_{s_1},\\dots ,d_{s_{2k}}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(D\\) contains \\((n-2)-2k\\) diagonals. Keep **all** of them; each of these lines is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\n\n   *Counting the lines.*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{2}_{\\ell_h,\\ell_v}\n   +\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny }S_j}\n   +\\underbrace{(n-2)-2k}_{\\text{remaining diagonals}}\n   = n .\n   \\]\n\n   All listed lines are distinct:\n   * \\(\\ell_h\\) and \\(\\ell_v\\) are horizontal and vertical;\n   * a sunny line \\(S_j\\) contains points from two different diagonals, so it cannot coincide with any diagonal line;\n   * different sunny lines are distinct because their defining point‑pairs are disjoint;\n   * the remaining diagonals are distinct by construction.\n\n   *Coverage of all required points.*  \n   – Points on the legs belong to \\(\\ell_h\\) or \\(\\ell_v\\).  \n   – Each interior point either is one of the selected \\(P_i\\) (hence lies on a sunny line) or lies on a diagonal that we kept in the last step.  \n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n   Hence for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary bound (B) is also sufficient by the explicit construction above. Therefore the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\;}\n   \\qquad (n\\ge 3). \\qquad\\blacksquare\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
