onstants satisfy the inequality defining \\(T_n\\). Hence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by the \\(n\\) lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nhn+vn+dn-(hv+hd+vd) = n^{2}-(hv+hd+vd). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf all lines were of the same family then \\(hv+hd+vd=0\\) and the right\u2013hand side of (2) equals \\(n^{2}\\). However, in that case the \\(n\\) parallel lines are pairwise disjoint on \\(T_n\\); each contributes at most \\(n\\) points, so at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, far fewer than \\(|T_n|\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\). Therefore at least two families must appear, which forces \\(hv+hd+vd\\ge 1\\). Hence the number of covered points is at most \\(n^{2}-1\\).\n\nFrom (1),\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}-1\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\na contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain **at least one sunny line**.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Base constructions for \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) has three points. The following three configurations realise all possible values of \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le3\\).\n\n| Desired \\(k\\) | Lines (written as equations) | Sunny lines |\n|---|---|---|\n| \\(k=1\\) | \\(y=1,\\; x=1,\\; x+y=3\\) | only \\(x+y=3\\) |\n| \\(k=2\\) | \\(y=1,\\; x+y=2,\\; x+y=3\\) | the two diagonal lines |\n| \\(k=3\\) | \\(y=1,\\; x=1,\\; x+y=2\\) | all three are sunny (none is parallel to the forbidden directions) |\n\nEach list consists of three distinct lines and each point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of them. Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 extending a configuration from \\(n\\) to \\(n+1\\)\n\n*Statement.*  \nAssume we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(1\\le k\\le n\\). Then for the next integer \\(n+1\\) there exist configurations covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with either \\(k\\) or \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(T_{n+1}=T_n\\cup S\\), where  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}\n\\]\n\nis the new \u201couter\u201d diagonal of lattice points that appears when the bound is increased by one.\n\nTake the given configuration \\(\\mathcal C_n\\). All points of \\(T_n\\) are already covered. We only have to ensure that the points of \\(S\\) are also covered, while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n+1\\).\n\nTwo possibilities are considered.\n\n#### (a) Keeping the number of sunny lines unchanged (\\(k\\) sunny)\n\nAdd a **new non\u2011sunny line** that passes through *all* points of \\(S\\).  \nObserve that the set \\(S\\) lies on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{d}: \\; x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{d}\\) is parallel to the forbidden diagonal direction, hence it is non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\(\\ell_{d}\\) is not already present among the \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) (which is always the case, because \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) only needs to cover points with sum at most \\(n+1\\)), we simply add \\(\\ell_{d}\\) to obtain a configuration of \\(n+1\\) lines. The number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\).\n\n#### (b) Increasing the number of sunny lines by one (\\(k+1\\) sunny)\n\nAdd a **new sunny line** that also covers the whole set \\(S\\).  \nChoose a line with slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) that passes through the two extreme points of \\(S\\), namely \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\). The equation of this line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{s}:\\; y = -(n)\\,x + (n+2).\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(-n\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{s}\\) is sunny.  \nAgain \\(\\ell_{s}\\) is not among the original \\(n\\) lines (the original lines never contain a point with sum \\(n+2\\)), so adding it yields a configuration of \\(n+1\\) lines with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nIn both cases the lines remain distinct, and every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) lies on at least one line. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Induction proof of the main statement  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\ge3\\) that the set of feasible numbers of sunny lines is \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n*Base case.*  \nFor \\(n=3\\) the explicit constructions given in Section\u202f3 realise \\(k=1,2,3\\); Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is impossible. Hence the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the claim holds for some \\(n\\ge3\\); i.e. for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet \\(k'\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le k'\\le n+1\\).  \n\n* If \\(k'\\le n\\), apply the induction hypothesis to obtain a configuration for \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines, and then use Lemma\u202f2\u202f(a) to extend it to a configuration for \\(T_{n+1}\\) that still has \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n* If \\(k'=n+1\\), start from the configuration for \\(T_n\\) that has \\(k=n\\) sunny lines (which exists by the induction hypothesis) and apply Lemma\u202f2\u202f(b) to add one more sunny line, obtaining a configuration for \\(T_{n+1}\\) with \\(n+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every \\(k'\\) with \\(1\\le k'\\le n+1\\) a suitable configuration exists for \\(n+1\\). By induction the statement is true for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nCombined with Lemma\u202f1, which excludes \\(k=0\\), we have completely determined the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
