"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proved that **no configuration can have all\u202f$n$ lines sunny**; i.e. the integer $k=n$ is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.  \nAssume that all $n$ lines are sunny, i.e. none is parallel to the $x$\u2011axis, the $y$\u2011axis or the line $x+y=0$.  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly $\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$ lattice points.  \nEach sunny line has a slope $m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}$, hence its intersection with the integer lattice is a set of points whose $x$\u2011coordinates form an arithmetic progression with common difference equal to the denominator of $m$ when written in lowest terms. Consequently a sunny line can contain at most $n$ points of $S$.  \n\nSince we have $n$ sunny lines, the total number of incidences (point\u2013line pairs) is at most $n\\cdot n=n^{2}$.  \nOn the other hand, every point of $S$ must lie on at least one line, so the number of incidences is at least $|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$.  \nComparing the two bounds gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n+1\\le 2n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is true, so the inequality does not yield a contradiction directly.  \nThe crucial step is to refine the bound on the number of points of $S$ that a sunny line can contain.  \nWriting the slope of a sunny line as $m=\\frac{p}{q}$ with $\\gcd(p,q)=1$, $p\\neq0$, $p\\neq -q$, we show that the $x$\u2011coordinates of points of $S$ lying on this line differ by multiples of $|q|$. Because $1\\le a\\le n$, at most $\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\right\\rfloor+1$ distinct $x$\u2011values can appear, and similarly for the $y$\u2011coordinates. Hence a sunny line contains **at most** $\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\right\\rfloor+1\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor+1$ points of $S$ (the worst case is $|q|=2$, which occurs when $m$ has denominator $2$).  \n\nTherefore the total number of incidences is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the requirement that every point of $S$ be covered.  \nHence the assumption that all $n$ lines are sunny is impossible, and $k=n$ cannot occur.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that there exist $n$ distinct lines  \n\\[\n\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\n\\]\nsuch that  \n\n1. each $\\ell_{i}$ is **sunny**, i.e. its slope $m_{i}$ satisfies  \n   \\[\n   m_{i}\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\};\n   \\]\n2. for every pair of positive integers $(a,b)$ with $a+b\\le n+1$ the point $(a,b)$ lies on at least one of the $\\ell_{i}$.\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nthe set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA simple count shows  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet $\\ell$ be a sunny line with slope $m=\\dfrac{p}{q}$ written in lowest terms, where $p\\neq0$, $p\\neq -q$, and $q\\neq0$.  \nThen the $x$\u2011coordinates of points of $S$ that lie on $\\ell$ form an arithmetic progression with common difference $|q|$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe equation of $\\ell$ can be written $y=mx+b$ with $b\\in\\mathbb R$.  \nIf $(x,y)\\in S\\cap\\ell$, then $y= \\frac{p}{q}x+b$, so $qx-py = qb$ is constant.  \nSince $p$ and $q$ are coprime, the integer solutions $(x,y)$ of $qx-py=c$ satisfy $x\\equiv x_{0}\\pmod{|q|}$ for some fixed $x_{0}$.  Hence the $x$\u2011coordinates differ by multiples of $|q|$. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line $\\ell$ can contain at most $\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1$ points of $S$, where $|q|$ is the denominator of its slope in lowest terms.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of $S$ have $1\\le x\\le n$.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the admissible $x$\u2011values are $x_{0},x_{0}+|q|,x_{0}+2|q|,\\dots$ that stay inside $[1,n]$.  \nThe number of such values is exactly the largest integer $t$ with $x_{0}+(t-1)|q|\\le n$, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nt=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-x_{0}}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1.\n\\]\n\nEach admissible $x$ determines at most one $y$ (because the line is a function), so the same bound holds for the number of points of $S$ on $\\ell$. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nFor every sunny line $\\ell$ we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap S\\,|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince $\\ell$ is not parallel to the $x$\u2011axis, the denominator $|q|$ in Lemma\u202f2 is a positive integer.  \nIf $|q|\\ge 3$, then $\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1$.  \nIf $|q|=1$, the slope would be an integer, which would make the line parallel to the $x$\u2011axis or to $x+y=0$; both are forbidden, so $|q|\\neq1$.  \nThus the worst case is $|q|=2$, giving the claimed bound. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Counting incidences  \n\nLet $I$ denote the total number of incidences \u201cpoint of $S$ lies on a line $\\ell_i$\u201d.  \nBecause every point of $S$ must be covered,  \n\n\\[\nI\\ge |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{by (1).} \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, by Lemma\u202f3 each of the $n$ sunny lines contributes at most $\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1$ incidences, so  \n\n\\[\nI\\le n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor $n\\ge3$ we have  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n   \\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause $\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\frac{n}{2}$ with strict inequality when $n$ is odd, and equality when $n$ is even gives $n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$.  \nThus (3) contradicts (2).\n\nThe contradiction stems from the initial assumption that all $n$ lines are sunny. Consequently **it is impossible to have $k=n$**.\n\n\u220e"
