            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  No configuration with \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume that there are \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) covering all points of \\(S\\).\n\nWrite the slope of a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) as a reduced fraction  \n\n\\[\nm_{\\ell}=\\frac{p}{q},\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q,\\;q\\neq0 .\n\\]\n\nThe equation of \\(\\ell\\) can be written \\(y=m_{\\ell}x+b\\) for some real \\(b\\).  \nMultiplying by \\(q\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nqx-py = qb . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor any integer solution \\((x,y)\\) of (2) we have \\(x\\equiv x_{0}\\pmod{|q|}\\) for a fixed residue class \\(x_{0}\\) (because \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are coprime).  \nHence the set of \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(S\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\) is an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(|q|\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\); therefore the number of admissible \\(x\\)-values on \\(\\ell\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(p\\neq0\\)) and not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(p\\neq -q\\)), we cannot have \\(|q|=1\\).  \nThus \\(|q|\\ge 2\\), and the worst case for the bound (4) is \\(|q|=2\\).  \nHence for every sunny line  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSumming (5) over the \\(n\\) sunny lines gives an upper bound for the total number of incidences  \n\n\\[\nI:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\\ell_{i}\\cap S|\n   \\le n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, by the covering requirement each point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one line, so  \n\n\\[\nI\\ge |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) one checks that  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (6)–(7).  \nTherefore a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist; i.e. \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k\\;( \\ge 1).\n\\]\n\n**Lines that are not sunny.**  \nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical lines, hence not sunny.\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\;y = x-(r+j-1) .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(M_j\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently every \\(M_j\\) is sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nWe have produced exactly \\(r+k=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) are sunny.\n\n**Verification that all points of \\(S\\) are covered.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le r\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\).\n\n*If \\(a>r\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(r+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b=j\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\ge r+1\\) and \\(a\\le n+1-j\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na-(r+j-1)=b=j .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a-(r+j-1)=b\\), i.e. \\(a-b=r+j-1\\), which is exactly the equation of the line \\(M_j\\).  \nTherefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_j\\).\n\nIn all cases the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, so the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final answer  \n\nThe integer \\(k=n\\) is impossible, while for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration exists (the construction above).  \n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
