for a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(3\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \nHence the total number of points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+3k\\;\\ge\\;T_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\neq 3\\) we solve (2) for \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(n-k)n+3k &\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\\\\n2n^2-2nk+6k &\\ge n^2+n\\\\\nn^2-n-2nk+6k &\\ge 0\\\\\nk(6-2n) &\\ge -(n^2-n) .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n>3\\) the factor \\(6-2n\\) is negative, so the inequality reverses when we divide:\n\n\\[\nk\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-3)} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary bound  \n\n\\[\nk\\le K_{\\max }(n):=\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-3)}\\Big\\rfloor ,\\qquad n\\ge 6 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3,4,5\\) a direct check of (2) gives \\(k\\le n\\); thus (4) also holds if we define  \n\n\\[\nK_{\\max }(n)=n\\qquad (n=3,4,5).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficient construction\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max }(n)\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Construction of the sunny lines\n\nWe build the sunny lines one by one.\n\n* Choose any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) that is not yet covered and such that the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), the vertical line \\(x=a\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) have not been used by a previously chosen sunny line.  \n* There exists a unique sunny line passing through \\((a,b)\\) and intersecting the three unused directions (its slope is forced to be different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n* This line automatically contains the two other points \\((a',b)\\) and \\((a,b')\\) where \\(a'\\) (resp. \\(b'\\)) is the other integer that together with \\(b\\) (resp. \\(a\\)) satisfies \\(a'+b\\le n+1\\) (resp. \\(a+b'\\le n+1\\)). Thus the line contains exactly three points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nBecause each sunny line consumes three previously unused directions (one horizontal, one vertical, one diagonal), after having placed \\(k\\) sunny lines we have used at most \\(3k\\) distinct horizontals, verticals and diagonals.  \nThe bound (3) guarantees  \n\n\\[\n3k\\le (n-k)n,\n\\]\n\nso there remain at least \\(n-k\\) unused horizontal lines. Consequently the greedy procedure can be continued until exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines have been produced, all of them distinct and each containing three new points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Covering the remaining points\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the set of horizontal lines \\(y=b\\) that have **not** been used by any sunny line.  \nBy the previous paragraph \\(|H|=n-k\\).  \n\nFor each \\(y=b\\in H\\) we take the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); it is non‑sunny and contains **all** points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\).  \nSince every point not already covered by a sunny line lies on one of these unused horizontals, the collection of the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Verification\n\n*All lines are distinct*: the sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0\\); the horizontal lines have slope \\(0\\).  \n*Number of lines*: exactly \\(k\\) sunny + \\((n-k)\\) horizontal = \\(n\\).  \n*Coverage*: by construction every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs either to a sunny line (three points per sunny line) or to the horizontal line having its \\(y\\)-co