s\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b_0\\) with \\(1\\le b_0\\le n\\), then the condition \\(a+b_0\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_0\\); hence at most \\(n\\) integer values of \\(a\\) are possible.  \nIf the line is vertical, the same argument with the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) exchanged gives the same bound.  \nIf the line has slope \\(-1\\), it is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\) satisfying \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\). The points of \\(T_n\\) on this line are precisely the pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); there are exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) such pairs. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\). The three points are collinear, so the slopes between any two of them are equal. Computing the slope between \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and \\((a_j,b_j)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{b_j-b_i}{a_j-a_i}\\in\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\nbecause the differences \\(a_j-a_i\\) and \\(b_j-b_i\\) are integers whose sum is either \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(0\\) (vertical) or \\(-\\) (diagonal). Hence the common slope of \\(L\\) would be one of the forbidden values, contradicting the assumption that \\(L\\) is sunny. Therefore a sunny line can meet \\(T_n\\) in at most two points. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Argument\n\nSuppose that we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem, and that **at most one** of these lines is sunny. Let  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{0,1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\). Then the number of non\u2011sunny lines is \\(n-s\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma\u202f2 the (possible) sunny line covers at most \\(2\\) points. Consequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the whole family is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\n(n-s)\\cdot n + s\\cdot 2 \\le (n-1)n + 2 = n^{2}-n+2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, condition\u202f1 of the problem requires that **every** point of \\(T_n\\) be covered, i.e. at least \\(|T_n|\\) points must be covered. Using (1),\n\n\\[\n|T_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}-n+2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - (n^{2}-n+2)=\\frac{n^{2}+n-2n^{2}+2n-4}{2}\n= \\frac{-n^{2}+3n-4}{2}\n= \\frac{-(n-1)(n-4)}{2}>0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(n-1>0\\) and \\(n-4\\le -1\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThus the upper bound (3) is strictly smaller than the required number of points (4). Hence it is impossible to cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(n\\) lines when the number of sunny lines is at most one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there is **no** configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the problem\u2019s covering condition that contains \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) sunny lines. Consequently any admissible value of \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\n(Determining which values \\(k\\ge 2\\) are actually attainable remains open.) \u220e"
