oes **not** require a vertical line is \\(C_{n}\\), which consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \nTherefore any admissible configuration can contain **at most one** sunny line:\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)  \n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny, and each column \\(C_{c}\\) is exactly the set of points of \\(S\\) lying on the line \\(x=c\\).  \nHence every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and the configuration contains \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(k=1\\)  \n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(S\\) that belong to the columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\).  \nThe only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\in C_{n}\\).\n\nChoose any line that passes through \\((n,1)\\) and whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); for definiteness take  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = 2x - 2n + 2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) satisfies the “sunny’’ condition, so \\(\\ell\\) is a sunny line.  \nBy construction \\(\\ell\\) contains the point \\((n,1)\\).  \nAll other points of \\(S\\) are already covered by the vertical lines (6).  \n\nThus we have a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny, and every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition (4) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \nThe explicit constructions in Sections 2 and 3 demonstrate that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.  \n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists i