--------------------------------------------------\n#### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(x=c\\) contains precisely the points of the column \\(C_{c}\\); therefore the family (7) covers every point of \\(S\\). All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny. Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of the columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\). The only point of \\(S\\) not yet covered is the unique element of \\(C_{n}\\), namely \\((n,1)\\).\n\nChoose any line through \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); such a line is sunny. For concreteness let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = 2x-2n+2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) is \\(2\\), which satisfies the definition of a sunny line. The set consisting of the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines (8) together with \\(\\ell\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(S\\), and has precisely one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nThe Lemma proves that any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines can contain at most one sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le 1\\). The explicit constructions above show that both possibilities \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). Consequently the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, j