ust be covered by a vertical line, forcing \\(v=n-1\\) and leaving only **one** non\u2011vertical line, which may be sunny.  \n   - Therefore the number of sunny lines is at most one. \u220e\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\)** \u2013 the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover all points of \\(S\\); none is sunny.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=1\\)** \u2013 use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (covering every column except \\(C_{n}\\)). The remaining uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\). Add any sunny line through this point, e.g.  \n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y = 2x-2n+2 ,\n   \\]  \n   whose slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\). The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly one sunny line.\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013 the lemma gives \\(k\\le 1\\); the two explicit families show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) define the **column**\n\n\\[\nC_{c}:=\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;c+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom the inequality \\(c+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|C_{c}| = n+1-c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|C_{c}|=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }c=n,\\\\[2pt]\n\\ge 2 &\\text{if }1\\le c\\le n-1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma (upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a collection \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(S\\), then at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is sunny; i.e. the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA **vertical** line has equation \\(x=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\); a **non\u2011vertical** line meets a fixed column in at most one point, because all points of a column share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.\n\nLet \\(v\\) be the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\). Those \\(v\\) lines cover completely the \\(v\\) columns they belong to.\n\nConsider a column \\(C_{c}\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) that is **not** covered by a vertical line. By (4) such a column contains at least two points. Since a non\u2011vertical line can contribute at most one point from this column, covering all its points requires at least two distinct non\u2011vertical lines. Consequently the total number of non\u2011vertical lines is at least  \n\n\\[\n2\\bigl((n-1)-v\\bigr),\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nbecause there are \\((n-1)-v\\) columns among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) that lack a vertical line.\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the number of non\u2011vertical lines equals \\(n-v\\). Combining this with (5) yields  \n\n\\[\nn-v\\;\\ge\\;2\\bigl((n-1)-v\\bigr)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nn-v\\;\\ge\\;2n-2-2v\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nv\\;\\ge\\;n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus at most two lines are non\u2011vertical.\n\n---\n\nNow suppose, for contradiction, that **both** non\u2011vertical lines are present.  \nSince column \\(C_{n}\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\), this point must lie on one of the two non\u2011vertical lines (otherwise it would be uncovered). Let \\(\\ell\\) be a non\u2011vertical line containing \\((n,1)\\).\n\nIf the other non\u2011vertical line does **not** intersect column \\(C_{n-1}\\), then column \\(C_{n-1}\\) would have to be covered by a vertical line. But we already have at most \\(v=n-2\\) vertical lines by (6); the columns already occupied by vertical lines are exactly those \\(v\\) columns, and there remain at least two columns among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) without a vertical line (otherwise \\(v\\) would be \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\)). Hence at least one of those uncovered columns, and in particular \\(C_{n-1}\\), must receive points from the two non\u2011vertical lines. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) must also pass through a point of \\(C_{n-1}\\).\n\nThe points of \\(C_{n-1}\\) are \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\). The slopes of the lines through \\((n,1)\\) and these points are respectively  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-1}{n-(n-1)} = 0 ,\\qquad\n\\frac{2-1}{n-(n-1)} = -1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) are prohibited for a sunny line (a sunny line must have slope different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\)). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) cannot be sunny. Hence **at most one** of the non\u2011vertical lines can be sunny.\n\nIf there is exactly **one** non\u2011vertical line, it may be sunny. In this situation the inequality (6) forces \\(v\\ge n-2\\). Because the column \\(C_{n}\\) must still be covered, the unique non\u2011vertical line must contain \\((n,1)\\). As shown above, any line through \\((n,1)\\) that also meets a point of \\(C_{n-1}\\) has forbidden slope, so the column \\(C_{n-1}\\) must be covered by a vertical line \\(x=n-1\\). Consequently we need vertical lines for all columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) except possibly one; that gives \\(v=n-1\\). Hence there is exactly **one** non\u2011vertical line, and it may be sunny.\n\nThus any covering family of \\(n\\) lines contains at most one sunny line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(x=c\\) contains precisely the points of the column \\(C_{c}\\); therefore the family (7) covers every point of \\(S\\). All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the