            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) define the **column**\n\n\\[\nC_{c}:=\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;c+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom the inequality \\(c+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|C_{c}| = n+1-c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|C_{c}|=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }c=n,\\\\[2pt]\n\\ge 2 &\\text{if }1\\le c\\le n-1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma (at most one sunny line)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S\\).  \nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThen \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\nA line is **vertical** if its equation is \\(x=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\); otherwise it is **non‑vertical**.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nv:=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nm:=n-v\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nbe the numbers of vertical and non‑vertical lines, respectively.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 1.  Columns covered by vertical lines.**  \n\nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains exactly the points of the column \\(C_{c}\\).  \nHence the \\(v\\) vertical lines cover precisely the \\(v\\) distinct columns among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n}\\) whose indices equal the \\(x\\)-coordinates of those lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 2.  How many columns may lack a vertical line?**  \n\nSuppose that among the columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) there are at least two columns that are **not** covered by a vertical line.  \nChoose two such columns, say \\(C_{p}\\) and \\(C_{q}\\) with \\(1\\le p<q\\le n-1\\).  \nEach of these columns contains at least two points (by (4)).  \n\nA non‑vertical line meets a fixed column in at most one point, because all points of a column share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate while a non‑vertical line has a unique \\(x\\)-value for each point on it.  \nConsequently, to cover the at least two points of \\(C_{p}\\) we need **at least two** distinct non‑vertical lines, and similarly for \\(C_{q}\\).  \n\nIf \\(m\\le 1\\) (i.e. at most one non‑vertical line) this is impossible.  \nIf \\(m=2\\) the two non‑vertical lines could each cover at most one point of \\(C_{p}\\) and at most one point of \\(C_{q}\\); together they could cover at most two points total, but we need at least four (two in each column).  \nThus with \\(m\\le 2\\) it is impossible to have two columns without vertical lines.\n\nTherefore, **when \\(m\\le 2\\) there can be at most one column among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) that lacks a vertical line.**  \n(If \\(m\\ge 3\\) the lemma will already be proved, because we shall see that any configuration with \\(m\\ge 2\\) forces a non‑sunny line, see Step 4.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 3.  The column \\(C_{n}\\).**  \n\nThe column \\(C_{n}\\) consists of the single point  \n\n\\[\nP_{n}:=(n,1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(v=n\\) then all columns are covered by vertical lines and there is no non‑vertical line; in this case \\(k=0\\).  \nHence we may assume \\(m\\ge 1\\).  \nBecause the vertical lines cover only columns with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the point \\(P_{n}\\) must lie on a non‑vertical line.  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a non‑vertical line that contains \\(P_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 4.  When \\(m\\ge 2\\) the line \\(\\ell\\) cannot be sunny.**  \n\nAssume \\(m\\ge 2\\).  \nBy Step 2 there is at most one column among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) without a vertical line.  \nConsider the column \\(C_{n-1}\\); it contains the two points  \n\n\\[\nQ_{1}:=(n-1,1),\\qquad Q_{2}:=(n-1,2).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThere are two possibilities.\n\n*Case A: \\(C_{n-1}\\) is covered by a vertical line.*  \nThen the vertical line is \\(x=n-1\\); it covers both \\(Q_{1}\\) and \\(Q_{2}\\).  \nAll other columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-2}\\) are covered by the remaining \\(v-1\\) vertical lines, so the only columns possibly lacking a vertical line are \\(C_{n}\\) (already handled) and, if any, at most one among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-2}\\).  \nSince \\(m\\ge 2\\), at least one non‑vertical line other than \\(\\ell\\) exists; denote it by \\(\\ell'\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) already contains \\(P_{n}\\), the only way \\(\\ell\\) could also pass through a point of \\(C_{n-1}\\) is to have slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-1}{n-(n-1)}=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\frac{2-1}{n-(n-1)}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes are prohibited for a sunny line.  \nHence, if \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, it must **avoid** both \\(Q_{1}\\) and \\(Q_{2}\\).\n\n*Case B: \\(C_{n-1}\\) is **not** covered by a vertical line.*  \nThen, by Step 2, this is the **only** column among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) without a vertical line.  \nConsequently every other column \\(C_{c}\\) with \\(c\\le n-2\\) is covered by a vertical line, and the two points \\(Q_{1},Q_{2}\\) must be covered by the non‑vertical lines \\(\\ell\\) and \\(\\ell'\\).  \nSince a non‑vertical line meets a column in at most one point, each of \\(\\ell,\\ell'\\) can contain at most one of \