ts of the columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\).  \nThe only point of \\(S\\) not yet covered is the unique element of \\(C_{n}\\), namely \\((n,1)\\).\n\nChoose any line through \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); such a line is sunny.  \nFor concreteness we may take  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = 2x-2n+2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) is \\(2\\), which satisfies the definition of a sunny line.  \nThe set consisting of the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines (9) together with \\(\\ell\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(S\\), and has precisely one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nThe Lemma proves that any family of \\(n\\) lines covering the set \\(S\\) can contain at most one sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le 1\\).  \nThe explicit families constructed above show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
