lumns may lack a vertical line?**  \n\nAssume that among the columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) there are at least two columns that are **not** covered by a vertical line.  \nChoose two such columns, say \\(C_{p}\\) and \\(C_{q}\\) with \\(1\\le p<q\\le n-1\\).  \nEach of these columns contains at least two points (by (4)).  \n\nA non‑vertical line meets a fixed column in at most one point, because all points of a column share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate while a non‑vertical line has a unique \\(x\\)-value for each point on it.  \nConsequently, to cover the at least two points of \\(C_{p}\\) we need **at least two** distinct non‑vertical lines, and similarly for \\(C_{q}\\).  \n\nIf \\(m\\le 1\\) this is impossible.  \nIf \\(m=2\\) the two non‑vertical lines could each cover at most one point of \\(C_{p}\\) and at most one point of \\(C_{q}\\); together they could cover at most four points, but we need at least four (two in each column).  This forces each of the two non‑vertical lines to pass through **both** points of a column, which would give them the same slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\) (see Step 4) and hence they would not be sunny; more importantly, the covering would be impossible because a line cannot contain two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate unless it is vertical, contradicting the assumption that the lines are non‑vertical.  \n\nThus, **when \\(m\\le 2\\) there can be at most one column among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) that lacks a vertical line.**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 3.  The column \\(C_{n}\\).**  \n\nThe column \\(C_{n}\\) consists of the single point  \n\n\\[\nP_{n}:=(n,1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(v=n\\) then all columns are covered by vertical lines and there is no non‑vertical line; in this case \\(k=0\\).  \nHence we may assume \\(m\\ge 1\\).  \nBecause the vertical lines cover only columns with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the point \\(P_{n}\\) must lie on a non‑vertical line.  \nLe