ical lines and there is no non‑vertical line; in this case \\(k=0\\).  \nHence we may assume \\(m\\ge 1\\).  \nBecause the vertical lines cover only columns with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the point \\(P_{n}\\) must lie on a non‑vertical line.  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a non‑vertical line that contains \\(P_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Step 4.  When \\(m\\ge 2\\) the line \\(\\ell\\) cannot be sunny.**  \n\nAssume \\(m\\ge 2\\).  \nBy Step 2 there is at most one column among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) without a vertical line.  \nConsider the column \\(C_{n-1}\\); it contains the two points  \n\n\\[\nQ_{1}:=(n-1,1),\\qquad Q_{2}:=(n-1,2).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nTwo possibilities arise.\n\n*Case A: \\(C_{n-1}\\) is covered by a vertical line.*  \nThen the vertical line is \\(x=n-1\\); it covers both \\(Q_{1}\\) and \\(Q_{2}\\).  \nAll other columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-2}\\) are covered by the remaining \\(v-1\\) vertical lines, so the only columns possibly lacking a vertical line are \\(C_{n}\\) (already handled) and, if any, at most one among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-2}\\).  \nSince \\(m\\ge 2\\), at least one non‑vertical line other than \\(\\ell\\) exists; denote it by \\(\\ell'\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) already contains \\(P_{n}\\), the only way \\(\\ell\\) could also pass through a point of \\(C_{n-1}\\) is to have slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-1}{n-(n-1)}=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\frac{2-1}{n-(n-1)}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes are prohibited for a sunny line.  \nHence, if \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, it must avoid both \\(Q_{1}\\) and \\(Q_{2}\\).\n\n*Case B: \\(C_{n-1}\\) is **not** covered by a vertical line.*  \nThen, by Step 2, this is the **only** column among \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) without a vertical line.  \nConsequently every other column \\(C_{c}\\) with \\(c\\le n-2\\) is covered by a vertical line, and the two points \\(Q_{1},Q_{2}\\) must be covered by the non‑vertical lines \\(\\ell\\) and \\(\\ell'\\).  \nSince 