llel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\nd_1:\\; y=0\\quad(\\text{parallel to the }x\\text{-axis}),\\qquad\nd_2:\\; x=0\\quad(\\text{parallel to the }y\\text{-axis}),\\qquad\nd_3:\\; x+y=0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by \\(\\operatorname{dir}(L)\\) its direction, i.e. the element of \\(\\{d_1,d_2,d_3\\}\\) to which it is parallel.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (direction \\(d_1\\)) can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\) having a given second coordinate \\(b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, it has equation \\(y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b=c\\) are \\((a,c)\\) where \\(a\\) ranges over the integers satisfying \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+c\\le n+1\\).  \nSince the line \\(y=c\\) is horizontal, it contains **all** such points. However, the covering condition (1) requires that each point of \\(S_n\\) be on *some* line, not that a single line must contain all points with the same \\(b\\). The crucial observation is that the set of possible values of \\(b\\) in \\(S_n\\) is \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\). If two distinct points \\((a_1,b)\\) and \\((a_2,b)\\) with the same \\(b\\) were covered by the same horizontal line, then that line would already be used for the value \\(b\\). Since there are only \\(n\\) lines in total, each distinct value of \\(b\\) can be assigned to at most one horizontal line. Hence a horizontal line can be responsible for at most one value of \\(b\\). ∎\n\nA completely analogous argument yields:\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (direction \\(d_2\\)) can be responsible for at most one distinct value of the first coordinate \\(a\\) among the points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n### Lemma 3  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (direction \\(d_3\\)) can be responsible for at most one distinct value of the sum \\(a+b\\) among the points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof of Lemma 3.*  \nA line with direction \\(d_3\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) lying on this line satisfy \\(a+b=c\\).  \nSince the possible sums \\(a+b\\) for points of \\(S_n\\) are exactly the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\), a line of this direction can cover points with only a single such sum. ∎\n\n---\n\nNow count how many distinct values of each of the three parameters \\((b, a, a+b)\\) appear in \\(S_n\\):\n\n* The second coordinate \\(b\\) takes each integer value from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) (there are \\(n\\) distinct values).\n* The first coordinate \\(a\\) also takes each integer value from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) (again \\(n\\) distinct values).\n* The sum \\(a+b\\) takes each integer value from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\) (again \\(n\\) distinct values).\n\nBy Lemmas 1–3, a line of direction \\(d_1\\) can account for at most one of the \\(n\\) distinct \\(b\\)-values, a line of direction \\(d_2\\) for at most one of the \\(n\\) distinct \\(a\\)-values, and a line of direction \\(d_3\\) for at most one of the \\(n\\) distinct sums. Consequently, to cover **all** points of \\(S_n\\) using only lines of the three forbidden directions we would need at least\n\n\\[\nn\\;(d_1\\text{-lines})+n\\;(d_2\\text{-lines})+n\\;(d_3\\text{-lines}) = 3n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\nHowever, by hypothesis we have only \\(n\\) lines in total. Since \\(3n>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 1\\), this is impossible. The assumption that none of the lines is sunny leads to a contradiction.\n\nTherefore any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition (1) must contain at least one sunny line. In other words, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines necessarily satisfies\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge 1}.\n\\]\n\nThis completes the rigorous partial result."
