\(L\\) have the same first coordinate.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line has equation \\(x=c\\). Any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\cap L\\) satisfies \\(a=c\\). ∎\n\nThus a vertical line can be responsible for at most one value of \\(a\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 3 (Slope \\(-1\\) lines)\n\nIf \\(\\operatorname{dir}(L)=d_{-1}\\) then all points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) have the same sum \\(a+b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\). For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\cap L\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\). ∎\n\nHence a slope‑\\(-1\\) line can be responsible for at most one value of the sum \\(a+b\\).\n\n---\n\n### Counting argument\n\nIn the set \\(S_n\\) we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\{b\\mid (a,b)\\in S_n\\}&=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n\\{a\\mid (a,b)\\in S_n\\}&=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n\\{a+b\\mid (a,b)\\in S_n\\}&=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus there are exactly \\(n\\) distinct values of each of the three parameters \\(b\\), \\(a\\) and \\(a+b\\).\n\nBy Lemmas 1–3, a line of direction \\(d_x\\) can cover points with only one of the \\(n\\) possible \\(b\\)-values, a line of direction \\(d_y\\) can cover points with only one of the \\(n\\) possible \\(a\\)-values, and a line of direction \\(d_{-1}\\) can cover points with only one of the \\(n\\) possible sums \\(a+b\\).\n\nTherefore, to cover **all** points of \\(S_n\\) using only lines of the three forbidden directions we would need at least\n\n\\[\nn\\;(d_x\\text{-lines})+n\\;(d_y\\text{-lines})+n\\;(d_{-1}\\text{-lines})=3n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\nBut the hypothesis provides only \\(n\\) lines. Since \\(3n>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 1\\), this is impossible. The assumption that every line is non‑sunny leads to a contradiction.\n\nHence any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition (1) must contain at least one sunny line. In other words, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines necessarily fulfills  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge 1}.\n\\]\n\nThis concludes the rigorous partial result.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
