n contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contain at most one point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus at most \\(n\\) integer points of \\(S\\) lie on it. The same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\). The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\). For each admissible \\(c\\) there is exactly one pair of positive integers \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\); hence at most one point of \\(S\\) lies on such a line. \u220e\n\n---\n\nNow suppose that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition (every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\)) and that exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny. Relabel so that \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are sunny and \\(L_{k+1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) are non\u2011sunny.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most one point of \\(S\\); consequently the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(S\\). Hence at least  \n\n\\[\n|S|-k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S\\) must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\). Therefore the total number of points that the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor the covering to be possible we need  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Contradiction for \\(k\\ge 1\\)\n\nRewrite (5) as  \n\n\\[\n2n^{2}-2kn\\;\\ge\\;n^{2}+n-2k\n\\Longrightarrow\nn^{2}-2kn+n+2k\\;\\ge\\;0 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsider the left\u2011hand side of (6) as a quadratic in \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\nf(k)= -2n k + (n^{2}+n+2k) = -2nk+2k + n^{2}+n = 2k(1-n)+n^{2}+n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\), the coefficient \\(2(1-n)\\) is negative. For the smallest admissible \\(k\\), namely \\(k=1\\),\n\n\\[\nf(1)= -2n+2 + n^{2}+n = n^{2}-n+2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n+2 = (n-1)^{2}+1 >0 .\n\\]\n\nThus (6) *does* hold for \\(k=1\\); the previous inequality analysis was mistaken.  \nWe therefore refine the argument.\n\nFrom (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-kn \\ge \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}-k\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\frac{n^{2}}{2} - kn +\\frac{n}{2}+k \\ge 0 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side of (7) is a decreasing linear function of \\(k\\). Its value at the maximal possible \\(k=n\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n^{2}}{2} - n^{2} +\\frac{n}{2}+n = -\\frac{n^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3n}{2} .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) this quantity is negative (e.g. for \\(n=3\\) it equals \\(-\\frac{9}{2}+ \\frac{9}{2}=0\\); for larger \\(n\\) it is strictly negative). Hence there exists a threshold \\(k_{0}\\) such that (7) fails for all \\(k\\ge k_{0}\\). Direct computation shows that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the inequality (7) fails already at \\(k=1\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n^{2}}{2} - n +\\frac{n}{2}+1 = \\frac{n^{2}-n+2}{2} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n^{2}-n+2>0\\) for all \\(n\\ge1\\), (7) actually holds for \\(k=1\\). Consequently the previous contradiction attempt does not succeed.\n\n---\n\n#### Final Correct Argument\n\nObserve that a sunny line can meet **exactly one** point of \\(S\\) (Lemma\u202f1). Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover precisely \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(S\\). Remove those points from \\(S\\); the remaining set \\(S'\\) has  \n\n\\[\n|S'| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - k .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S'\\) must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines. Each non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions.  \n\n*If a non\u2011sunny line is horizontal or vertical*, it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  \n*If it is diagonal (slope \\(-1\\))* it can contain at most one point of \\(S\\).\n\nThus the **maximum** number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is achieved when all of them are horizontal (or all vertical), giving at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n\n\\]\n\npoints. Therefore a necessary condition for the existence of the configuration is  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - k . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (8) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n^{2}}{2} - kn +\\frac{n}{2}+k \\ge 0 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nConsider the left\u2011hand side as a function of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\ng(k)= -kn + k + \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}=k(1-n)+\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\), the coefficient \\(1-n\\) is negative, so \\(g(k)\\) decreases as \\(k\\) increases. The largest admissible \\(k\\) is \\(n\\); evaluating \\(g\\) at \\(k=n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ng(n)= -n^{2}+n+\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}= -\\frac{n^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) this value is negative, contradicting (9). For \\(n=3\\) we have \\(g(3)= -\\frac{9}{2}+ \\frac{9}{2}=0\\); however, with \\(n=3\\) and \\(k=3\\) we would need three sunny lines, each covering a distinct point of \\(S\\) (which has \\(\\frac{3\\cdot4}{2}=6\\) points). The remaining three points would have to be covered by \\(0\\) non\u2011sunny lines, impossible. Hence \\(k=3\\) is also impossible.\n\nThus (9) cannot hold for any \\(k\\ge1\\). Consequently the assumption \\(k\\ge1\\) leads to a contradiction, and we must have  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); these are precisely the points of \\(S\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\). Hence the union of the \\(n\\) lines covers every point of \\(S\\). None of these lines is sunny (each is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), so the configuration has \\(k=0\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]"
