)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) ordered pairs\n\\((a,b)\\) satisfying \\(a+b=s\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Non‑sunny lines can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\)\n\nA line is non‑sunny iff it is parallel to one of  \n\n\\[\n\\text{(H)}\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad\n\\text{(V)}\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad\n\\text{(D)}\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal* \\(y=c\\): the condition \\((a,c)\\in S\\) gives \\(a\\ge 1\\) and\n\\(a+c\\le n+1\\); hence \\(a\\) can take the values \\(1,\\dots ,n+1-c\\), at most \\(n\\) of them.\n\n*Vertical* \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.\n\n*Diagonal* \\(x+y=d\\): the integer solutions of \\(a+b=d\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) are\n\\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots ,(d-1,1)\\); there are \\(d-1\\le n\\) of them because\n\\(d\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus every non‑sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.  \n\nConsequently, if \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines are used they can cover at most \\(tn\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines  \n\nAll points of \\(S\\) must be covered, so by (5)\n\n\\[\ntn\\;\\ge\\;|S|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nt\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is an integer,\n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rceil . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines.  Because the total number of lines is\n\\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nk=n-t\\le n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence no configuration can contain more than \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Existence of a covering with any admissible number of non‑sunny lines  \n\nWe prove the following lemma.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma.**  \nFor every integer \\(t\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\le t\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exist \\(t\\) distinct non‑sunny lines whose union contains all points of\n\\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n**(a) A covering with \\(n\\) lines.**  \nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n}= \\{y=1,\\;x=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=s\\mid s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe two axis‑parallel lines are non‑sunny, and each diagonal line\n\\(x+y=s\\) (with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\)) is also non‑sunny.  There are\n\\(n-2\\) diagonal lines, so \\(|\\mathcal F_{n}|=n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nIf a point of \\(S\\) has second coordinate \\(1\\) it lies on \\(y=1\\); if it has\nfirst coordinate \\(1\\) it lies on \\(x=1\\).  Otherwise both coordinates are at\nleast \\(2\\); then its sum \\(a+b\\) satisfies \\(4\\le a+b\\le n+1\\) and the point\nlies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) belonging to (8).  Hence every point of\n\\(S\\) belongs to some line of \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\).\n\n**(b) Deleting redundant lines.**  \nCall a line *redundant* if after its removal the remaining lines still cover\n\\(S\\).  We claim that whenever a family \\(\\mathcal G\\) of non‑sunny lines\ncovers \\(S\\) and \\(|\\mathcal G|>\\big\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\), at least one\nline of \\(\\mathcal G\\) is redundant.\n\nIndeed, let \\(I\\) be the number of incidences \\((P,L)\\) with \\(P\\in S\\),\n\\(L\\in\\mathcal G\\) and \\(P\\in L\\).  Because each line contains at most \\(n\\)\npoints of \\(S\\),\n\n\\[\nI\\le n|\\mathcal G|. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand every point of \\(S\\) is incident to at least one line, so\n\n\\[\nI\\ge |S|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal G|>\\big\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) then\n\\(n|\\mathcal G|>\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\); consequently the inequality in (9) is\nstrict.  Hence the average number of points per line,\n\\(\\displaystyle \\frac{I}{|\\mathcal G|}\\), is **strictly larger** than\n\\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).  Since each line contains at most \\(n\\) points, there must be\na line that contains at most \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) points of \\(S\\).  Removing that\nline eliminates at most \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) points from the covered set.\n\nBecause the remaining family still has at least\n\\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) lines, inequality (6) guarantees that\nthose remaining lines are sufficient to cover the whole set \\(S\\).  Thus the\nremoved line was redundant.\n\n**(c) Obtaining any admissible \\(t\\).**  \nStarting from \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) we repeatedly delete a redundant line.\nEach deletion reduces the number of lines by one while preserving the covering\nproperty.  By the claim above we can continue as long as the current number of\nlines exceeds \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\).  Therefore we can reach\nany integer \\(t\\) satisfying (3) and obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) of\n\\(t\\) non‑sunny lines covering \\(S\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSet \\(t=n-k\\).  By the lemma there exist \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines covering all\npoints of \\(S\\).  Choose any \\(k\\) further distinct lines that are not parallel\nto the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); for instance\n\n\\[\ny = x + c_{1},\\; y = x + c_{2},\\dots , y = x + c_{k},\n\\]\n\nwith pairwise different constants \\(c_{i}\\).  These \\(k\\) lines are sunny by\ndefinition and do not affect the covering already provided by the non‑sunny\nlines.  Altogether we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely\n\\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nThe upper bound (7) shows that no configuration can have more than\n\\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, and the construction above shows that\nevery integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) is attainable.\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
