ne \\(L_{0}\\) satisfies \\(e(L_{0})=0\\); that line has **no exclusive\n   points**.  Removing \\(L_{0}\\) cannot uncover any point of \\(S\\), so the\n   remaining family still covers \\(S\\).  Thus \\(L_{0}\\) is redundant.\n\n   **(iii) Obtaining any admissible \\(t\\).**  \n   Starting from the covering \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) we repeatedly delete a\n   redundant line.  After each deletion the family still covers \\(S\\), and\n   the number of lines decreases by one.  By the argument above we can keep\n   deleting as long as the current number of lines exceeds\n   \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\).  Hence we can reach **any**\n   integer \\(t\\) satisfying (2) and obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) of\n   \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines covering \\(S\\). ∎\n\n6. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) and\n   put \\(t=n-k\\).  By the Lemma there exist \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines covering all\n   points of \\(S\\).  Add any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, for instance the\n   lines \\(y=x+c_{1},\\dots ,y=x+c_{k}\\) with pairwise different constants\n   \\(c_{i}\\).  The resulting collection consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and it still covers every point\n   of \\(S\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that no configuration can have more than\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, while the construction\n   above shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le\n   \\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) is attainable.  Therefore the set of\n   possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
