ce the total number of lines is \\(n\\), letting \\(k\\) denote the number of sunny lines we obtain  \n   \\[\n   k=n-t\\le n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lemma (existence of a covering with any admissible number of non‑sunny lines).**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   For every integer \\(t\\) satisfying  \n   \\[\n   \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\le t\\le n ,\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   there exist \\(t\\) distinct non‑sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n\n   **(i) A covering with \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n}= \\{y=1,\\;x=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=s\\mid s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   The two axis‑parallel lines and the \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines are all non‑sunny, and\n   \\(|\\mathcal F_{n}|=n\\).  \n   Every point of \\(S\\) belongs to one of them:\n   * points with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(y=1\\);\n   * points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(x=1\\);\n   * the remaining points have \\(a,b\\ge 2\\), hence \\(a+b\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\n   **(ii) Redundant‑line deletion.**  \n   Call a line *redundant* if after its removal the remaining lines still cover \\(S\\).\n   Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be any family of non‑sunny lines covering \\(S\\) with \\(|\\mathcal G|> \\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\).\n   For \\(L\\in\\mathcal G\\) define its *exclusive* points\n   \\[\n   e(L)=\\bigl|\\{P\\in S\\mid P\\in L\\text{ and }P\\notin L'\\text{ for any }L'\\in\\mathcal G\\setminus\\{L\\}\\}\\bigr|.\n   \\]\n   Summing over all lines,\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal G} e(L)=|S|-\\bigl|\\{P\\in S\\mid P\\text{ lies on at least two lines of }\\mathcal G\\}\\bigr|\n   \\le |S|.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Each line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); hence the total number of incidences\n   \\((P,L)\\) with \\(P\\in S,\\;L\\in\\mathcal G,\\;P\\in L\\) does not exceed \\(n|\\mathcal G|\\).\n   Removing the exclusive points leaves at most\n   \\(n|\\mathcal G|-\\sum e(L)\\) *non‑exclusive* incidences.\n   Every non‑exclusive point is counted at least twice, therefore\n   \\[\n   n|\\mathcal G|-\\sum e(L)\\;\\ge\\;2\\bigl(|S|-\\sum e(L)\\bigr).\n   \\]\n   Rearranging gives\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal G} e(L)\\;\\le\\;n|\\mathcal G|-2|S|\n   =n|\\mathcal G|-n(n+1).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(|\\mathcal G|> \\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) then\n   \\(n|\\mathcal G|-n(n+1)<n\\), and (7) yields\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal G} e(L)<n .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Since the left‑hand side is a sum of non‑negative integers, (8) forces the existence of a line \\(L_{0}\\) with \\(e(L_{0})=0\\); that line has **no exclusive points**.  Deleting \\(L_{0}\\) cannot uncover any point of \\(S\\), so \\(L_{0}\\) is redundant.\n\n   Consequently, as long as the current number of lines exceeds \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) we can delete a redundant line while preserving the covering property.\n\n   **(iii) Obtaining any admissible \\(t\\).**  \n   Starting from the covering \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) we repeatedly delete a redundant line.\n   After each deletion the family still covers \\(S\\), and the number of lines drops by one.\n   By the argument above we may continue until exactly \\(t\\) lines remain, for any integer \\(t\\) satisfying (4).  The resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) consists solely of non‑sunny lines and covers all points of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n6. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with  \n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Set \\(t=n-k\\).  By the Lemma there exist \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines covering \\(S\\).\n   Add any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, for instance the lines\n   \\[\n   y=x+c_{1},\\;y=x+c_{2},\\dots ,y=x+c_{k},\n   \\]\n   where the constants \\(c_{i}\\) are pairwise different and chosen so that none of these lines coincides with a line already used.\n   The resulting collection consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and it still covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (3) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n   The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the range\n   \\(0\\le k\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) is attainable.\n   Hence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
