ines).**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   For every integer \\(t\\) with  \n   \\[\n   \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\le t\\le n ,\n   \\]\n   there exist \\(t\\) distinct non‑sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n\n   - **(i) A covering with \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.**  \n     \\[\n     \\mathcal F_{n}= \\{y=1,\\;x=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=s\\mid s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n     \\]\n     These \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny and together cover \\(S\\).\n\n   - **(ii) Redundant‑line deletion.**  \n     Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be any family of non‑sunny lines covering \\(S\\) with \\(|\\mathcal G|> \\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\).  \n     For each line \\(L\\in\\mathcal G\\) define its *exclusive* points\n     \\[\n     e(L)=\\bigl|\\{P\\in S\\mid P\\in L\\text{ and }P\\notin L'\\text{ for any }L'\\in\\mathcal G\\setminus\\{L\\}\\}\\bigr|.\n     \\]\n     Summing over all lines,\n     \\[\n     \\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal G} e(L)=|S|-\\bigl|\\{P\\in S\\mid P\\text{ lies on at least two lines of }\\mathcal G\\}\\bigr|\n     \\le |S|.\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n     Let \\(I\\) be the total number of incidences \\((P,L)\\) with \\(P\\in S\\) and \\(P\\in L\\).  \n     Because each line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\),\n     \\[\n     I\\le n|\\mathcal G|.\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     The incidences split into *exclusive* and *non‑exclusive* ones.  \n     The number of exclusive incidences is exactly \\(\\sum e(L)\\).  \n     Hence the number of non‑exclusive incidences is \\(I-\\sum e(L)\\).\n\n     Every non‑exclusive point of \\(S\\) is counted at least twice in the incidence list (it lies on two or more lines). Therefore\n     \\[\n     I-\\sum e(L)\\;\\ge\\;2\\bigl(|S|-\\sum e(L)\\bigr).\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n     The right‑hand side is twice the number of *non‑exclusive* points, because the set of non‑exclusive points has cardinality \\(|S|-\\sum e(L)\\).\n\n     Substituting the bound (2) for \\(I\\) into (3) gives\n     \\[\n     n|\\mathcal G|-\\sum e(L)\\;\\ge\\;2\\bigl(|S|-\\sum e(L)\\bigr).\n     \\]\n     Rearranging,\n     \\[\n     \\sum e(L)\\;\\le\\;n|\\mathcal G|-2|S|.\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n     This is the previously quoted inequality (7), now derived rigorously.\n\n     Because \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), inequality (4) becomes\n     \\[\n     \\sum e(L)\\le n|\\mathcal G|-n(n+1).\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n     If \\(|\\mathcal G|> \\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) then\n     \\[\n     n|\\mathcal G|-n(n+1)<n,\n     \\]\n     and (5) yields \\(\\sum e(L)<n\\).  Since the left‑hand side is an integer, it follows that some line \\(L_{0}\\) has \\(e(L_{0})=0\\); i.e. \\(L_{0}\\) possesses **no exclusive points**.  Removing \\(L_{0}\\) cannot uncover any point of \\(S\\), so \\(L_{0}\\) is redundant.\n\n     Consequently, as long as the current family contains more than \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) lines, we can delete a redundant line while preserving the covering property.\n\n   - **(iii) Obtaining any admissible \\(t\\).**  \n     Starting from the covering \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) (which has \\(n\\) lines) we repeatedly delete a redundant line, as justified above, until only \\(t\\) lines remain.  The resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) consists solely of non‑sunny lines and still covers all points of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n6. **Realising every admissible number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) and set \\(t=n-k\\).  \n   By the Lemma there exist \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines covering \\(S\\).  \n   Add any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, for example the lines\n   \\[\n   y=x+c_{1},\\;y=x+c_{2},\\dots ,y=x+c_{k},\n   \\]\n   where the constants \\(c_{i}\\) are pairwise different and chosen so that none coincides with a line already used.  \n   The resulting collection consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and still covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n