oth endpoints lie in the interval \\([1,n]\\).  \nTherefore \\(|L\\cap S|\\le n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA line that is not sunny is precisely one of the three types considered in Lemma\u202f1.  \nConsequently each non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nIf \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines are used, the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered is at most \\(tn\\).  \nSince every point of \\(S\\) must be covered, (1) gives  \n\n\\[\ntn\\ge |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nt\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nThe whole configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nIf \\(k\\) of them are sunny, then the remaining \\(t=n-k\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nCombining this with (2) yields  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can have more than \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (existence of a covering with any admissible number of non\u2011sunny lines)  \n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(t\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\le t\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exist \\(t\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **A covering with \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n}= \\{y=1,\\;x=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=s\\mid s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The first two lines are horizontal and vertical; the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines have slope \\(-1\\).  \n   All together they are non\u2011sunny and there are exactly \\(n\\) of them.  \n   One checks directly that every \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on at least one of these lines, so \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) is a covering of \\(S\\).\n\n2. **Redundant\u2011line deletion.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be any family of non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(S\\) with \\(|\\mathcal G|> \\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\).  \n   For each line \\(L\\in\\mathcal G\\) define its *exclusive* points  \n\n   \\[\n   e(L)=\\bigl|\\{P\\in S\\mid P\\in L\\text{ and }P\\notin L'\\text{ for any }L'\\in\\mathcal G\\setminus\\{L\\}\\}\\bigr|.\n   \\]\n\n   Summing over all lines,\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal G} e(L)=|S|-\\bigl|\\{P\\in S\\mid P\\text{ lies on at least two lines of }\\mathcal G\\}\\bigr|\n   \\le |S|. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(I\\) be the total number of incidences \\((P,L)\\) with \\(P\\in S\\) and \\(P\\in L\\).  \n   Because each line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) (Lemma\u202f1),\n\n   \\[\n   I\\le n|\\mathcal G|. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The incidences split into exclusive and non\u2011exclusive ones.  \n   The number of exclusive incidences equals \\(\\sum e(L)\\).  \n   Hence the number of non\u2011exclusive incidences is \\(I-\\sum e(L)\\).\n\n   Every non\u2011exclusive point of \\(S\\) is counted at least twice in the incidence list (it lies on two or more lines). Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   I-\\sum e(L)\\;\\ge\\;2\\bigl(|S|-\\sum e(L)\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the bound (5) for \\(I\\) into (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n|\\mathcal G|-\\sum e(L)\\;\\ge\\;2\\bigl(|S|-\\sum e(L)\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging,\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum e(L)\\;\\le\\;n|\\mathcal G|-2|S|. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) from (1),\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum e(L)\\le n|\\mathcal G|-n(n+1). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(|\\mathcal G|> \\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) then \\(n|\\mathcal G|-n(n+1)<n\\); consequently (8) yields \\(\\sum e(L)<n\\).  \n   Since the left\u2011hand side is a non\u2011negative integer, there exists a line \\(L_{0}\\in\\mathcal G\\) with \\(e(L_{0})=0\\); i.e. \\(L_{0}\\) possesses **no exclusive points**.  \n   Removing \\(L_{0}\\) cannot uncover any point of \\(S\\), so \\(L_{0}\\) is redundant.\n\n   Thus, as long as a covering contains more than \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) lines, we can delete a redundant line while preserving the covering property.\n\n3. **Obtaining any admissible \\(t\\).**  \n   Begin with the covering \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) (which has \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines).  \n   Repeatedly apply the redundancy\u2011deletion procedure described above until only \\(t\\) lines remain.  \n   The resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) consists solely of non\u2011sunny lines and still covers all points of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSet \\(t=n-k\\). By Lemma\u202f2 there exist \\(t\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(S\\).  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that are not among those \\(t\\) lines; for instance\n\n\\[\ny=x+c_{1},\\;y=x+c_{2},\\dots ,y=x+c_{k},\n\\]\n\nwhere the constants \\(c_{i}\\) are pairwise different and chosen so that none of the lines coincides with a line already used (this is always possible because there are infinitely many sunny lines).  \n\nThe union of the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines and the \\(k\\) sunny lines still covers every point of \\(S\\) (the sunny lines add no new constraints).  \nThus we have produced a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, satisfying the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final conclusion  \n\nInequality (3) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.  \nThe construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the range  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nis attainable.  \n\nHence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
