 1.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, trivially  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nWe exhibit explicit families for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_{3,0}=\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=2\\}.\n  \\]\n  All three lines have slopes in \\(S\\); they cover the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\), i.e. \\(T_3\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  Replace the diagonal line by a sunny line, e.g. \\(y=2x\\):\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_{3,1}=\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;y=2x\\}.\n  \\]\n  The new line passes through \\((1,2)\\); the other two lines cover \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\). Exactly one line (\\(y=2x\\)) is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=2\\).  \n  Use two sunny lines, e.g.\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_{3,2}=\\{y=1,\\;y=2x,\\;y=3x-2\\}.\n  \\]\n  The three lines contain \\((1,1)\\) (on \\(y=1\\)), \\((1,2)\\) (on \\(y=2x\\)), and \\((2,1)\\) (on \\(y=3x-2\\)). Two lines are sunny.\n\n* \\(k=3\\).  \n  Take three sunny lines with distinct slopes, for instance\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_{3,3}=\\{y=2x,\\;y=3x-2,\\;y=4x-3\\}.\n  \\]\n  They pass respectively through \\((1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,1)\\). All three are sunny.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\nsatisfying (1) for \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Inductive step: construction for \\(n+1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).  \nWe shall produce a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines satisfying (1) for \\(T_{n+1}\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nObserve that  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}=T_n\\;\\cup\\;\\{(1,n+1),(2,n),\\dots ,(n,2),(n+1,1)\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the only points that are possibly uncovered when we pass from \\(T_n\\) to \\(T_{n+1}\\) are the points on the two new \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 edges of the triangle, namely the points \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\).\n\nWe treat three cases.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Case \\(k\\le n\\) (the number of sunny lines does not increase)\n\nTake the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) given by the inductive hypothesis.  \nAdd to it a new line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) that is **non\u2011sunny**, i.e. parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, and that passes through the point \\((1,n+1)\\).  \n\n*If we choose a horizontal line* \\(y=n+1\\) (slope \\(0\\)), it is non\u2011sunny and clearly distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) because none of those lines contains the point \\((1,n+1)\\) (all points of \\(T_n\\) have second coordinate at most \\(n\\)).  \n\nThe enlarged family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{y=n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and now covers the whole set \\(T_{n+1}\\) because the only newly required point \\((1,n+1)\\) lies on the added line, while \\((n+1,1)\\) is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) that belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) (such a vertical line exists in every \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) by the explicit construction used in the base case; if it were absent we could instead add the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) \u2013 the argument is symmetric).\n\nHence for every \\(k\\le n\\) we have built \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Case \\(k=n+1\\) (all lines must be sunny)\n\nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) (which has \\(n\\) sunny lines).  \nReplace one of its non\u2011sunny lines (such a line exists because in the construction of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) we may keep a single horizontal line for covering the point \\((1,n)\\)) by a **new sunny line** that passes through the point \\((1,n+1)\\) and has a slope not belonging to \\(S\\) and different from the slopes of all existing sunny lines.  \n\nBecause there are infinitely many slopes outside the finite set  \n\n\\[\nS\\cup\\{\\text{slopes of the }n\\text{ sunny lines in }\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\},\n\\]\n\nsuch a slope can always be chosen, guaranteeing that the new line is sunny and distinct from all previous lines.  \n\nThe resulting family has \\(n+1\\) lines, all of which are sunny, and covers \\(T_{n+1}\\) (the new line covers \\((1,n+1)\\); the other new border point \\((n+1,1)\\) is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) that appears in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) after the replacement).  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,n+1}\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Intermediate values \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) but obtained from a smaller \\(k\\)\n\nThe two constructions above already give families for every \\(k\\le n\\) (by case\u202f4.1) and for \\(k=n+1\\) (by case\u202f4.2).  \nConsequently **all** integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\) are attainable for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) supplies families for all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \nThe inductive step shows that if the statement holds for a given \\(n\\), then it also holds for \\(n+1\\).  \nTherefore, by mathematical induction, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answe