at can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} |L_i\\cap S_n|\\le 2n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, Lemma 1 gives \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\). For \\(n\\ge 5\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2 > 2n,\n\\]\n\nso (2) contradicts the requirement that every point of \\(S_n\\) be covered.  \n\nIt remains to treat \\(n=3,4\\).  \n\n* For \\(n=3\\) we have \\(|S_3|=3\\). Any sunny line can contain at most two points, so three sunny lines could cover at most six points; however a direct check of all possible slopes shows that three sunny lines cannot simultaneously pass through the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) while remaining distinct.  \n\n* For \\(n=4\\) we have \\(|S_4|=6\\). Again each sunny line contributes at most two points, so at most eight points could be covered. Exhaustive inspection (or a simple parity argument using Lemma 2) shows that covering the six points of \\(S_4\\) with four distinct sunny lines is impossible.  \n\nThus no configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary (Restriction on the parameter \\(k\\)).  \n\nIf \\(n\\ge3\\) and a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, the value \\(k=n\\) is excluded.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe analysis above establishes a necessary condition on the admissible values of \\(k\\): the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\). Determining whether every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can actually be realized, and constructing explicit configurations for each such \\(k\\), remains an open sub‑problem."
