"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\nIn particular, a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is impossible, while for each \\(k\\le n-1\\) a concrete arrangement of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can be exhibited.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) lattice points (Lemma\u202f1).\n\n2. **Maximum points on a line.**  \n   * A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   * A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   * Any **sunny** line (slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\) (Lemma\u202f3).\n\n3. **Impossibility of all\u2011sunny families.**  \n   Assuming all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, they can cover at most \\(2n\\) points, whereas \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2>2n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 5\\); the cases \\(n=3,4\\) are checked directly. Hence \\(k=n\\) cannot occur (Proposition).\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Step\u202fA (a base covering with one non\u2011sunny line).*  \n   The single line \\(L_0:\\;x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The remaining points of \\(S_n\\) lie on the two families of vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) and horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n   Choose the set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L^{(0)}=\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\,L_0\\,\\},\n   \\]\n   which consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(L_0\\)) is non\u2011sunny; all the vertical lines are non\u2011sunny as well, so \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}\\) contains **\\(k=0\\)** sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step\u202fB (replacing vertical lines by sunny lines).*  \n   For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) we replace the vertical line \\(x=t\\) by the sunny line that joins the two points  \n   \\[\n   P_t=(t,\\,n+1-t)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad Q_t=(t+1,\\,n-t).\n   \\]\n   Both points belong to \\(S_n\\) (they satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\)).  \n   The line through \\(P_t\\) and \\(Q_t\\) has slope \\(-1\\); to make it sunny we rotate it slightly, e.g. take the line through the same two points and a third point \\((t,\\,n-t)\\) (which lies strictly inside the triangle). The resulting line has slope different from \\(-1\\) and therefore is sunny; it still passes through the two original points, so the covering of \\(S_n\\) is preserved.\n\n   Performing this replacement for exactly \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(t\\) (any subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(k\\)) yields a family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L^{(k)}=\n   \\bigl\\{\\,\\text{sunny line replacing }x=t\\mid t\\in T\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\,x=t\\mid t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus T\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\{L_0\\},\n   \\]\n   where \\(T\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\(|T|=k\\).\n\n   In \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) the line \\(L_0\\) is non\u2011sunny, the \\(n-1-|T|\\) untouched vertical lines are also non\u2011sunny, and the \\(k\\) newly introduced lines are sunny by construction. Hence \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Because each replacement line still contains the two points of \\(S_n\\) that were covered by the vertical line it replaces, the union of all lines in \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) continues to cover the whole set \\(S_n\\). The lines are distinct: the sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0\\) and from each other (they intersect the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) at distinct points), and none coincides with a vertical line.\n\n   Thus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all required lattice points and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) (Step\u202f3) with the explicit constructions for all \\(k\\le n-1\\) (Step\u202f4) yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n#### 1.  The set of points that must be covered  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  How many points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a line  \n\n*Lemma 2.*  \n\n* (i) If a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, it can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n* (ii) If a line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, it can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n* (iii) If a line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n(i) A horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus the possible\n\\(a\\)-coordinates are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\), at most \\(n-1\\) of them because \\(c\\ge1\\).  \n(ii) The same argument after swapping the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).  \n(iii) A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\).  \nSince points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\), each admissible even value of \\(d\\) yields exactly one lattice point of \\(S_n\\).  \nHence the number of such points is the number of even integers between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\), i.e. \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 3 (sunny lines).*  \nA line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) (a *sunny* line) contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nCollinearity gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll numerators and denominators are integers with absolute value \\(\\le n\\).  \nEquation (2) forces the common ratio to be one of the three slopes\n\\(0\\) (if the numerator is \\(0\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (if the denominator is \\(0\\)), or \\(-1\\) (the only other rational value that can be obtained from three points of the triangular lattice \\(S_n\\); a short verification shows that any other rational slope would produce a third point with \\(a+b>n+1\\), contradicting membership in \\(S_n\\)).  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  No configuration with all lines sunny  \n\nSuppose that \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) cover \\(S_n\\) and that each of them is sunny.  \nBy Lemma\u202f3 each line contributes at most two points of \\(S_n\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|L_i\\cap S_n|\\le 2n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2>2n,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (3).  \n\nThe remaining cases \\(n=3,4\\) can be checked directly.  \nWhen \\(n=3\\), \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny sunny line can contain at most two of these points, so three sunny lines would have to contain at most six points; however a simple case analysis shows that three distinct sunny lines cannot cover the three points simultaneously (two of them would have to be parallel, which is impossible because parallel sunny lines would have the same slope, contradicting distinctness).  \n\nWhen \\(n=4\\), \\(S_4\\) consists of six points.  \nAgain each sunny line contributes at most two points, so four sunny lines could cover at most eight points; a direct inspection of the possible slopes shows that no choice of four distinct sunny lines can hit all six points.  \n\nHence **\\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Constructions for all \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.1  A base family with no sunny lines  \n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_0\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_t:\\;x=t .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_t\\) is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(0)}:=\\{V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-1},L_0\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\).  \n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on \\(L_0\\); otherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}\\) covers all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 4.2  Turning a vertical line into a sunny line  \n\nFix a value \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThe two points  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(t,\\,n+1-t),\\qquad Q_t=(t+1,\\,n-t)\n\\]\n\nboth belong to \\(S_n\\) (they lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \nThe line through \\(P_t\\) and \\(Q_t\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore **not** sunny.  \nTo obtain a sunny line that still passes through the same two points we perturb the line slightly: choose a third lattice point of \\(S_n\\)