ce \\(S_t\\) contains exactly the same two points of \\(S_n\\) as the vertical line \\(V_t\\).  \nMoreover, because the slopes \\(-(t-1)\\) are all distinct, the lines \\(S_t\\) are pairwise distinct and none of them coincides with any vertical line \\(V_{t'}\\) or with the diagonal line \\(L_0\\).\n\nThus **replacing** a vertical line \\(V_t\\) by the sunny line \\(S_t\\) leaves the covering of \\(S_n\\) unchanged.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.3  Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(T\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) be any subset with \\(|T|=k\\).  \nForm the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(k)}:=\\bigl\\{\\,S_t\\mid t\\in T\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,V_t\\mid t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus T\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_0\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n- The line \\(L_0\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \n- Each \\(V_t\\) that remains is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n- Each \\(S_t\\) (with \\(t\\in T\\)) is sunny by construction.  \n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause every replacement \\(V_t\\mapsto S_t\\) preserves the two diagonal points that \\(V_t\\) originally covered, the union of all lines in \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) still equals \\(S_n\\).  \nAll lines are distinct: the slopes of the sunny lines are all different, they differ from the vertical slope, and none coincides with the diagonal line \\(L_0\\).\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have exhibited a concrete set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) (Step\u202f3) with the explicit constructions for all \\(k\\le n-1\\) (Step\u202f4) we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nHence the set of admissible num