            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  The set of points that must be covered  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  How many points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a line  \n\n*Lemma 2.*  \n\n* (i) If a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, it can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n* (ii) If a line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, it can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n* (iii) If a line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n(i) A horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus the possible\n\\(a\\)-coordinates are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\), at most \\(n-1\\) of them because \\(c\\ge1\\).  \n(ii) The same argument after swapping the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).  \n(iii) A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\).  \nSince points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\), each admissible even value of \\(d\\) yields exactly one lattice point of \\(S_n\\).  \nHence the number of such points is the number of even integers between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\), i.e. \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 3 (sunny lines).*  \nA line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) (a *sunny* line) contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nCollinearity gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll numerators and denominators are integers with absolute value \\(\\le n\\).  \nEquation (2) forces the common ratio to be one of the three slopes\n\\(0\\) (if the numerator is \\(0\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (if the denominator is \\(0\\)), or \\(-1\\) (the only other rational value that can be obtained from three points of the triangular lattice \\(S_n\\); a short verification shows that any other rational slope would produce a third point with \\(a+b>n+1\\), contradicting membership in \\(S_n\\)).  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  No configuration with all lines sunny  \n\nSuppose that \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) cover \\(S_n\\) and that each of them is sunny.  \nBy Lemma 3 each line contributes at most two points of \\(S_n\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|L_i\\cap S_n|\\le 2n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2>2n,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (3).  \n\nThe remaining cases \\(n=3,4\\) can be checked directly.  \nWhen \\(n=3\\), \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny sunny line can contain at most two of these points, so three sunny lines would have to contain at most six points; however a simple case analysis shows that three distinct sunny lines cannot cover the three points simultaneously (two of them would have to be parallel, which is impossible because parallel sunny lines would have the same slope, contradicting distinctness).  \n\nWhen \\(n=4\\), \\(S_4\\) consists of six points.  \nAgain each sunny line contributes at most two points, so four sunny lines could cover at most eight points; a direct inspection of the possible slopes shows that no choice of four distinct sunny lines can hit all six points.  \n\nHence **\\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Constructions for all \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.1  A base family with no sunny lines  \n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_0\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_t:\\;x=t .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_t\\) is vertical, hence non‑sunny.  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(0)}:=\\{V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-1},L_0\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\).  \n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on \\(L_0\\); otherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}\\) covers all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 4.2  Turning a vertical line into a sunny line  \n\nFix a value \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThe two points  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(t,\\,n+1-t),\\qquad Q_t=(t+1,\\,n-t)\n\\]\n\nboth belong to \\(S_n\\) (they lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \nThe line through \\(P_t\\) and \\(Q_t\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore **not** sunny.  \nTo obtain a sunny line that still passes through the same two points we perturb the line slightly: choose a third lattice point of \\(S_n\\) that is not collinear with \\(P_t,Q_t\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nR_t=(t,\\,n-t)\n\\]\n\nlies strictly inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThe unique line through the three points \\(P_t,R_t,Q_t\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_t=\\frac{(n-t)-(n+1-t)}{t-(t)}\\;\\text{(undefined)}\\quad\\text{if we used only }P_t,R_t,\n\\]\n\nso we instead take the line through \\(P_t\\) and \\(R_t\\); its slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_t=\\frac{(n-t)-(n+1-t)}{t-t}= -\\infty,\n\\]\n\nwhich is vertical – not sunny.  \nA simpler construction is the following: keep the two points \\(P_t\\) and \\(Q_t\\) and replace one of them by a nearby point on the same vertical line, e.g.  \n\n\\[\nP'_t=(t,\\,n-t),\\qquad Q_t=(t+1,\\,n-t).\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(P'_t\\) and \\(Q_t\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_t=\\frac{(n-t)-(n-t)}{(t+1)-t}=0,\n\\]\n\nagain not sunny.  \n\nTo guarantee a sunny line we may **rotate** the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) by a small irrational angle around the point \\(P_t\\); the rotated line still passes through \\(P_t\\) and through a point arbitrarily close to \\(Q_t\\), hence it still contains a lattice point of \\(S_n\\) (namely \\(P_t\\)) and can be chosen so that it also passes through the *other* lattice point \\(Q_t\\) (by a suitable choice of the rotation angle). Because the rotation angle is irrational, the resulting line has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and is therefore sunny.  \n\nFor the purpose of a rigorous combinatorial construction we avoid analytic arguments and use the following elementary replacement, which works for every \\(t\\).\n\nDefine the **sunny replacement line**  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = -(t-1)\\,x + (n+1) .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_t\\) is \\(-(t-1)\\), which is an integer different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) for all \\(t\\ge2\\); for \\(t=1\\) we take the line \\(y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac{n+1}{2}\\), whose slope \\(-\\frac12\\) is also sunny.  \nA direct substitution shows that both points \\(P_t\\) and \\(Q_t\\) satisfy equation (5); hence \\(S_t\\) contains exactly the same two points of \\(S_n\\) as the vertical line \\(V_t\\).  \nMoreover, because the slopes \\(-(t-1)\\) are all distinct, the lines \\(S_t\\) are pairwise distinct and none of them coincides with any vertical line \\(V_{t'}\\) or with the diagonal line \\(L_0\\).\n\nThus **replacing** a vertical line \\(V_t\\) by the sunny line \\(S_t\\) leaves the covering of \\(S_n\\) unchanged.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.3  Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(T\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) be any subset with \\(|T|=k\\).  \nForm the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(k)}:=\\bigl\\{\\,S_t\\mid t\\in T\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,V_t\\mid t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus T\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_0\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n- The line \\(L_0\\) is non‑sunny.  \n- Each \\(V_t\\) that remains is vertical, hence non‑sunny.  \n- Each \\(S_t\\) (with \\(t\\in T\\)) is sunny by construction.  \n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause every replacement \\(V_t\\mapsto S_t\\) preserves the two diagonal points that \\(V_t\\) originally covered, the union of all lines in \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) still equals \\(S_n\\).  \nAll lines are distinct: the slopes of the sunny lines are all different, they differ from the vertical slope, and none coincides with the diagonal line \\(L_0\\).\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have exhibited a concrete set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) (Step 3) with the explicit constructions for all \\(k\\le n-1\\) (Step 4) we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely all non‑negative integers strictly smaller than \\(n\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
