(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(2k\\) points, so the remaining \\(T-2k\\) points must be covered by the other \\(n-k\\) lines, each of which is non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Since a non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) (the longest vertical, horizontal or \\(-1\\)-slope diagonal inside the triangle has length \\(n\\)), we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   T-2k\\le n\\,(n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality is automatically satisfied for all \\(k\\ge0\\); the only restriction comes from the obvious bound \\(2k\\le T\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{T}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n     =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) and put  \n\n   \\[\n   r:=T-2k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 1 – cover \\(r\\) points with vertical (non‑sunny) lines.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the vertical line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); therefore it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{r}{n}\\Big\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   Choose the first \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\).  \n   Because each contributes at most \\(n\\) points, together they cover at least \\(r\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   Moreover, using (1) one checks that  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\le n-k,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   so the number of vertical lines does not exceed the number of non‑sunny lines allowed.\n\n   *Step 2 – cover the remaining \\(2k\\) points with sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).*  \n   After removing the points already covered, exactly \\(2k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) remain uncovered.  \n   These points can be paired as  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)\\ \\text{with}\\ (a+1,b+1),\\qquad\n   1\\le a\\le n-1,\\;1\\le b\\le n-a .\n   \\]\n\n   Each pair lies on the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{c}:\\;x-y=c,\\qquad c=a-b .\n   \\]\n\n   All lines \\(S_{c}\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently they are **sunny** (they are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   The constants \\(c\\) range from \\(-(n-1)\\) to \\(n-1\\), giving \\(2n-1\\) distinct sunny lines of this form.  \n   Because \\(2n-1\\ge 2k\\) for every admissible \\(k\\) (the maximal admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\lfloor n(n+1)/4\\rfloor\\le n\\)), we can select \\(k\\) distinct constants \\(c_{1},\\dots ,c_{k}\\) and let the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{c_j}:\\;x-y=c_j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k)\n   \\]\n\n   each pass through a different pair of the uncovered points.  \n   Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover all the remaining \\(2k\\) points, and no two of them coincide.\n\n   *Step 3 – total number of lines.*  \n   We have used \\(m\\) vertical lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(m+k\\) distinct lines.  \n   By (2) we have \\(m+k\\le (n-k)+k=n\\).  \n   Hence we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\lfloor n(n+1)/4\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, and the construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the interval  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\Big\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   is attainable. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly this interval.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le\\big\\lfloor\\tfrac{n(n+1)}{4}\\big\\rfloor\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as s