ter removing the \\(k\\) points \\(\\{P_i\\}\\) the set \\(T_n\\) still contains, for every integer \\(x\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   k+1\\le x\\le n,\n   \\]\n\n   at least one point (for instance \\((x,1)\\)).  \n   For each such \\(x\\) define the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_x:\\; x=\\text{constant}=x .\n   \\]\n\n   All vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence **not sunny**.  \n   The collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},V_{k+2},\\dots ,V_{n}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-k)=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.\n\n   *Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\).*  \n   \u2013 Each point \\(P_i\\) lies on its dedicated sunny line \\(L_i\\).  \n   \u2013 Any remaining point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a\\ge k+1\\) (otherwise it would be one of the \\(P_i\\)).  \n     Hence \\(a\\) belongs to \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n   Thus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n   Consequently, for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k>n\\).**  \n   Since we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines. Hence no configuration with \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\nCombining the existence part (step\u202f3) with the trivial upper bound (step\u202f2) yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line may have any slope except \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Upper bound)\n\nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The definition of \\(k\\) is the number of sunny lines among the \\(n\\) lines, so clearly \\(k\\le n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction for any prescribed \\(k\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Selection of \\(k\\) special points.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(i+1\\le n+1\\), each \\(P_i\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\), and the \\(x\\)-coordinates of the \\(P_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n2. **Sunny lines through the special points.**  \n   Choose real numbers \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n   \\]\n\n   (Such a choice is possible because the set \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) is infinite.)  \n   For each \\(i\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y-1=\\alpha_i\\,(x-i).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\) and has slope \\(\\alpha_i\\); consequently it is sunny.  \n   Distinct slopes guarantee that the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\n3. **Vertical lines for the remaining \\(x\\)-coordinates.**  \n   For every integer \\(x\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   k+1\\le x\\le n,\n   \\]\n\n   define the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_x:\\; x=\\text{constant}=x .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(V_x\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence not sunny.  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V=\\{V_{k+1},V_{k+2},\\dots ,V_{n}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct lines.\n\n4. **The whole family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal V .\n   \\]\n\n   By construction \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=k+(n-k)=n\\), and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny.\n\n5. **Covering property.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n\n   *If \\(a\\le k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) coincides with the special point \\(P_a\\) (because \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\) when \\(a\\le k\\)). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\).  \n\n   *If \\(a\\ge k+1\\)*, then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\n   Thus every element of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Non\u2011existence for \\(k>n\\))\n\nNo configuration with more than \\(n\\) sunny lines can satisfy the conditions.\n\n*Proof.*  Any admissible configuration uses precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines; therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  \nLemma\u202f3 rules out any \\(k>n\\).\n\nConsequently, for a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

