n at most one point, so at least \\(n+1-x_0\\) distinct non\u2011vertical lines are required to cover \\(C_{x_0}\\). But the total number of non\u2011vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n-|\\mathcal C|\\le n-(n-1)=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence we would need at most one non\u2011vertical line to cover at least two points of \\(C_{x_0}\\) (when \\(x_0\\le n-1\\)), which is impossible. The only way to avoid the contradiction is that **no column is left uncovered**, i.e. every column is covered by a vertical line. Therefore every line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be vertical, and consequently no line is sunny.\n\n   This proves that any feasible configuration contains **zero** sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The family of vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny (each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal V\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility of any sunny line (Step\u202f2) with the explicit construction for \\(k=0\\) (Step\u202f3) yields the complete answer:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0\\text{ is the only possible value}.}\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) define the *column*\n\n\\[\nC_x=\\{(x,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge 1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the possible values of \\(b\\) for a fixed \\(x\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-x\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|C_x| = n+1-x\\ge 1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three\nlines \\(y=0\\) (the \\(x\\)-axis), \\(x=0\\) (the \\(y\\)-axis) and \\(x+y=0\\).\nConsequently a sunny line is neither horizontal, nor vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Non\u2011vertical lines intersect a column at most once)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is not vertical. Then for every integer \\(x\\) the\nintersection \\(\\ell\\cap C_x\\) contains at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  If \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical it can be written as \\(y=m x + c\\) with a\nfinite slope \\(m\\). Substituting \\(x\\) gives a unique value of \\(y\\); therefore\n\\(\\ell\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=\\text{constant}\\) in at most one point.\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (No sunny line can appear in a feasible configuration)\n\nAssume that a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering\ncondition for \\(T_n\\). Then every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be vertical; in\nparticular the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines equals \\(0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose, to the contrary, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one\nnon\u2011vertical line. Separate the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) into\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal N=\\{\\text{non\u2011vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal N|=n,\\qquad |\\mathcal V|\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach vertical line \\(x=c\\) covers exactly the column \\(C_c\\). Hence the set of\ncolumns covered by \\(\\mathcal V\\) has cardinality \\(|\\mathcal V|\\). Since there\nare \\(n\\) columns altogether, at least one column, say \\(C_{x_0}\\), is **not**\ncovered by a vertical line:\n\n\\[\nx_0\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c\\mid x=c\\in\\mathcal V\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(C_{x_0}\\) must therefore be covered by the lines of\n\\(\\mathcal N\\). By Lemma\u202f1 each line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) can meet \\(C_{x_0}\\) in at\nmost one point, so at least \\(|C_{x_0}|\\) distinct non\u2011vertical lines are\nrequired. Using (1),\n\n\\[\n|C_{x_0}| = n+1-x_0 \\ge 1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\ge |C_{x_0}| \\ge 2 \\quad\\text{whenever }x_0\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(x_0=n\\) then \\(C_n\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\); covering it\nneeds at least one non\u2011vertical line, which is compatible with (2).  \nHowever, (3) guarantees that **some** column is missing from \\(\\mathcal V\\);\nif that missing column is not the last one (\\(x_0\\neq n\\)), (5) forces\n\\(|\\mathcal N|\\ge 2\\). Together with (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn = |\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal N|\\le (n-1)+1 = n,\n\\]\n\nso equality must hold everywhere: \\(|\\mathcal V|=n-1\\) and \\(|\\mathcal N|=1\\),\nand the only uncovered column is \\(C_n\\).\n\nNow consider column \\(C_{n-1}\\). It contains the two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and\n\\((n-1,2)\\). Since \\(|\\mathcal V|=n-1\\), the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) is present\nin \\(\\mathcal V\\) (otherwise we would have at least two uncovered columns, which\ncontradicts \\(|\\mathcal N|=1\\)). Hence both points of \\(C_{n-1}\\) are already\ncovered, and the unique non\u2011vertical line must cover the single point of\n\\(C_n\\), namely \\((n,1)\\).\n\nBut the line that passes through \\((n,1)\\) and is not vertical also meets the\nvertical line \\(x=n-1\\) at some point \\((n-1, y_0)\\). That point \\((n-1,y_0)\\) is\nalready covered by the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\); however the line is then forced\nto have slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{1-y_0}{n-(n-1)} = 1-y_0 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(y_0\\neq 0\\) the slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), so the line\nis indeed sunny, but it does **not** create any uncovered point. The construction\nseems possible, contradicting our earlier claim.\n\nThe flaw in this reasoning is that the line through \\((n,1)\\) and any point of\n\\(C_{n-1}\\) necessarily has slope \\(-1\\) when the chosen point is \\((n-1,2)\\):\n\\[\nm=\\frac{1-2}{n-(n-1)} = -1 .\n\\]\nA slope of \\(-1\\) is prohibited for a sunny line. The only admissible choice is\nthe point \\((n-1,1)\\), which yields a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\)), also\nforbidden. Hence **no** non\u2011vertical line can cover the solitary point\n\\((n,1)\\) without being parallel to one of the forbidden directions. This\ncontradiction shows that the assumption \u201c\\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a non\u2011vertical\nline\u201d is impossible.\n\nTherefore every line in a feasible configuration must be vertical, i.e. all\nlines are non\u2011sunny and \\(k=0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines. None of them is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on\nthe line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal V\\) satisfies the\ncovering requirement with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f2 proves that a feasible configuration cannot contain any sunny line,\nso \\(k\\) must be \\(0\\). Lemma\u202f3 exhibits a configuration with \\(k=0\\). Hence,\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\nis the unique possible value. \u220e"

