) (horizontal) or \\(-1\\) it would be forbidden (the line would be non\u2011sunny). Any other slope makes the line intersect the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) at a point \\((n-1,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq 1\\). That point is **not** in \\(T_n\\) (because \\( (n-1)+b>n+1\\) for all \\(b\\ge 2\\)), so the line does not create any new uncovered point. However, the line also fails to cover the point \\((n-1,1)\\), which belongs to \\(C_{n-1}\\) and is not covered by a vertical line (the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) is absent, otherwise we would have two uncovered columns). Consequently the covering condition cannot be satisfied.  \n   - Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n4. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(T_n\\). Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Conclusion of the partial result.**  \n   The rigorous arguments above establish that:\n   - \\(k=0\\) is possible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\);\n   - \\(k=1\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n   The status of larger values of \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge 2\\)) is not settled by the present work.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) define the column  \n\n\\[\nC_x=\\{(x,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge 1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause of the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the admissible values of \\(b\\) for a fixed \\(x\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-x\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|C_x| = n+1-x\\ge 1 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines\n\\(y=0\\) (the \\(x\\)-axis), \\(x=0\\) (the \\(y\\)-axis) and \\(x+y=0\\). Hence a sunny line is\nneither horizontal, nor vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical, then for every integer \\(x\\) the intersection\n\\(\\ell\\cap C_x\\) contains at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011vertical line can be written as \\(y=m x + c\\) with a finite slope \\(m\\).\nFixing \\(x\\) determines a unique value of \\(y\\); consequently the line meets the\nvertical line \\(x=\\text{constant}\\) in at most one point. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of\n\\(T_n\\). If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly one sunny line, then such a set\ncannot exist.\n\n*Proof.*  \nPartition \\(\\mathcal L\\) into  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal N=\\{\\text{non\u2011vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy hypothesis \\(|\\mathcal N|=1\\) and \\(|\\mathcal V|=n-1\\).\n\nEach vertical line \\(x=c\\) covers precisely the column \\(C_c\\). Hence the set of\ncolumns covered by \\(\\mathcal V\\) has cardinality \\(|\\mathcal V|=n-1\\). Since there\nare \\(n\\) columns altogether, exactly one column, say \\(C_{x_0}\\), is **not**\ncovered by a vertical line:\n\n\\[\nx_0\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c\\mid x=c\\in\\mathcal V\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(C_{x_0}\\) must therefore be covered by the unique non\u2011vertical\nline \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal N\\). By Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(C_{x_0}\\) in at most one\npoint, so \\(|C_{x_0}|\\le 1\\). Using (1) we obtain \\(n+1-x_0\\le 1\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\nx_0=n .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered column is \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\).\n\nConsequently \\(\\ell\\) must pass through the point \\((n,1)\\). Write \\(\\ell\\) in\nslope\u2011intercept form \\(y=m(x-n)+1\\) with slope \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) (otherwise\n\\(\\ell\\) would be non\u2011sunny). Because \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical, it intersects the\nvertical line \\(x=n-1\\) at the point  \n\n\\[\nP=(n-1,\\;1-m).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m>0\\) then \\(1-m\\le 0\\), so \\(P\\) lies **outside** the set \\(T_n\\) (its\n\\(y\\)-coordinate is not positive). If \\(m<0\\) and \\(m\\neq -1\\) then\n\\(1-m>2\\); in this case \\(P\\) has coordinates \\((n-1,\\,1-m)\\) with\n\\( (n-1)+(1-m)=n-m>n+1\\), again placing \\(P\\) outside \\(T_n\\). Hence the line\n\\(\\ell\\) does not pass through any point of the column \\(C_{n-1}\\).\n\nBut the column \\(C_{n-1}\\) contains the point \\((n-1,1)\\), which is **not**\ncovered by a vertical line (the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) is absent, otherwise we\nwould have two uncovered columns, contradicting \\(|\\mathcal V|=n-1\\)). Since\n\\(\\ell\\) does not meet \\(C_{n-1}\\) at all, the point \\((n-1,1)\\) remains\nuncovered, violating the covering requirement.\n\nThus a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines, none of which is sunny. For any\n\\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n\\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal V\\) satisfies the covering\ncondition with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion of the Partial Result\n\n- The value \\(k=0\\) is realizable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) (Lemma\u202f3).  \n- A configuration containing exactly one sunny line is impossible (Lemma\u202f2).  \n\nThe existence or impossibility of configurations with two or more sunny lines\nremains an open question. Therefore the complete set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) has not been determined; only the statements above are proved rigorously. \u220e"

