ecisely the column \\(C_c\\). Hence the set of\ncolumns covered by \\(\\mathcal V\\) has cardinality \\(|\\mathcal V|=n-1\\). Since there\nare \\(n\\) columns altogether, exactly one column, say \\(C_{x_0}\\), is **not**\ncovered by a vertical line:\n\n\\[\nx_0\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c\\mid x=c\\in\\mathcal V\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(C_{x_0}\\) must therefore be covered by the unique non‑vertical\nline \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal N\\). By Lemma 1, \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(C_{x_0}\\) in at most one\npoint, so \\(|C_{x_0}|\\le 1\\). Using (1) we obtain \\(n+1-x_0\\le 1\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\nx_0=n .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered column is \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\), and \\(\\ell\\) must pass through\n\\((n,1)\\). Write \\(\\ell\\) in slope‑intercept form\n\n\\[\ny=m(x-n)+1,\n\\]\n\nwhere the slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be\nnon‑sunny).\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) at the point  \n\n\\[\nP=(n-1,\\,1-m).\n\\]\n\n*If \\(m>0\\):* then \\(1-m<1\\); consequently the \\(y\\)-coordinate of \\(P\\) is\n\\(\\le 0\\), so \\(P\\notin T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(m<0\\) and \\(m\\neq -1\\):* then \\(1-m>2\\) and  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(1-m)=n-m>n+1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(P\\) lies outside the region defined by \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); again \\(P\\notin\nT_n\\).\n\nThus \\(\\ell\\) does **not** intersect the column \\(C_{n-1}\\). Because there is no\nvertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (otherwise we would have two uncovered columns, contradicting\n\\(|\\mathcal V|=n-1\\)), the point \\((n-1,1)\\in C_{n-1}\\) remains uncovered, which\ncontradicts the covering requirement. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (No configuration with two or more sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\),\nthen \\(\\mathcal L\\) cannot contain \\(s\\ge 2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny (hence non‑vertical) lines and let\n\\(v=n-s\\) be the number of vertical lines. As in Lemma 2, the vertical lines\ncover exactly \\(v\\) distinct columns, leaving exactly \\(s\\) columns uncovered.\nDenote these uncovered columns by  \n\n\\[\nC_{x_1},C_{x_2},\\dots ,C_{x_s},\\qquad 1\\le x_1<x_2<\\dots <x_s\\le n .\n\\]\n\nBecause the sizes of the columns decrease with the index, the **largest**\namong them is \\(C_{x_1}\\) and has size  \n\n\\[\n|C_{x_1}|=n+1-x_1\\ge s .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(|C_{x_1}|<s\\) then the total number of points lying in the\nuncovered columns would be at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{s}|C_{x_i}|\\le s(s-1)/2 < s\\cdot s/2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible because the whole set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\ge\n\\frac{s(s+1)}{2}\\) points and the vertical lines already cover all points of the\nother \\(v\\) columns.\n\nEach sunny line is non‑vertical, so by Lemma 1 it can meet a given column in at\nmost one point. Consequently the \\(s\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(s\\) points\nof the column \\(C_{x_1}\\). Since \\(|C_{x_1}|\\ge s\\) by (3), the only way to\ncover all points of \\(C_{x_1}\\) is to have \\(|C_{x_1}|=s\\) and for every sunny\nline to pass through a **different** point of \\(C_{x_1}\\).\n\nNow consider the next uncovered column \\(C_{x_2}\\), whose size is\n\\(|C_{x_2}|=|C_{x_1}|-1=s-1\\). The same \\(s\\) sunny lines must also cover all\npoints of \\(C_{x_2}\\). Because each line already uses one point of \\(C_{x_1}\\),\nit can meet \\(C_{x_2}\\) in at most one additional point; thus the total number\nof points of \\(C_{x_2}\\) that can be covered is at most \\(s\\). However,\n\\(|C_{x_2}|=s-1\\), so this is not contradictory yet.\n\nProceeding to the last uncovered column \\(C_{x_s}\\), whose size is\n\\(|C_{x_s}|=1\\), we see that the \\(s\\) sunny lines would have to pass through\nthe same point of \\(C_{x_s}\\) (the only point of that column). That would mean\nthat two distinct sunny lines share a common point of \\(T_n\\). By Lemma 1 this is\nimpossible, because two distinct non‑vertical lines cannot intersect the same\ncolumn in the same point. Hence a configuration with \\(s\\ge 2\\) sunny lines\ncannot exist. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe family of vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny. For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)\nthe abscissa \\(a\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the\nline \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V\\). Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered\nand exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines are present. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\n- Lemma 2 excludes the case \\(k=1\\).  \n- Lemma 3 excludes all cases \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n- Lemma 4 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"
