on \\(\\ell_n\\).\n* Otherwise \\(2\\le a\\le n-1\\). Setting \\(i=a+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{(a+1)-2}{(a+1)-1}\\,x + 1 = \\frac{a-1}{a}\\,x + 1 .\n\\]\n\nPlugging \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y = \\frac{a-1}{a}\\,a + 1 = a-1+1 = a\\).  \nSince \\(b = n+1-a - (a-1) = n+2-2a\\) and the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le a\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) indeed satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_{a+1}\\). Hence each such point is covered.\n\nThus we have a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Inductive step)\n\n*Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen for \\(n+1\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) by \\(n+1\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nFix a covering \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (exists by the induction hypothesis).  \nThe new points that have to be covered are those on the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe enlarge \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) to a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines.\n\n*Case 1 – keep the number of sunny lines unchanged.*  \nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{new}: x = n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) with abscissa \\(n+1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{new}\\).  \nEvery other point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) has abscissa \\(\\le n\\); subtracting \\(1\\) from its second coordinate yields a point of \\(T_n\\), which is already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell_{new}\\) coincides with an existing line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) (the only possible coincidence is with a vertical line), replace that existing vertical line by a parallel translate (e.g. \\(x=n\\)); this does not affect the covering of the finite set \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \nThus we obtain a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n*Case 2 – increase the number of sunny lines by one.*  \nProceed as in Case 1, but instead of the vertical line we add a sunny line, for instance  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{new}: y = \\alpha x + \\beta ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (so the line is sunny) and \\(\\beta\\) is chosen so that \\(\\ell_{new}\\) passes through the point \\((n+1,1)\\in D_{n+1}\\).  \nAll other points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) are covered exactly as in Case 1.  \nIf by accident \\(\\ell_{new}\\) coincides with a line already present, perturb \\(\\beta\\) slightly; the finite set \\(T_{n+1}\\) remains covered because the perturbed line still contains \\((n+1,1)\\) and the other points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) are handled by the unchanged lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nHence we obtain a covering with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nStarting from the configurations for \\(n\\) that realise every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), the two constructions give configurations for \\(n+1\\) that realise every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the Induction\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three points of \\(T_3\\) are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three axis‑parallel lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=2\\) cover them and none is sunny.  \n* \\(k=1\\): the lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=-x+3\\) cover them; only the last line is sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 1, \\(k=2\\) or \\(k=3\\) are impossible. Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nLemma 3 yields the statement for \\(n+1\\).  \nThus, by induction, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFinally, Lemma 1 shows that no larger value of \\(k\\) can occur, completing the classification.\n\n-----------------------------