\\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\ge 1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(b=a+(j-1)\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\n*If \\(k+2\\le a\\le n-1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\) which is part of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nHence for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non‑existence for \\(k\\ge n-1\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that there is a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing at most one sunny line.  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are parallel to one of \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y,\\ell_{-1}\\).\n\nConsider the set of points in the first column  \n\n\\[\nC_1:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nEach line that is parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\) (i.e. a vertical line) can contain **at most one** point of \\(C_1\\), because distinct vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates.  \nA line parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\) (horizontal) cannot meet \\(C_1\\) at all, and a line parallel to \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) meets \\(C_1\\) in at most one point as well (the equation \\(x+y=c\\) together with \\(x=1\\) gives a unique \\(y\\)).  \n\nHence **any** line, regardless of its direction, contains at most one point of \\(C_1\\).  \nSince \\(|C_1|=n\\), covering all points of \\(C_1\\) requires at least \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nIf \\(\\mathcal F\\) had at most one sunny line, then at most one line could be non‑parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines would be parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\) (vertical).  \nAll vertical lines are parallel to each other, so they share the same direction; consequently they can intersect the column \\(C_1\\