1.  How many required points can a non‑sunny line contain?\n\nThe set of points that must be covered is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nIt contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence at most \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nA vertical line has equation \\(x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nIt contains at most \\(n+1-a\\le n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nA diagonal line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) on this line are precisely \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\ge 1\\).  \nThus the number of such points equals \\(\\min\\{c-1,\\,2n+2-c\\}\\).  \nSince \\(c\\le n+1\\), this minimum is at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). Consequently each diagonal line contains at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Counting the points covered by the non‑sunny lines\n\nLet \\(N_{\\text{cov}}\\) be the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that lie on at least one non‑sunny line.  \nUsing the bounds above we obtain\n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{cov}}\n\\;\\le\\;\nh\\cdot n\\;+\\;v\\cdot n\\;+\\;d\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must be covered by *some* line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{cov}}\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) yields  \n\n\\[\nh\\,n+v\\,n+d\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Deriving a