            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed and let a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two conditions of the problem.\n\nDenote by  \n\n* \\(h\\) the number of horizontal lines (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(v\\) the number of vertical lines (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(d\\) the number of diagonal lines (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)) in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nAll remaining lines are sunny, so the number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nk=n-(h+v+d).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points that a non‑sunny line can contain\n\nDefine the set of points that must be covered:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is obtained by summing, for each possible sum \\(s=a+b\\) (with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)), the number of ordered pairs \\((a,b)\\) giving that sum:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nIt contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); therefore it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nA vertical line has equation \\(x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nIt contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) by the same reasoning.\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nA diagonal line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) on this line are exactly \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); hence the line contains precisely \\(c-1\\le n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nConsequently each non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Counting the points covered by the non‑sunny lines\n\nLet \\(N_{\\text{cov}}\\) be the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that lie on at least one non‑sunny line.  \nUsing the bound above we obtain\n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{cov}}\\;\\le\\;(h+v+d)\\,n \\;=\\;(n-k)\\,n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must be covered by *some* line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), we have\n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{cov}}\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) gives\n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nDividing by the positive integer \\(n\\) yields\n\n\\[\nn-k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince the left‑hand side is an integer, this is equivalent to\n\n\\[\nn-k\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nFinally, using (1) we obtain the required lower bound on the number of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nAny collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the two conditions of the problem must contain at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe converse direction (that every integer \\(k\\) in this interval can actually be realised) has not been proved here.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
