e \\(y=c\\) can contain at most the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+c\\le n+1\\); hence at most \\(n+1-c\\) points. Since \\(c\\ge1\\), the maximal possible number of points on any horizontal line is \\(n\\). The same bound holds for a vertical line.\n\nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) contains exactly the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\ge1\\); therefore it contains at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points.\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\ns\\cdot n .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints. If \\(s<\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) then \\(s\\le \\frac{n-1}{2}\\) and\n\n\\[\ns\\cdot n\\le \\frac{n-1}{2}\\,n = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the assumption that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers all points. Hence \\(s\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(s\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le s\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines that covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nr:=s-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\qquad (0\\le r\\le n-\\lceil n/2\\rceil).\n\\]\n\nWe shall construct \\(\\mathcal L\\) as the union of three sub‑families:\n\n1. **Diagonal lines.**  \n   Take the \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_i:\\;x+y=i+1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(D_i\\) lies completely inside the triangle bounded by the axes and the line \\(x+y=n+1\\); therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(i+1\\) lies on \\(D_i\\).\n\n2. **Additional non‑sunny lines (if any).**  \n   If \\(r>0\\) we need \\(r\\) more non‑sunny lines.  \n   Choose the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,r)\n   \\]\n\n   (