*\n\n1. **Problem Restatement**  \n   The task is to determine, for a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\), all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n2. **Counting Argument (Necessary Condition)**  \n\n   *Quoted text:*  \n   \u201cFor a line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions we compute the maximal number of points of the set  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]  \n   that it can contain.  \n   \u2013 A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points.  \n   \u2013 A vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points.  \n   \u2013 A diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) contains at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points.  \n\n   Hence any family of \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(sn\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), the inequality \\(sn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) forces  \n   \\[\n   s\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\u201d\n\n   **Verification:**  \n   - The maximal numbers of points on a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line are correctly identified: for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) the admissible \\(a\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), giving \\(n+1-c\\) points, which is indeed \\(\\le n\\). The same reasoning holds for vertical lines.  \n   - For a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), the integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) are \\((1,c-1), (2,c-2),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\) of them, and because \\(c\\le n+1\\) we have \\(c-1\\le n\\). Thus each non\u2011sunny line covers **at most** \\(n\\) points.  \n\n   - Consequently a set of \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines covers at most \\(sn\\) points.  \n\n   - The total number of points in \\(T_n\\) is \\(|T_n| = 1+2+\\dots + n = n(n+1)/2\\).  \n\n   - The inequality required for covering all points is therefore  \n     \\[\n     sn \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n     \\]  \n     Dividing by \\(n\\) (which is positive) yields  \n     \\[\n     s \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n+1}{2}.\n     \\]  \n     Since \\(s\\) is an integer, the correct bound is  \n     \\[\n     s \\;\\ge\\; \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n     \\]  \n\n   - The solution incorrectly writes \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) instead of \\(\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\). For even \\(n\\) this underestimates the required number of non\u2011sunny lines by one (e.g. for \\(n=4\\) the correct bound is \\(s\\ge3\\), but the solution claims \\(s\\ge2\\)).  \n\n   **Conclusion:** This is a **Critical Error** because the derived necessary condition on \\(s\\) (and hence on \\(k=n-s\\)) is false for even \\(n\\). The subsequent claim that \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is therefore not justified; the true maximal \\(k\\) is \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). The error breaks the logical chain of the proof, and any later construction that assumes the weaker bound may not be sufficient.\n\n   *Because a Critical Error has been found, the remainder of the argument that depends on this bound cannot be taken as valid. Nevertheless, we continue to examine the construction to identify any further gaps.*\n\n3. **Construction (Sufficiency)**  \n\n   *Quoted text (first part):*  \n   \u201cLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and put \\(s=n-k\\) (\\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le s\\le n\\)).  \n   Write \\(d:=\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) and \\(t:=s-d\\) (so \\(0\\le t\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   - **Diagonal lines.** For each \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,d+1\\) take the line  \n     \\[\n     D_i:\\;x+y=i .\n     \\]  \n   - **Vertical lines.** For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) take the line  \n     \\[\n     V_j:\\;x=j .\n     \\]\u201d\n\n   **Verification