\\(U\\), writing simply \u201c\\(b\\ge1\\)\u201d. This omission is a **Justification Gap**: the set \\(U\\) as written includes points that may violate \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). The subsequent counting (formula (2)) implicitly uses the correct triangular bound, but the explicit description of \\(U\\) is inaccurate.\n\n5. **Counting Uncovered Points**  \n\n   *Quoted text:*  \n   \u201cIts cardinality is\n   \\[\n   |U|=\\sum_{a=t+1}^{n} (n+1-a)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\u201d\n\n   **Verification:**  \n   - Assuming the correct set of uncovered points is \\(\\{(a,b)\\in T_n : a\\ge t+1\\}\\), the number of admissible \\(b\\) for a fixed \\(a\\) is indeed \\(n+1-a\\). Summing from \\(a=t+1\\) to \\(n\\) yields \\(\\frac{(n-t)(n-t+1)}2\\). Since \\(k=n-s = n-(d+t) = n-(\\lceil n/2\\rceil + t)\\) and a short algebraic check shows \\(n-t = k\\), the formula \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) is correct.  \n   - The computation itself is sound, but it relies on the (previously noted) inaccurate description of \\(U\\). Hence this step is acceptable **provided** the intended set of uncovered points is the one that respects the triangle inequality.\n\n6. **Choosing Points and Adding Sunny Lines**  \n\n   *Quoted text:*  \n   \u201cChoose the \\(k\\) uncovered points\n   \\[\n   P_i=(t+i,\\;k+1-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k)\n   \\]  \n   \u2026 Through each \\(P_i\\) draw a line of slope \\(2\\) (any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) would work).  \n   Denote these lines by \\(S_i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\\).  \n   Because the slopes are equal but the points are distinct, the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct and, by construction, each contains one of the previously uncovered points.\u201d\n\n   **Verification:**  \n   - The points \\(P_i\\) indeed satisfy \\(a = t+i \\ge t+1\\) and \\(b = k+1-i \\ge 1\\); they also satisfy \\(a+b = t+i + k+1-i = t+k+1 = n+1 - (d-1)\\). A brief check shows they lie inside the triangle, so they belong to the uncovered set.  \n   - All \\(P_i\\) are distinct because the first coordinate increases with \\(i\\).  \n   - Lines of slope\u202f2 through distinct points are distinct (they cannot coincide because coincident lines would have infinitely many common points).  \n   - Since slope\u202f2 is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\), each new line is indeed sunny.  \n\n   **Gap:** The solution does not explicitly argue that none of these sunny lines coincides with any of the previously introduced non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, diagonal). While the slope argument excludes coincidence with horizontal, vertical, or slope\u202f\\(-1\\) lines, a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) has slope \\(-1\\), so there is no risk. Hence the omission is minor, but a rigorous proof would mention this.\n\n7. **Final Count and Conclusion**  \n\n   *Quoted text:*  \n   \u201cThe total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   d\\;(\\text{diagonal})\\;+\\;t\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})=d+t+k=n,\n   \\]  \n   and exactly \\(s=d+t=n-k\\) of them are non\u2011sunny.  Thus we have built a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\u201d\n\n   **Verification:** Algebraically correct given the definitions of \\(d,t,k\\).  \n\n8. **Overall Assessment**  \n\n   The construction works **provided** the necessary condition on \\(k\\) is the correct one. However, the solution\u2019s necessary\u2011condition derivation contains a **Critical Error**: it underestimates the minimal number of non\u2011sunny lines for even \\(n\\). Consequently the claimed admissible range \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is too large when \\(n\\) is even (the true maximum is \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\)). Because this error invalidates the main theorem, the solution as a whole is incorrect. The later steps, while mostly sound, also contain minor justification gaps as noted."

