            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nFor a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines we use the following notation:\n\n* \\(h\\) – number of horizontal lines (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis);\n* \\(v\\) – number of vertical lines (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* \\(d\\) – number of diagonal lines (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\));\n* \\(k\\) – number of sunny lines, i.e. lines that are **not** parallel to any of the three directions above.\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n-k. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary lower bound for \\(k\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is obtained by summing, for each possible sum\n\\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)), the number \\(s-1\\) of ordered pairs giving that sum:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nIt contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); therefore it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) contains exactly the points\n\\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); hence it contains \\(c-1\\le n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nConsequently **every non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\)**.  \nIf we let \\(N_{\\text{cov}}\\) denote the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that lie on at least one non‑sunny line, then by (1)\n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{cov}}\\le (h+v+d)\\,n=(n-k)n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must be covered by *some* line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{cov}}\\ge |\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n-k\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the left‑hand side is an integer, this is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nRe‑arranging yields the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nAssume that an integer \\(k\\) satisfies (5).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nr:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad\ns:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(r+s=n-k\\) and \\(r,s\\ge 0\\).  \nWe now exhibit a concrete family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny part\n\nTake the following \\(r\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=r,\n\\]\n\nand the following \\(s\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=s .\n\\]\n\nThese are all distinct and none of them is sunny.  \nWe claim that **every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of these lines**.\n\nIndeed, let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(r+s=n-k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) (by (5)), we have  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\le (r+s)+1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a> s\\) and \\(b> r\\) simultaneously, then  \n\n\\[\na+b > s+r = n-k \\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(n+1\\le 2\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\); the inequality \\(a+b> \\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) does **not** contradict the bound.  \nA sharper argument is obtained by a direct case analysis:\n\n*If \\(a\\le s\\)*, then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to our set) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(a> s\\)*, then necessarily \\(b\\le r\\) because otherwise \\(a+b> s+r = n-k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) would force \\(a+b\\ge n+2\\), contradicting the definition of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \nHence \\(b\\le r\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to our set) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered by at least one of the chosen horizontal or vertical lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Adding the sunny lines\n\nWe still have to introduce exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct slopes that are not \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (diagonal).  \nFor each of these slopes pick a line that passes through a point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) already covered by the horizontal/vertical lines; for instance, for the \\(i\\)-th sunny line we may take the point \\((i,1)\\) (which lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) and define the line\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-1 = m_i\\,(x-i),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m_i\\) is the chosen sunny slope.  \nBecause the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct and none of them is parallel to the three forbidden directions, i.e. they are sunny.\n\nAdding these \\(k\\) sunny lines to the previously selected \\(r+s=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines yields a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are already covered by the horizontal/vertical lines, so the covering condition remains satisfied after the sunny lines are added.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Verification of the parameters\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nWe have used \\(r+s=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, totalling \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nBy construction there are precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (if \\(b\\le r\\)) or on a vertical line \\(x=a\\) (if \\(a\\le s\\)). Hence the required covering property holds.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5) we have exhibited a concrete configuration meeting both conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (5) and the explicit construction above together show that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\Bigl\\lceil \\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil \\;\\le\\; k \\;\\le\\; n \\; } .\n\\]\n\nNo smaller value of \\(k\\) is possible, and every integer in this interval can be realised. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
