t most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)): contains exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nThus **any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).**  \n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then it has \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, which together can cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n\\ge |\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n>0\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is integral,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\) satisfies (2) and set  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }r\\ge0).\n\\]\n\n#### 3.1  The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(r\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct and non\u2011sunny.\n\n#### 3.2  The sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = x-(r-t). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(L_t\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny (it is not horizontal, not vertical, and not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) are pairwise distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n#### 3.3  Proof that every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le r\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (3) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a>r\\).*  \nHence \\(a\\ge r+1\\). From the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1=r+k+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le r+k+1-a\\le k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le b\\le k\\); write \\(b=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\